// Expanded explanations for JJ QBank (FBS). Generated; edit the source notes, not this file.
window.XP = Object.assign(window.XP || {}, {"fa1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cytosol = the fluid (gel) portion of the cell; cytoplasm = cytosol + organelles + cytoskeleton + inclusions (everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus).</p><p>Think \"cytosol = solution.\" Many pathways run in the cytosol (glycolysis, fatty acid synthesis, pentose phosphate pathway, translation on free ribosomes). The nucleus has its own fluid, the nucleoplasm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cytoplasm is the nuclear contents</b> — The nuclear contents are the nucleoplasm.</li><li><b>They are identical</b> — They differ: cytosol is only the fluid part of the cytoplasm.</li><li><b>Cytosol is extracellular</b> — Cytosol is intracellular, not extracellular.</li><li><b>Cytoplasm is only the fluid; cytosol is the fluid plus the nucleus and organelles</b> — This reverses the two terms.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytosolic pathways: glycolysis, FA synthesis, HMP shunt.</p>","w":["The nuclear contents are the nucleoplasm.","They differ: cytosol is only the fluid part of the cytoplasm.","Cytosol is intracellular, not extracellular.","Correct — cytosol is the fluid; cytoplasm is cytosol plus organelles.","This reverses the two terms."]},"fa2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Meiosis I: 1 diploid cell → 2 haploid cells (homologs separate). Meiosis II: 2 haploid cells → 4 haploid cells (sister chromatids separate).</p><p>Ploidy halves in meiosis I (reductional division); DNA content halves again in meiosis II (equational division). In DNA terms: 2n/4c → 1n/2c → 1n/1c.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Meiosis I: 1 diploid → 4 haploid</b> — Four cells form only after meiosis II, not meiosis I.</li><li><b>Meiosis II: 2 diploid → 2 diploid</b> — Meiosis II starts with haploid cells and ends with four haploid cells.</li><li><b>Both divisions keep diploidy</b> — Ploidy halves in meiosis I, so diploidy isn't kept.</li><li><b>Meiosis I: 1 haploid in → 2 diploid out; meiosis II: 4 diploid cells out</b> — The starting cell is diploid, and the products are haploid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MI = reductional; MII = equational.</p>","w":["Four cells form only after meiosis II, not meiosis I.","Meiosis II starts with haploid cells and ends with four haploid cells.","Correct — 1 diploid → 2 haploid in meiosis I; 4 haploid after meiosis II.","Ploidy halves in meiosis I, so diploidy isn't kept.","The starting cell is diploid, and the products are haploid."]},"fa3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth ER synthesizes lipids and steroid hormones (and detoxifies drugs); it's abundant in Leydig cells, adrenal cortex and ovarian steroid cells.</p><p>Steroidogenic cells look pale/foamy because of lipid droplets and abundant SER. Several steroidogenic enzymes (P450s) sit in the SER and mitochondria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomes degrade macromolecules.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins.</li><li><b>Rough ER</b> — The rough ER makes secretory and membrane proteins.</li><li><b>Nucleolus</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SER-rich cells: Leydig, adrenal cortex, hepatocytes.</p>","w":["Lysosomes degrade macromolecules.","The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins.","The rough ER makes secretory and membrane proteins.","Correct — smooth ER.","The nucleolus makes rRNA."]},"fa4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> COPII vesicles carry newly folded proteins forward (anterograde) from ER exit sites to the Golgi; COPI carries escaped proteins back (retrograde).</p><p>Mnemonic: \"COPII goes to (two) the Golgi; COPI goes back.\" Clathrin coats vesicles from the trans-Golgi to lysosomes/endosomes and from the plasma membrane (endocytosis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Plasma membrane → endosome</b> — Plasma membrane → endosome transport uses clathrin.</li><li><b>Retrograde, Golgi → ER, of escaped ER proteins</b> — Retrograde Golgi → ER transport uses COPI.</li><li><b>Nucleus → cytoplasm</b> — Nucleus → cytoplasm transport passes through nuclear pores.</li><li><b>Golgi → lysosome</b> — Golgi → lysosome transport uses M6P receptors in clathrin-coated vesicles.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> COPII anterograde; COPI retrograde; clathrin endocytosis + TGN→lysosome.</p>","w":["Correct — anterograde ER → Golgi.","Plasma membrane → endosome transport uses clathrin.","Retrograde Golgi → ER transport uses COPI.","Nucleus → cytoplasm transport passes through nuclear pores.","Golgi → lysosome transport uses M6P receptors in clathrin-coated vesicles."]},"fa5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> rRNA is transcribed in the nucleolus by RNA polymerase I (45S → 28S, 18S, 5.8S); 5S rRNA is made by Pol III in the nucleoplasm.</p><p>RNA polymerases: Pol I → rRNA (most), Pol II → mRNA (and snRNA, miRNA), Pol III → tRNA and 5S rRNA. α-Amanitin (death cap mushroom) inhibits Pol II.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mitochondria; RNA polymerase III only</b> — Mitochondria transcribe only their own small rRNAs.</li><li><b>Golgi; RNA polymerase II</b> — The Golgi doesn't transcribe RNA.</li><li><b>Rough ER; RNA polymerase I</b> — The rough ER translates proteins; it doesn't transcribe rRNA.</li><li><b>Cytoplasm; RNA polymerase II (all rRNAs)</b> — Pol II makes mRNA, and rRNA isn't made in the cytoplasm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"I, II, III → rRNA, mRNA, tRNA\"; α-amanitin blocks Pol II.</p>","w":["Correct — nucleolus; RNA polymerase I.","Mitochondria transcribe only their own small rRNAs.","The Golgi doesn't transcribe RNA.","The rough ER translates proteins; it doesn't transcribe rRNA.","Pol II makes mRNA, and rRNA isn't made in the cytoplasm."]},"fa6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ribosomal subunits (40S and 60S) are assembled in the nucleolus, exported through nuclear pores, and join on mRNA to form the 80S ribosome.</p><p>Eukaryotic: 40S + 60S = 80S. Prokaryotic (and mitochondrial-like): 30S + 50S = 70S — a target for antibiotics (aminoglycosides/tetracyclines at 30S; macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid at 50S).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>In mitochondria; they form 80S ribosomes</b> — Cytosolic 80S subunits are made in the nucleolus, not mitochondria.</li><li><b>On the rough ER; they form 70S ribosomes</b> — Ribosomes bind the RER only to translate, and 70S ribosomes are prokaryotic.</li><li><b>In the Golgi; they form 60S ribosomes</b> — The Golgi doesn't assemble ribosomes.</li><li><b>In the cytoplasm; they form 70S ribosomes</b> — 70S ribosomes are prokaryotic/mitochondrial, and subunits are assembled in the nucleolus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Antibiotics: 30S (AT — aminoglycosides, tetracyclines) vs 50S (CCEL).</p>","w":["Cytosolic 80S subunits are made in the nucleolus, not mitochondria.","Ribosomes bind the RER only to translate, and 70S ribosomes are prokaryotic.","The Golgi doesn't assemble ribosomes.","70S ribosomes are prokaryotic/mitochondrial, and subunits are assembled in the nucleolus.","Correct — nucleolus; 60S + 40S form 80S."]},"fa7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An N-terminal ER signal sequence, recognized by the signal recognition particle (SRP), directs a ribosome to the rough ER.</p><p>All translation starts on free ribosomes; only those making proteins with an ER signal sequence become membrane-bound. SRP pauses translation and docks the complex at the SRP receptor and translocon; the signal peptide is cleaved in the ER.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The ribosome's rRNA type</b> — The rRNA type doesn't decide ribosome location.</li><li><b>The final size of the protein</b> — The protein's final size doesn't decide location.</li><li><b>Random chance</b> — Targeting isn't random; it's signal-directed.</li><li><b>The length of the mRNA's poly-A tail</b> — Poly-A tail length affects mRNA stability, not ribosome targeting.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Signal sequence + SRP → RER.</p>","w":["The rRNA type doesn't decide ribosome location.","The protein's final size doesn't decide location.","Targeting isn't random; it's signal-directed.","Correct — an SRP-recognized signal sequence.","Poly-A tail length affects mRNA stability, not ribosome targeting."]},"fa8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Free ribosomes make proteins for the cytosol, nucleus, mitochondria and peroxisomes; RER-bound ribosomes make secretory, lysosomal, plasma membrane, ER and Golgi proteins.</p><p>Mnemonic: things that stay inside or are imported post-translationally → free; things that go through the secretory pathway → RER.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Extracellular matrix</b> — Extracellular matrix (secreted) proteins come from RER-bound ribosomes.</li><li><b>Secreted proteins, lysosomes and plasma membrane</b> — Secreted, lysosomal and plasma membrane proteins come from RER-bound ribosomes.</li><li><b>ER lumen only</b> — ER lumen proteins are made on RER-bound ribosomes.</li><li><b>Golgi and plasma membrane</b> — Golgi and plasma membrane proteins are made on RER-bound ribosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Secretory pathway proteins = RER.</p>","w":["Correct — cytosol, mitochondria, peroxisomes, nucleus.","Extracellular matrix (secreted) proteins come from RER-bound ribosomes.","Secreted, lysosomal and plasma membrane proteins come from RER-bound ribosomes.","ER lumen proteins are made on RER-bound ribosomes.","Golgi and plasma membrane proteins are made on RER-bound ribosomes."]},"fa9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal enzymes are translated on free polysomes and imported directly (via peroxins recognizing PTS signals), bypassing the ER and Golgi.</p><p>Defective import (PEX mutations) causes Zellweger syndrome. Lysosomal enzymes, in contrast, use the RER → Golgi → M6P route.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>On the rough ER, then via the Golgi apparatus</b> — RER → Golgi is the route for secreted and lysosomal proteins.</li><li><b>In the nucleolus</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA and ribosomes, not peroxisomal enzymes.</li><li><b>Inside the peroxisome itself</b> — Peroxisomes have no ribosomes or DNA and can't make their own proteins.</li><li><b>In mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria make only 13 of their own proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PTS1 (C-terminal SKL) → peroxisome.</p>","w":["Correct — free polysomes, then direct import.","RER → Golgi is the route for secreted and lysosomal proteins.","The nucleolus makes rRNA and ribosomes, not peroxisomal enzymes.","Peroxisomes have no ribosomes or DNA and can't make their own proteins.","Mitochondria make only 13 of their own proteins."]},"fa10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Golgi has no DNA; mitochondria (and chloroplasts) have their own circular DNA, reflecting bacterial (endosymbiotic) origin.</p><p>Organelles with DNA: nucleus, mitochondria (mtDNA, 37 genes), chloroplasts (plants). All other organelles rely on nuclear-encoded proteins.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chloroplast</b> — Chloroplasts have their own DNA.</li><li><b>All of these have DNA</b> — Not all of these organelles have DNA — the Golgi doesn't.</li><li><b>Nucleus</b> — The nucleus contains the genome.</li><li><b>Mitochondrion</b> — Mitochondria have their own mtDNA.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> mtDNA = maternal inheritance.</p>","w":["Chloroplasts have their own DNA.","Not all of these organelles have DNA — the Golgi doesn't.","The nucleus contains the genome.","Correct — the Golgi apparatus.","Mitochondria have their own mtDNA."]},"fa11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The nuclear lamina (lamins A, B, C) maintains nuclear shape; the LINC complex links it to the cytoskeleton.</p><p>LINC = SUN proteins (inner nuclear membrane) + nesprins (outer membrane) connecting lamins to actin, microtubules and intermediate filaments. This transmits mechanical forces to the nucleus. Lamin A defects cause progeria and laminopathies.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Centrosome; microtubules</b> — The centrosome organizes microtubules, not nuclear shape.</li><li><b>Chromatin; actin filaments</b> — Chromatin doesn't maintain nuclear shape, and actin isn't the shape scaffold inside the nucleus.</li><li><b>Nucleolus; the nuclear pore complexes</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA, and pores handle transport, not shape.</li><li><b>Spectrin; ankyrin</b> — Spectrin and ankyrin form the RBC membrane skeleton.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> LINC = SUN + nesprin; lamin A → progeria.</p>","w":["Correct — nuclear lamina; LINC complex.","The centrosome organizes microtubules, not nuclear shape.","Chromatin doesn't maintain nuclear shape, and actin isn't the shape scaffold inside the nucleus.","The nucleolus makes rRNA, and pores handle transport, not shape.","Spectrin and ankyrin form the RBC membrane skeleton."]},"fa12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Blocking mRNA export through nuclear pores directly prevents translation in the cytoplasm; transcription and nuclear processing continue.</p><p>Capping, splicing and polyadenylation occur in the nucleus before export; only processed mRNA is exported (via NXF1/TAP). Without export, mRNA can't reach ribosomes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transcription</b> — Transcription occurs in the nucleus and is unaffected.</li><li><b>DNA replication</b> — DNA replication isn't directly affected by mRNA export.</li><li><b>Splicing</b> — Splicing occurs in the nucleus.</li><li><b>Capping</b> — Capping occurs in the nucleus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nuclear processing: 5′ cap, splicing, poly-A tail.</p>","w":["Transcription occurs in the nucleus and is unaffected.","DNA replication isn't directly affected by mRNA export.","Splicing occurs in the nucleus.","Correct — translation is directly impaired.","Capping occurs in the nucleus."]},"fa13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> mRNA path: transcribed and processed in the nucleus → exported through nuclear pores → translated in the cytoplasm.</p><p>Processing includes the 5′ 7-methylguanosine cap, removal of introns by spliceosomes and the 3′ poly-A tail. In prokaryotes, by contrast, transcription and translation occur together in the cytoplasm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Translated in the nucleus, then exported as protein</b> — Translation is cytoplasmic, not nuclear.</li><li><b>Made in the nucleolus</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA, not mRNA.</li><li><b>Made on the rough ER</b> — The RER translates mRNA but doesn't make it.</li><li><b>Made in the cytoplasm, then imported</b> — mRNA is transcribed in the nucleus, not the cytoplasm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Prokaryotes: coupled transcription–translation.</p>","w":["Translation is cytoplasmic, not nuclear.","Correct — processed in the nucleus → exported → translated.","The nucleolus makes rRNA, not mRNA.","The RER translates mRNA but doesn't make it.","mRNA is transcribed in the nucleus, not the cytoplasm."]},"fa14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> SRP binds the ER signal sequence, pauses translation and docks the ribosome on the SRP receptor/translocon at the RER.</p><p>Translation then resumes, threading the protein into the ER lumen (or membrane). Signal peptidase removes the signal sequence. Without SRP targeting, secretory proteins would be made in the cytosol.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Exports mRNA through nuclear pores</b> — mRNA export uses NXF1/TAP through nuclear pores.</li><li><b>Degrades misfolded proteins at the ER membrane</b> — Degradation of misfolded ER proteins is ERAD with proteasomes.</li><li><b>Forms COPII vesicles</b> — COPII coats ER exit vesicles.</li><li><b>Adds mannose-6-phosphate tags</b> — M6P tags are added by the phosphotransferase in the Golgi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SRP = pause + dock.</p>","w":["Correct — pauses translation and docks the ribosome at the RER.","mRNA export uses NXF1/TAP through nuclear pores.","Degradation of misfolded ER proteins is ERAD with proteasomes.","COPII coats ER exit vesicles.","M6P tags are added by the phosphotransferase in the Golgi."]},"fa15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> High proinsulin with poor glucose control → failed proinsulin cleavage (prohormone convertases/carboxypeptidase E in secretory granules).</p><p>Proinsulin has only ~5–10% of insulin's activity. Normally convertases remove C-peptide to make insulin. Insulin isn't glycosylated, so glycosylation failure is a distractor.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Failure of M6P tagging</b> — M6P tagging targets lysosomal enzymes.</li><li><b>Excess insulin gene transcription in beta cells</b> — More insulin gene transcription wouldn't raise the proinsulin-to-insulin ratio.</li><li><b>Failure of SRP binding</b> — SRP failure would block ER entry, not cause proinsulin buildup.</li><li><b>Failure of glycosylation</b> — Insulin isn't glycosylated — a classic distractor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proinsulin processing occurs in secretory granules.</p>","w":["M6P tagging targets lysosomal enzymes.","More insulin gene transcription wouldn't raise the proinsulin-to-insulin ratio.","SRP failure would block ER entry, not cause proinsulin buildup.","Insulin isn't glycosylated — a classic distractor.","Correct — failed C-peptide cleavage by convertases."]},"fa16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Preproinsulin (with signal peptide) → proinsulin (signal removed; disulfides formed in ER) → insulin + C-peptide (cleaved in secretory granules).</p><p>Insulin = A chain (21 aa) + B chain (30 aa) joined by two disulfides (plus one within A). Equal amounts of insulin and C-peptide are secreted.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Insulin → proinsulin → preproinsulin</b> — This reverses the sequence.</li><li><b>Proinsulin is made in the nucleus</b> — Proteins aren't made in the nucleus.</li><li><b>Insulin is made in the cytosol and then glycosylated</b> — Insulin is made on the RER and isn't glycosylated.</li><li><b>C-peptide is added in the Golgi</b> — C-peptide is removed, not added.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Insulin:C-peptide secreted 1:1.</p>","w":["This reverses the sequence.","Proteins aren't made in the nucleus.","Correct — preproinsulin → proinsulin → insulin + C-peptide.","Insulin is made on the RER and isn't glycosylated.","C-peptide is removed, not added."]},"fa17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> C-peptide reflects endogenous insulin secretion; exogenous insulin contains no C-peptide.</p><p>Hypoglycemia workup: high insulin + high C-peptide → insulinoma or sulfonylurea (check drug screen); high insulin + low C-peptide → exogenous insulin (factitious). In type 1 diabetes, C-peptide is low.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It measures protein glycosylation</b> — Glycosylation is assessed by tests like HbA1c, not C-peptide.</li><li><b>It is present in injected insulin</b> — Commercial insulin lacks C-peptide.</li><li><b>It measures glucagon secretion</b> — Glucagon is measured directly.</li><li><b>It is a sensitive marker of liver function</b> — C-peptide isn't a liver function marker.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑insulin, ↓C-peptide = exogenous insulin.</p>","w":["Glycosylation is assessed by tests like HbA1c, not C-peptide.","Correct — reflects the body's own insulin secretion.","Commercial insulin lacks C-peptide.","Glucagon is measured directly.","C-peptide isn't a liver function marker."]},"fa18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes: single-membrane organelles whose major function is β-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids (&gt;22 C) — also phytanic acid α-oxidation and plasmalogen/bile acid synthesis.</p><p>They contain oxidases that generate H₂O₂ and catalase that destroys it. Disorders: Zellweger (no peroxisomes), X-ALD (ABCD1), Refsum (phytanic acid).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Single membrane; acid hydrolysis of macromolecules</b> — Single membrane with acid hydrolysis describes lysosomes.</li><li><b>Double membrane; steroid synthesis</b> — Double membranes belong to mitochondria; steroid synthesis occurs in SER.</li><li><b>Double membrane; ATP production</b> — Double membrane with ATP production describes mitochondria.</li><li><b>No membrane; protein synthesis</b> — No membrane with protein synthesis describes ribosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑VLCFA → Zellweger or X-ALD.</p>","w":["Single membrane with acid hydrolysis describes lysosomes.","Double membranes belong to mitochondria; steroid synthesis occurs in SER.","Double membrane with ATP production describes mitochondria.","No membrane with protein synthesis describes ribosomes.","Correct — single membrane; VLCFA β-oxidation."]},"fa19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal oxidases produce H₂O₂, which catalase converts to H₂O + O₂.</p><p>Catalase can also use H₂O₂ to oxidize ethanol, methanol and formaldehyde. Catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Nocardia) are problematic in chronic granulomatous disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>CO₂; carbonic anhydrase</b> — CO₂ isn't the toxic peroxisomal byproduct.</li><li><b>Ammonia; urease</b> — Ammonia comes from amino acid catabolism, and urease is bacterial.</li><li><b>Lactate; lactate dehydrogenase</b> — Lactate comes from anaerobic glycolysis.</li><li><b>Superoxide; SOD only</b> — Catalase, not SOD alone, handles peroxisomal H₂O₂ (SOD converts superoxide to H₂O₂).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CGD: catalase-positive organisms.</p>","w":["Correct — H₂O₂; catalase.","CO₂ isn't the toxic peroxisomal byproduct.","Ammonia comes from amino acid catabolism, and urease is bacterial.","Lactate comes from anaerobic glycolysis.","Catalase, not SOD alone, handles peroxisomal H₂O₂ (SOD converts superoxide to H₂O₂)."]},"fa20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal β-oxidation isn't coupled to ATP synthesis: electrons go straight to O₂, making H₂O₂ and heat. Mitochondrial β-oxidation feeds the ETC to make ATP.</p><p>The first peroxisomal enzyme is acyl-CoA oxidase (vs acyl-CoA dehydrogenase in mitochondria). Peroxisomes only shorten VLCFAs; mitochondria finish oxidation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroxisomal β-oxidation makes more ATP</b> — This is reversed — mitochondrial β-oxidation makes ATP.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial β-oxidation is what produces the H₂O₂</b> — This is reversed — peroxisomal β-oxidation makes H₂O₂.</li><li><b>The two processes are identical</b> — The two processes differ in enzymes and energy coupling.</li><li><b>Neither process uses O₂</b> — Both ultimately use O₂.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oxidase (peroxisome) vs dehydrogenase (mitochondria).</p>","w":["This is reversed — mitochondrial β-oxidation makes ATP.","This is reversed — peroxisomal β-oxidation makes H₂O₂.","Correct — peroxisomal β-oxidation isn't ATP-coupled; it makes H₂O₂.","The two processes differ in enzymes and energy coupling.","Both ultimately use O₂."]},"fa21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes shorten VLCFAs to medium-chain fatty acids, which are transferred (as carnitine esters) to mitochondria to finish β-oxidation to acetyl-CoA.</p><p>The division of labor: peroxisomes handle the first few cycles for very long or branched chains; mitochondria complete oxidation and capture energy as ATP.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are stored in lysosomes</b> — Lysosomes don't store fatty acids for oxidation.</li><li><b>They are secreted into the blood</b> — Shortened fatty acids aren't secreted; they're completed in mitochondria.</li><li><b>They are converted to cholesterol</b> — Shortened fatty acids aren't converted to cholesterol.</li><li><b>They are used only to make plasmalogens in place</b> — Plasmalogen synthesis is a separate peroxisomal function.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Peroxisome starts, mitochondrion finishes.</p>","w":["Lysosomes don't store fatty acids for oxidation.","Correct — they go to mitochondria to finish β-oxidation.","Shortened fatty acids aren't secreted; they're completed in mitochondria.","Shortened fatty acids aren't converted to cholesterol.","Plasmalogen synthesis is a separate peroxisomal function."]},"fa22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxins (PEX proteins) in the peroxisomal membrane recognize targeting signals (PTS1/PTS2) and import proteins; PEX mutations cause Zellweger spectrum disorders.</p><p>PEX5 binds PTS1 cargo (C-terminal SKL) in the cytosol and delivers it into the peroxisome; PEX7 handles PTS2. Without import, peroxisomes are empty \"ghosts.\"</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lamins</b> — Lamins form the nuclear lamina.</li><li><b>Caveolins</b> — Caveolins form caveolae in the plasma membrane.</li><li><b>Clathrins</b> — Clathrin coats pits and vesicles for endocytosis and Golgi-to-lysosome transport.</li><li><b>Porins (outer-membrane channels)</b> — Porins are channels in the outer mitochondrial (and bacterial) membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PEX mutation → Zellweger.</p>","w":["Lamins form the nuclear lamina.","Caveolins form caveolae in the plasma membrane.","Correct — peroxins.","Clathrin coats pits and vesicles for endocytosis and Golgi-to-lysosome transport.","Porins are channels in the outer mitochondrial (and bacterial) membrane."]},"fa23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes are adaptable: they proliferate when needed (e.g., high-fat diet, certain drugs via PPARα) and return to baseline numbers afterward through selective autophagy (pexophagy).</p><p>Peroxisome number is regulated by growth and fission and by degradation. PPARα agonists (fibrates) induce peroxisome proliferation in rodent liver.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are stored permanently</b> — Extra peroxisomes are removed, not stored permanently.</li><li><b>They fuse with mitochondria</b> — Peroxisomes don't fuse with mitochondria.</li><li><b>They are secreted from the cell</b> — Organelles aren't secreted from the cell.</li><li><b>They convert into lysosomes permanently</b> — Peroxisomes don't convert into lysosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pexophagy removes excess peroxisomes.</p>","w":["Extra peroxisomes are removed, not stored permanently.","Correct — they return to baseline numbers.","Peroxisomes don't fuse with mitochondria.","Organelles aren't secreted from the cell.","Peroxisomes don't convert into lysosomes."]},"fa24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes also synthesize bile acids (liver) and plasmalogens (ether phospholipids important in myelin and heart).</p><p>Plasmalogen deficiency contributes to neurologic problems in Zellweger syndrome and rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata. Peroxisomes also help make cholesterol and DHA.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen synthesis and the urea cycle</b> — Glycogen synthesis is cytosolic, and the urea cycle is mitochondrial/cytosolic.</li><li><b>Insulin processing</b> — Insulin is processed in secretory granules.</li><li><b>rRNA synthesis</b> — rRNA is made in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>Collagen hydroxylation</b> — Collagen hydroxylation occurs in the RER.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Plasmalogens = myelin lipids made in peroxisomes.</p>","w":["Glycogen synthesis is cytosolic, and the urea cycle is mitochondrial/cytosolic.","Insulin is processed in secretory granules.","Correct — bile acid and plasmalogen synthesis.","rRNA is made in the nucleolus.","Collagen hydroxylation occurs in the RER."]},"fa25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy = ABCD1 transporter deficiency → VLCFAs can't enter peroxisomes → accumulate → adrenal insufficiency + cerebral demyelination.</p><p>Boys present with behavioral and school decline, vision/hearing problems and adrenal insufficiency (hyperpigmentation, fatigue). Diagnosis: elevated plasma VLCFAs. Early stem cell transplant or gene therapy can halt progression.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Acid hydrolase</b> — Acid hydrolases are lysosomal enzymes, not the ALD defect.</li><li><b>Dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin is a muscle protein (Duchenne/Becker).</li><li><b>PEX peroxins (peroxisome biogenesis)</b> — PEX mutations cause Zellweger syndrome (no peroxisomes).</li><li><b>Catalase</b> — Catalase deficiency doesn't cause ALD.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> X-ALD: boy + adrenal insufficiency + demyelination + ↑VLCFA.</p>","w":["Acid hydrolases are lysosomal enzymes, not the ALD defect.","Dystrophin is a muscle protein (Duchenne/Becker).","Correct — ABCD1 transporter.","PEX mutations cause Zellweger syndrome (no peroxisomes).","Catalase deficiency doesn't cause ALD."]},"fa26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Refsum disease = defective peroxisomal α-oxidation (phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase) → phytanic acid accumulates.</p><p>Phytanic acid (from dairy and ruminant fat) has a methyl branch at C3 that blocks β-oxidation; α-oxidation removes one carbon to allow β-oxidation. Features: retinitis pigmentosa, peripheral neuropathy, ataxia, ichthyosis, cardiac arrhythmias. Treatment: restrict dietary phytanic acid.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ω-oxidation</b> — ω-oxidation is a minor ER pathway, not the phytanic acid pathway.</li><li><b>Glycolysis</b> — Glycolysis breaks down glucose, not fatty acids.</li><li><b>Plasmalogen synthesis</b> — Plasmalogen synthesis isn't the defect in Refsum disease.</li><li><b>β-oxidation</b> — β-oxidation handles straight-chain fatty acids; it can't start on phytanic acid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Refsum: phytanic acid ↑ → retinitis pigmentosa + neuropathy.</p>","w":["ω-oxidation is a minor ER pathway, not the phytanic acid pathway.","Glycolysis breaks down glucose, not fatty acids.","Plasmalogen synthesis isn't the defect in Refsum disease.","β-oxidation handles straight-chain fatty acids; it can't start on phytanic acid.","Correct — α-oxidation."]},"fa27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Zellweger = global failure of peroxisome biogenesis (PEX mutations); X-ALD = single transporter (ABCD1) defect in otherwise normal peroxisomes.</p><p>Zellweger presents in newborns with hypotonia, seizures, liver dysfunction, renal cysts and dysmorphic facies, and is fatal early. X-ALD presents in childhood boys with demyelination and adrenal insufficiency. Both raise VLCFAs, but Zellweger also raises phytanic and pipecolic acids and lowers plasmalogens.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Zellweger affects only the liver</b> — Zellweger is multisystem (brain, liver, kidney), not liver-only.</li><li><b>ALD is a mitochondrial DNA disease</b> — ALD is an X-linked nuclear gene disorder, not mitochondrial.</li><li><b>They are the same disease</b> — They're different diseases with different mechanisms.</li><li><b>Zellweger is a lysosomal enzyme-deficiency disease</b> — Zellweger is peroxisomal, not lysosomal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Zellweger = no peroxisomes; X-ALD = one transporter.</p>","w":["Zellweger is multisystem (brain, liver, kidney), not liver-only.","ALD is an X-linked nuclear gene disorder, not mitochondrial.","They're different diseases with different mechanisms.","Zellweger is peroxisomal, not lysosomal.","Correct — no peroxisomes vs one failed transporter."]},"fa28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysosomes contain acid hydrolases (proteases, nucleases, lipases, glycosidases, phosphatases, sulfatases) active at pH ~5.</p><p>A V-type H⁺-ATPase acidifies the lumen. Acid pH both activates the enzymes and protects the cell if they leak (they're much less active at cytosolic pH 7.2).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Catalases and peroxidases</b> — Catalases and peroxidases are peroxisomal enzymes.</li><li><b>Peroxins</b> — Peroxins are peroxisomal import proteins.</li><li><b>Kinases</b> — Kinases phosphorylate proteins; they don't hydrolyze macromolecules.</li><li><b>Caspases</b> — Caspases are apoptotic proteases in the cytosol.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lysosome pH ~5 via V-ATPase.</p>","w":["Catalases and peroxidases are peroxisomal enzymes.","Peroxins are peroxisomal import proteins.","Correct — acid hydrolases.","Kinases phosphorylate proteins; they don't hydrolyze macromolecules.","Caspases are apoptotic proteases in the cytosol."]},"fa29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysosomal enzymes are tagged with mannose-6-phosphate in the (cis-)Golgi and packaged into vesicles budding from the trans-Golgi network, which deliver them to endosomes/lysosomes.</p><p>M6P receptors bind the tagged enzymes at neutral pH in the TGN and release them in acidic late endosomes; receptors recycle back to the Golgi.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>From the nucleus, via the nuclear envelope</b> — The nucleus doesn't bud lysosomes.</li><li><b>From mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria are separate organelles and don't form lysosomes.</li><li><b>From peroxisomes</b> — Peroxisomes are separate organelles and don't form lysosomes.</li><li><b>From the plasma membrane</b> — Endocytic vesicles fuse with lysosomes, but lysosomal enzymes come from the Golgi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> M6P receptor: binds at pH 7, releases at pH ~5.</p>","w":["The nucleus doesn't bud lysosomes.","Mitochondria are separate organelles and don't form lysosomes.","Peroxisomes are separate organelles and don't form lysosomes.","Endocytic vesicles fuse with lysosomes, but lysosomal enzymes come from the Golgi.","Correct — from the trans-Golgi after M6P tagging."]},"fa30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> I-cell disease: GlcNAc phosphotransferase deficiency → no M6P tag → lysosomal enzymes secreted into blood; lysosomes fill with undegraded material.</p><p>Classic presentation: coarse facies, gingival hyperplasia, restricted joints, skeletal dysplasia, developmental delay, high plasma lysosomal enzymes. Lysosomes form but are functionally empty (inclusion bodies).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosomes fail to form at all in the cell</b> — Lysosomes are present; they just lack enzymes.</li><li><b>Failure of peroxisome biogenesis</b> — Failure of peroxisome biogenesis is Zellweger syndrome.</li><li><b>Hexosaminidase A deficiency</b> — Hexosaminidase A deficiency is Tay–Sachs disease.</li><li><b>A defective M6P receptor</b> — The tag is never made, so a receptor defect isn't the root problem.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> I-cell: ↑plasma lysosomal enzymes.</p>","w":["Lysosomes are present; they just lack enzymes.","Failure of peroxisome biogenesis is Zellweger syndrome.","Hexosaminidase A deficiency is Tay–Sachs disease.","The tag is never made, so a receptor defect isn't the root problem.","Correct — GlcNAc phosphotransferase deficiency."]},"fa31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In I-cell disease, the primary failure is that the M6P tag is never added; the receptor is normal but has nothing to bind.</p><p>Order of events: phosphotransferase adds GlcNAc-1-P → uncovering enzyme exposes M6P → M6P receptor binds → vesicle to lysosome. The defect is at step one, so later steps never occur.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The M6P receptor is overexpressed and saturated</b> — Receptor overexpression isn't the problem — the tag is missing.</li><li><b>Lysosomes are completely absent</b> — Lysosomes are present in I-cell disease.</li><li><b>The receptor is trapped in the nucleus</b> — The M6P receptor isn't nuclear.</li><li><b>M6P plays no role in this disease</b> — M6P is central to the disease mechanism.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Root cause = missing tag, not missing receptor.</p>","w":["Receptor overexpression isn't the problem — the tag is missing.","Correct — the tag is never added, so there's nothing to bind.","Lysosomes are present in I-cell disease.","The M6P receptor isn't nuclear.","M6P is central to the disease mechanism."]},"fa32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> I-cell disease (mucolipidosis II) is autosomal recessive (GNPTAB) and usually fatal in early childhood.</p><p>Most lysosomal storage disorders are autosomal recessive; exceptions are Fabry disease and Hunter syndrome (X-linked recessive). Enzyme synthesis is normal in I-cell disease; only targeting fails.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Autosomal dominant; mild course</b> — It isn't dominant, and the course is severe.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial; variable severity between siblings</b> — It isn't mitochondrial.</li><li><b>X-linked; adult onset</b> — It isn't X-linked, and it presents in infancy.</li><li><b>Acquired; reversible</b> — It's genetic and progressive, not acquired or reversible.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> X-linked LSDs: Fabry, Hunter.</p>","w":["It isn't dominant, and the course is severe.","It isn't mitochondrial.","It isn't X-linked, and it presents in infancy.","It's genetic and progressive, not acquired or reversible.","Correct — autosomal recessive; fatal in childhood."]},"fa33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Classic lysosomal storage diseases = deficiency of one lysosomal enzyme → buildup of its specific substrate; I-cell disease = trafficking (tagging) failure of all enzymes.</p><p>Examples: Tay–Sachs (hexosaminidase A → GM2 ganglioside), metachromatic leukodystrophy (arylsulfatase A → sulfatides), Gaucher (glucocerebrosidase → glucocerebroside; most common LSD; Gaucher cells \"crumpled tissue paper\"), Niemann–Pick (sphingomyelinase), Fabry (α-galactosidase A), Krabbe (galactocerebrosidase).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are also M6P tagging defects</b> — Their enzymes are tagged and delivered normally; they're just defective.</li><li><b>They are also trafficking (M6P-tagging) defects</b> — They aren't trafficking defects.</li><li><b>They are peroxisomal diseases</b> — They're lysosomal, not peroxisomal, diseases.</li><li><b>They are mitochondrial diseases</b> — They're lysosomal, not mitochondrial, diseases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gaucher = most common LSD; treat with enzyme replacement.</p>","w":["Their enzymes are tagged and delivered normally; they're just defective.","They aren't trafficking defects.","They're lysosomal, not peroxisomal, diseases.","They're lysosomal, not mitochondrial, diseases.","Correct — one lysosomal enzyme is missing or nonfunctional."]},"fa34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysosomal enzyme route: RER (synthesis) → cis-Golgi (M6P tagging) → trans-Golgi (M6P receptor binding, budding) → endosome/lysosome.</p><p>If tagging fails (I-cell disease), enzymes follow the default constitutive secretory pathway out of the cell.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Plasma membrane → endosome → Golgi → lysosome</b> — Plasma membrane → endosome → lysosome describes endocytic uptake, not the biosynthetic route.</li><li><b>Cytosol → peroxisome → lysosome</b> — Peroxisomes aren't on the lysosomal enzyme route.</li><li><b>Nucleus → nuclear pore → lysosome</b> — Enzymes aren't made in the nucleus.</li><li><b>Golgi → RER → lysosome</b> — This order is wrong — proteins go from RER to Golgi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> No tag → default secretion.</p>","w":["Correct — RER → cis-Golgi → trans-Golgi → lysosome.","Plasma membrane → endosome → lysosome describes endocytic uptake, not the biosynthetic route.","Peroxisomes aren't on the lysosomal enzyme route.","Enzymes aren't made in the nucleus.","This order is wrong — proteins go from RER to Golgi."]},"fa35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cristae are folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane that increase surface area for the electron transport chain and ATP synthase (oxidative phosphorylation).</p><p>Cells with high energy demand (cardiac muscle, kidney tubules) have many mitochondria with dense cristae. Steroid-producing cells have mitochondria with tubular cristae.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lamellae; steroid hormone synthesis</b> — Lamellae isn't the name for mitochondrial folds, and steroid synthesis occurs mainly in SER.</li><li><b>Cisternae; protein sorting</b> — Cisternae are flattened sacs of the Golgi and ER.</li><li><b>Thylakoids; photosynthesis</b> — Thylakoids are in chloroplasts (photosynthesis).</li><li><b>Villi; absorption</b> — Villi are intestinal surface projections.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tubular cristae = steroid-producing cells.</p>","w":["Correct — cristae; oxidative phosphorylation.","Lamellae isn't the name for mitochondrial folds, and steroid synthesis occurs mainly in SER.","Cisternae are flattened sacs of the Golgi and ER.","Thylakoids are in chloroplasts (photosynthesis).","Villi are intestinal surface projections."]},"fa36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> mtDNA is maternally inherited because zygotic mitochondria come from the oocyte; sperm mitochondria are destroyed after fertilization.</p><p>Pattern: affected mothers pass the disease to all children (with variable severity); affected fathers pass it to none. MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibers) is caused by a tRNA-Lys point mutation. Ragged-red fibers on Gomori trichrome show accumulated abnormal mitochondria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>X-linked inheritance</b> — X-linked inheritance wouldn't explain transmission only from mothers to all children.</li><li><b>A somatic mutation</b> — A somatic mutation wouldn't be inherited.</li><li><b>Autosomal dominant inheritance</b> — Autosomal dominant traits pass from either parent.</li><li><b>Genomic imprinting of the paternal allele</b> — Imprinting isn't the explanation for mtDNA disease.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mitochondrial disorders: MELAS, MERRF, LHON, Kearns–Sayre.</p>","w":["X-linked inheritance wouldn't explain transmission only from mothers to all children.","Correct — mtDNA comes almost only from the oocyte.","A somatic mutation wouldn't be inherited.","Autosomal dominant traits pass from either parent.","Imprinting isn't the explanation for mtDNA disease."]},"fa37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> mtDNA's 37 genes: 13 oxidative phosphorylation proteins, 22 tRNAs and 2 rRNAs.</p><p>The 13 proteins are subunits of complexes I, III, IV and V; complex II is fully nuclear-encoded. All other mitochondrial proteins are nuclear-encoded and imported.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>All 37 encode rRNA</b> — Only 2 genes encode rRNAs.</li><li><b>2 proteins; 35 tRNAs</b> — This split is wrong — 13 encode proteins and 22 encode tRNAs.</li><li><b>13 tRNAs; the rest encode 22 proteins and 2 rRNAs</b> — This swaps the numbers of tRNA and protein genes.</li><li><b>All 37 encode proteins</b> — Only 13 genes encode proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Complex II = no mtDNA subunits.</p>","w":["Only 2 genes encode rRNAs.","This split is wrong — 13 encode proteins and 22 encode tRNAs.","This swaps the numbers of tRNA and protein genes.","Only 13 genes encode proteins.","Correct — 13 proteins; 22 tRNAs and 2 rRNAs."]},"fa38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Endosymbiotic evidence: mitochondria have their own circular DNA, bacteria-like (70S-type) ribosomes, a double membrane, and divide by binary fission.</p><p>Mitochondrial ribosomes' similarity to bacterial ribosomes explains some antibiotic toxicities (e.g., aminoglycoside ototoxicity in people with mitochondrial rRNA variants; linezolid and chloramphenicol mitochondrial toxicity).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They contain catalase, like bacterial cells</b> — Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme, not evidence of bacterial origin for mitochondria.</li><li><b>They are made by the Golgi</b> — Mitochondria aren't made by the Golgi.</li><li><b>They bud from the ER</b> — Mitochondria don't bud from the ER.</li><li><b>They lack their own DNA</b> — Mitochondria do have their own DNA.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mitochondrial ribosomes → some antibiotic toxicities.</p>","w":["Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme, not evidence of bacterial origin for mitochondria.","Mitochondria aren't made by the Golgi.","Correct — circular DNA and division by fission.","Mitochondria don't bud from the ER.","Mitochondria do have their own DNA."]},"fa39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heteroplasmy: cells contain a mixture of normal and mutant mtDNA, and the proportion varies among cells, tissues and offspring.</p><p>Disease appears when the mutant fraction exceeds a tissue's threshold. Random distribution of mitochondria at cell division (and the oocyte \"bottleneck\") explains why siblings from the same mother can be affected very differently.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>X-inactivation</b> — X-inactivation affects X-linked genes in females.</li><li><b>Genomic imprinting</b> — Imprinting is parent-of-origin gene silencing.</li><li><b>Anticipation</b> — Anticipation is worsening across generations from repeat expansion.</li><li><b>Codominance</b> — Codominance means both alleles are fully expressed.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heteroplasmy + threshold effect → variable expression.</p>","w":["Correct — heteroplasmy.","X-inactivation affects X-linked genes in females.","Imprinting is parent-of-origin gene silencing.","Anticipation is worsening across generations from repeat expansion.","Codominance means both alleles are fully expressed."]},"fa40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mitochondrial diseases mostly affect high-energy tissues: skeletal muscle, brain, heart and retina.</p><p>These tissues depend heavily on oxidative phosphorylation. Features: myopathy (ragged-red fibers), seizures, stroke-like episodes (MELAS), ophthalmoplegia, cardiomyopathy, optic neuropathy (LHON), lactic acidosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Bone</b> — Bone isn't a classic target of mitochondrial disease.</li><li><b>Red blood cells</b> — Red blood cells lack mitochondria.</li><li><b>Adipose tissue</b> — Adipose tissue isn't a classic target.</li><li><b>Skin and hair, which divide quickly</b> — Rapid turnover isn't the main factor; energy demand is.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MELAS: mitochondrial myopathy, encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes.</p>","w":["Bone isn't a classic target of mitochondrial disease.","Red blood cells lack mitochondria.","Adipose tissue isn't a classic target.","Correct — muscle, brain, heart and retina.","Rapid turnover isn't the main factor; energy demand is."]},"fa41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mitochondrial dysfunction blocks oxidative phosphorylation → cells rely on anaerobic glycolysis → pyruvate converted to lactate → lactic acidosis, with energy-starved muscle and brain.</p><p>When the ETC fails, NADH can't be reoxidized, so pyruvate is shunted to lactate to regenerate NAD⁺. A high lactate-to-pyruvate ratio suggests an ETC defect; a normal ratio with high both suggests PDH deficiency. Newborn mitochondrial disorders (e.g., Leigh syndrome) present with hypotonia, seizures and lactic acidosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleus; transcription fails</b> — Failed transcription isn't the picture here, and it wouldn't explain lactic acidosis.</li><li><b>Golgi; glycosylation fails</b> — Glycosylation disorders present differently and don't cause lactic acidosis.</li><li><b>Peroxisome; hydrogen peroxide builds up</b> — Peroxisomal disease doesn't explain lactic acidosis.</li><li><b>Lysosome; enzymes leak out</b> — Lysosomal disorders don't cause lactic acidosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑Lactate:pyruvate → ETC defect.</p>","w":["Correct — mitochondrion; impaired oxidative phosphorylation.","Failed transcription isn't the picture here, and it wouldn't explain lactic acidosis.","Glycosylation disorders present differently and don't cause lactic acidosis.","Peroxisomal disease doesn't explain lactic acidosis.","Lysosomal disorders don't cause lactic acidosis."]},"fa42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ragged red fibers = accumulated abnormal mitochondria under the sarcolemma, seen with modified Gomori trichrome — the hallmark of mitochondrial myopathy.</p><p>Seen in MERRF, Kearns–Sayre and other mtDNA disorders (not typically LHON). COX (cytochrome oxidase)-negative fibers are another clue. Other muscle biopsy findings: nemaline rods (nemaline myopathy), central cores (central core disease — RYR1), ringed sideroblasts are a bone marrow finding.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nemaline rods</b> — Nemaline rods indicate nemaline (congenital) myopathy, not mitochondrial disease.</li><li><b>Ringed sideroblasts</b> — Ringed sideroblasts are a bone marrow finding in sideroblastic anemia.</li><li><b>Central cores</b> — Central cores indicate central core disease (RYR1), not mitochondrial myopathy.</li><li><b>Spherocytes</b> — Spherocytes are a red blood cell finding.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ragged red fibers: MERRF, Kearns–Sayre.</p>","w":["Correct — ragged red fibers.","Nemaline rods indicate nemaline (congenital) myopathy, not mitochondrial disease.","Ringed sideroblasts are a bone marrow finding in sideroblastic anemia.","Central cores indicate central core disease (RYR1), not mitochondrial myopathy.","Spherocytes are a red blood cell finding."]},"fa43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> MELAS = Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, Stroke-like episodes (young adults/children); Leigh syndrome = infantile subacute necrotizing encephalomyelopathy with basal ganglia/brainstem lesions.</p><p>MELAS commonly involves a tRNA-Leu mutation (A3243G); stroke-like lesions don't follow vascular territories. Leigh presents in infancy with regression, hypotonia, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia and respiratory problems. MERRF: myoclonic epilepsy with ragged-red fibers. LHON: optic neuropathy. Kearns–Sayre: ophthalmoplegia, retinopathy, heart block.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Leigh — stroke-like episodes in adults</b> — This swaps the syndromes — Leigh is infantile; stroke-like episodes are MELAS.</li><li><b>MERRF — optic neuropathy</b> — Optic neuropathy is LHON, not MERRF.</li><li><b>MELAS — infantile basal ganglia/brainstem lesions</b> — Infantile basal ganglia/brainstem lesions describe Leigh, not MELAS.</li><li><b>LHON — ragged red fibers only</b> — LHON causes optic neuropathy; ragged red fibers characterize MERRF/myopathy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MELAS = stroke-like episodes not in vascular territories.</p>","w":["This swaps the syndromes — Leigh is infantile; stroke-like episodes are MELAS.","Optic neuropathy is LHON, not MERRF.","Correct — MELAS stroke-like episodes; Leigh infantile.","Infantile basal ganglia/brainstem lesions describe Leigh, not MELAS.","LHON causes optic neuropathy; ragged red fibers characterize MERRF/myopathy."]},"fa44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> LHON is mitochondrial: transmitted only by mothers; an affected father can't pass it to any child.</p><p>The family history through the maternal uncle fits maternal inheritance (his mother and the uncle's mother are the same). LHON causes painless subacute bilateral central vision loss in young men, often from complex I (ND) mutations. Penetrance is incomplete and higher in males; smoking and alcohol may trigger onset.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Only if his partner carries an X-linked allele</b> — LHON isn't X-linked, so the partner's alleles don't matter.</li><li><b>Yes, with a 50% chance per child</b> — LHON isn't Mendelian with a 50% risk per child.</li><li><b>Yes, but only to his daughters</b> — LHON isn't X-linked, so it doesn't pass only to daughters.</li><li><b>Yes, to all of his sons</b> — Fathers don't pass on mtDNA, so sons aren't affected through him.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Affected father → 0% transmission of mtDNA disease.</p>","w":["LHON isn't X-linked, so the partner's alleles don't matter.","LHON isn't Mendelian with a 50% risk per child.","LHON isn't X-linked, so it doesn't pass only to daughters.","Correct — no, it's mitochondrial (LHON).","Fathers don't pass on mtDNA, so sons aren't affected through him."]},"fa45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cytosolic cytochrome c binds Apaf-1 to form the apoptosome, which activates caspase-9 → caspases 3/6/7.</p><p>In mitochondria, cytochrome c normally shuttles electrons from complex III to IV. Its release is the point of no return in intrinsic apoptosis, controlled by the Bcl-2 family.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It synthesizes ATP directly in the cytosol</b> — Cytochrome c doesn't synthesize ATP; ATP synthase in the inner membrane does.</li><li><b>Repairs damaged DNA</b> — Cytochrome c doesn't repair DNA.</li><li><b>Exports mRNA from the nucleus</b> — Cytochrome c is unrelated to mRNA export.</li><li><b>Blocks caspase activation</b> — Cytochrome c activates, rather than blocks, caspases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apoptosome = cytochrome c + Apaf-1 + caspase-9.</p>","w":["Cytochrome c doesn't synthesize ATP; ATP synthase in the inner membrane does.","Cytochrome c doesn't repair DNA.","Cytochrome c is unrelated to mRNA export.","Cytochrome c activates, rather than blocks, caspases.","Correct — it forms the apoptosome and activates caspase-9."]},"fa46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> BAX/BAK oligomerize in the outer mitochondrial membrane, forming pores (MOMP) that release cytochrome c from the intermembrane space.</p><p>BH3-only proteins (BIM, BID, PUMA, NOXA) activate BAX/BAK or neutralize BCL-2/BCL-XL, which normally restrain them. BCL-2 inhibitors (venetoclax) restore apoptosis in CLL.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Through the inner membrane, via the cristae</b> — Cytochrome c is in the intermembrane space and exits through the outer, not inner, membrane.</li><li><b>Through nuclear pores</b> — Nuclear pores are in the nuclear envelope.</li><li><b>Through the ATP synthase complex</b> — ATP synthase is an inner membrane enzyme, not an exit pore.</li><li><b>By fusing with a lysosome</b> — Cytochrome c doesn't leave via lysosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MOMP = point of no return.</p>","w":["Cytochrome c is in the intermembrane space and exits through the outer, not inner, membrane.","Correct — through BAX/BAK pores in the outer membrane.","Nuclear pores are in the nuclear envelope.","ATP synthase is an inner membrane enzyme, not an exit pore.","Cytochrome c doesn't leave via lysosomes."]},"fa47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ATP synthase is anchored in the inner mitochondrial membrane, so it can't be released; cytochrome c is a soluble intermembrane-space protein that can escape through BAX/BAK pores.</p><p>Only soluble intermembrane-space proteins (cytochrome c, SMAC/DIABLO, AIF) escape during MOMP. ATP synthase (complex V) uses the proton gradient to make ATP and stays embedded in the membrane.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is already cytosolic</b> — ATP synthase isn't cytosolic.</li><li><b>It is a nuclear protein</b> — ATP synthase isn't a nuclear protein.</li><li><b>It sits in the outer membrane, beyond the pores</b> — ATP synthase is in the inner, not outer, membrane.</li><li><b>It is destroyed by catalase</b> — Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme for H₂O₂ and doesn't destroy ATP synthase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Released during MOMP: cytochrome c, SMAC/DIABLO, AIF.</p>","w":["ATP synthase isn't cytosolic.","ATP synthase isn't a nuclear protein.","Correct — it's fixed in the inner membrane and can't escape.","ATP synthase is in the inner, not outer, membrane.","Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme for H₂O₂ and doesn't destroy ATP synthase."]},"fa48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pro-apoptotic: BAX, BAK (effectors), BIM, BID, BAD, PUMA, NOXA (BH3-only). Anti-apoptotic: BCL-2, BCL-XL, MCL-1.</p><p>The balance between these determines whether the mitochondrial outer membrane is permeabilized. Cancers often overexpress anti-apoptotic members (BCL-2 in follicular lymphoma and CLL).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>All of them are pro-apoptotic</b> — BCL-2 and BCL-XL are anti-apoptotic, not pro-apoptotic.</li><li><b>All of them are anti-apoptotic</b> — BAX, BAK, BIM, BID and BAD are pro-apoptotic.</li><li><b>Pro: p53 only; anti: all others</b> — p53 isn't a Bcl-2 family member, and this ignores the pro-apoptotic members.</li><li><b>Pro: BCL-2, BCL-XL; anti: BAX, BAK, BIM</b> — This reverses the two groups.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Venetoclax = BCL-2 inhibitor.</p>","w":["BCL-2 and BCL-XL are anti-apoptotic, not pro-apoptotic.","BAX, BAK, BIM, BID and BAD are pro-apoptotic.","p53 isn't a Bcl-2 family member, and this ignores the pro-apoptotic members.","Correct — pro: BAX, BAK, BIM; anti: BCL-2, BCL-XL.","This reverses the two groups."]},"fa49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Follicular lymphoma: t(14;18) puts BCL2 under the immunoglobulin heavy-chain promoter → BCL-2 overexpression → B cells evade apoptosis.</p><p>Classic translocations: t(8;14) MYC — Burkitt; t(11;14) cyclin D1 — mantle cell; t(15;17) PML-RARA — acute promyelocytic leukemia (treat with ATRA/arsenic); t(9;22) BCR-ABL — CML (imatinib).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Burkitt lymphoma; t(8;14)</b> — Burkitt lymphoma involves t(8;14) with MYC.</li><li><b>Mantle cell; t(11;14)</b> — Mantle cell lymphoma involves t(11;14) with cyclin D1.</li><li><b>APL; t(15;17)</b> — APL involves t(15;17) with PML-RARA.</li><li><b>CML; t(9;22)</b> — CML involves t(9;22) with BCR-ABL.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Follicular lymphoma = t(14;18), BCL-2.</p>","w":["Correct — follicular lymphoma; t(14;18).","Burkitt lymphoma involves t(8;14) with MYC.","Mantle cell lymphoma involves t(11;14) with cyclin D1.","APL involves t(15;17) with PML-RARA.","CML involves t(9;22) with BCR-ABL."]},"fa50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway → cytochrome c → apoptosome → caspase-9; extrinsic (death receptor: FasL, TNF) → FADD/DISC → caspase-8. Both → caspases 3/6/7.</p><p>Intrinsic triggers: DNA damage, growth factor withdrawal, oxidative stress. Extrinsic triggers: FasL on cytotoxic T cells, TNF. Caspase-8 can also cleave BID to amplify via mitochondria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both pathways start with caspase-8</b> — Caspase-8 initiates only the extrinsic pathway.</li><li><b>Neither pathway uses caspases</b> — Both pathways use caspases.</li><li><b>Intrinsic uses FAS ligand; extrinsic uses cytochrome c</b> — This reverses the two: intrinsic uses cytochrome c; extrinsic uses Fas ligand.</li><li><b>The intrinsic pathway is necrosis</b> — Necrosis is a different, unregulated form of death.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caspase-9 intrinsic; caspase-8 extrinsic.</p>","w":["Caspase-8 initiates only the extrinsic pathway.","Correct — intrinsic → caspase-9; extrinsic → caspase-8.","Both pathways use caspases.","This reverses the two: intrinsic uses cytochrome c; extrinsic uses Fas ligand.","Necrosis is a different, unregulated form of death."]},"fa51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> p53, the \"guardian of the genome,\" senses DNA damage and triggers cell-cycle arrest (via p21) or apoptosis (via BAX, PUMA, NOXA).</p><p>TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in cancer. Germline TP53 mutations cause Li–Fraumeni syndrome (sarcomas, breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemias, adrenocortical carcinoma). HPV E6 promotes p53 degradation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caveolin</b> — Caveolin forms caveolae.</li><li><b>Lamin</b> — Lamins form the nuclear lamina.</li><li><b>Survivin</b> — Survivin inhibits apoptosis.</li><li><b>BCL-2</b> — BCL-2 is anti-apoptotic.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Li–Fraumeni = germline TP53; HPV E6 degrades p53.</p>","w":["Caveolin forms caveolae.","Lamins form the nuclear lamina.","Survivin inhibits apoptosis.","BCL-2 is anti-apoptotic.","Correct — p53."]},"fa52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Survivin (an IAP — inhibitor of apoptosis protein) blocks caspases and prevents apoptosis; it's overexpressed in many cancers.</p><p>IAPs bind and inhibit caspases-3, -7 and -9. SMAC/DIABLO released from mitochondria neutralizes IAPs, allowing apoptosis. High survivin expression contributes to chemoresistance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Repair damaged DNA</b> — Survivins don't repair DNA.</li><li><b>Flip phosphatidylserine outward</b> — Phosphatidylserine flipping is part of apoptosis, which survivin opposes.</li><li><b>Release cytochrome c</b> — Releasing cytochrome c is the job of BAX/BAK pores.</li><li><b>Activate caspases to speed apoptosis</b> — Survivins block, rather than activate, caspases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> IAPs vs SMAC/DIABLO.</p>","w":["Survivins don't repair DNA.","Correct — survivins inhibit apoptosis by blocking caspases.","Phosphatidylserine flipping is part of apoptosis, which survivin opposes.","Releasing cytochrome c is the job of BAX/BAK pores.","Survivins block, rather than activate, caspases."]},"fa53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis is ATP-dependent; if ATP is depleted, the cell dies by necrosis instead.</p><p>Energy is needed for apoptosome assembly, caspase cascades, cytoskeletal reorganization (blebbing) and phosphatidylserine exposure. Severe ischemia depletes ATP and shifts death toward necrosis (swelling, membrane rupture, inflammation).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Yes — but it proceeds anyway without ATP</b> — Without ATP the cell can't proceed with apoptosis; it dies by necrosis.</li><li><b>It doesn't matter</b> — ATP availability determines the mode of death.</li><li><b>No — it is passive</b> — Apoptosis isn't passive; it needs ATP.</li><li><b>No — it needs only Ca²⁺</b> — Ca²⁺ alone doesn't drive apoptosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ATP available → apoptosis; depleted → necrosis.</p>","w":["Without ATP the cell can't proceed with apoptosis; it dies by necrosis.","ATP availability determines the mode of death.","Apoptosis isn't passive; it needs ATP.","Ca²⁺ alone doesn't drive apoptosis.","Correct — yes; without ATP the cell undergoes necrosis."]},"fa54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Phosphatidylserine flipping from the inner to the outer leaflet is an \"eat-me\" signal on apoptotic cells.</p><p>Caspases inactivate flippases and activate scramblases, exposing PS. Macrophages recognize PS and engulf the apoptotic cells quietly. PS exposure on activated platelets also provides a surface for coagulation factor complexes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sphingomyelin moves to the inner leaflet</b> — Sphingomyelin is an outer-leaflet lipid and doesn't move inward in apoptosis.</li><li><b>Glycolipids are lost from the surface</b> — Loss of glycolipids isn't the hallmark of apoptosis.</li><li><b>Cholesterol leaves the membrane</b> — Cholesterol loss isn't the hallmark of apoptosis.</li><li><b>Caveolae form</b> — Caveolae formation isn't an apoptotic marker.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PS exposure → annexin V binding.</p>","w":["Sphingomyelin is an outer-leaflet lipid and doesn't move inward in apoptosis.","Loss of glycolipids isn't the hallmark of apoptosis.","Cholesterol loss isn't the hallmark of apoptosis.","Caveolae formation isn't an apoptotic marker.","Correct — phosphatidylserine flips to the outer leaflet."]},"fa55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Annexin V binds externalized phosphatidylserine, detecting early apoptotic cells (usually by flow cytometry, often with propidium iodide to identify late apoptotic/necrotic cells).</p><p>Early apoptosis: annexin V positive, PI negative (membrane intact). Late apoptosis/necrosis: both positive. Other apoptosis markers: TUNEL, cleaved caspase-3, cleaved PARP, DNA laddering.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Gram stain</b> — The Gram stain classifies bacteria.</li><li><b>Western blot for actin</b> — Western blot for actin doesn't detect phosphatidylserine exposure.</li><li><b>Gomori trichrome stain</b> — Gomori trichrome is a muscle stain (ragged red fibers).</li><li><b>Benedict's test</b> — Benedict's test detects reducing sugars.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Annexin V+/PI− = early apoptosis.</p>","w":["The Gram stain classifies bacteria.","Western blot for actin doesn't detect phosphatidylserine exposure.","Gomori trichrome is a muscle stain (ragged red fibers).","Benedict's test detects reducing sugars.","Correct — annexin V staining."]},"fa56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Phagocytes that engulf apoptotic cells release anti-inflammatory IL-10 and TGF-β, so apoptosis is non-inflammatory.</p><p>Apoptotic cells keep their membranes intact and are removed before they release contents. In contrast, necrosis releases intracellular contents (DAMPs), recruiting neutrophils and causing inflammation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Complement is activated</b> — Complement activation is an inflammatory process.</li><li><b>Neutrophils are recruited</b> — Neutrophil recruitment indicates inflammation, typical of necrosis.</li><li><b>Cells burst and release their contents</b> — Cells bursting and releasing their contents is necrosis.</li><li><b>Mast cells release histamine</b> — Mast cell histamine release is an inflammatory allergic response.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apoptosis = no inflammation; necrosis = inflammation.</p>","w":["Complement activation is an inflammatory process.","Correct — phagocytes release IL-10 and TGF-β.","Neutrophil recruitment indicates inflammation, typical of necrosis.","Cells bursting and releasing their contents is necrosis.","Mast cell histamine release is an inflammatory allergic response."]},"fa57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The centrosome (two centrioles + pericentriolar material containing γ-tubulin ring complexes) is the main microtubule-organizing center.</p><p>Centrosomes duplicate once per cycle (during S phase) and separate in prophase to form the two spindle poles. Centrioles have a 9 × 3 triplet microtubule arrangement and also act as basal bodies for cilia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caveolae; made of caveolin</b> — Caveolae are cholesterol-rich membrane invaginations made of caveolin.</li><li><b>Nucleolus; made of rRNA</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA and doesn't organize the spindle.</li><li><b>Kinetochore; a protein–DNA complex</b> — Kinetochores attach to microtubules but don't nucleate or organize them.</li><li><b>Golgi; made of cisternae</b> — The Golgi doesn't organize the spindle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Centrioles: 9 triplets; basal bodies for cilia.</p>","w":["Caveolae are cholesterol-rich membrane invaginations made of caveolin.","Correct — centrosome; centrioles with γ-tubulin rings.","The nucleolus makes rRNA and doesn't organize the spindle.","Kinetochores attach to microtubules but don't nucleate or organize them.","The Golgi doesn't organize the spindle."]},"fa58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Spindle microtubules: kinetochore (attach and move chromosomes), polar/interpolar (overlap at the midzone, push poles apart), astral (anchor poles to the cell cortex, position the spindle).</p><p>In anaphase A, kinetochore microtubules shorten to pull chromatids to the poles; in anaphase B, polar microtubules slide apart and astral microtubules pull poles outward.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>All three attach chromosomes</b> — Only kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes.</li><li><b>Astral — attach chromosomes</b> — Astral microtubules anchor the poles; kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes.</li><li><b>Polar — anchor the poles to the cortex</b> — Polar microtubules overlap to stabilize the spindle; astral microtubules anchor poles.</li><li><b>Kinetochore — anchor the spindle poles to the cortex</b> — Kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes; they don't anchor poles to the cortex.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anaphase A (kinetochore MTs shorten) vs B (polar/astral elongate spindle).</p>","w":["Only kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes.","Astral microtubules anchor the poles; kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes.","Correct — kinetochore move chromosomes; astral anchor poles.","Polar microtubules overlap to stabilize the spindle; astral microtubules anchor poles.","Kinetochore microtubules attach chromosomes; they don't anchor poles to the cortex."]},"fa59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Without functional centrosomes, cells can't properly form a bipolar mitotic spindle (risking chromosome missegregation).</p><p>DNA replication, transcription, translation and nuclear lamina maintenance don't depend on centrosomes. Abnormal centrosome numbers (amplification) cause multipolar spindles and aneuploidy, common in cancer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transcribe genes</b> — Transcription is centrosome-independent.</li><li><b>Maintain its nuclear shape and lamina</b> — Nuclear shape is maintained by the nuclear lamina, not the centrosome.</li><li><b>Translate mRNA</b> — Translation is centrosome-independent.</li><li><b>Replicate DNA</b> — DNA replication is centrosome-independent.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Centrosome amplification → multipolar spindles → aneuploidy.</p>","w":["Transcription is centrosome-independent.","Nuclear shape is maintained by the nuclear lamina, not the centrosome.","Translation is centrosome-independent.","DNA replication is centrosome-independent.","Correct — properly forming a bipolar mitotic spindle."]},"fa60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Plasma membrane core functions: envelop the cell, protect contents, regulate traffic (semipermeable barrier) and participate in signaling.</p><p>Additional roles: cell adhesion and junctions, cell recognition (glycocalyx), endocytosis/exocytosis, and maintaining membrane potential. Energy production, protein and DNA synthesis occur in organelles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Photosynthesis and respiration</b> — Photosynthesis and respiration aren't human plasma membrane functions.</li><li><b>Making and storing hormones</b> — Hormones are made and stored by organelles, not by the plasma membrane itself.</li><li><b>Make ATP, proteins, DNA and stored lipid</b> — ATP, protein, DNA and lipid storage occur in organelles.</li><li><b>Only acting as a passive barrier</b> — The membrane isn't only a passive barrier; it actively regulates transport and signals.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Membrane = barrier + transport + signaling + adhesion.</p>","w":["Photosynthesis and respiration aren't human plasma membrane functions.","Hormones are made and stored by organelles, not by the plasma membrane itself.","ATP, protein, DNA and lipid storage occur in organelles.","The membrane isn't only a passive barrier; it actively regulates transport and signals.","Correct — envelope, protect, regulate traffic, signal."]},"fa61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The plasma membrane and most organelle membranes are single lipid bilayers; they differ in their protein and lipid composition (which gives each membrane its function).</p><p>Examples: the inner mitochondrial membrane is rich in proteins (ETC) and cardiolipin; the plasma membrane is rich in cholesterol; lysosomal membranes have heavily glycosylated proteins. Mitochondria and the nucleus have two bilayers each (double membranes).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A carbohydrate lattice</b> — Carbohydrates are only on the outer surface; they don't form the membrane.</li><li><b>A double bilayer; no differences</b> — Most membranes are a single bilayer, and compositions do differ.</li><li><b>A protein sheet; organelles differ only in lipid</b> — Membranes are lipid bilayers with embedded proteins, not protein sheets.</li><li><b>A cholesterol monolayer</b> — Membranes are bilayers, not cholesterol monolayers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cardiolipin = inner mitochondrial membrane.</p>","w":["Carbohydrates are only on the outer surface; they don't form the membrane.","Most membranes are a single bilayer, and compositions do differ.","Correct — a single lipid bilayer that differs in composition.","Membranes are lipid bilayers with embedded proteins, not protein sheets.","Membranes are bilayers, not cholesterol monolayers."]},"fa62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amphipathic = having both hydrophilic (phosphate head) and hydrophobic (fatty acid tails) regions; leaflets are held together by weak van der Waals/hydrophobic interactions between tails.</p><p>No covalent bonds join the leaflets, so membranes are fluid, flexible, self-sealing and able to fuse with vesicles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fully hydrophilic; ionic bonds</b> — Phospholipids aren't fully hydrophilic, and leaflets aren't held by ionic bonds.</li><li><b>Charged; disulfide bonds</b> — Disulfide bonds join cysteines in proteins, not membrane leaflets.</li><li><b>Neutral; hydrogen bonds between the head groups</b> — Head groups face water on opposite sides; the tails hold the leaflets together.</li><li><b>Fully hydrophobic; covalent bonds</b> — Phospholipids aren't fully hydrophobic, and the leaflets aren't covalently bonded.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amphipathic: phospholipids, bile salts, detergents.</p>","w":["Phospholipids aren't fully hydrophilic, and leaflets aren't held by ionic bonds.","Disulfide bonds join cysteines in proteins, not membrane leaflets.","Correct — hydrophilic + hydrophobic parts; van der Waals forces.","Head groups face water on opposite sides; the tails hold the leaflets together.","Phospholipids aren't fully hydrophobic, and the leaflets aren't covalently bonded."]},"fa63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane asymmetry: outer leaflet — phosphatidylcholine, sphingomyelin (and glycolipids); inner leaflet — phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylinositol.</p><p>ATP-dependent flippases maintain PS and PE on the inner leaflet. PS exposure marks apoptotic cells and activated platelets. Inner-leaflet PI is the precursor of PIP₂ for signaling.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Outer: PS; inner: sphingomyelin</b> — This reverses PS and sphingomyelin.</li><li><b>Both leaflets are identical</b> — The leaflets are asymmetric, not identical.</li><li><b>Inner: phosphatidylcholine only; outer: all others</b> — Phosphatidylcholine is an outer-leaflet lipid.</li><li><b>Outer: PI only; inner: everything else</b> — Phosphatidylinositol is an inner-leaflet lipid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Choline lipids outside\" (PC, sphingomyelin).</p>","w":["This reverses PS and sphingomyelin.","The leaflets are asymmetric, not identical.","Phosphatidylcholine is an outer-leaflet lipid.","Correct — outer PC and sphingomyelin; inner PE, PS, PI.","Phosphatidylinositol is an inner-leaflet lipid."]},"fa64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cholesterol lies parallel to the fatty acid tails (–OH at the head groups) and fills gaps from kinks in unsaturated tails, restricting tail motion.</p><p>This stiffens the membrane and decreases permeability to small water-soluble molecules. Cholesterol is concentrated in lipid rafts. RBC membranes with excess cholesterol (liver disease) form acanthocytes/spur cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Bound to glycolipids</b> — Cholesterol isn't bound to glycolipids.</li><li><b>Only on the membrane surface</b> — Cholesterol is inserted within the bilayer, not just on the surface.</li><li><b>Free in the cytosol</b> — Membrane cholesterol isn't free in the cytosol.</li><li><b>Perpendicular to the tails; it breaks their bonds</b> — Cholesterol lies parallel to the tails and doesn't break bonds.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholesterol ↓ permeability, ↑ mechanical stability.</p>","w":["Cholesterol isn't bound to glycolipids.","Cholesterol is inserted within the bilayer, not just on the surface.","Correct — parallel to the tails; fills gaps from kinks.","Membrane cholesterol isn't free in the cytosol.","Cholesterol lies parallel to the tails and doesn't break bonds."]},"fa65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer: at low temperature it prevents tight packing (↑ fluidity); at high temperature it restrains tail movement (↓ fluidity).</p><p>This keeps membranes in a functional state over a range of temperatures. At normal body temperature, its overall effect is to decrease fluidity and increase stability.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Always increases fluidity</b> — Cholesterol doesn't always increase fluidity.</li><li><b>No effect at either temperature</b> — Cholesterol does affect fluidity — it buffers it.</li><li><b>Always decreases fluidity</b> — Cholesterol doesn't always decrease fluidity.</li><li><b>Low temp: decreases fluidity; high temp: increases</b> — This reverses the effects at low and high temperature.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholesterol = membrane fluidity buffer.</p>","w":["Cholesterol doesn't always increase fluidity.","Cholesterol does affect fluidity — it buffers it.","Cholesterol doesn't always decrease fluidity.","Correct — increases at low temperature, decreases at high temperature.","This reverses the effects at low and high temperature."]},"fa66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Without cholesterol: higher temperature → more fluid; more unsaturated (cis double bonds, kinks) → more fluid; more saturated → more rigid.</p><p>Shorter fatty acid chains also increase fluidity. Cells adapt to cold by increasing unsaturated and shorter fatty acids (homeoviscous adaptation).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Temperature affects only membrane proteins</b> — Temperature directly affects lipid fluidity, not only proteins.</li><li><b>More saturation increases fluidity</b> — More saturation decreases fluidity.</li><li><b>Neither temperature nor saturation matters</b> — Both temperature and saturation matter.</li><li><b>↑ temperature ↓ fluidity; ↑ unsaturation ↓ fluidity too</b> — This reverses both relationships.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Unsaturation → kinks → fluidity.</p>","w":["Temperature directly affects lipid fluidity, not only proteins.","More saturation decreases fluidity.","Both temperature and saturation matter.","Correct — ↑ temperature and ↑ unsaturation both increase fluidity.","This reverses both relationships."]},"fa67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hypothermia stiffens membranes (reduced fluidity), lowering permeability and impairing transporter function.</p><p>Reduced membrane fluidity slows the activity of membrane enzymes, transporters and channels, contributing to reduced cellular uptake of glucose and O₂ and slower metabolism. Therapeutic hypothermia exploits reduced metabolic rate to protect the brain after cardiac arrest.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fluidity increases and membrane permeability rises</b> — Cold decreases, rather than increases, fluidity and permeability.</li><li><b>Cholesterol leaves the membrane</b> — Cholesterol stays in the membrane in the cold.</li><li><b>No effect on the membrane</b> — Cold does affect the membrane.</li><li><b>The membrane dissolves</b> — Cold stiffens the membrane; it doesn't dissolve it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cold → rigid membrane → ↓ permeability.</p>","w":["Cold decreases, rather than increases, fluidity and permeability.","Cholesterol stays in the membrane in the cold.","Cold does affect the membrane.","Cold stiffens the membrane; it doesn't dissolve it.","Correct — the membrane stiffens and permeability falls."]},"fa68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycolipids are lipids with carbohydrates that always face the extracellular space; they contribute to the glycocalyx, cell recognition, blood group antigens and serve as toxin receptors.</p><p>Gangliosides (sialic acid–containing glycolipids) are abundant in neurons; GM1 binds cholera toxin. Defective breakdown causes sphingolipidoses (e.g., GM2 in Tay–Sachs).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Free sugars inside the nucleus</b> — Free sugars inside the nucleus aren't glycolipids.</li><li><b>Proteins with internal sugars; enzymes</b> — Proteins with sugars are glycoproteins, and their sugars face outward.</li><li><b>Lipids with sugars facing the cytosol; energy storage</b> — Glycolipid sugars face outward, and they aren't energy stores.</li><li><b>Cholesterol derivatives</b> — Cholesterol derivatives aren't glycolipids.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GM1 = cholera toxin receptor.</p>","w":["Free sugars inside the nucleus aren't glycolipids.","Proteins with sugars are glycoproteins, and their sugars face outward.","Glycolipid sugars face outward, and they aren't energy stores.","Cholesterol derivatives aren't glycolipids.","Correct — lipids with outward-facing sugars for recognition and toxin binding."]},"fa69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cholera toxin binds GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes; its A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gsα, keeping it permanently on → ↑ cAMP → CFTR opens → massive Cl⁻ and water secretion.</p><p>Result: profuse \"rice-water\" diarrhea and dehydration. Treatment: oral rehydration (SGLT1 still works). Other AB toxins: pertussis (ADP-ribosylates Gi, inactivating it → ↑cAMP), diphtheria and Pseudomonas exotoxin A (ADP-ribosylate EF-2 → stop translation), botulinum (cleaves SNAREs → blocks ACh release), tetanus (blocks GABA/glycine release from inhibitory neurons).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Botulinum toxin; blocks ACh release</b> — Botulinum toxin blocks ACh release but doesn't use GM1 in this classic association.</li><li><b>Pertussis toxin; inhibits Gi</b> — Pertussis toxin inactivates Gi; it isn't the GM1 classic.</li><li><b>Diphtheria toxin; ADP-ribosylates and inactivates EF-2</b> — Diphtheria toxin inactivates EF-2; it doesn't use GM1.</li><li><b>Tetanus toxin; blocks glycine release</b> — Tetanus toxin also binds gangliosides, but the classic GM1 toxin is cholera.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholera: Gs ↑ (ADP-ribosylation) → ↑cAMP.</p>","w":["Botulinum toxin blocks ACh release but doesn't use GM1 in this classic association.","Pertussis toxin inactivates Gi; it isn't the GM1 classic.","Diphtheria toxin inactivates EF-2; it doesn't use GM1.","Correct — cholera toxin; ↑ cAMP → Cl⁻ and water secretion.","Tetanus toxin also binds gangliosides, but the classic GM1 toxin is cholera."]},"fa70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane proteins follow the secretory pathway: translated on the RER (inserted into the ER membrane) → processed in the Golgi → carried in vesicles to the plasma membrane.</p><p>Topology is set during translation in the ER: the luminal side of ER/Golgi becomes the extracellular face after vesicle fusion, so glycosylated domains face outward.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleus → nuclear pore → membrane</b> — Proteins aren't made in the nucleus.</li><li><b>Made in mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria make only a few of their own proteins.</li><li><b>Made in lysosomes</b> — Lysosomes degrade proteins rather than make them.</li><li><b>Free ribosome → cytosol → membrane</b> — Free ribosomes make cytosolic proteins, not plasma membrane proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ER lumen ≈ extracellular side.</p>","w":["Proteins aren't made in the nucleus.","Mitochondria make only a few of their own proteins.","Lysosomes degrade proteins rather than make them.","Free ribosomes make cytosolic proteins, not plasma membrane proteins.","Correct — RER → Golgi → vesicle → membrane."]},"fa71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peripheral membrane proteins attach noncovalently (to integral proteins or lipid heads) and don't enter the bilayer; examples include electron carriers (cytochrome c), cytoskeletal proteins (spectrin, ankyrin) and signaling proteins.</p><p>They're released by high-salt or pH changes, without detergents. Integral proteins require detergents for extraction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They span the bilayer; transporters</b> — Spanning the bilayer describes integral proteins.</li><li><b>Covalently to cholesterol; channels</b> — Peripheral proteins aren't covalently attached to cholesterol.</li><li><b>Disulfide bonds; adhesion</b> — Peripheral proteins aren't attached by disulfide bonds.</li><li><b>Through GPI anchors only; they act as receptors</b> — GPI-anchored proteins are lipid-anchored, not peripheral.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Peripheral = salt-extractable; integral = detergent-extractable.</p>","w":["Spanning the bilayer describes integral proteins.","Peripheral proteins aren't covalently attached to cholesterol.","Peripheral proteins aren't attached by disulfide bonds.","Correct — noncovalent attachment; e.g., electron carriers.","GPI-anchored proteins are lipid-anchored, not peripheral."]},"fa72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Integral proteins are anchored by hydrophobic transmembrane domains (or lipid anchors) and function as receptors, transporters, channels, enzymes and adhesion molecules.</p><p>Single-pass proteins (e.g., receptor tyrosine kinases) cross once; multipass proteins (e.g., GPCRs with seven helices, transporters) cross several times.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They float in the cytosol; enzymes</b> — Integral proteins are embedded in the membrane, not floating in the cytosol.</li><li><b>Peripherally; electron carriers</b> — Peripheral attachment with electron carrier function describes peripheral proteins.</li><li><b>By disulfide bonds to the cytoskeleton; as motors</b> — Integral proteins are embedded in the bilayer, not disulfide-linked to the cytoskeleton.</li><li><b>By sugars only; storage</b> — Sugars don't anchor proteins, and storage isn't their role.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GPCR = 7-pass transmembrane.</p>","w":["Correct — hydrophobic transmembrane domain; receptors and transporters.","Integral proteins are embedded in the membrane, not floating in the cytosol.","Peripheral attachment with electron carrier function describes peripheral proteins.","Integral proteins are embedded in the bilayer, not disulfide-linked to the cytoskeleton.","Sugars don't anchor proteins, and storage isn't their role."]},"fa73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> G proteins are lipid-anchored (prenylated/palmitoylated) to the inner leaflet and transmit signals from GPCRs.</p><p>Heterotrimeric G proteins (α, β, γ) cycle between GDP (off) and GTP (on). Gs activates adenylyl cyclase (↑cAMP), Gi inhibits it, and Gq activates phospholipase C (IP₃, DAG). Small G proteins like Ras are farnesylated — statins and farnesyltransferase inhibitors affect this lipid anchoring.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Spectrin; maintaining RBC shape</b> — Spectrin is a cytoskeletal protein bound via ankyrin, not lipid-anchored for signaling.</li><li><b>Hemoglobin; O₂ carrying</b> — Hemoglobin is a cytosolic RBC protein.</li><li><b>Histones; DNA packaging</b> — Histones are nuclear proteins.</li><li><b>Collagen; tensile strength</b> — Collagen is an extracellular structural protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ras = farnesylated (lipid-anchored) small G protein.</p>","w":["Spectrin is a cytoskeletal protein bound via ankyrin, not lipid-anchored for signaling.","Correct — G proteins; cell signaling.","Hemoglobin is a cytosolic RBC protein.","Histones are nuclear proteins.","Collagen is an extracellular structural protein."]},"fa74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Transmembrane segments cross the bilayer as α-helices (often bundles) or as β-barrels (rolled β-sheets, e.g., porins).</p><p>A transmembrane α-helix needs ~20 hydrophobic amino acids to span the ~3-nm hydrophobic core. β-barrels occur in the outer membranes of bacteria, mitochondria and chloroplasts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Collagen-like triple helices</b> — Collagen-like triple helices are extracellular structural proteins.</li><li><b>Only random coils</b> — Random coils aren't stable within the bilayer.</li><li><b>Only disulfide loops</b> — Disulfide loops don't span membranes.</li><li><b>Zinc fingers</b> — Zinc fingers are DNA-binding motifs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> α-helix (~20 hydrophobic residues) spans the membrane.</p>","w":["Collagen-like triple helices are extracellular structural proteins.","Random coils aren't stable within the bilayer.","Disulfide loops don't span membranes.","Correct — α-helical bundles or β-barrels.","Zinc fingers are DNA-binding motifs."]},"fa75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The three functional types of transmembrane proteins: transporters (carriers/pumps), channels and membrane receptors.</p><p>Transporters bind solutes and change conformation (saturable). Channels form pores for fast ion flow (gated). Receptors bind extracellular signals and relay them inside.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Actin, myosin, tubulin</b> — Actin, myosin and tubulin are cytoskeletal/motor proteins.</li><li><b>Lamins, keratins, desmin</b> — Lamins, keratins and desmin are intermediate filaments.</li><li><b>Enzymes, hormones and circulating antibodies</b> — Hormones and circulating antibodies aren't transmembrane proteins.</li><li><b>Histones, nucleoporins and nuclear lamins</b> — Histones, nucleoporins and lamins are nuclear proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Transporter vs channel vs receptor.</p>","w":["Actin, myosin and tubulin are cytoskeletal/motor proteins.","Lamins, keratins and desmin are intermediate filaments.","Correct — transporters, channels and receptors.","Hormones and circulating antibodies aren't transmembrane proteins.","Histones, nucleoporins and lamins are nuclear proteins."]},"fa76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycoproteins are proteins with covalently attached carbohydrates facing the extracellular space; GAGs (glycosaminoglycans) are long, strongly negatively charged polysaccharides.</p><p>GAGs attach to core proteins to form proteoglycans, bind water (forming gels) and bind positively charged molecules like growth factors (e.g., FGF on heparan sulfate). Glycoprotein sugars mediate recognition (e.g., ABO antigens, selectin ligands).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sugars only; GAGs are lipids</b> — A glycoprotein is a protein, and GAGs are polysaccharides, not lipids.</li><li><b>Proteins in the nucleus; GAGs are DNA</b> — These aren't correct definitions; GAGs aren't DNA.</li><li><b>Proteins with lipids; GAGs are neutral sugars</b> — Glycoproteins carry sugars, not lipids, and GAGs are negatively charged.</li><li><b>Lipids with sugars; GAGs are positively charged chains</b> — Lipids with sugars are glycolipids, and GAGs are negatively charged.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GAGs = polyanions (bind cations, growth factors, water).</p>","w":["Correct — protein with outward-facing sugars; GAGs negatively charged.","A glycoprotein is a protein, and GAGs are polysaccharides, not lipids.","These aren't correct definitions; GAGs aren't DNA.","Glycoproteins carry sugars, not lipids, and GAGs are negatively charged.","Lipids with sugars are glycolipids, and GAGs are negatively charged."]},"fa77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ABO type is determined by terminal surface carbohydrates (added by glycosyltransferases to the H antigen); type O has neither A nor B sugar.</p><p>A allele adds N-acetylgalactosamine; B allele adds galactose; O allele encodes an inactive enzyme. Rh (D antigen) is a separate protein. Type O people have anti-A and anti-B (IgM) antibodies.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lipid rafts; type O has no rafts</b> — Lipid rafts don't determine blood type.</li><li><b>Hemoglobin type; type O has no globin</b> — Blood type isn't determined by hemoglobin.</li><li><b>The Rh protein; type O has no Rh antigen</b> — The Rh antigen is a separate system from ABO.</li><li><b>Spectrin; type O has no spectrin</b> — Spectrin doesn't determine blood type.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Type O = universal RBC donor.</p>","w":["Lipid rafts don't determine blood type.","Blood type isn't determined by hemoglobin.","The Rh antigen is a separate system from ABO.","Spectrin doesn't determine blood type.","Correct — surface carbohydrates; type O lacks both A and B."]},"fa78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Uniporter = one solute (GLUT1); symporter = two solutes in the same direction (SGLT1: Na⁺ + glucose); antiporter = opposite directions (NCX: Na⁺ in, Ca²⁺ out).</p><p>GLUT1 (RBCs, BBB) uses facilitated diffusion. SGLT1 (intestine) and SGLT2 (proximal tubule) are secondary active transporters. The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is a primary active pump (ATP), technically an antiporter pump.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Symporter — the Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX)</b> — NCX is an antiporter, not a symporter.</li><li><b>Antiporter — GLUT1</b> — GLUT1 is a uniporter, not an antiporter.</li><li><b>Uniporter — SGLT1</b> — SGLT1 is a symporter, not a uniporter.</li><li><b>Uniporter — Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase</b> — The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is an ATP-driven pump, not a uniporter.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SGLT2 inhibitors → glucosuria (diabetes treatment).</p>","w":["NCX is an antiporter, not a symporter.","GLUT1 is a uniporter, not an antiporter.","Correct — GLUT1 uniporter; SGLT1 symporter.","SGLT1 is a symporter, not a uniporter.","The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is an ATP-driven pump, not a uniporter."]},"fa79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ABC transporters are ATP-powered pumps; MDR1 (P-glycoprotein) effluxes chemotherapy drugs, causing multidrug resistance in cancer.</p><p>Other ABC members: CFTR (Cl⁻ channel), ABCD1 (VLCFA import into peroxisomes — X-ALD), ABCA1 (cholesterol efflux to HDL — Tangier disease), ABCG5/G8 (sterol efflux — sitosterolemia).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ion channels for Na⁺</b> — ABC transporters aren't Na⁺ ion channels.</li><li><b>Water channels</b> — ABC transporters aren't water channels (aquaporins).</li><li><b>Glucose uniporters</b> — ABC transporters aren't glucose uniporters.</li><li><b>Passive channels; they cause cystic fibrosis only</b> — ABC transporters use ATP; CFTR is one member, but they don't cause only CF.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ABCA1 → Tangier disease (very low HDL).</p>","w":["Correct — ATP-powered pumps; MDR effluxes chemotherapy.","ABC transporters aren't Na⁺ ion channels.","ABC transporters aren't water channels (aquaporins).","ABC transporters aren't glucose uniporters.","ABC transporters use ATP; CFTR is one member, but they don't cause only CF."]},"fa80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> P-glycoprotein at the blood–brain barrier pumps many drugs back into the blood, limiting CNS penetration.</p><p>P-gp in endothelial cells of brain capillaries, intestine (limits absorption) and kidney/liver (enhances excretion) protects tissues from xenobiotics. Drug interactions: P-gp inhibitors (verapamil, amiodarone, macrolides) raise levels of substrates like digoxin; loperamide stays out of the brain because P-gp excludes it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>P-gp is found only in the kidney</b> — P-gp is found in many tissues, including the BBB, not only the kidney.</li><li><b>P-gp destroys neurons</b> — P-gp doesn't destroy neurons.</li><li><b>P-gp rapidly metabolizes drugs in the liver</b> — P-gp transports, rather than metabolizes, drugs.</li><li><b>P-gp blocks synapses</b> — P-gp doesn't block synapses.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Loperamide: P-gp keeps it out of the CNS.</p>","w":["P-gp is found in many tissues, including the BBB, not only the kidney.","Correct — P-gp pumps drugs out of the brain at the BBB.","P-gp doesn't destroy neurons.","P-gp transports, rather than metabolizes, drugs.","P-gp doesn't block synapses."]},"fa81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Channels form narrow, selective, hydrophilic pores that let specific ions (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻) flow down their gradients; the lecture emphasizes their role in the kidney (concentrating urine).</p><p>Channels are fast (millions of ions/second), gated (voltage, ligand, mechanical) and selective. In the nephron, channels such as ENaC, ROMK and CFTR and the aquaporins move ions and water to adjust urine composition.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transport vesicles; the brain</b> — Transport vesicles aren't channels.</li><li><b>ATP-driven ion pumps; the heart</b> — ATP-driven pumps are transporters, not channels.</li><li><b>Large nonselective pores; the liver</b> — Channels are selective, not large nonselective pores.</li><li><b>Sugar carriers; the gut</b> — Sugar carriers are transporters, not channels.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Channels: fast, gated, selective, passive.</p>","w":["Transport vesicles aren't channels.","Correct — narrow selective ion pores; the kidneys.","ATP-driven pumps are transporters, not channels.","Channels are selective, not large nonselective pores.","Sugar carriers are transporters, not channels."]},"fa82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane receptors have extracellular (ligand-binding), transmembrane (spans bilayer) and intracellular (signal-transducing) domains.</p><p>The intracellular domain may have kinase activity (receptor tyrosine kinases), couple to G proteins (GPCRs) or recruit adaptor proteins (cytokine receptors → JAK–STAT).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Head, neck and tail</b> — Head, neck and tail aren't receptor domains.</li><li><b>A single hydrophobic domain</b> — Receptors have three domains, not a single hydrophobic one.</li><li><b>Catalytic, regulatory and storage domains</b> — Catalytic, regulatory and storage domains aren't the receptor domain scheme.</li><li><b>Nuclear, cytosolic, membrane</b> — Receptors don't have nuclear and cytosolic domains as separate parts.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Receptor = ligand binding + transmembrane + signaling domains.</p>","w":["Head, neck and tail aren't receptor domains.","Receptors have three domains, not a single hydrophobic one.","Catalytic, regulatory and storage domains aren't the receptor domain scheme.","Correct — extracellular, transmembrane, intracellular.","Receptors don't have nuclear and cytosolic domains as separate parts."]},"fa83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A ligand receptor spans the membrane, so it's an integral (transmembrane) protein.</p><p>Integral proteins have hydrophobic membrane-spanning segments and are removed only with detergents. Peripheral and lipid-anchored proteins sit on one side and can't relay signals across the bilayer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peripheral protein</b> — Peripheral proteins don't span the membrane.</li><li><b>Lipid raft</b> — A lipid raft is a membrane region, not a single protein.</li><li><b>Lipid-anchored (GPI-linked) protein</b> — GPI-anchored proteins don't span the membrane.</li><li><b>Glycolipid</b> — Receptors are proteins, not glycolipids.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Signal relay across membrane = transmembrane protein.</p>","w":["Correct — integral (transmembrane) membrane protein.","Peripheral proteins don't span the membrane.","A lipid raft is a membrane region, not a single protein.","GPI-anchored proteins don't span the membrane.","Receptors are proteins, not glycolipids."]},"fa84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fluid mosaic model: proteins float within a fluid phospholipid bilayer and move laterally; this fluidity enables exocytosis, endocytosis, trafficking and membrane biogenesis.</p><p>Supported by the mouse–human cell fusion experiment (proteins intermix within ~40 min). Fluidity is regulated by temperature, fatty acid saturation and cholesterol.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A rigid protein lattice; it doesn't matter</b> — The membrane is fluid, not a rigid protein lattice, and fluidity matters.</li><li><b>A double protein layer; for strength</b> — The membrane isn't a protein double layer.</li><li><b>A carbohydrate shell; for protection</b> — The membrane isn't a carbohydrate shell.</li><li><b>A static lipid crystal; mainly for insulation</b> — The membrane is fluid, not a static crystal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Frye–Edidin experiment → lateral diffusion.</p>","w":["Correct — proteins float in a lipid sea; needed for trafficking.","The membrane is fluid, not a rigid protein lattice, and fluidity matters.","The membrane isn't a protein double layer.","The membrane isn't a carbohydrate shell.","The membrane is fluid, not a static crystal."]},"fa85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cell polarity = regional differences in membrane structure and function; epithelial cells are the classic example (apical vs basolateral surfaces).</p><p>Tight junctions keep apical and basolateral proteins separate (fence function). Polarity enables directional transport (e.g., absorption across enterocytes).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cell size; RBCs</b> — Cell size doesn't define polarity.</li><li><b>Differences in electrical charge; seen only in neurons</b> — Polarity here means structural/functional asymmetry, not electrical charge.</li><li><b>Ploidy; gametes</b> — Ploidy has nothing to do with cell polarity.</li><li><b>Nuclear position; muscle</b> — Nuclear position isn't the defining feature here.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tight junctions = fence for polarity.</p>","w":["Cell size doesn't define polarity.","Polarity here means structural/functional asymmetry, not electrical charge.","Ploidy has nothing to do with cell polarity.","Correct — regional differences; epithelium.","Nuclear position isn't the defining feature here."]},"fa86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epithelial cells are joined into sheets by tight junctions (zonula occludens), adherens junctions (zonula adherens) and desmosomes (macula adherens).</p><p>Tight junctions (claudins, occludin) seal the paracellular space; adherens junctions (E-cadherin + actin) form a belt; desmosomes (desmogleins + keratin) are spot welds. Gap junctions (connexins) allow communication; hemidesmosomes (integrins) anchor to the basement membrane.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hemidesmosomes, focal adhesions and caveolae</b> — Hemidesmosomes bind the basement membrane, and caveolae aren't junctions.</li><li><b>Gap junctions, synapses, plasmodesmata</b> — Gap junctions communicate; synapses and plasmodesmata aren't the epithelial sheet-forming junctions.</li><li><b>Lipid rafts, caveolae, cilia</b> — Lipid rafts, caveolae and cilia aren't cell–cell junctions.</li><li><b>Nuclear pores, lamins, LINC</b> — Nuclear pores, lamins and LINC are nuclear structures.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pemphigus = desmoglein antibodies; E-cadherin loss → metastasis.</p>","w":["Hemidesmosomes bind the basement membrane, and caveolae aren't junctions.","Gap junctions communicate; synapses and plasmodesmata aren't the epithelial sheet-forming junctions.","Correct — tight junctions, desmosomes and adherens junctions.","Lipid rafts, caveolae and cilia aren't cell–cell junctions.","Nuclear pores, lamins and LINC are nuclear structures."]},"fa87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The glycocalyx is the carbohydrate coat on the outer cell surface; it provides attachment, antigen binding, cell recognition and protection.</p><p>It's formed by the sugar chains of glycoproteins, glycolipids and proteoglycans. It contributes to blood group antigens, immune recognition and protection from mechanical and chemical injury.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A mitochondrial membrane; ATP</b> — The glycocalyx isn't a mitochondrial membrane and doesn't make ATP.</li><li><b>A nuclear layer; DNA protection</b> — The glycocalyx isn't a nuclear layer.</li><li><b>A lipid layer; insulation</b> — The glycocalyx isn't a lipid insulation layer.</li><li><b>A cytoskeletal mesh under the membrane; maintains shape</b> — The cytoskeleton lies inside the cell, under the membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycocalyx = sugar coat for recognition and protection.</p>","w":["The glycocalyx isn't a mitochondrial membrane and doesn't make ATP.","The glycocalyx isn't a nuclear layer.","The glycocalyx isn't a lipid insulation layer.","The cytoskeleton lies inside the cell, under the membrane.","Correct — sugar coat for attachment, recognition and protection."]},"fa88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Endothelial glycocalyx limits fluid and protein leak (normal circulatory fluid dynamics); enterocyte glycocalyx contains brush-border enzymes aiding digestion and absorption.</p><p>Shedding of the endothelial glycocalyx (sepsis, hyperglycemia) increases permeability and edema. The enterocyte glycocalyx holds disaccharidases and peptidases.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Enterocytes: make insulin</b> — Insulin is produced by pancreatic β-cells, not enterocytes.</li><li><b>Endothelium: makes surfactant</b> — Surfactant is produced by type II pneumocytes.</li><li><b>Endothelium: absorbs nutrients; gut: controls blood flow</b> — This swaps the roles of endothelium and gut.</li><li><b>It has no special role in either</b> — The glycocalyx does have specialized roles in both tissues.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sepsis sheds endothelial glycocalyx → capillary leak.</p>","w":["Insulin is produced by pancreatic β-cells, not enterocytes.","Correct — endothelium limits fluid loss; gut aids absorption.","Surfactant is produced by type II pneumocytes.","This swaps the roles of endothelium and gut.","The glycocalyx does have specialized roles in both tissues."]},"fa89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lipid rafts are cholesterol- and glycosphingolipid-rich, more saturated membrane regions that decrease local fluidity and cluster signaling proteins.</p><p>Rafts act as ordered platforms for receptors and signaling molecules, GPI-anchored proteins and viral entry/budding. Caveolae are a raft subtype.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Carbohydrate patches on the surface</b> — Rafts are lipid domains, not carbohydrate patches.</li><li><b>Nuclear membrane domains</b> — Rafts are plasma membrane domains, not nuclear membrane domains.</li><li><b>Unsaturated-lipid regions that increase local fluidity</b> — Rafts are enriched in saturated lipids and decrease fluidity — the opposite.</li><li><b>Protein-free holes in the membrane</b> — Rafts contain proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rafts: ↑cholesterol, ↑sphingolipids, ↓fluidity.</p>","w":["Rafts are lipid domains, not carbohydrate patches.","Rafts are plasma membrane domains, not nuclear membrane domains.","Rafts are enriched in saturated lipids and decrease fluidity — the opposite.","Rafts contain proteins.","Correct — cholesterol-rich regions that decrease fluidity."]},"fa90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caveolae are flask-shaped plasma membrane invaginations — a type of lipid raft rich in cholesterol and sphingolipids — formed by caveolin.</p><p>They function in transcytosis (endothelium), signaling, mechanosensing and clathrin-independent endocytosis. Clathrin-coated pits are a different structure for receptor-mediated endocytosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Microvilli; actin</b> — Microvilli are actin-supported surface projections.</li><li><b>Nuclear pores; nucleoporins</b> — Nuclear pores are formed by nucleoporins.</li><li><b>Desmosomes; cadherins</b> — Desmosomes are cell junctions built with cadherins.</li><li><b>Clathrin-coated pits; formed by clathrin</b> — Clathrin-coated pits are a different endocytic structure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caveolin → caveolae; clathrin → coated pits.</p>","w":["Microvilli are actin-supported surface projections.","Nuclear pores are formed by nucleoporins.","Desmosomes are cell junctions built with cadherins.","Clathrin-coated pits are a different endocytic structure.","Correct — flask-shaped lipid-raft invaginations formed by caveolin."]},"fa91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caveolae are abundant in endothelial cells (also adipocytes, smooth muscle) and absent in neurons; they function in signaling, endocytosis and membrane dynamics.</p><p>In endothelium they carry albumin and other molecules across the vessel wall (transcytosis) and regulate eNOS (via caveolin-1).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Found only in red blood cells</b> — Caveolae aren't specific to red blood cells.</li><li><b>Found in the nucleus</b> — Caveolae are in the plasma membrane, not the nucleus.</li><li><b>Found in neurons; absent from endothelial cells</b> — This reverses their distribution.</li><li><b>Absent from all cell types</b> — Caveolae are present in endothelium and other cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caveolin-1 regulates eNOS.</p>","w":["Caveolae aren't specific to red blood cells.","Caveolae are in the plasma membrane, not the nucleus.","This reverses their distribution.","Caveolae are present in endothelium and other cells.","Correct — found in endothelium; absent in neurons."]},"fa92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The cytoskeleton is a network of protein filaments: microfilaments (actin, ~7 nm) &lt; intermediate filaments (~8–10 nm) &lt; microtubules (~25 nm).</p><p>Functions: cell shape, movement of the cell and organelles, intracellular transport, division and anchoring to junctions. Actin and microtubules are dynamic (assemble/disassemble); intermediate filaments are more stable.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lipid mesh; microtubules &lt; IF &lt; microfilaments in size</b> — This reverses the size order, and the cytoskeleton isn't a lipid mesh.</li><li><b>Sugar network; all the same size</b> — The cytoskeleton is protein, with different filament sizes.</li><li><b>DNA scaffold; chromatin &lt; lamins</b> — The cytoskeleton isn't a DNA scaffold.</li><li><b>Only actin; one size</b> — The cytoskeleton has three filament types, not only actin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 7 nm &lt; 10 nm &lt; 25 nm.</p>","w":["This reverses the size order, and the cytoskeleton isn't a lipid mesh.","The cytoskeleton is protein, with different filament sizes.","Correct — protein filaments: microfilaments < IF < microtubules.","The cytoskeleton isn't a DNA scaffold.","The cytoskeleton has three filament types, not only actin."]},"fa93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Microfilaments are built from ATP-bound G-actin, which polymerizes into double-helical F-actin, growing mainly at the plus end.</p><p>Microfilaments are concentrated under the plasma membrane (cell cortex) and form microvilli cores, stress fibers, lamellipodia and the contractile ring. Drugs: phalloidin stabilizes, cytochalasin blocks polymerization.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Spectrin; tetramers</b> — Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not the microfilament subunit.</li><li><b>Tubulin dimers; 13 parallel protofilaments</b> — Tubulin dimers in 13 protofilaments form microtubules.</li><li><b>Lamin; a meshwork</b> — Lamins form a nuclear intermediate filament meshwork.</li><li><b>Keratin; rope-like coils</b> — Keratin forms rope-like intermediate filaments.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytochalasin blocks actin polymerization.</p>","w":["Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not the microfilament subunit.","Tubulin dimers in 13 protofilaments form microtubules.","Lamins form a nuclear intermediate filament meshwork.","Keratin forms rope-like intermediate filaments.","Correct — ATP-bound G-actin; a double helix."]},"fa94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Actin microfilaments use ATP for polymerization and show treadmilling (subunits added at + end, lost from − end).</p><p>Microtubules use GTP and show dynamic instability; intermediate filaments don't need nucleotides to assemble.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin isn't a filament type that treadmills.</li><li><b>Lamin</b> — Lamins are intermediate filaments and don't need ATP to polymerize.</li><li><b>Microtubule</b> — Microtubules use GTP, not ATP.</li><li><b>Intermediate filament</b> — Intermediate filaments don't use ATP to polymerize.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Actin = ATP + treadmilling.</p>","w":["Spectrin isn't a filament type that treadmills.","Lamins are intermediate filaments and don't need ATP to polymerize.","Correct — actin microfilament.","Microtubules use GTP, not ATP.","Intermediate filaments don't use ATP to polymerize."]},"fa95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Actin microfilament functions: anchoring membrane proteins, cell–ECM contacts, stress fibers, the cytokinesis contractile ring and folding epithelia into tubes.</p><p>Microtubules, in contrast, form the mitotic spindle, cilia/flagella and tracks for vesicle transport; lamins line the nuclear envelope.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lining the nuclear membrane</b> — Lining the nuclear membrane is the role of lamins.</li><li><b>Serving as railways for vesicle transport</b> — Microtubules serve as railways for vesicle transport.</li><li><b>Forming cilia</b> — Cilia are built from microtubules.</li><li><b>Building the mitotic spindle</b> — The mitotic spindle is built from microtubules.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytokinesis = actin; spindle = microtubules.</p>","w":["Lining the nuclear membrane is the role of lamins.","Microtubules serve as railways for vesicle transport.","Correct — forming the cytokinesis contractile ring.","Cilia are built from microtubules.","The mitotic spindle is built from microtubules."]},"fa96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In RBCs, spectrin (α/β) links short actin filaments into a flexible network, tethered to the membrane by ankyrin (to band 3) and band 4.1 (to glycophorin).</p><p>This skeleton gives RBCs the deformability to squeeze through capillaries and splenic sinuses. Defects cause hereditary spherocytosis or elliptocytosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Spectrin is a microtubule motor</b> — Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not a microtubule motor.</li><li><b>Spectrin binds keratin</b> — Spectrin binds actin, not keratin.</li><li><b>Spectrin is an intermediate filament anchored by keratin</b> — Spectrin isn't an intermediate filament.</li><li><b>Spectrin is found only in neurons</b> — Spectrin is crucial in RBCs, not only neurons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ankyrin = most commonly mutated in hereditary spherocytosis.</p>","w":["Correct — spectrin–actin network tethered by ankyrin.","Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not a microtubule motor.","Spectrin binds actin, not keratin.","Spectrin isn't an intermediate filament.","Spectrin is crucial in RBCs, not only neurons."]},"fa97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Red blood cells rely on an extensive spectrin cytoskeleton to withstand the mechanical stress of circulation.</p><p>RBCs travel ~250 km of vessels in their 120-day life, deforming repeatedly through capillaries narrower than their diameter. Without nuclei or organelles, they depend on the membrane skeleton for shape and resilience.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osteocytes</b> — Osteocytes aren't the deck's answer.</li><li><b>Hepatocytes</b> — Hepatocytes aren't the deck's answer.</li><li><b>Adipocytes</b> — Adipocytes aren't the deck's answer.</li><li><b>Neurons, to support their very long axons</b> — Neurons rely mainly on neurofilaments and microtubules, not the deck's answer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RBC lifespan ~120 days.</p>","w":["Osteocytes aren't the deck's answer.","Hepatocytes aren't the deck's answer.","Adipocytes aren't the deck's answer.","Correct — red blood cells, from circulatory stress.","Neurons rely mainly on neurofilaments and microtubules, not the deck's answer."]},"fa98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hereditary spherocytosis: autosomal dominant defects in RBC membrane skeleton proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3) → spherocytes → splenic destruction → hemolytic anemia.</p><p>Findings: spherocytes without central pallor, ↑MCHC, reticulocytosis, splenomegaly, jaundice, pigment gallstones, negative Coombs test, increased osmotic fragility. Splenectomy improves anemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>G6PD deficiency</b> — G6PD deficiency is an enzyme defect causing oxidative hemolysis with Heinz bodies and bite cells.</li><li><b>Absence of dystrophin</b> — Absent dystrophin causes Duchenne muscular dystrophy.</li><li><b>Kartagener syndrome</b> — Kartagener syndrome is a ciliary dynein defect.</li><li><b>Sickle cell disease (HbS polymerization)</b> — Sickle cell disease is a hemoglobin defect producing sickle cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spherocytosis: ↑MCHC, Coombs negative.</p>","w":["Correct — hereditary spherocytosis (spectrin defect).","G6PD deficiency is an enzyme defect causing oxidative hemolysis with Heinz bodies and bite cells.","Absent dystrophin causes Duchenne muscular dystrophy.","Kartagener syndrome is a ciliary dynein defect.","Sickle cell disease is a hemoglobin defect producing sickle cells."]},"fa99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dystrophin is an actin-binding \"shock absorber\" linking intracellular actin to the dystroglycan complex and the basal lamina (ECM) in muscle.</p><p>It stabilizes the sarcolemma during contraction. Its absence (Duchenne) causes membrane tears, Ca²⁺ influx and fiber necrosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A microtubule motor</b> — Microtubule motors are dynein and kinesin.</li><li><b>An RBC membrane protein</b> — The RBC membrane protein in question would be spectrin.</li><li><b>A nuclear lamin</b> — Lamins are nuclear intermediate filaments.</li><li><b>An intermediate filament that supports glial cells</b> — The glial intermediate filament is GFAP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dystrophin: actin → dystroglycan → laminin.</p>","w":["Microtubule motors are dynein and kinesin.","The RBC membrane protein in question would be spectrin.","Lamins are nuclear intermediate filaments.","Correct — an actin-binding shock absorber linking actin to the ECM.","The glial intermediate filament is GFAP."]},"fa100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Duchenne: no dystrophin (frameshift) — early, rapid course. Becker: partially functional dystrophin (in-frame) — later onset, slower course.</p><p>Both are X-linked recessive with high CK, calf pseudohypertrophy and risk of dilated cardiomyopathy. Duchenne presents at ~2–5 years with Gowers sign; Becker in adolescence or adulthood.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>DMD: partial dystrophin; BMD: no dystrophin at all</b> — This reverses the two diseases.</li><li><b>BMD is a microtubule disorder</b> — Becker is a dystrophin (actin-linker) disorder, not a microtubule disorder.</li><li><b>Both have normal dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin is absent or partial, not normal.</li><li><b>DMD mainly affects RBCs</b> — DMD is a muscle disease, not an RBC disease.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Duchenne = frameshift; Becker = in-frame.</p>","w":["This reverses the two diseases.","Becker is a dystrophin (actin-linker) disorder, not a microtubule disorder.","Dystrophin is absent or partial, not normal.","Correct — DMD has no dystrophin; BMD has partial dystrophin.","DMD is a muscle disease, not an RBC disease."]},"fb1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones (or compounds that hydrolyze to them), general formula (CH₂O)n.</p><p>Every monosaccharide has several –OH groups and one carbonyl group (aldehyde → aldose, ketone → ketose). Carbohydrates serve as the main energy source (glucose), energy stores (glycogen, starch), structural material (cellulose, GAGs), parts of nucleotides (ribose, deoxyribose), recognition molecules (glycoproteins, glycolipids, ABO antigens) and drugs (heparin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A steroid ring with a hydroxyl group at C3</b> — A steroid ring with a C3 hydroxyl describes cholesterol.</li><li><b>A long-chain fatty acid esterified to glycerol</b> — A fatty acid esterified to glycerol describes a lipid (e.g., triglyceride).</li><li><b>A polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds</b> — A polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds is a protein.</li><li><b>A nitrogenous base attached to a pentose and phosphate</b> — A nitrogenous base + pentose + phosphate is a nucleotide.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Aldose = –CHO; ketose = C=O.</p>","w":["A steroid ring with a C3 hydroxyl describes cholesterol.","A fatty acid esterified to glycerol describes a lipid (e.g., triglyceride).","A polymer of amino acids joined by peptide bonds is a protein.","Correct — carbohydrates are polyhydroxy aldehydes or ketones.","A nitrogenous base + pentose + phosphate is a nucleotide."]},"fb2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> By number of units on hydrolysis: disaccharide = 2, oligosaccharide = 3–10, polysaccharide = &gt;10.</p><p>Monosaccharides (glucose, fructose, galactose) can't be hydrolyzed further. Disaccharides: maltose (glucose–glucose), lactose (galactose–glucose), sucrose (glucose–fructose). Oligosaccharides: maltotriose, raffinose, and the oligosaccharide chains on glycoproteins. Polysaccharides: starch, glycogen, cellulose (homopolysaccharides) and GAGs (heteropolysaccharides).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Monosaccharide, disaccharide, oligosaccharide</b> — Monosaccharides can't be hydrolyzed into smaller sugars.</li><li><b>Disaccharide, polysaccharide, oligosaccharide</b> — Polysaccharides have more than 10 units; 3–10 is an oligosaccharide.</li><li><b>Disaccharide, trisaccharide, heteropolysaccharide</b> — A trisaccharide is just one kind of oligosaccharide, and &gt;10 units isn't necessarily a heteropolysaccharide.</li><li><b>Oligosaccharide, disaccharide, polysaccharide</b> — Oligosaccharides have 3–10 units, not 2.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Di- (2), oligo- (3–10), poly- (&gt;10).</p>","w":["Correct — disaccharide, oligosaccharide, polysaccharide.","Monosaccharides can't be hydrolyzed into smaller sugars.","Polysaccharides have more than 10 units; 3–10 is an oligosaccharide.","A trisaccharide is just one kind of oligosaccharide, and >10 units isn't necessarily a heteropolysaccharide.","Oligosaccharides have 3–10 units, not 2."]},"fb3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glucose = aldohexose (aldehyde at C1); fructose = ketohexose (keto at C2); same formula (C₆H₁₂O₆) = structural isomers.</p><p>Name a sugar by functional group + carbon number: aldo/keto + triose (3), tetrose (4), pentose (5), hexose (6), heptose (7). Aldohexoses: glucose, galactose, mannose. Ketohexose: fructose. Glucose and fructose are isomers but not epimers (epimers differ at a single –OH on otherwise identical structures) or enantiomers (mirror images).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both are aldopentoses</b> — They have 6 carbons, so they're hexoses, not pentoses.</li><li><b>Glucose is a ketohexose; fructose is an aldohexose</b> — This reverses them.</li><li><b>They are C4 epimers</b> — Epimers differ at one –OH; glucose and fructose differ in functional group.</li><li><b>They are enantiomers</b> — Enantiomers are mirror images, like D- and L-glucose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucose (aldose) ⇌ fructose (ketose) — isomers.</p>","w":["They have 6 carbons, so they're hexoses, not pentoses.","This reverses them.","Epimers differ at one –OH; glucose and fructose differ in functional group.","Enantiomers are mirror images, like D- and L-glucose.","Correct — glucose is an aldohexose; fructose is a ketohexose."]},"fb4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Trioses: glyceraldehyde (aldotriose) and dihydroxyacetone (ketotriose).</p><p>Each size class has aldose/ketose pairs: trioses (glyceraldehyde/DHA), tetroses (erythrose/erythrulose), pentoses (ribose, xylose / ribulose, xylulose), hexoses (glucose, galactose, mannose / fructose), heptose (sedoheptulose). Phosphorylated trioses (glyceraldehyde-3-P and DHAP) are key glycolytic intermediates interconverted by triose phosphate isomerase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sedoheptulose and xylulose</b> — Sedoheptulose is a heptose and xylulose a pentose.</li><li><b>Glucose and fructose</b> — Glucose and fructose are hexoses.</li><li><b>Erythrose and erythrulose</b> — Erythrose and erythrulose are tetroses.</li><li><b>Ribose and ribulose</b> — Ribose and ribulose are pentoses.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glyceraldehyde is the reference for D/L configuration.</p>","w":["Sedoheptulose is a heptose and xylulose a pentose.","Glucose and fructose are hexoses.","Correct — glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone.","Erythrose and erythrulose are tetroses.","Ribose and ribulose are pentoses."]},"fb5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epimers differ in the configuration of –OH at only one carbon; glucose and galactose are C4 epimers (glucose and mannose are C2 epimers).</p><p>UDP-galactose 4-epimerase interconverts UDP-galactose and UDP-glucose — a key step in galactose metabolism (deficiency causes a rare form of galactosemia). Distinguish: enantiomers (D vs L — complete mirror images), anomers (α vs β — differ at the anomeric carbon after ring formation), epimers (differ at one non-anomeric carbon).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>C2 epimers</b> — Glucose and mannose are the C2 epimers.</li><li><b>Enantiomers</b> — Enantiomers are complete mirror images (D- vs L-glucose).</li><li><b>Anomers</b> — Anomers differ at the anomeric carbon (C1 of glucose).</li><li><b>Aldose–ketose isomers</b> — Both are aldoses, so they aren't aldose–ketose isomers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucose–galactose = C4 epimers; glucose–mannose = C2 epimers.</p>","w":["Glucose and mannose are the C2 epimers.","Correct — C4 epimers.","Enantiomers are complete mirror images (D- vs L-glucose).","Anomers differ at the anomeric carbon (C1 of glucose).","Both are aldoses, so they aren't aldose–ketose isomers."]},"fb6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ring formation makes C1 of glucose a new chiral (anomeric) carbon, giving α and β anomers — not mirror images.</p><p>In α-D-glucose, the anomeric –OH is on the opposite side to C6 (down in Haworth); in β it's on the same side (up). In solution they interconvert through the open chain (mutarotation), with β predominating (~64%). Enzymes are anomer-specific: amylase digests α-1,4 bonds (starch, glycogen) but not β-1,4 bonds (cellulose), and lactase digests the β-1,4 bond of lactose.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>D and L isomers; no</b> — D/L describes the configuration at the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl, not the anomeric carbon.</li><li><b>Aldose and ketose; yes</b> — Ring closure doesn't convert an aldose into a ketose.</li><li><b>Enantiomers; yes</b> — Enantiomers are whole-molecule mirror images.</li><li><b>Epimers at C4; yes</b> — C4 epimers are glucose versus galactose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mutarotation = interconversion of α and β anomers.</p>","w":["D/L describes the configuration at the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl, not the anomeric carbon.","Ring closure doesn't convert an aldose into a ketose.","Enantiomers are whole-molecule mirror images.","Correct — α and β anomers, not mirror images.","C4 epimers are glucose versus galactose."]},"fb7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> 2-Deoxyribose (DNA) lacks the 2′-OH present in ribose (RNA) — a deoxy sugar.</p><p>The missing 2′-OH makes DNA more chemically stable (RNA's 2′-OH can attack the phosphodiester backbone, especially in alkali). Ribonucleotide reductase makes deoxyribonucleotides from ribonucleotides; hydroxyurea inhibits it. Other sugar derivatives: sugar acids (glucuronic acid for conjugation), sugar alcohols (sorbitol — accumulates in diabetic lens/nerves), amino sugars (glucosamine), and phosphate esters (glucose-6-phosphate).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>C5; sugar acid</b> — Sugar acids carry a –COOH group (e.g., glucuronic acid).</li><li><b>C4; polyalcohol</b> — Polyalcohols (e.g., sorbitol) are reduction products of sugars.</li><li><b>C1; glycoside</b> — A glycoside forms when the anomeric –OH bonds to another group.</li><li><b>C3; amino sugar</b> — Amino sugars carry an –NH₂ group (e.g., glucosamine).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ribonucleotide reductase makes dNTPs; hydroxyurea inhibits it.</p>","w":["Correct — C2, a deoxy sugar.","Sugar acids carry a –COOH group (e.g., glucuronic acid).","Polyalcohols (e.g., sorbitol) are reduction products of sugars.","A glycoside forms when the anomeric –OH bonds to another group.","Amino sugars carry an –NH₂ group (e.g., glucosamine)."]},"fb8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glucose is a reducing sugar (free anomeric carbon/aldehyde), so it reduces Cu²⁺ to red Cu₂O in Benedict's test — used to detect glycosuria.</p><p>When blood glucose exceeds the renal threshold (~180 mg/dL), the SGLT transporters saturate and glucose appears in urine. Benedict's test detects any reducing sugar (glucose, galactose, fructose, lactose), which is why it was used to screen for galactosemia. Modern dipsticks use glucose oxidase, which is specific for glucose — a positive Benedict's test with a negative dipstick suggests a non-glucose reducing sugar (e.g., galactose, fructose).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Isomerisation</b> — Isomerization (e.g., glucose ⇌ fructose) isn't the basis of urine sugar testing.</li><li><b>Carboxylation</b> — Carboxylation adds CO₂ and isn't used to identify sugars.</li><li><b>Oxidation</b> — The sugar itself is oxidized, but the property used is its ability to reduce Cu²⁺ — it's a reducing sugar test.</li><li><b>Hydroxylation</b> — Hydroxylation adds –OH groups and isn't a sugar-detection reaction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Benedict's +, glucose oxidase − → non-glucose reducing sugar (galactosemia, fructosuria).</p>","w":["Correct — the reducing property identifies urinary sugar.","Isomerization (e.g., glucose ⇌ fructose) isn't the basis of urine sugar testing.","Carboxylation adds CO₂ and isn't used to identify sugars.","The sugar itself is oxidized, but the property used is its ability to reduce Cu²⁺ — it's a reducing sugar test.","Hydroxylation adds –OH groups and isn't a sugar-detection reaction."]},"fb9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycosides link the anomeric –OH of a sugar to another group (aglycone) through O (O-glycoside) or N (N-glycoside) bonds.</p><p>Examples: cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitoxin — inhibit Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), streptomycin, ouabain, and nucleosides (N-glycosidic bond between ribose C1′ and a base, as in ATP). Glycosidic bonds also join sugars in disaccharides and polysaccharides. Once the anomeric carbon is in a glycosidic bond, it can't open to the aldehyde, so that sugar unit becomes non-reducing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are formed between two amino acids</b> — Amino acids link by peptide bonds, not glycosidic bonds.</li><li><b>They form by oxidation of the sugar's C6 hydroxyl group</b> — Oxidation of C6 gives uronic acids, not glycosides.</li><li><b>They can never contain nitrogen (N-glycosidic) linkages</b> — The adenine–ribose bond in ATP is an N-glycosidic bond.</li><li><b>They are always linked to the other group through sulfur</b> — O- and N-glycosides are the types described, not sulfur links.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nucleosides = base + sugar via N-glycosidic bond.</p>","w":["Amino acids link by peptide bonds, not glycosidic bonds.","Oxidation of C6 gives uronic acids, not glycosides.","The adenine–ribose bond in ATP is an N-glycosidic bond.","O- and N-glycosides are the types described, not sulfur links.","Correct — glycosides link the anomeric –OH to another group."]},"fb10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sucrose is non-reducing because its α1→β2 bond ties up both anomeric carbons (C1 of glucose and C2 of fructose).</p><p>A disaccharide is reducing if at least one anomeric carbon is free. Maltose (glucose α1→4 glucose) and lactose (galactose β1→4 glucose) each have a free anomeric carbon on their glucose, so they're reducing. Sucrose and trehalose (α1→1) have both anomeric carbons engaged — non-reducing. Sucrase–isomaltase in the brush border hydrolyzes sucrose; its deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea with sucrose ingestion.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Maltose, because it has an α(1→4) bond</b> — Maltose is reducing; one anomeric carbon remains free.</li><li><b>Lactose, because it has a β(1→4) bond</b> — Lactose is reducing despite its β(1→4) bond.</li><li><b>Lactose, because it contains galactose</b> — Lactose is reducing because its glucose's anomeric carbon is free.</li><li><b>Maltose, because it contains two glucose units</b> — Maltose is reducing.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Non-reducing disaccharides: sucrose, trehalose.</p>","w":["Maltose is reducing; one anomeric carbon remains free.","Lactose is reducing despite its β(1→4) bond.","Correct — both anomeric carbons are locked in sucrose's bond.","Lactose is reducing because its glucose's anomeric carbon is free.","Maltose is reducing."]},"fb11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sucrose = α-D-glucose (C1) linked to β-D-fructose (C2): α1β2 — both anomeric carbons named because the sugars are in different anomeric forms.</p><p>Disaccharide bonds to know: maltose — glucose α(1→4) glucose; isomaltose — glucose α(1→6) glucose; lactose — galactose β(1→4) glucose; sucrose — glucose α1→β2 fructose; trehalose — glucose α(1→1) glucose. Brush-border enzymes: maltase, isomaltase, lactase, sucrase, trehalase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>β(1→4), both sugars in the β form</b> — β(1→4) is the bond in lactose (and cellulose).</li><li><b>α(1→1), both glucose units</b> — α(1→1) is trehalose.</li><li><b>α(1→6), the branch-point bond</b> — α(1→6) is the branch bond in glycogen and amylopectin.</li><li><b>α(1→4), both sugars in the same anomeric form</b> — α(1→4) is the bond in maltose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sucrose: glucose + fructose (α1→β2), non-reducing.</p>","w":["β(1→4) is the bond in lactose (and cellulose).","Correct — α1β2.","α(1→1) is trehalose.","α(1→6) is the branch bond in glycogen and amylopectin.","α(1→4) is the bond in maltose."]},"fb12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Benedict's test: reducing sugars reduce Cu²⁺ (blue) to Cu₂O (red precipitate) in hot alkaline solution.</p><p>Color ranges from green (trace) through yellow and orange to brick red (high concentration), allowing semi-quantitative estimation. The sugar's aldehyde is oxidized while copper is reduced. Benedict's test detects any reducing sugar, not only glucose; ketone bodies are detected by nitroprusside tests (acetoacetate, acetone).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ketone bodies reacting with copper</b> — Ketone bodies are detected with nitroprusside, not Benedict's reagent.</li><li><b>Cupric hydroxide formed by oxidation of copper</b> — Copper is reduced, not oxidized, and the product is cuprous oxide.</li><li><b>Glucose crystals</b> — The precipitate is a copper oxide, not sugar crystals.</li><li><b>Copper ions bound to urinary protein</b> — Benedict's test doesn't detect urinary protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Benedict's = reducing sugars; nitroprusside = ketones.</p>","w":["Ketone bodies are detected with nitroprusside, not Benedict's reagent.","Copper is reduced, not oxidized, and the product is cuprous oxide.","The precipitate is a copper oxide, not sugar crystals.","Correct — red Cu₂O forms when the sugar reduces Cu²⁺.","Benedict's test doesn't detect urinary protein."]},"fb13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycogen: α(1→4) bonds in chains and α(1→6) bonds at branch points (every 8–10 residues).</p><p>Glycogen synthase makes α(1→4) bonds; branching enzyme creates α(1→6) branches. During breakdown, glycogen phosphorylase cleaves α(1→4) bonds, and debranching enzyme handles α(1→6) branches. High branching gives many non-reducing ends for rapid release of glucose and increases solubility.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>α-1,4</b> — α-1,4 bonds link glucose residues within the straight chains, not at branch points.</li><li><b>β-1,4</b> — β-1,4 linkages are found in cellulose and lactose.</li><li><b>α-1,1</b> — α-1,1 links the two glucoses of trehalose.</li><li><b>α-1→β2</b> — α1→β2 is the linkage in sucrose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Branching enzyme (Andersen) and debranching enzyme (Cori) handle α-1,6 bonds.</p>","w":["α-1,4 bonds link glucose residues within the straight chains, not at branch points.","Correct — branch points are α-1,6.","β-1,4 linkages are found in cellulose and lactose.","α-1,1 links the two glucoses of trehalose.","α1→β2 is the linkage in sucrose."]},"fb14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amylose: unbranched, helical α(1→4)-linked glucose; blue with iodine; water-soluble. Amylopectin: branched (α1→6 every 24–30), insoluble.</p><p>Iodine slips into the amylose helix, producing the deep blue color (amylopectin gives red-violet; glycogen red-brown). Starch is ~20–30% amylose and 70–80% amylopectin. Salivary and pancreatic α-amylase hydrolyze internal α(1→4) bonds; brush-border enzymes finish digestion to glucose.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inulin</b> — Inulin is a fructose polymer used to measure GFR.</li><li><b>Cellulose</b> — Cellulose has β(1→4) linkages and isn't digested by humans.</li><li><b>Glycogen</b> — Glycogen is the highly branched animal storage polysaccharide.</li><li><b>Amylopectin</b> — Amylopectin is branched and insoluble.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Iodine: amylose blue; amylopectin violet; glycogen red-brown.</p>","w":["Correct — amylose.","Inulin is a fructose polymer used to measure GFR.","Cellulose has β(1→4) linkages and isn't digested by humans.","Glycogen is the highly branched animal storage polysaccharide.","Amylopectin is branched and insoluble."]},"fb15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycogen and amylopectin share α(1→4) chains and α(1→6) branches, but glycogen branches more often (every 8–10 vs 24–30 residues).</p><p>Glycogen (\"animal starch\") is stored in liver (~10% wet weight) and muscle (~1–2%, but larger total mass). Its extensive branching creates many ends for rapid synthesis and breakdown. Amylopectin is the plant storage form within starch granules.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen is water-soluble and stains blue with iodine</b> — Being water-soluble and blue with iodine describes amylose.</li><li><b>Glycogen is found in plants; amylopectin in animals</b> — This is reversed — glycogen is in animals, amylopectin in plants.</li><li><b>Glycogen has β(1→4) bonds; amylopectin has α(1→4) bonds</b> — Both use α linkages; β(1→4) describes cellulose.</li><li><b>Glycogen is unbranched, while amylopectin is branched</b> — Both are branched.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> More branching = faster mobilization.</p>","w":["Being water-soluble and blue with iodine describes amylose.","This is reversed — glycogen is in animals, amylopectin in plants.","Both use α linkages; β(1→4) describes cellulose.","Both are branched.","Correct — same bonds, but glycogen branches more often."]},"fb16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose (has glucose-6-phosphatase); muscle glycogen fuels only the muscle itself.</p><p>During fasting, liver glycogenolysis releases free glucose into the blood for ~12–24 hours, after which gluconeogenesis dominates. Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so its G6P enters glycolysis for local ATP production during exercise. This explains different disease presentations: liver GSDs cause hypoglycemia; muscle GSDs cause exercise intolerance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Brain maintains blood glucose; heart uses its own glycogen</b> — The brain doesn't store significant glycogen for maintaining blood glucose.</li><li><b>Red cells maintain blood glucose; muscle uses its own</b> — Red cells don't store glycogen.</li><li><b>Skeletal muscle maintains blood glucose; liver uses glycogen for itself</b> — This reverses the roles of liver and muscle.</li><li><b>Adipose tissue maintains blood glucose; kidney uses its own</b> — Adipose stores fat, not significant glycogen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Liver GSD → hypoglycemia; muscle GSD → cramps, myoglobinuria.</p>","w":["The brain doesn't store significant glycogen for maintaining blood glucose.","Red cells don't store glycogen.","Correct — liver maintains blood glucose; muscle uses its own glycogen.","This reverses the roles of liver and muscle.","Adipose stores fat, not significant glycogen."]},"fb17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heteropolysaccharides contain more than one kind of sugar unit (e.g., GAGs like chondroitin sulfate); homopolysaccharides contain only one (starch, glycogen, cellulose, chitin, inulin).</p><p>Chondroitin sulfate is built from repeating N-acetylgalactosamine (sulfated) + glucuronic acid units. Other heteropolysaccharides: hyaluronic acid, heparin, heparan sulfate, dermatan sulfate, keratan sulfate. Homopolysaccharides are named by their sugar: glucans (glucose: starch, glycogen, cellulose), fructans (inulin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen</b> — Glycogen contains only glucose.</li><li><b>Chitin</b> — Chitin contains a single repeating unit (N-acetylglucosamine).</li><li><b>Cellulose</b> — Cellulose contains only glucose.</li><li><b>Starch</b> — Starch contains only glucose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GAGs = heteropolysaccharides.</p>","w":["Glycogen contains only glucose.","Correct — chondroitin sulfate is a heteropolysaccharide.","Chitin contains a single repeating unit (N-acetylglucosamine).","Cellulose contains only glucose.","Starch contains only glucose."]},"fb18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GAGs = long unbranched chains of repeating (amino sugar + uronic acid) disaccharides, highly negatively charged (carboxyl and sulfate groups).</p><p>Amino sugars: glucosamine or galactosamine (often N-acetylated and sulfated). Uronic acids: glucuronic or iduronic acid (keratan sulfate uses galactose instead). The many negative charges attract Na⁺ and water, making GAGs bulky hydrated gels. Most GAGs attach to core proteins to form proteoglycans (except hyaluronic acid).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glucose–glucose units that carry no charge at all</b> — GAGs are heteropolysaccharides with many negative charges, not uncharged glucose chains.</li><li><b>Amino acid–amino acid units forming α-helices</b> — Amino acid chains forming α-helices describe proteins.</li><li><b>Fatty acid–glycerol units joined by ester bonds</b> — Fatty acid–glycerol units joined by ester bonds describe lipids.</li><li><b>Fructose–glucose units with positive charges</b> — GAGs are negatively, not positively, charged.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GAG = (amino sugar–uronic acid)n, polyanionic.</p>","w":["GAGs are heteropolysaccharides with many negative charges, not uncharged glucose chains.","Amino acid chains forming α-helices describe proteins.","Fatty acid–glycerol units joined by ester bonds describe lipids.","Correct — amino sugar–uronic acid units with many negative charges.","GAGs are negatively, not positively, charged."]},"fb19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GAGs are hydrated polyanions: they bind water and repel each other, so they compress and spring back and slip past each other.</p><p>In cartilage, aggrecan's GAG chains hold water; compression squeezes water out, and release lets it return — shock absorption. Hyaluronic acid makes synovial fluid viscous and lubricating. In mucus, GAG-rich glycoproteins create slipperiness. Loss of proteoglycans (osteoarthritis) reduces cartilage resilience.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are hydrophobic and exclude water from tissues</b> — GAGs are highly hydrophilic and attract rather than exclude water.</li><li><b>They are rigid crystalline polymers</b> — GAGs form hydrated gels, not rigid crystals.</li><li><b>They form covalent cross-links like collagen</b> — GAGs aren't covalently cross-linked like collagen.</li><li><b>They are positively charged and attract one another</b> — GAGs are negatively charged and repel one another.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hydration + repulsion → shock absorption and lubrication.</p>","w":["Correct — hydrated polyanions repel and slip past each other.","GAGs are highly hydrophilic and attract rather than exclude water.","GAGs form hydrated gels, not rigid crystals.","GAGs aren't covalently cross-linked like collagen.","GAGs are negatively charged and repel one another."]},"fb20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heparin is the only intracellular GAG (mast cell granules), acts as an anticoagulant (activates antithrombin) and releases lipoprotein lipase.</p><p>Heparin's sulfated pentasaccharide binds antithrombin, accelerating inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa. Its release of lipoprotein lipase from endothelium clears triglyceride-rich lipoproteins (the \"clearing factor\"). GAG locations: hyaluronic acid (synovial fluid, vitreous, umbilical cord), chondroitin sulfate (most abundant — cartilage, bone), keratan sulfate (cornea, cartilage), dermatan sulfate (skin, vessels, heart valves), heparan sulfate (basement membranes, cell surfaces).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dermatan sulfate</b> — Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, blood vessels and heart valves.</li><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid lubricates joints and isn't the anticoagulant.</li><li><b>Chondroitin sulfate</b> — Chondroitin sulfate is the most abundant GAG, in cartilage.</li><li><b>Keratan sulfate</b> — Keratan sulfate is found in cornea, cartilage and intervertebral discs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heparin: intracellular, anticoagulant, releases lipoprotein lipase.</p>","w":["Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, blood vessels and heart valves.","Hyaluronic acid lubricates joints and isn't the anticoagulant.","Chondroitin sulfate is the most abundant GAG, in cartilage.","Keratan sulfate is found in cornea, cartilage and intervertebral discs.","Correct — heparin."]},"fb21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chondroitin sulfate is the most abundant GAG and gives cartilage its compressibility.</p><p>In articular cartilage, aggrecan core proteins carry ~100 chondroitin sulfate chains (plus keratan sulfate) and bind hyaluronic acid. The sulfate and carboxyl groups trap water, so cartilage resists compression and rebounds. Chondroitin sulfate is also in bone, cornea, skin and the aorta. (It's marketed as a joint supplement, though evidence of benefit is limited.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dermatan sulfate</b> — Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, vessels and heart valves.</li><li><b>Heparin</b> — Heparin is an intracellular anticoagulant GAG.</li><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid is in synovial fluid and connective tissue and acts mainly as a lubricant.</li><li><b>Heparan sulfate</b> — Heparan sulfate is found in basement membranes and arteries.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Most abundant GAG = chondroitin sulfate.</p>","w":["Dermatan sulfate is found in skin, vessels and heart valves.","Heparin is an intracellular anticoagulant GAG.","Correct — chondroitin sulfate.","Hyaluronic acid is in synovial fluid and connective tissue and acts mainly as a lubricant.","Heparan sulfate is found in basement membranes and arteries."]},"fb22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heparan sulfate is found in basement membranes (including the glomerular basement membrane), lungs, arteries and on cell surfaces.</p><p>In the glomerular basement membrane, negatively charged heparan sulfate repels anionic proteins like albumin (charge barrier); its loss contributes to proteinuria in nephrotic syndrome. Cell-surface heparan sulfate proteoglycans (syndecans, glypicans) bind growth factors and serve as receptors for some viruses. Heparan sulfate accumulates in Hurler, Hunter and Sanfilippo syndromes. Heparin is related but intracellular (mast cells).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Heparin</b> — Heparin is intracellular (mast cells), not in basement membranes.</li><li><b>Chondroitin sulfate</b> — Chondroitin sulfate is in cartilage, cornea, bone and skin.</li><li><b>Hyaluronic acid</b> — Hyaluronic acid is in connective tissue and synovial fluid.</li><li><b>Keratan sulfate</b> — Keratan sulfate is in cornea, cartilage and intervertebral discs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GBM heparan sulfate = charge barrier against albumin.</p>","w":["Heparin is intracellular (mast cells), not in basement membranes.","Correct — heparan sulfate.","Chondroitin sulfate is in cartilage, cornea, bone and skin.","Hyaluronic acid is in connective tissue and synovial fluid.","Keratan sulfate is in cornea, cartilage and intervertebral discs."]},"fb23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycine is the only optically inactive standard amino acid: its R group is H, so the α-carbon isn't chiral.</p><p>A chiral carbon has four different substituents and produces D/L enantiomers that rotate polarized light. Glycine's α-carbon bears two hydrogens, so it has no mirror-image form. Glycine's small size lets it fit at every third position of collagen and in tight protein turns. It's also an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord (blocked by strychnine) and a precursor of heme, purines, creatine and glutathione.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tryptophan</b> — Tryptophan's α-carbon has four different groups and is chiral.</li><li><b>Proline</b> — Proline is an imino acid but still chiral.</li><li><b>Lysine</b> — Lysine is chiral.</li><li><b>Alanine</b> — Alanine (R = CH₃) is chiral, existing as D- and L-alanine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycine: achiral, collagen every third residue, inhibitory neurotransmitter.</p>","w":["Tryptophan's α-carbon has four different groups and is chiral.","Correct — glycine is optically inactive.","Proline is an imino acid but still chiral.","Lysine is chiral.","Alanine (R = CH₃) is chiral, existing as D- and L-alanine."]},"fb24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Serine's side-chain hydroxyl (–OH) acts as a nucleophile in enzyme active sites (serine proteases) and is a phosphorylation and O-glycosylation site.</p><p>In serine proteases (trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, thrombin, clotting factors), a catalytic triad (Ser–His–Asp) activates serine's –OH to attack peptide bonds. Organophosphates (insecticides, nerve agents) irreversibly phosphorylate the active-site serine of acetylcholinesterase, causing cholinergic crisis. Serine is also the precursor of glycine, cysteine and sphingolipids.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Aliphatic side chain</b> — Aliphatic side chains (Gly, Ala, Val, Leu, Ile) are nonpolar and unreactive.</li><li><b>Aromatic side chain</b> — Aromatic side chains belong to Phe, Tyr and Trp.</li><li><b>Basic group</b> — Basic side chains belong to Lys, Arg and His.</li><li><b>Imino group</b> — The imino group belongs to proline.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Organophosphates phosphorylate AChE's active-site serine.</p>","w":["Aliphatic side chains (Gly, Ala, Val, Leu, Ile) are nonpolar and unreactive.","Aromatic side chains belong to Phe, Tyr and Trp.","Correct — serine's hydroxy group participates in catalysis.","Basic side chains belong to Lys, Arg and His.","The imino group belongs to proline."]},"fb25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysine and arginine have basic side chains (amino and guanidino groups) that accept protons and are positively charged at pH 7.4.</p><p>Basic amino acids: lysine (pKa ~10.5), arginine (~12.5; most basic), histidine (~6.0; partly charged at physiologic pH — useful as a proton donor/acceptor in active sites and as a buffer in hemoglobin). Positive charges let lysine- and arginine-rich histones bind DNA's phosphate backbone. Trypsin cleaves after lysine and arginine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Acidic</b> — Acidic side chains (Asp, Glu) donate protons and are negatively charged.</li><li><b>Aromatic</b> — Aromatic side chains (Phe, Tyr, Trp) aren't proton acceptors at physiologic pH.</li><li><b>Hydroxyl</b> — Hydroxyl side chains (Ser, Thr, Tyr) are uncharged polar.</li><li><b>Sulfhydryl</b> — The sulfhydryl of cysteine forms disulfide bonds and isn't a basic proton acceptor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Histidine (pKa ~6) is the best physiologic buffer side chain; trypsin cuts after Lys/Arg.</p>","w":["Acidic side chains (Asp, Glu) donate protons and are negatively charged.","Aromatic side chains (Phe, Tyr, Trp) aren't proton acceptors at physiologic pH.","Hydroxyl side chains (Ser, Thr, Tyr) are uncharged polar.","Correct — basic side chains.","The sulfhydryl of cysteine forms disulfide bonds and isn't a basic proton acceptor."]},"fb26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tryptophan is aromatic (indole ring), essential, and both glucogenic and ketogenic.</p><p>Tryptophan is the precursor of serotonin (and melatonin) and niacin (vitamin B3) — about 60 mg tryptophan yields 1 mg niacin. Hartnup disease (defective neutral amino acid transporter) causes tryptophan loss and pellagra-like symptoms. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan to serotonin, also risking niacin deficiency. Amino acids that are both glucogenic and ketogenic: isoleucine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, tyrosine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tyrosine</b> — Tyrosine is aromatic and both glucogenic/ketogenic but non-essential (made from phenylalanine).</li><li><b>Threonine</b> — Threonine is essential but not aromatic.</li><li><b>Glutamate</b> — Glutamate is non-essential, acidic and purely glucogenic.</li><li><b>Lysine</b> — Lysine is essential but purely ketogenic and not aromatic.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tryptophan → serotonin, melatonin, niacin; Hartnup → pellagra-like rash.</p>","w":["Tyrosine is aromatic and both glucogenic/ketogenic but non-essential (made from phenylalanine).","Threonine is essential but not aromatic.","Glutamate is non-essential, acidic and purely glucogenic.","Correct — tryptophan.","Lysine is essential but purely ketogenic and not aromatic."]},"fb27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proline is an imino acid: its side chain bonds back to its amino nitrogen, forming a rigid ring that breaks α-helices and favors turns.</p><p>The ring restricts rotation and the nitrogen lacks the H needed for backbone hydrogen bonding, so proline disrupts helices (a \"helix breaker\"). It's common in β-turns and in collagen (Gly-Pro-Hyp repeats), where hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix. Prolyl hydroxylation needs vitamin C.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycine</b> — Glycine is the smallest amino acid (R = H) and achiral, not cyclic.</li><li><b>Histidine</b> — Histidine has an imidazole ring but a normal amino group.</li><li><b>Tryptophan</b> — Tryptophan has an indole ring but a normal amino group.</li><li><b>Alanine</b> — Alanine has a simple methyl side chain.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proline (and glycine) = helix breakers.</p>","w":["Glycine is the smallest amino acid (R = H) and achiral, not cyclic.","Histidine has an imidazole ring but a normal amino group.","Tryptophan has an indole ring but a normal amino group.","Correct — proline.","Alanine has a simple methyl side chain."]},"fb28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Human proteins contain only L-amino acids; D-amino acids occur in bacteria (e.g., D-alanine in peptidoglycan).</p><p>Ribosomes and aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases select L-amino acids. Bacteria use D-alanine–D-alanine in peptidoglycan cross-links; vancomycin binds this D-Ala-D-Ala terminus (resistance arises when it changes to D-Ala-D-Lac). D-amino acids also occur in some peptide antibiotics and as neuromodulators (D-serine at NMDA receptors).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A mixture of D and L; D in bacteria</b> — Proteins aren't a mixture of D and L amino acids.</li><li><b>All D; D-amino acids in human proteins</b> — Human proteins use L-amino acids, not D.</li><li><b>All achiral</b> — Only glycine is achiral.</li><li><b>All L; D-amino acids are found in human collagen</b> — Collagen is made of L-amino acids.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala; resistance = D-Ala-D-Lac.</p>","w":["Correct — all L; D forms in bacteria.","Proteins aren't a mixture of D and L amino acids.","Human proteins use L-amino acids, not D.","Only glycine is achiral.","Collagen is made of L-amino acids."]},"fb29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ornithine (and citrulline) are urea cycle intermediates not incorporated into proteins.</p><p>Urea cycle: ornithine + carbamoyl phosphate → citrulline (mitochondria) → argininosuccinate → arginine → urea + ornithine (cytosol). Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency (X-linked) is the most common urea cycle disorder, causing hyperammonemia and orotic aciduria. Other non-protein amino acids: DOPA, homocysteine, GABA, thyroxine. Some protein residues are modified after translation (hydroxyproline, hydroxylysine, γ-carboxyglutamate).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysine</b> — Lysine is a standard amino acid found in proteins.</li><li><b>Glutamine</b> — Glutamine is a standard amino acid.</li><li><b>Aspartate</b> — Aspartate is a standard amino acid (it also donates N to the urea cycle).</li><li><b>Arginine</b> — Arginine is a standard amino acid, although it also participates in the urea cycle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> OTC deficiency: ↑ammonia + ↑orotic acid, normal BUN low.</p>","w":["Lysine is a standard amino acid found in proteins.","Glutamine is a standard amino acid.","Aspartate is a standard amino acid (it also donates N to the urea cycle).","Arginine is a standard amino acid, although it also participates in the urea cycle.","Correct — ornithine."]},"fb30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Valine, leucine and isoleucine are branched-chain amino acids — nonpolar, aliphatic and essential.</p><p>BCAAs are metabolized mainly in muscle. Their transaminated α-keto acids are decarboxylated by branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase (needs thiamine). Deficiency causes maple syrup urine disease: sweet-smelling urine, poor feeding, vomiting, seizures and encephalopathy in neonates. Treatment: restrict BCAAs, give thiamine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They contain sulfur</b> — The sulfur amino acids are cysteine and methionine.</li><li><b>They are aromatic and absorb UV light at 280 nm</b> — The aromatic amino acids are Phe, Tyr and Trp.</li><li><b>They are imino acids</b> — Proline is the imino acid.</li><li><b>They are acidic and negatively charged at pH 7.4</b> — The acidic amino acids are Asp and Glu.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MSUD = branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency (\"I Love Vermont maple syrup\": Ile, Leu, Val).</p>","w":["The sulfur amino acids are cysteine and methionine.","The aromatic amino acids are Phe, Tyr and Trp.","Correct — branched-chain, nonpolar aliphatic amino acids.","Proline is the imino acid.","The acidic amino acids are Asp and Glu."]},"fb31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cysteine's thiol (–SH) forms disulfide bonds and is the active part of glutathione (γ-Glu-Cys-Gly).</p><p>Reduced glutathione (GSH) donates electrons to neutralize H₂O₂ (via glutathione peroxidase) and conjugates toxins (e.g., acetaminophen's NAPQI). Oxidized GSSG is regenerated by glutathione reductase using NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway (G6PD). N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione in acetaminophen overdose. Disulfide bonds stabilize extracellular proteins (insulin, antibodies). Cystinuria (defective dibasic transporter) causes cystine kidney stones.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Threonine</b> — Threonine has a hydroxyl group, not a thiol.</li><li><b>Methionine</b> — Methionine is converted to SAM (methyl donor) and doesn't form disulfides.</li><li><b>Serine</b> — Serine has a hydroxyl group, not a thiol.</li><li><b>Homocysteine</b> — Homocysteine is a methionine metabolite, not a glutathione residue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Acetaminophen overdose → N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione.</p>","w":["Threonine has a hydroxyl group, not a thiol.","Correct — cysteine.","Methionine is converted to SAM (methyl donor) and doesn't form disulfides.","Serine has a hydroxyl group, not a thiol.","Homocysteine is a methionine metabolite, not a glutathione residue."]},"fb32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> S-adenosylmethionine (SAM), formed from methionine + ATP, is the major methyl donor.</p><p>SAM methylates DNA, histones, phospholipids, neurotransmitters (norepinephrine → epinephrine via PNMT), creatine synthesis and drugs. After donating its methyl, it becomes S-adenosylhomocysteine → homocysteine. Homocysteine is either remethylated to methionine (methionine synthase, needs B12 and folate) or converted to cysteine (cystathionine β-synthase, needs B6). Deficiencies or CBS defects raise homocysteine (homocystinuria: lens dislocation, thrombosis, intellectual disability).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Taurine</b> — Taurine (from cysteine) conjugates bile acids; it isn't the main methyl donor.</li><li><b>Homocysteine</b> — Homocysteine is what remains after methyl donation.</li><li><b>Cystine</b> — Cystine is two cysteines linked by a disulfide.</li><li><b>Glutathione</b> — Glutathione is an antioxidant tripeptide.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Homocysteine → methionine (B12, folate) or → cysteine (B6).</p>","w":["Taurine (from cysteine) conjugates bile acids; it isn't the main methyl donor.","Homocysteine is what remains after methyl donation.","Cystine is two cysteines linked by a disulfide.","Correct — SAM.","Glutathione is an antioxidant tripeptide."]},"fb33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Aromatic amino acids (mainly tryptophan and tyrosine) absorb UV light at ~280 nm, used to quantify protein.</p><p>The conjugated ring systems absorb UV. Nucleic acids absorb maximally at 260 nm (bases), so the 260/280 ratio assesses DNA/RNA purity (~1.8 for pure DNA). Phenylalanine absorbs weakly at ~257 nm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Aliphatic amino acids</b> — Aliphatic side chains don't absorb at 280 nm.</li><li><b>Acidic amino acids</b> — Acidic side chains don't account for 280 nm absorbance.</li><li><b>Hydroxyl-containing amino acids (Ser, Thr)</b> — Serine and threonine don't absorb at 280 nm (tyrosine does because of its ring).</li><li><b>Sulfur-containing amino acids (Cys, Met)</b> — Sulfur amino acids absorb little at 280 nm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proteins 280 nm; nucleic acids 260 nm.</p>","w":["Correct — aromatic amino acids (Trp, Tyr).","Aliphatic side chains don't absorb at 280 nm.","Acidic side chains don't account for 280 nm absorbance.","Serine and threonine don't absorb at 280 nm (tyrosine does because of its ring).","Sulfur amino acids absorb little at 280 nm."]},"fb34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> N-linked oligosaccharides attach to the amide nitrogen of asparagine (in Asn-X-Ser/Thr sequences) in the ER.</p><p>A preformed 14-sugar oligosaccharide on dolichol phosphate is transferred en bloc by oligosaccharyltransferase to asparagine on the nascent chain. It's then trimmed (helping quality control with calnexin/calreticulin) and modified in the Golgi. O-linked sugars attach to Ser/Thr hydroxyls in the Golgi. Congenital disorders of glycosylation affect multiple organs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Serine</b> — Serine is used for O-linked glycosylation.</li><li><b>Threonine</b> — Threonine is used for O-linked glycosylation.</li><li><b>Tyrosine</b> — Tyrosine (in glycogenin) primes glycogen synthesis.</li><li><b>Glutamate</b> — Glutamate isn't the N-glycosylation site.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> N-linked = Asn, ER, dolichol; O-linked = Ser/Thr, Golgi.</p>","w":["Serine is used for O-linked glycosylation.","Threonine is used for O-linked glycosylation.","Tyrosine (in glycogenin) primes glycogen synthesis.","Glutamate isn't the N-glycosylation site.","Correct — asparagine."]},"fb35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycogenin primes glycogen synthesis by attaching glucose to its own tyrosine through an O-glycosidic bond.</p><p>Glycogenin autoglucosylates using UDP-glucose, building a short α(1→4) chain of ~8 residues on Tyr194. Glycogen synthase then extends it, and branching enzyme adds α(1→6) branches. Glycogenin remains at the core of each glycogen granule.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysine</b> — Lysine isn't used for priming glycogen.</li><li><b>Asparagine</b> — Asparagine is used for N-linked glycosylation.</li><li><b>Cysteine</b> — Cysteine forms disulfide bonds.</li><li><b>Serine</b> — Serine is used for O-linked glycosylation of other proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycogenin Tyr = glycogen primer.</p>","w":["Lysine isn't used for priming glycogen.","Asparagine is used for N-linked glycosylation.","Correct — tyrosine.","Cysteine forms disulfide bonds.","Serine is used for O-linked glycosylation of other proteins."]},"fb36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kinases phosphorylate hydroxyl-containing residues: serine, threonine and tyrosine.</p><p>Serine/threonine kinases include PKA, PKC, CaM kinases and CDKs. Tyrosine kinases include receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, EGF, PDGF receptors) and non-receptor kinases (JAK, Src, BCR-ABL). Phosphatases remove the phosphates. Many cancer drugs are tyrosine kinase inhibitors (imatinib for BCR-ABL in CML). Phosphorylation switches enzyme activity (e.g., glycogen phosphorylase on, glycogen synthase off).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysine and arginine</b> — Lysine and arginine have basic side chains, not hydroxyls.</li><li><b>Aspartate and glutamate</b> — Aspartate and glutamate have carboxyl side chains.</li><li><b>Leucine and isoleucine</b> — Leucine and isoleucine are nonpolar.</li><li><b>Methionine and cysteine</b> — Methionine and cysteine contain sulfur.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Imatinib inhibits BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase.</p>","w":["Lysine and arginine have basic side chains, not hydroxyls.","Correct — serine, threonine and tyrosine.","Aspartate and glutamate have carboxyl side chains.","Leucine and isoleucine are nonpolar.","Methionine and cysteine contain sulfur."]},"fb37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Histones are rich in positively charged lysine and arginine, which bind DNA's negatively charged phosphate backbone.</p><p>DNA wraps ~1.7 times around a histone octamer (H2A, H2B, H3, H4 ×2) to form a nucleosome; H1 links adjacent nucleosomes. Histone tail modifications regulate gene expression: acetylation of lysines neutralizes their positive charge, loosening chromatin and activating transcription; deacetylation tightens it (histone deacetylase inhibitors are used in some cancers). Methylation can activate or repress.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are rich in cysteine disulfide bridges</b> — Disulfide bridges don't explain DNA binding.</li><li><b>They are rich in phenylalanine</b> — Phenylalanine is nonpolar.</li><li><b>They are rich in proline</b> — Proline doesn't carry charge.</li><li><b>They are rich in aspartate and glutamate</b> — Aspartate and glutamate are negatively charged and would repel DNA.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Histone acetylation → euchromatin (active); deacetylation → heterochromatin.</p>","w":["Disulfide bridges don't explain DNA binding.","Phenylalanine is nonpolar.","Proline doesn't carry charge.","Correct — histones are rich in lysine and arginine.","Aspartate and glutamate are negatively charged and would repel DNA."]},"fb38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In soluble globular proteins, nonpolar side chains are buried in the interior, forming hydrophobic interactions; polar and charged residues face water.</p><p>The hydrophobic effect is a major driving force for protein folding: nonpolar residues cluster to minimize contact with water. In membrane proteins, the transmembrane segments display hydrophobic residues to the lipid bilayer. Mutations that put a hydrophobic residue on the surface can cause aggregation — sickle cell HbS (Glu6Val) is the classic example.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>On the surface, hydrogen-bonding with water</b> — Polar residues face water on the surface; nonpolar ones hide inside.</li><li><b>Randomly, forming covalent bonds</b> — Hydrophobic interactions are non-covalent and the location isn't random.</li><li><b>In the active site only, forming ionic bonds</b> — Ionic bonds involve charged side chains, not nonpolar ones.</li><li><b>On the surface, forming disulfide bonds</b> — Disulfide bonds involve cysteines.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> HbS: surface hydrophobic valine → polymerization.</p>","w":["Correct — interior, forming hydrophobic interactions.","Polar residues face water on the surface; nonpolar ones hide inside.","Hydrophobic interactions are non-covalent and the location isn't random.","Ionic bonds involve charged side chains, not nonpolar ones.","Disulfide bonds involve cysteines."]},"fb39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Essential amino acids (must come from diet): PVT TIM HaLL — phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, lysine (+ arginine in growth).</p><p>Non-essential amino acids can be made from glucose intermediates or other amino acids: tyrosine from phenylalanine (phenylalanine hydroxylase — deficient in PKU, making tyrosine essential), cysteine from methionine. Inadequate essential amino acid intake causes negative nitrogen balance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Asparagine, alanine, glycine</b> — Asparagine, alanine and glycine are all non-essential.</li><li><b>Glutamate, glutamine, proline</b> — Glutamate, glutamine and proline are all non-essential.</li><li><b>Glycine, alanine, serine</b> — Glycine, alanine and serine are all non-essential.</li><li><b>Tyrosine, cysteine, aspartate</b> — Tyrosine, cysteine and aspartate are all non-essential (Tyr from Phe; Cys from Met).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PKU: tyrosine becomes essential.</p>","w":["Asparagine, alanine and glycine are all non-essential.","Glutamate, glutamine and proline are all non-essential.","Glycine, alanine and serine are all non-essential.","Tyrosine, cysteine and aspartate are all non-essential (Tyr from Phe; Cys from Met).","Correct — leucine, lysine and phenylalanine are essential."]},"fb40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Only leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic (yield acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate, never glucose).</p><p>Glucogenic amino acids are converted to pyruvate or TCA intermediates that can feed gluconeogenesis. Ketogenic ones yield acetyl-CoA or acetoacetate, which can't make net glucose. Both: isoleucine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, tyrosine. All others are purely glucogenic (alanine is the main gluconeogenic amino acid released by muscle).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tyrosine and tryptophan</b> — Tyrosine and tryptophan are both glucogenic and ketogenic.</li><li><b>Isoleucine and threonine</b> — Isoleucine and threonine are both glucogenic and ketogenic.</li><li><b>Alanine and serine</b> — Alanine and serine are glucogenic.</li><li><b>Glutamate and aspartate</b> — Glutamate and aspartate are glucogenic.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Purely ketogenic: Leucine and Lysine (\"the two L's\").</p>","w":["Tyrosine and tryptophan are both glucogenic and ketogenic.","Isoleucine and threonine are both glucogenic and ketogenic.","Correct — leucine and lysine.","Alanine and serine are glucogenic.","Glutamate and aspartate are glucogenic."]},"fb41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Polar: acidic, basic, hydroxyl-containing (Ser, Thr, Tyr), amide (Asn, Gln) and cysteine. Nonpolar: aliphatic, Phe, Trp, Met and Pro.</p><p>Tyrosine's ring is aromatic, but its –OH makes it polar (it can hydrogen bond and be phosphorylated). Cysteine's –SH is weakly polar and can ionize. Methionine's thioether is hydrophobic. Proline's ring makes it nonpolar. Polarity predicts location: polar residues on protein surfaces; nonpolar residues in the core or in membrane-spanning segments.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>All sulfur amino acids are nonpolar</b> — Cysteine is classified as polar; only methionine is nonpolar.</li><li><b>Proline is polar because of its cyclic ring structure</b> — Proline is nonpolar despite its ring.</li><li><b>All aromatic amino acids are polar</b> — Tyrosine is polar, but phenylalanine and tryptophan are nonpolar.</li><li><b>Acidic and basic amino acids are nonpolar</b> — Charged (acidic and basic) amino acids are polar.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tyrosine = aromatic but polar.</p>","w":["Cysteine is classified as polar; only methionine is nonpolar.","Correct — Tyr and Cys are polar; Met and Pro are nonpolar.","Proline is nonpolar despite its ring.","Tyrosine is polar, but phenylalanine and tryptophan are nonpolar.","Charged (acidic and basic) amino acids are polar."]},"fb42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> At pH 7.4 a free amino acid is a zwitterion: –COO⁻ (pKa ~2) and –NH₃⁺ (pKa ~9–10), with no net charge (for neutral side chains).</p><p>Rule: if pH &gt; pKa, the group is mostly deprotonated; if pH &lt; pKa, mostly protonated. At 7.4, the carboxyl (pKa ~2) loses its proton and the amino group (pKa ~9) keeps it. Zwitterions are highly water-soluble and have high melting points. In very acidic solution the amino acid is net positive; in very basic solution, net negative.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>–COOH and –NH3⁺ (net positive)</b> — At pH 7.4 the carboxyl is deprotonated, not –COOH.</li><li><b>Both groups are covalently bonded to each other</b> — The groups aren't covalently bonded; they exchange protons with water.</li><li><b>–COOH and –NH2 (both uncharged)</b> — Both groups are ionized at pH 7.4.</li><li><b>–COO⁻ and –NH2 (net negative)</b> — At pH 7.4 the amino group is protonated.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> pH &gt; pKa → deprotonated; pH &lt; pKa → protonated.</p>","w":["At pH 7.4 the carboxyl is deprotonated, not –COOH.","The groups aren't covalently bonded; they exchange protons with water.","Both groups are ionized at pH 7.4.","Correct — –COO⁻ and –NH₃⁺, a zwitterion.","At pH 7.4 the amino group is protonated."]},"fb43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A buffer is a weak acid/conjugate base pair that resists pH change, working best within about ±1 pH unit of its pKa.</p><p>Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]). At pH = pKa, [A⁻] = [HA] and buffering capacity is maximal. Physiologic buffers: bicarbonate/CO₂ (pKa 6.1, effective because CO₂ is regulated by the lungs and HCO₃⁻ by the kidneys), phosphate (pKa 6.8, important in urine and ICF) and proteins (histidine imidazole, hemoglobin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A strong acid that lowers pH</b> — A strong acid doesn't resist pH change.</li><li><b>A molecule with no ionizable groups</b> — Buffering requires ionizable groups.</li><li><b>A solution whose pH changes sharply when acid is added</b> — Buffers resist sharp pH changes.</li><li><b>A base that cannot accept protons</b> — A base by definition accepts protons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> pH = pKa + log(base/acid).</p>","w":["Correct — a conjugate pair buffering best near its pKa.","A strong acid doesn't resist pH change.","Buffering requires ionizable groups.","Buffers resist sharp pH changes.","A base by definition accepts protons."]},"fb44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> For a simple amino acid, pI = (pKa₁ + pKa₂)/2; alanine: (2.3 + 9.1)/2 = 5.7.</p><p>Below pH 2.3, alanine is mostly +1; between 2.3 and 9.1, it's mostly the zwitterion (0); above 9.1, it's −1. The pI is the pH at which the average net charge is zero — the midpoint between the two pKas bracketing the neutral form. At its pI an amino acid is least soluble and doesn't migrate in electrophoresis (basis of isoelectric focusing).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>9.1</b> — 9.1 is the amino group pKa alone.</li><li><b>6.8</b> — 6.8 isn't the average of the two pKas.</li><li><b>11.4</b> — 11.4 is the sum, not the average.</li><li><b>2.3</b> — 2.3 is the carboxyl pKa alone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> pI = average of pKas flanking the neutral species.</p>","w":["9.1 is the amino group pKa alone.","6.8 isn't the average of the two pKas.","11.4 is the sum, not the average.","2.3 is the carboxyl pKa alone.","Correct — (2.3 + 9.1)/2 = 5.7."]},"fb45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> For acidic amino acids, average the two lowest pKas (both carboxyls): aspartate pI = (2.1 + 3.9)/2 = 3.0.</p><p>List the forms from low to high pH: +1 (all protonated) → 0 (α-COO⁻) → −1 (side chain COO⁻) → −2 (amino deprotonated). The neutral species exists between pKa 2.1 and 3.9, so pI = 3.0. Basic amino acids (Lys, Arg, His) use the two highest pKas. Acidic amino acids have low pIs and are negative at physiologic pH.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>5.95</b> — 5.95 averages the wrong pair of pKas (2.1 and 9.8).</li><li><b>6.85</b> — 6.85 averages 3.9 and 9.8; the neutral species doesn't lie between them.</li><li><b>6.0</b> — 6.0 isn't the average of the flanking pKas.</li><li><b>9.8</b> — 9.8 is the amino group pKa.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Acidic AA → average two lowest pKas; basic AA → average two highest.</p>","w":["5.95 averages the wrong pair of pKas (2.1 and 9.8).","6.85 averages 3.9 and 9.8; the neutral species doesn't lie between them.","Correct — pI = 3.0.","6.0 isn't the average of the flanking pKas.","9.8 is the amino group pKa."]},"fb46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> For basic amino acids, average the two highest pKas: histidine pI = (6.0 + 9.2)/2 = 7.6.</p><p>Forms: +2 (all protonated, pH &lt; 1.8) → +1 (carboxyl ionized) → 0 (imidazole deprotonated, above pH 6.0) → −1 (amino deprotonated, above 9.2). The neutral species lies between 6.0 and 9.2. Histidine's imidazole pKa (~6) means it can accept or donate protons near physiologic pH — why it's important in enzyme active sites and in hemoglobin's buffering (Bohr effect).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>3.9</b> — 3.9 averages 1.8 and 6.0 — the wrong pair.</li><li><b>9.2</b> — 9.2 is the amino group pKa alone.</li><li><b>6.0</b> — 6.0 is the imidazole pKa alone.</li><li><b>5.5</b> — 5.5 averages 1.8 and 9.2.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> His pI ~7.6; Lys ~9.7; Arg ~10.8; Asp ~3.0; Glu ~3.2.</p>","w":["3.9 averages 1.8 and 6.0 — the wrong pair.","Correct — (6.0 + 9.2)/2 = 7.6.","9.2 is the amino group pKa alone.","6.0 is the imidazole pKa alone.","5.5 averages 1.8 and 9.2."]},"fb47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In electrophoresis: pH &lt; pI → net positive → cathode (−); pH = pI → no movement; pH &gt; pI → net negative → anode (+).</p><p>At pH 5.7: lysine (pI 9.7) has pH below its pI, so it's positive and moves to the cathode. Alanine (pI 5.7) is at its pI — it stays put. Aspartate (pI 3.0) has pH above its pI, so it's negative and moves to the anode. The same principle separates serum proteins and hemoglobin variants (HbS migrates slower toward the anode than HbA because Val replaces negatively charged Glu).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>All three move together toward the anode</b> — Lysine is positively charged at pH 5.7, so it doesn't go to the anode.</li><li><b>None of them move</b> — Lysine and aspartate are charged at pH 5.7, so they move.</li><li><b>Asp → cathode, Ala stays, Lys → anode</b> — This reverses lysine and aspartate.</li><li><b>Ala moves fastest toward the cathode</b> — Alanine is at its pI — no net charge, no movement.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hb electrophoresis toward anode: A &gt; S &gt; C (\"A Fast Sick Cat\").</p>","w":["Lysine is positively charged at pH 5.7, so it doesn't go to the anode.","Lysine and aspartate are charged at pH 5.7, so they move.","This reverses lysine and aspartate.","Correct — Lys → cathode, Ala stays, Asp → anode.","Alanine is at its pI — no net charge, no movement."]},"fb48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> At pH 7.4, glutamate (pI ~3.2) is net negative and moves farthest toward the positive pole (anode).</p><p>Compare each amino acid's pI with pH 7.4: Glu (3.2) — strongly negative → anode. Gly and Ala (~6) — slightly negative, move little. His (7.6) — nearly neutral. Arg (10.8) — positive → cathode. The farther pH is from the pI, the greater the net charge and the faster the migration (for similar sizes).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycine</b> — Glycine (pI ~6) has little net charge at pH 7.4.</li><li><b>Arginine</b> — Arginine is positive and moves toward the negative pole.</li><li><b>Tyrosine</b> — Tyrosine isn't released from this peptide and is nearly neutral at pH 7.4.</li><li><b>Alanine</b> — Alanine (pI ~6) has little net charge at pH 7.4.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Distance between pH and pI ≈ size of net charge.</p>","w":["Glycine (pI ~6) has little net charge at pH 7.4.","Arginine is positive and moves toward the negative pole.","Correct — glutamate moves most toward the positive pole.","Tyrosine isn't released from this peptide and is nearly neutral at pH 7.4.","Alanine (pI ~6) has little net charge at pH 7.4."]},"fb49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> CF's commonest mutation, ΔF508 (deletion of phenylalanine 508), alters CFTR's primary structure, causing misfolding and ER degradation.</p><p>Primary structure (amino acid sequence) dictates secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure. ΔF508 CFTR misfolds, is retained by ER quality control, and is degraded by proteasomes, so little reaches the plasma membrane. Corrector drugs (lumacaftor, tezacaftor, elexacaftor) help folding/trafficking, and potentiators (ivacaftor) increase channel opening. Sickle cell (Glu6Val) is another single primary-structure change with major consequences.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Disruption of quaternary structure of the protein</b> — The defect originates in the sequence, not in subunit assembly.</li><li><b>Complete loss of secondary structure</b> — The protein doesn't lose all secondary structure.</li><li><b>Breakdown of hydrogen bonds</b> — Hydrogen bond breakdown would be a consequence, not the underlying cause.</li><li><b>Replacement of glycine by serine</b> — The classic CFTR defect is a phenylalanine deletion, not a Gly→Ser substitution.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ΔF508 = misfolding/trafficking defect (class II CFTR mutation).</p>","w":["The defect originates in the sequence, not in subunit assembly.","Correct — altered primary structure.","The protein doesn't lose all secondary structure.","Hydrogen bond breakdown would be a consequence, not the underlying cause.","The classic CFTR defect is a phenylalanine deletion, not a Gly→Ser substitution."]},"fb50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proteins are large colloidal molecules that can't pass through dialysis membranes, while small solutes can.</p><p>Dialysis uses a semipermeable membrane with pores that retain macromolecules. It's used in the lab to remove salts from protein preparations and clinically (hemodialysis) to remove urea, creatinine and electrolytes from blood while retaining plasma proteins. Proteins also act as plasma buffers (amphoteric) and create colloid osmotic pressure because they're retained in capillaries.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are covalently bound to the dialysis membrane</b> — The protein is free in solution, not bound to the membrane.</li><li><b>They are hydrophobic and precipitate out of solution</b> — Many proteins are soluble and remain in solution.</li><li><b>They have a low molecular weight but a strong charge</b> — Proteins have high molecular weight.</li><li><b>They are always positively charged at any pH</b> — A protein's net charge depends on pH; it isn't always positive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proteins non-dialyzable → plasma oncotic pressure.</p>","w":["Correct — large colloids can't cross the membrane.","The protein is free in solution, not bound to the membrane.","Many proteins are soluble and remain in solution.","Proteins have high molecular weight.","A protein's net charge depends on pH; it isn't always positive."]},"fb51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hemoglobin is a conjugated protein (chromoprotein) with a heme prosthetic group.</p><p>Conjugated proteins = protein + non-protein group: glycoproteins (sugar), lipoproteins (lipid), nucleoproteins (nucleic acid), metalloproteins (metal ions: ceruloplasmin, ferritin), chromoproteins (colored prosthetic groups: hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochromes), flavoproteins (FAD/FMN), phosphoproteins (casein). Simple proteins (albumin, globulins) contain only amino acids. Derived proteins come from denaturation (gelatin) or digestion (peptones).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peptone</b> — Peptone is a secondary derived (digested) protein.</li><li><b>Collagen</b> — Collagen is a fibrous structural protein without a prosthetic group.</li><li><b>Albumin</b> — Albumin is a simple protein.</li><li><b>Gelatin</b> — Gelatin is a primary derived (denatured) protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heme = protoporphyrin IX + Fe²⁺.</p>","w":["Peptone is a secondary derived (digested) protein.","Collagen is a fibrous structural protein without a prosthetic group.","Albumin is a simple protein.","Gelatin is a primary derived (denatured) protein.","Correct — hemoglobin."]},"fb52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Shape classification: fibrous proteins (long, structural, insoluble, repetitive — collagen, keratin, elastin) vs globular (compact, soluble, functional — hemoglobin, enzymes, antibodies).</p><p>Fibrous proteins have axial ratios &gt;10 and are built from repetitive sequences forming extended secondary structures (collagen triple helix, keratin α-helices), giving mechanical strength. Globular proteins fold compactly with hydrophobic cores and polar surfaces, allowing specific binding and catalysis; they're more easily denatured.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Storage vs transport proteins</b> — Storage vs transport is a functional, not shape-based, classification.</li><li><b>Homo-oligomers vs hetero-oligomers</b> — Homo- vs hetero-oligomers describe quaternary structure.</li><li><b>Simple vs conjugated proteins</b> — Simple vs conjugated proteins is a composition-based classification.</li><li><b>Primary vs secondary derived proteins</b> — Derived proteins come from denaturation or digestion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fibrous = structural; globular = functional.</p>","w":["Storage vs transport is a functional, not shape-based, classification.","Homo- vs hetero-oligomers describe quaternary structure.","Correct — fibrous vs globular.","Simple vs conjugated proteins is a composition-based classification.","Derived proteins come from denaturation or digestion."]},"fb53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ferritin stores iron (mainly intracellularly in liver, marrow, spleen); transferrin transports iron in plasma.</p><p>Iron absorbed by enterocytes is exported by ferroportin and carried by transferrin to cells with transferrin receptors (e.g., erythroid precursors). Excess is stored as ferritin (and hemosiderin). Hepcidin (from liver) blocks ferroportin. Lab patterns: iron deficiency — ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC (transferrin), ↓saturation; anemia of chronic disease — ↑ferritin, ↓TIBC; hemochromatosis — ↑ferritin, ↑saturation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both are contractile proteins</b> — Contractile proteins are actin and myosin.</li><li><b>Both are structural proteins</b> — Structural proteins are collagen and keratin.</li><li><b>Ferritin: toxin; transferrin: protective</b> — Toxins are proteins like diphtheria toxin; ferritin isn't a toxin.</li><li><b>Ferritin: enzyme; transferrin: hormone</b> — Neither ferritin nor transferrin is an enzyme or hormone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Low ferritin = most specific test for iron deficiency.</p>","w":["Contractile proteins are actin and myosin.","Correct — ferritin stores iron; transferrin transports it.","Structural proteins are collagen and keratin.","Toxins are proteins like diphtheria toxin; ferritin isn't a toxin.","Neither ferritin nor transferrin is an enzyme or hormone."]},"fb54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The peptide bond has partial double-bond character (resonance), making it rigid, planar and usually trans; rotation occurs only around Cα bonds (φ and ψ).</p><p>Resonance between C=O and C–N shortens the bond and prevents rotation, so the six atoms of each peptide unit lie in a plane. The trans configuration avoids clashes between neighboring side chains (except some X–Pro bonds, which can be cis). The bond is uncharged but polar: C=O and N–H form hydrogen bonds that build α-helices and β-sheets. Formation releases water (condensation); hydrolysis requires proteases or strong acid/base.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It forms between two side chains</b> — The peptide bond links α-carboxyl and α-amino groups, not side chains.</li><li><b>It is charged at physiologic pH</b> — The peptide bond is uncharged (but polar).</li><li><b>It forms without loss of water</b> — Formation releases a water molecule.</li><li><b>It rotates freely and is usually in the cis form</b> — There's no free rotation, and trans (not cis) is typical.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> φ/ψ angles (Ramachandran plot) define backbone conformation.</p>","w":["The peptide bond links α-carboxyl and α-amino groups, not side chains.","The peptide bond is uncharged (but polar).","Formation releases a water molecule.","Correct — rigid, planar and usually trans.","There's no free rotation, and trans (not cis) is typical."]},"fb55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Insulin: A chain (21 aa) + B chain (30 aa) linked by 3 disulfide bonds — 2 interchain and 1 intrachain (within the A chain).</p><p>Insulin was the first protein sequenced (Sanger). It's synthesized as preproinsulin; the signal peptide is removed in the ER, disulfide bonds form while it's proinsulin, and C-peptide is cleaved in secretory granules. Reducing the disulfide bonds separates the chains and inactivates insulin. Insulin analogs (lispro, aspart, glargine) alter a few residues to change absorption kinetics.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1 interchain bridge</b> — Insulin has three disulfide bridges, not one.</li><li><b>3 bridges, all intrachain within the B chain</b> — The intrachain bridge is in the A chain, and two are interchain.</li><li><b>None; the two chains are joined by peptide bonds</b> — The A and B chains are joined by disulfide bonds, not peptide bonds.</li><li><b>2 bridges: both intrachain</b> — There are three bridges, two of which are interchain.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Insulin: 51 aa, 3 disulfides; C-peptide marks endogenous insulin.</p>","w":["Correct — two interchain and one A-chain intrachain bridge.","Insulin has three disulfide bridges, not one.","The intrachain bridge is in the A chain, and two are interchain.","The A and B chains are joined by disulfide bonds, not peptide bonds.","There are three bridges, two of which are interchain."]},"fb56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A single amino acid substitution (β6 Glu → Val in HbS) is a change in primary structure.</p><p>The GAG → GTG point mutation (missense) changes glutamate to valine. The hydrophobic valine on the surface of deoxy-HbS binds a pocket on adjacent molecules, so HbS polymerizes into fibers that deform RBCs into sickles — affecting tertiary interactions and quaternary assembly secondarily. Triggers: hypoxia, acidosis, dehydration. Hydroxyurea increases HbF, reducing polymerization.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>No structural level</b> — The amino acid sequence changed, so a structural level is altered.</li><li><b>Secondary structure</b> — Secondary structure isn't the primary change.</li><li><b>Quaternary structure only</b> — Quaternary effects follow from the sequence change but aren't the primary alteration.</li><li><b>Tertiary structure only</b> — Tertiary effects follow from the sequence change but aren't the primary alteration.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hydroxyurea ↑HbF in sickle cell disease.</p>","w":["The amino acid sequence changed, so a structural level is altered.","Secondary structure isn't the primary change.","Quaternary effects follow from the sequence change but aren't the primary alteration.","Tertiary effects follow from the sequence change but aren't the primary alteration.","Correct — primary structure."]},"fb57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Edman degradation sequences peptides from the N-terminus: phenyl isothiocyanate labels the N-terminal residue, which is cleaved as a PTH-amino acid and identified, then the cycle repeats.</p><p>Each cycle removes one residue without hydrolyzing the rest of the peptide. It works for ~30–50 residues; longer proteins are first cleaved into fragments (trypsin cuts after Lys/Arg; chymotrypsin after aromatic residues; cyanogen bromide after Met), and overlapping sequences are pieced together. Today, mass spectrometry is more common. Sanger's reagent (FDNB) identifies only the N-terminal residue.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sanger dideoxy sequencing</b> — Sanger dideoxy sequencing sequences DNA, not peptides.</li><li><b>Western blotting</b> — Western blotting detects proteins with antibodies.</li><li><b>TUNEL assay</b> — TUNEL detects DNA fragmentation.</li><li><b>Benedict's test</b> — Benedict's test detects reducing sugars.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Edman = sequential N-terminal sequencing.</p>","w":["Sanger dideoxy sequencing sequences DNA, not peptides.","Western blotting detects proteins with antibodies.","Correct — Edman degradation.","TUNEL detects DNA fragmentation.","Benedict's test detects reducing sugars."]},"fb58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glutathione (γ-glutamyl-cysteinyl-glycine) is the main intracellular antioxidant tripeptide; its cysteine thiol does the work.</p><p>Unusually, the glutamate is linked through its γ-carboxyl. GSH reduces H₂O₂ via glutathione peroxidase (selenium-dependent), becoming GSSG, which glutathione reductase regenerates using NADPH (from G6PD). GSH also conjugates drugs and toxins (glutathione S-transferases) and helps transport amino acids (γ-glutamyl cycle). G6PD deficiency → low NADPH → low GSH → oxidative hemolysis (Heinz bodies, bite cells).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Thyrotropin-releasing hormone</b> — TRH is pyroglutamyl-histidyl-prolinamide.</li><li><b>Oxytocin</b> — Oxytocin is a nonapeptide.</li><li><b>Carnosine</b> — Carnosine is a dipeptide (β-alanine + histidine).</li><li><b>Anserine</b> — Anserine is a dipeptide (β-alanine + methylhistidine).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> G6PD deficiency: ↓NADPH → ↓GSH → hemolysis.</p>","w":["TRH is pyroglutamyl-histidyl-prolinamide.","Correct — glutathione.","Oxytocin is a nonapeptide.","Carnosine is a dipeptide (β-alanine + histidine).","Anserine is a dipeptide (β-alanine + methylhistidine)."]},"fb59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH) are posterior pituitary nonapeptides that differ at only two residues.</p><p>Both are synthesized in hypothalamic nuclei (supraoptic and paraventricular) and transported down axons to the posterior pituitary. Oxytocin: uterine contraction (labor induction, Pitocin) and milk ejection. Vasopressin: V₁ receptors → vasoconstriction; V₂ receptors → aquaporin-2 insertion in collecting ducts (water reabsorption). Because they're similar, high doses of oxytocin can have antidiuretic effects (water intoxication/hyponatremia).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Carnosine and anserine</b> — Carnosine and anserine are muscle dipeptides.</li><li><b>TRH and PTH</b> — TRH is a tripeptide, and PTH (84 aa) comes from the parathyroid.</li><li><b>Insulin and glucagon</b> — Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic hormones.</li><li><b>Glucagon and ACTH</b> — Glucagon (29 aa) is from pancreatic α-cells; ACTH (39 aa) is from the anterior pituitary.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oxytocin overdose → water intoxication (ADH-like effect).</p>","w":["Carnosine and anserine are muscle dipeptides.","TRH is a tripeptide, and PTH (84 aa) comes from the parathyroid.","Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic hormones.","Correct — oxytocin and vasopressin.","Glucagon (29 aa) is from pancreatic α-cells; ACTH (39 aa) is from the anterior pituitary."]},"fb60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glucagon: 29-amino-acid peptide from pancreatic α-cells that raises blood glucose (glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis in the liver).</p><p>Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled receptor on hepatocytes → cAMP → PKA → activation of glycogen phosphorylase, gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis. It's released during fasting and hypoglycemia, and suppressed by insulin and glucose. IM glucagon is used for severe hypoglycemia and β-blocker overdose. Glucagonoma causes necrolytic migratory erythema, diabetes and weight loss.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ACTH</b> — ACTH has 39 amino acids and comes from the anterior pituitary.</li><li><b>Parathyroid hormone</b> — PTH has 84 amino acids and regulates calcium.</li><li><b>Vasopressin</b> — Vasopressin is a nonapeptide from the posterior pituitary.</li><li><b>Insulin</b> — Insulin (51 amino acids) lowers blood glucose and comes from β-cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glucagon treats hypoglycemia and β-blocker overdose.</p>","w":["Correct — glucagon.","ACTH has 39 amino acids and comes from the anterior pituitary.","PTH has 84 amino acids and regulates calcium.","Vasopressin is a nonapeptide from the posterior pituitary.","Insulin (51 amino acids) lowers blood glucose and comes from β-cells."]},"fb61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary structure (α-helix, β-sheet) is stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups.</p><p>Levels and bonds: primary — peptide bonds; secondary — backbone H-bonds; tertiary — side-chain interactions (hydrophobic, ionic, H-bonds, van der Waals, disulfides); quaternary — non-covalent (and sometimes disulfide) links between subunits. In an α-helix, each C=O hydrogen-bonds to the N–H four residues ahead; in β-sheets, bonds form between adjacent strands.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hydrophobic interactions only</b> — Hydrophobic interactions mainly drive tertiary folding.</li><li><b>Disulfide bonds between cysteine side chains</b> — Disulfide bonds mainly stabilize tertiary and quaternary structure.</li><li><b>Peptide bonds between R groups</b> — Peptide bonds form the primary structure; they don't link R groups.</li><li><b>Ionic bonds between oppositely charged side chains</b> — Ionic bonds between side chains are mainly tertiary.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Secondary = backbone H-bonds only.</p>","w":["Hydrophobic interactions mainly drive tertiary folding.","Disulfide bonds mainly stabilize tertiary and quaternary structure.","Peptide bonds form the primary structure; they don't link R groups.","Ionic bonds between side chains are mainly tertiary.","Correct — backbone C=O···H–N hydrogen bonds."]},"fb62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> α-Helix: right-handed coil, 3.6 residues per turn, 5.4 Å pitch, side chains pointing outward; proline breaks it.</p><p>Each turn's C=O hydrogen-bonds to the N–H four residues later, parallel to the helix axis. Helix-destabilizing residues: proline (rigid ring, no N–H), glycine (too flexible), clustered like charges (Glu, Asp, Lys, Arg), bulky (Trp) or β-branched (Val, Ile, Thr) residues. α-Helices dominate keratin, myoglobin and hemoglobin and form transmembrane segments (~20 hydrophobic residues).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is always left-handed</b> — Right-handed, not left-handed, helices are the stable form.</li><li><b>It is the main structure of silk fibroin and β-keratin</b> — Silk fibroin is a β-sheet protein; α-keratin is the α-helix example.</li><li><b>It is held by H-bonds between adjacent, side-by-side strands</b> — Hydrogen bonds between side-by-side strands describe β-sheets.</li><li><b>It is a flat sheet with all side chains inside the core</b> — A flat sheet with side chains inside isn't an α-helix; helices are rods with side chains outward.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Proline = helix breaker; transmembrane α-helix ≈ 20 hydrophobic residues.</p>","w":["Right-handed, not left-handed, helices are the stable form.","Silk fibroin is a β-sheet protein; α-keratin is the α-helix example.","Correct — 3.6 residues/turn, side chains outward, broken by proline.","Hydrogen bonds between side-by-side strands describe β-sheets.","A flat sheet with side chains inside isn't an α-helix; helices are rods with side chains outward."]},"fb63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> β-Pleated sheet: extended zig-zag strands lying side by side (parallel or antiparallel), H-bonded between strands; ~3.5 Å per residue.</p><p>Side chains alternate above and below the sheet. Antiparallel sheets (strands running in opposite directions, often joined by β-turns) have straighter, stronger H-bonds than parallel sheets. Silk fibroin is mainly β-sheet. β-sheets are also the core of amyloid fibrils (Alzheimer Aβ, prions, light-chain amyloidosis), which are stained by Congo red (apple-green birefringence).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>β-Turn</b> — A β-turn is a 4-residue reversal of chain direction.</li><li><b>Random coil</b> — A random coil lacks this regular structure.</li><li><b>Collagen triple helix</b> — The collagen triple helix is a special helix of three chains, not a pleated sheet.</li><li><b>α-Helix</b> — α-Helix residues are 1.5 Å apart, with H-bonds within one coil.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amyloid = β-pleated sheet; Congo red apple-green birefringence.</p>","w":["A β-turn is a 4-residue reversal of chain direction.","Correct — β-pleated sheet.","A random coil lacks this regular structure.","The collagen triple helix is a special helix of three chains, not a pleated sheet.","α-Helix residues are 1.5 Å apart, with H-bonds within one coil."]},"fb64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> β-Bends (reverse turns) are 4-residue turns, often with proline and glycine, that reverse chain direction on the protein surface (connecting antiparallel strands).</p><p>The C=O of residue 1 hydrogen-bonds with the N–H of residue 4. Proline's fixed angle and glycine's flexibility (and small size) make them common at turns. Turns are usually on the surface, where they're exposed to solvent and often form antibody-binding sites.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Disulfide loop</b> — Disulfide loops are covalent links between cysteines, not 4-residue turns.</li><li><b>Zinc finger</b> — A zinc finger is a super-secondary DNA-binding motif.</li><li><b>Random coil</b> — A random coil has no defined reversal.</li><li><b>α-Helix</b> — An α-helix is a long, regular coil.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Turns: Pro and Gly; connect antiparallel β-strands.</p>","w":["Disulfide loops are covalent links between cysteines, not 4-residue turns.","A zinc finger is a super-secondary DNA-binding motif.","A random coil has no defined reversal.","Correct — β-bend (reverse turn).","An α-helix is a long, regular coil."]},"fb65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Super-secondary structures (motifs) are recurring combinations of secondary elements — e.g., helix-turn-helix, zinc finger, leucine zipper, β-α-β.</p><p>DNA-binding motifs: helix-turn-helix (homeodomain proteins — recognition helix fits the major groove), zinc finger (Zn²⁺ coordinated by Cys/His; in steroid/thyroid hormone receptors), leucine zipper (dimerization via Leu every seventh residue; e.g., Fos/Jun, CREB), and helix-loop-helix (MyoD, Myc). Domains are larger, independently folding functional units built from motifs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Quaternary structures</b> — Quaternary structure involves multiple polypeptide chains.</li><li><b>Domains</b> — Domains are larger, stable, independently folding functional units.</li><li><b>Random coils</b> — Random coils lack defined structure.</li><li><b>Primary structures</b> — Primary structure is the amino acid sequence.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Zinc fingers in steroid/thyroid hormone receptors.</p>","w":["Correct — super-secondary structures (motifs).","Quaternary structure involves multiple polypeptide chains.","Domains are larger, stable, independently folding functional units.","Random coils lack defined structure.","Primary structure is the amino acid sequence."]},"fb66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Motif = a recurring arrangement of secondary structures (not independently stable); domain = a compact, independently folding functional unit of tertiary structure.</p><p>A protein may have several domains with different functions (e.g., a receptor's ligand-binding, transmembrane and kinase domains; an antibody's variable and constant domains). Domains can fold independently and are often encoded by separate exons, allowing evolution by domain shuffling. Motifs like the Rossmann fold (nucleotide binding) occur within domains.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A motif is a stable functional unit; a domain is an unstable structural pattern</b> — This reverses the definitions.</li><li><b>Motifs are made of carbohydrates</b> — Both motifs and domains are made of polypeptide, not carbohydrate.</li><li><b>Domains are found only in quaternary structure</b> — Domains are features of tertiary structure, not only quaternary.</li><li><b>Motifs and domains are identical</b> — Motifs and domains are explicitly different.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Domain = independently folding functional unit.</p>","w":["This reverses the definitions.","Both motifs and domains are made of polypeptide, not carbohydrate.","Domains are features of tertiary structure, not only quaternary.","Motifs and domains are explicitly different.","Correct — a motif is a structural pattern; a domain is a stable functional unit."]},"fb67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tertiary structure is stabilized by interactions between R groups far apart in the sequence: hydrophobic interactions, ionic bonds (salt bridges), hydrogen bonds, van der Waals forces and disulfide bonds.</p><p>Hydrophobic interactions are the main driving force, burying nonpolar side chains. Salt bridges link oppositely charged side chains (e.g., Lys–Asp). Disulfide bonds (covalent) stabilize extracellular proteins against harsh environments. Denaturants disrupt these: heat, pH extremes (ionic bonds), urea and detergents (hydrophobic/H-bonds), reducing agents like β-mercaptoethanol (disulfides). Denaturation leaves peptide bonds intact.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Only peptide bonds between adjacent residues</b> — Peptide bonds between adjacent residues make the primary structure.</li><li><b>Only hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N–H groups</b> — Backbone hydrogen bonds define secondary structure.</li><li><b>Phosphodiester bonds</b> — Phosphodiester bonds join nucleotides.</li><li><b>Glycosidic bonds</b> — Glycosidic bonds join sugars.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Denaturation disrupts 2°–4° structure but not peptide bonds.</p>","w":["Peptide bonds between adjacent residues make the primary structure.","Backbone hydrogen bonds define secondary structure.","Correct — several kinds of interactions between distant R groups.","Phosphodiester bonds join nucleotides.","Glycosidic bonds join sugars."]},"fb68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hemoglobin (α₂β₂) has quaternary structure: a hetero-oligomer whose non-covalently associated subunits bind O₂ cooperatively.</p><p>Deoxy-Hb is in the T (taut, low affinity) state, held by salt bridges between subunits. O₂ binding to one heme shifts the iron into the porphyrin plane, pulling the proximal histidine and breaking salt bridges, favoring the R (relaxed, high affinity) state in the other subunits — producing the sigmoidal O₂ curve. Myoglobin is a monomer without cooperativity (hyperbolic curve). 2,3-BPG, H⁺ and CO₂ stabilize the T state.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Its four subunits are held together by peptide bonds</b> — Subunits are held together by non-covalent interactions, not peptide bonds.</li><li><b>It is a monomer with tertiary structure only</b> — Hemoglobin has four chains, so it isn't a monomer.</li><li><b>Quaternary structure; a non-cooperative homo-oligomer</b> — Its subunits differ (α and β) and it binds O₂ cooperatively.</li><li><b>Its subunits are joined by glycosidic bonds</b> — Glycosidic bonds join sugars.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hb sigmoidal (cooperative); Mb hyperbolic.</p>","w":["Subunits are held together by non-covalent interactions, not peptide bonds.","Correct — quaternary structure, a cooperative hetero-oligomer.","Hemoglobin has four chains, so it isn't a monomer.","Its subunits differ (α and β) and it binds O₂ cooperatively.","Glycosidic bonds join sugars."]},"fb69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Alzheimer disease: abnormal cleavage of APP (by β- and γ-secretase) releases Aβ peptide, which aggregates into neurotoxic β-sheet amyloid plaques; tau forms intracellular tangles.</p><p>Normally α-secretase cleaves APP within the Aβ region (non-amyloidogenic). Sequential β-secretase and γ-secretase (presenilin) cleavage produces Aβ40/42; Aβ42 aggregates readily into oligomers and fibrils. Risk factors: age, APOE ε4 allele, Down syndrome (APP on chromosome 21), and early-onset familial mutations in APP, PSEN1 and PSEN2. Pathology: atrophy (hippocampus first), plaques, tangles, amyloid angiopathy; reduced acetylcholine (nucleus basalis of Meynert) — treated with cholinesterase inhibitors.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is associated with β-amyloid, soluble proteins unaltered amino acid sequence.</b> — Amyloid β aggregates are insoluble fibrils, not soluble proteins.</li><li><b>It results from accumulation of denatured proteins that have random conformations.</b> — The deposits are ordered β-sheet fibrils, not randomly denatured proteins.</li><li><b>It is associated with the accumulation of amyloid precursor protein.</b> — The Aβ cleavage product, not intact APP, accumulates.</li><li><b>It is caused by the infectious β-sheet form of a host-cell protein.</b> — The infectious β-sheet form of a host protein describes prion disease.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Early-onset AD: APP, PSEN1, PSEN2, Down syndrome; late-onset risk: APOE ε4.</p>","w":["Amyloid β aggregates are insoluble fibrils, not soluble proteins.","The deposits are ordered β-sheet fibrils, not randomly denatured proteins.","The Aβ cleavage product, not intact APP, accumulates.","Correct — deposition of neurotoxic Aβ aggregates.","The infectious β-sheet form of a host protein describes prion disease."]},"fb70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Converting α-helices to β-sheets exposes hydrophobic surfaces, so PrPˢᶜ forms insoluble, protease-resistant aggregates.</p><p>Prion aggregates kill neurons, producing spongiform encephalopathy (vacuoles without inflammation). CJD presents with rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus (often startle-induced), ataxia and death within ~1 year. Findings: periodic sharp-wave complexes on EEG, 14-3-3 protein and RT-QuIC positivity in CSF, cortical ribboning on MRI. Prions resist standard sterilization (formalin, autoclaving at normal settings).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased degradation by proteasomes</b> — PrPˢᶜ is highly resistant to proteasomal and protease degradation.</li><li><b>Enhanced solubility in the cytoplasm</b> — β-sheet-rich prions are insoluble, not more soluble.</li><li><b>Improved interaction with cellular receptors</b> — The aggregates are toxic; they don't improve receptor interactions.</li><li><b>Increased phosphorylation leading to hyperactivity</b> — Hyperphosphorylation describes tau in Alzheimer disease, not prions.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CJD: rapid dementia + myoclonus + periodic sharp waves on EEG.</p>","w":["PrPˢᶜ is highly resistant to proteasomal and protease degradation.","β-sheet-rich prions are insoluble, not more soluble.","Correct — insoluble protein aggregates form.","The aggregates are toxic; they don't improve receptor interactions.","Hyperphosphorylation describes tau in Alzheimer disease, not prions."]},"fb71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The hydrophobic effect — burying nonpolar side chains away from water — is the main driving force of protein folding.</p><p>When hydrophobic residues cluster in the core, ordered water molecules around them are released, increasing entropy and favoring folding. Hydrogen bonds, ionic interactions, van der Waals forces and disulfide bonds then fine-tune and stabilize the native structure. Anfinsen's experiment (ribonuclease refolding after denaturation) showed that the amino acid sequence contains all information for folding.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Formation of disulfide bonds</b> — Disulfide bonds stabilize some folded proteins but don't drive folding.</li><li><b>Hydrogen bonding</b> — Hydrogen bonds stabilize secondary structure but aren't the main driving force.</li><li><b>Ionic interactions</b> — Ionic interactions (salt bridges) contribute only modestly.</li><li><b>Van der Waals forces</b> — Van der Waals forces are weak individually and add stability once packing occurs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anfinsen: sequence determines structure.</p>","w":["Disulfide bonds stabilize some folded proteins but don't drive folding.","Hydrogen bonds stabilize secondary structure but aren't the main driving force.","Correct — hydrophobic interactions drive folding.","Ionic interactions (salt bridges) contribute only modestly.","Van der Waals forces are weak individually and add stability once packing occurs."]},"fb72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Prion diseases are transmitted by misfolded protein alone (PrPˢᶜ) — no DNA or RNA.</p><p>Forms: sporadic CJD (most common, older adults), familial (PRNP mutations — familial CJD, Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker, fatal familial insomnia), and acquired (variant CJD from BSE-contaminated beef; iatrogenic from corneal transplants, dura grafts, contaminated instruments, cadaveric growth hormone; kuru from ritual cannibalism). All are progressive, untreatable and fatal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It is a disease process in which a messenger RNA secondary structure appears to be the sole pathophysiologic entity</b> — No nucleic acid, including mRNA, is the pathogenic entity.</li><li><b>The disease is only found in humans</b> — Prion diseases also occur in sheep (scrapie), cattle (BSE) and deer (chronic wasting disease).</li><li><b>Effective treatments are available for the disease</b> — No effective treatment exists.</li><li><b>The disease process is readily reversible</b> — The disease is fatal and irreversible.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Variant CJD = younger patients, psychiatric onset, linked to BSE.</p>","w":["Correct — proteins appear to be the sole pathogenic entity.","No nucleic acid, including mRNA, is the pathogenic entity.","Prion diseases also occur in sheep (scrapie), cattle (BSE) and deer (chronic wasting disease).","No effective treatment exists.","The disease is fatal and irreversible."]},"fb73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chaperones (heat shock proteins such as Hsp70, Hsp60/chaperonins) bind and stabilize folding intermediates, prevent aggregation and use ATP.</p><p>Hsp70 binds exposed hydrophobic segments of nascent chains, preventing premature folding or aggregation; ATP hydrolysis cycles binding and release. Chaperonins (Hsp60/GroEL–GroES) provide an enclosed chamber where proteins fold. ER chaperones (BiP, calnexin, calreticulin) assist secretory proteins. Stress (heat, toxins) increases chaperone expression. Persistently misfolded proteins are ubiquitinated and degraded by proteasomes (ERAD); overload triggers the unfolded protein response.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They work only in the nucleus and never bind ATP</b> — Chaperones are in the cytosol, mitochondria and ER, and most bind ATP/ADP.</li><li><b>They degrade misfolded proteins inside lysosomes</b> — Degradation is performed by proteasomes and lysosomes, not chaperones.</li><li><b>They form the peptide bonds during translation</b> — Ribosomes form peptide bonds.</li><li><b>They are carbohydrates that coat proteins</b> — Chaperones are proteins, not carbohydrates.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hsp70/Hsp60 use ATP; BiP = ER Hsp70.</p>","w":["Chaperones are in the cytosol, mitochondria and ER, and most bind ATP/ADP.","Degradation is performed by proteasomes and lysosomes, not chaperones.","Ribosomes form peptide bonds.","Correct — chaperones stabilize intermediates and most use ATP.","Chaperones are proteins, not carbohydrates."]},"fb74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Folding stages: local secondary structures form → hydrophobic collapse into a molten globule → rearrangement into the native protein (then subunit assembly if oligomeric).</p><p>Folding begins co-translationally as the chain leaves the ribosome. The molten globule has most secondary structure but loosely packed side chains. Final adjustments (often helped by chaperones, protein disulfide isomerase and peptidyl-prolyl isomerase) produce the compact native state. Oligomeric proteins fold their subunits first, then assemble.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tertiary structure → secondary structure → native protein</b> — Secondary units form before tertiary structure.</li><li><b>Subunit assembly → secondary structure → folding</b> — Subunits fold before they assemble.</li><li><b>Disulfide bonds form first, then the primary sequence is made</b> — The sequence is made first; folding begins during synthesis.</li><li><b>Folding happens only after the protein is secreted</b> — Folding begins during synthesis, not after secretion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Protein disulfide isomerase and prolyl isomerase speed folding.</p>","w":["Correct — secondary units → molten globule → native protein.","Secondary units form before tertiary structure.","Subunits fold before they assemble.","The sequence is made first; folding begins during synthesis.","Folding begins during synthesis, not after secretion."]},"fb75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Normal PrPᶜ is α-helix-rich; infectious PrPˢᶜ has the same sequence but is β-sheet-rich, aggregated and protease-resistant.</p><p>The key concept is that one sequence can adopt two stable conformations. PrPˢᶜ acts as a template that converts PrPᶜ into more PrPˢᶜ — an autocatalytic, self-propagating process. PrPᶜ is a GPI-anchored neuronal membrane protein; mice lacking it can't be infected.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>PrPsc is easily digested by proteases</b> — PrPˢᶜ is highly resistant to proteolysis.</li><li><b>PrPsc requires viral RNA to replicate</b> — No nucleic acid is involved in prion replication.</li><li><b>PrPc is β-sheet-rich; PrPsc is α-helix-rich</b> — This reverses the two forms.</li><li><b>Both forms have different amino acid sequences</b> — Both forms have the same amino acid sequence; they differ in folding.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Same sequence, different conformation = prion.</p>","w":["PrPˢᶜ is highly resistant to proteolysis.","No nucleic acid is involved in prion replication.","This reverses the two forms.","Both forms have the same amino acid sequence; they differ in folding.","Correct — PrPᶜ is α-helix-rich; PrPˢᶜ is β-sheet-rich."]},"fb76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Neurofibrillary tangles = intracellular aggregates of hyperphosphorylated, insoluble tau (paired helical filaments).</p><p>Tau normally binds and stabilizes neuronal microtubules. Hyperphosphorylation makes it detach and aggregate into tangles, disrupting axonal transport. Tangle burden correlates better with cognitive decline than plaque burden. Tauopathies also include frontotemporal dementia (Pick bodies), progressive supranuclear palsy and chronic traumatic encephalopathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycogen granules within lysosomes</b> — Tangles aren't glycogen granules.</li><li><b>Soluble amyloid precursor protein</b> — APP itself is soluble; its cleaved Aβ product forms extracellular plaques.</li><li><b>Prion protein in α-helical form</b> — Prion protein isn't the cause of Alzheimer tangles.</li><li><b>Normal microtubule-bound tau</b> — Normal tau stabilizes microtubules; in Alzheimer it is hyperphosphorylated and aggregates.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Plaques = extracellular Aβ; tangles = intracellular tau.</p>","w":["Tangles aren't glycogen granules.","APP itself is soluble; its cleaved Aβ product forms extracellular plaques.","Correct — hyperphosphorylated, insoluble tau.","Prion protein isn't the cause of Alzheimer tangles.","Normal tau stabilizes microtubules; in Alzheimer it is hyperphosphorylated and aggregates."]},"fb77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome = thiamine (B1) deficiency, classically in chronic alcohol use: confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, ataxia (Wernicke) + amnesia with confabulation (Korsakoff).</p><p>Thiamine pyrophosphate is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase and transketolase. Brain cells depend on glucose oxidation, so deficiency impairs ATP production, especially in mammillary bodies, medial thalamus and periaqueductal gray. Give IV thiamine before glucose — glucose without thiamine can precipitate or worsen Wernicke encephalopathy. Other thiamine deficiency forms: dry beriberi (neuropathy) and wet beriberi (high-output heart failure).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vitamin B12 deficiency</b> — B12 deficiency gives neuropathy and megaloblastic anemia, not this acute syndrome with confabulation.</li><li><b>Lead poisoning</b> — Lead poisoning causes neuropathy and anemia, not this classic triad.</li><li><b>Hypoglycemia</b> — Hypoglycemia causes confusion but not weeks of nystagmus and confabulation.</li><li><b>Herpes simplex encephalitis</b> — HSV encephalitis presents with fever and temporal lobe signs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thiamine before glucose in alcoholics; mammillary body atrophy.</p>","w":["Correct — thiamine deficiency.","B12 deficiency gives neuropathy and megaloblastic anemia, not this acute syndrome with confabulation.","Lead poisoning causes neuropathy and anemia, not this classic triad.","Hypoglycemia causes confusion but not weeks of nystagmus and confabulation.","HSV encephalitis presents with fever and temporal lobe signs."]},"fb78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thiamine's active coenzyme is thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), needed for oxidative decarboxylation of α-keto acids and for transketolase.</p><p>TPP-dependent enzymes (mnemonic \"ATP\" — α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase, pyruvate dehydrogenase, plus branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase). Deficiency decreases ATP production and increases pyruvate and lactate. Diagnosis: increased RBC transketolase activity after adding TPP. Alcohol impairs thiamine absorption and storage.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>PLP</b> — PLP (vitamin B6) is used for transamination and decarboxylation reactions.</li><li><b>NAD+</b> — NAD⁺ (niacin) deficiency causes pellagra (dementia with dermatitis and diarrhea).</li><li><b>FAD+</b> — FAD (riboflavin) deficiency causes cheilosis, glossitis and corneal vascularization.</li><li><b>Biotin</b> — Biotin deficiency is rare and causes rash, alopecia and organic aciduria.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RBC transketolase activity assay = thiamine status.</p>","w":["PLP (vitamin B6) is used for transamination and decarboxylation reactions.","NAD⁺ (niacin) deficiency causes pellagra (dementia with dermatitis and diarrhea).","Correct — TPP.","FAD (riboflavin) deficiency causes cheilosis, glossitis and corneal vascularization.","Biotin deficiency is rare and causes rash, alopecia and organic aciduria."]},"fb79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pellagra = niacin (B3) deficiency: the \"3 Ds\" — dermatitis (photosensitive: face, neck/Casal necklace, hands), diarrhea, dementia (± death).</p><p>Niacin forms NAD⁺ and NADP⁺. Causes: corn-based diets (niacin bound, low tryptophan), alcoholism, Hartnup disease (tryptophan malabsorption), carcinoid syndrome (tryptophan diverted to serotonin), isoniazid (B6 deficiency impairs niacin synthesis from tryptophan). High-dose niacin used for dyslipidemia causes flushing (prostaglandin-mediated; reduced by aspirin), hyperglycemia and hyperuricemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cobalamin</b> — B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy, not this photosensitive rash.</li><li><b>Folic acid</b> — Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia.</li><li><b>Vitamin C</b> — Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy (bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage).</li><li><b>Vitamin E</b> — Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolysis and neuropathy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia; Casal necklace.</p>","w":["Correct — niacin deficiency.","B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy, not this photosensitive rash.","Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia.","Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy (bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage).","Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolysis and neuropathy."]},"fb80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vitamins are organic micronutrients needed in tiny amounts as coenzymes (B vitamins) or hormone-like regulators (A, D), mostly from the diet.</p><p>Water-soluble vitamins (B complex, C) generally aren't stored (except B12 and folate) and wash out in urine, so deficiencies develop quickly and toxicity is rare. Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are absorbed with dietary fat (impaired in fat malabsorption, CF, cholestasis), stored in liver and fat, and can be toxic in excess (A and D). Some are produced endogenously (vitamin D in skin, K and biotin by gut bacteria, niacin from tryptophan).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are all fat soluble</b> — Many vitamins are water soluble (B vitamins, vitamin C).</li><li><b>All of them can be synthesized by humans</b> — Most vitamins can't be synthesized by humans and must come from the diet.</li><li><b>Their deficiencies all produce identical symptoms</b> — Each vitamin deficiency causes specific symptoms.</li><li><b>Needed in large amounts as sources of energy</b> — Vitamins aren't energy sources and are needed in small amounts.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fat-soluble ADEK — malabsorption deficiency, A/D toxicity.</p>","w":["Many vitamins are water soluble (B vitamins, vitamin C).","Correct — needed in tiny amounts as coenzymes or hormones.","Most vitamins can't be synthesized by humans and must come from the diet.","Each vitamin deficiency causes specific symptoms.","Vitamins aren't energy sources and are needed in small amounts."]},"fb81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Water-soluble vitamins: B complex (B1, B2, B3, B5, B6, biotin, folate, B12) and C. Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K.</p><p>Water-soluble vitamins are absorbed directly, mostly not stored (except B12 for years and folate for months) and excreted in urine, so they need regular intake and rarely cause toxicity. Most act as coenzymes. Fat-soluble vitamins need bile and pancreatic lipase for absorption and are stored, so deficiency develops slowly (with malabsorption) and excess can be toxic.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vitamin K, niacin, pyridoxine, biotin</b> — Vitamin K is fat soluble.</li><li><b>A, D, E, K</b> — A, D, E and K are the fat-soluble vitamins.</li><li><b>Vitamin D, vitamin C, vitamin B12</b> — Vitamin D is fat soluble.</li><li><b>Vitamin A, biotin, folic acid, niacin</b> — Vitamin A is fat soluble.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Stored water-soluble vitamins: B12 (years), folate (months).</p>","w":["Vitamin K is fat soluble.","A, D, E and K are the fat-soluble vitamins.","Vitamin D is fat soluble.","Correct — all are water-soluble.","Vitamin A is fat soluble."]},"fb82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thiamine requirement rises with carbohydrate/caloric intake because TPP is needed for pyruvate dehydrogenase and other carbohydrate-metabolizing enzymes.</p><p>Thiamine stores are small (~2–3 weeks), so high-carbohydrate diets, refeeding after starvation, or IV glucose in deficient patients can rapidly precipitate deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy, wet beriberi, lactic acidosis). That's why thiamine is given before or with glucose in alcoholics and malnourished patients.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vitamin B12</b> — B12 requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake.</li><li><b>Vitamin C</b> — Vitamin C requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake.</li><li><b>Folic acid</b> — Folate needs increase with cell division (pregnancy), not carbohydrate intake.</li><li><b>Biotin</b> — Biotin requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Refeeding/IV glucose can precipitate Wernicke in thiamine-deficient patients.</p>","w":["Correct — thiamine.","B12 requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake.","Vitamin C requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake.","Folate needs increase with cell division (pregnancy), not carbohydrate intake.","Biotin requirement isn't tied to carbohydrate intake."]},"fb83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Erythrocyte transketolase activity (and its increase when TPP is added) assesses thiamine status.</p><p>Transketolase, a pentose phosphate pathway enzyme, needs TPP. In deficiency, baseline activity is low and rises markedly when TPP is added in vitro (the \"TPP effect\"). Other markers: methylmalonic acid (B12), homocysteine (B12, folate, B6), FIGLU in urine (folate), plasma ascorbate (vitamin C), erythrocyte glutathione reductase (riboflavin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Serum methylmalonic acid</b> — Methylmalonic acid rises in B12 deficiency.</li><li><b>Urinary FIGLU</b> — Urinary FIGLU is a marker of folate deficiency.</li><li><b>Plasma ascorbate</b> — Plasma ascorbate measures vitamin C.</li><li><b>Serum homocysteine only</b> — Homocysteine rises in B12, folate and B6 deficiency — not specific for thiamine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RBC glutathione reductase = riboflavin status.</p>","w":["Methylmalonic acid rises in B12 deficiency.","Urinary FIGLU is a marker of folate deficiency.","Plasma ascorbate measures vitamin C.","Homocysteine rises in B12, folate and B6 deficiency — not specific for thiamine.","Correct — erythrocyte transketolase."]},"fb84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dry beriberi = thiamine deficiency polyneuropathy: symmetric, distal, legs first (foot drop, wrist drop, areflexia), with sensory loss and muscle wasting.</p><p>Thiamine deficiency forms: dry beriberi (peripheral neuropathy), wet beriberi (high-output cardiac failure with edema), Wernicke–Korsakoff (CNS). Causes: polished rice (thiamine is in the removed husk), alcoholism, hyperemesis gravidarum, bariatric surgery, chronic diuretics, dialysis. Treatment: thiamine replacement.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Scurvy</b> — Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency with bleeding.</li><li><b>Wet beriberi</b> — Wet beriberi is cardiovascular (high-output failure, edema).</li><li><b>Pernicious anemia</b> — Pernicious anemia is B12 malabsorption causing megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration.</li><li><b>Pellagra</b> — Pellagra is niacin deficiency (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Polished rice → beriberi (thiamine in the husk).</p>","w":["Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency with bleeding.","Wet beriberi is cardiovascular (high-output failure, edema).","Correct — dry beriberi.","Pernicious anemia is B12 malabsorption causing megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration.","Pellagra is niacin deficiency (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia)."]},"fb85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Wet beriberi: thiamine deficiency → peripheral vasodilation and AV shunting → high-output heart failure with edema and a dilated heart.</p><p>Without TPP, tissues can't oxidize pyruvate efficiently, lactate rises, and local metabolites dilate vessels, lowering systemic vascular resistance. The heart must pump more (warm extremities, bounding pulse), and the energy-starved myocardium dilates and fails. Other high-output states: anemia, AV fistula, thyrotoxicosis, Paget disease, sepsis. Shoshin beriberi is a fulminant form with shock and lactic acidosis; it responds dramatically to IV thiamine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Concentric hypertrophy from chronic hypertension</b> — Chronic hypertension with concentric hypertrophy isn't the mechanism.</li><li><b>Low-output failure from aortic valve stenosis</b> — This is high-output failure, not low-output failure from valve stenosis.</li><li><b>Myocardial infarction from atherosclerosis</b> — The failure isn't ischemic.</li><li><b>Pericardial tamponade</b> — Pericardial tamponade isn't part of beriberi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> High-output failure causes: thiamine deficiency, anemia, AV fistula, hyperthyroidism, Paget.</p>","w":["Chronic hypertension with concentric hypertrophy isn't the mechanism.","Correct — vasodilation with AV shunting causes high-output failure.","This is high-output failure, not low-output failure from valve stenosis.","The failure isn't ischemic.","Pericardial tamponade isn't part of beriberi."]},"fb86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Riboflavin (B2) deficiency: angular cheilosis, magenta glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis (nasolabial folds), corneal vascularization/keratitis; coenzymes FMN and FAD.</p><p>FAD and FMN serve oxidoreductases: succinate dehydrogenase (complex II), acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, PDH and α-KGDH (E3), glutathione reductase and xanthine oxidase. Riboflavin is light-sensitive — phototherapy for neonatal jaundice can cause deficiency. Assessed by RBC glutathione reductase activity. Remember \"FAD and FMN come from riboFlavin; B2 = 2 ATP\" (FADH₂ yields ~1.5–2 ATP).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pyridoxine; PLP</b> — B6 deficiency can mimic this but adds neuropathy and convulsions; riboflavin is the classic match.</li><li><b>Vitamin C; ascorbate</b> — Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy with bleeding.</li><li><b>Niacin; NAD+ and NADP+</b> — Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).</li><li><b>Thiamine; TPP</b> — Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B2: cheilosis + magenta tongue + corneal vascularization.</p>","w":["B6 deficiency can mimic this but adds neuropathy and convulsions; riboflavin is the classic match.","Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy with bleeding.","Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).","Correct — riboflavin (FMN and FAD).","Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff."]},"fb87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> High-dose niacin lowers LDL by reducing hepatic VLDL synthesis (it inhibits lipolysis in adipose tissue, reducing free fatty acid flux to the liver); it also raises HDL the most of any drug.</p><p>Niacin activates a receptor (GPR109A) on adipocytes, reducing hormone-sensitive lipase activity and FFA release. The liver then makes less triglyceride and VLDL, so LDL (derived from VLDL) falls. Side effects: flushing (prostaglandin-mediated; aspirin helps), hepatotoxicity, hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia (gout). It's now rarely used because outcome trials showed little added benefit with statins.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>By increasing intestinal cholesterol absorption</b> — Increasing intestinal cholesterol absorption would raise, not lower, cholesterol.</li><li><b>By activating lipoprotein lipase in adipose tissue</b> — Its main mechanism is reduced VLDL synthesis, not activation of lipoprotein lipase.</li><li><b>By inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase directly</b> — Direct HMG-CoA reductase inhibition is how statins work.</li><li><b>By binding bile acids in the gut</b> — Binding bile acids in the gut is how bile acid sequestrants work.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Niacin: ↑HDL most; side effects flushing, hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia.</p>","w":["Correct — niacin reduces hepatic VLDL synthesis.","Increasing intestinal cholesterol absorption would raise, not lower, cholesterol.","Its main mechanism is reduced VLDL synthesis, not activation of lipoprotein lipase.","Direct HMG-CoA reductase inhibition is how statins work.","Binding bile acids in the gut is how bile acid sequestrants work."]},"fb88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Corn is low in tryptophan, and its niacin is bound (niacytin) and poorly available — so corn-based diets predispose to pellagra.</p><p>About 60 mg of tryptophan makes 1 mg niacin, so diets low in both cause deficiency. Traditional nixtamalization (soaking corn in alkaline lime water, as in Mesoamerican cooking) releases bound niacin and prevents pellagra. Other causes: alcoholism, Hartnup disease, carcinoid syndrome, isoniazid.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Corn destroys thiamine</b> — Polished rice, not corn, is linked to thiamine deficiency.</li><li><b>Corn contains avidin</b> — Avidin is in raw egg white and binds biotin.</li><li><b>Corn is rich in leucine, which activates niacin</b> — Leucine excess has been proposed in sorghum-based diets but isn't the explanation for corn.</li><li><b>Corn blocks vitamin C absorption</b> — Corn doesn't block vitamin C absorption.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pellagra: corn diets, Hartnup, carcinoid, isoniazid, alcoholism.</p>","w":["Polished rice, not corn, is linked to thiamine deficiency.","Avidin is in raw egg white and binds biotin.","Leucine excess has been proposed in sorghum-based diets but isn't the explanation for corn.","Corn doesn't block vitamin C absorption.","Correct — corn is low in tryptophan and available niacin."]},"fb89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pantothenic acid (B5) is needed to make coenzyme A and the 4′-phosphopantetheine arm of the acyl carrier protein (ACP) in fatty acid synthase.</p><p>CoA carries acyl groups (acetyl-CoA, succinyl-CoA, fatty acyl-CoA) via a thioester bond, and is required in the TCA cycle, fatty acid oxidation and synthesis, cholesterol and ketone synthesis, and acetylcholine synthesis. Deficiency is very rare (pantothenic = \"everywhere\"); experimental deficiency causes burning feet syndrome, dermatitis, enteritis and adrenal insufficiency.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tetrahydrofolate for one-carbon transfers</b> — Tetrahydrofolate comes from folate.</li><li><b>Pyridoxal phosphate</b> — Pyridoxal phosphate comes from vitamin B6.</li><li><b>NAD+</b> — NAD⁺ comes from niacin.</li><li><b>Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) for decarboxylases</b> — TPP comes from thiamine.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B5 = CoA; deficiency → burning feet syndrome.</p>","w":["Tetrahydrofolate comes from folate.","Pyridoxal phosphate comes from vitamin B6.","Correct — coenzyme A and ACP.","NAD⁺ comes from niacin.","TPP comes from thiamine."]},"fb91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Biotin is the cofactor for carboxylases (pyruvate, acetyl-CoA, propionyl-CoA, 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylases); multiple carboxylase deficiency responds to biotin.</p><p>Biotin carries activated CO₂ (carboxybiotin). Inherited holocarboxylase synthetase or biotinidase deficiency impairs all carboxylases, causing lactic acidosis, organic aciduria (e.g., 3-hydroxyisovalerate), hypotonia, seizures, alopecia and eczematous rash in infants. Newborn screening detects biotinidase deficiency; lifelong biotin supplementation prevents symptoms.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Riboflavin</b> — Riboflavin forms FAD/FMN for redox reactions, not carboxylations.</li><li><b>Pyridoxine</b> — Pyridoxine (PLP) is for transamination and decarboxylation.</li><li><b>Cyanocobalamin</b> — B12 is for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.</li><li><b>Ascorbic acid</b> — Vitamin C is for hydroxylation reactions (collagen).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Biotin = carboxylation (pyruvate → OAA; acetyl-CoA → malonyl-CoA; propionyl-CoA → methylmalonyl-CoA).</p>","w":["Correct — biotin.","Riboflavin forms FAD/FMN for redox reactions, not carboxylations.","Pyridoxine (PLP) is for transamination and decarboxylation.","B12 is for methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","Vitamin C is for hydroxylation reactions (collagen)."]},"fb92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> PLP (active B6) is the cofactor for amino acid decarboxylases that make neurotransmitters: glutamate → GABA, histidine → histamine, DOPA → dopamine, 5-HTP → serotonin.</p><p>PLP forms a Schiff base with the amino acid, stabilizing reaction intermediates. It's also needed for transamination (ALT, AST), ALA synthase (heme), cystathionine synthase and cystathionase, glycogen phosphorylase, and niacin synthesis from tryptophan. B6 deficiency reduces GABA, explaining seizures in infants; it can also cause sideroblastic anemia (impaired heme synthesis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>NAD+</b> — NAD⁺ is a cofactor for dehydrogenases.</li><li><b>TPP</b> — TPP decarboxylates α-keto acids, not amino acids.</li><li><b>FAD</b> — FAD is a redox cofactor.</li><li><b>FMN</b> — FMN is a redox cofactor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B6 deficiency: seizures, neuropathy, sideroblastic anemia.</p>","w":["NAD⁺ is a cofactor for dehydrogenases.","TPP decarboxylates α-keto acids, not amino acids.","FAD is a redox cofactor.","Correct — PLP.","FMN is a redox cofactor."]},"fb93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isoniazid antagonizes vitamin B6 → neuropathy, glossitis, seborrheic dermatitis (and seizures); prevent with pyridoxine supplementation.</p><p>Isoniazid forms a hydrazone with pyridoxal, inactivating it and increasing its excretion. Slow acetylators (lower NAT2 activity) are at higher risk of toxicity. Isoniazid overdose causes refractory seizures (reduced GABA), treated with IV pyridoxine. Other B6 antagonists: hydralazine, penicillamine, oral contraceptives (estrogen). Alcoholics are also prone to B6 deficiency.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vitamin C</b> — Vitamin C isn't affected by isoniazid.</li><li><b>Biotin</b> — Biotin isn't affected by isoniazid.</li><li><b>Thiamine</b> — Thiamine deficiency isn't caused by isoniazid.</li><li><b>Riboflavin</b> — Riboflavin isn't the vitamin antagonized by isoniazid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> INH: neuropathy (give B6), hepatotoxicity, slow acetylators; overdose → seizures (IV B6).</p>","w":["Vitamin C isn't affected by isoniazid.","Correct — supplement pyridoxine.","Biotin isn't affected by isoniazid.","Thiamine deficiency isn't caused by isoniazid.","Riboflavin isn't the vitamin antagonized by isoniazid."]},"fb94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pyridoxine, pyridoxal and pyridoxamine are all phosphorylated by pyridoxal kinase (using ATP) and converted to the active coenzyme pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP).</p><p>Activation happens mainly in the liver. B6 requirement rises with protein intake, since PLP is central to amino acid metabolism. Excess B6 (megadoses) is one of the few water-soluble vitamin toxicities, causing sensory neuropathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pyridoxine is converted to thiamine pyrophosphate in the brain</b> — Thiamine pyrophosphate comes from thiamine, not pyridoxine.</li><li><b>B6 is activated by hydroxylation using vitamin C</b> — Vitamin C doesn't activate B6.</li><li><b>B6 is activated by methylation with SAM</b> — SAM methylation isn't involved in B6 activation.</li><li><b>B6 is active without modification</b> — B6 must be converted to PLP to act as a coenzyme.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B6 toxicity (megadoses) → sensory neuropathy.</p>","w":["Thiamine pyrophosphate comes from thiamine, not pyridoxine.","Vitamin C doesn't activate B6.","SAM methylation isn't involved in B6 activation.","Correct — pyridoxal kinase (ATP) forms PLP.","B6 must be converted to PLP to act as a coenzyme."]},"fb95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ALA synthase (glycine + succinyl-CoA → δ-aminolevulinic acid), the rate-limiting step of heme synthesis, requires PLP.</p><p>B6 deficiency (e.g., isoniazid) reduces heme synthesis; iron accumulates in mitochondria, producing ring sideroblasts — sideroblastic anemia (microcytic). Congenital X-linked sideroblastic anemia (ALAS2 mutation) can respond to pyridoxine. Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase. Heme negatively regulates ALA synthase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Methionine synthase</b> — Methionine synthase uses B12 (and folate).</li><li><b>Acetyl-CoA carboxylase</b> — Acetyl-CoA carboxylase uses biotin.</li><li><b>Ferrochelatase</b> — Ferrochelatase inserts iron and doesn't use PLP.</li><li><b>Glycogen synthase</b> — Glycogen synthase doesn't use PLP; glycogen phosphorylase does.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> B6 deficiency → sideroblastic anemia (ring sideroblasts).</p>","w":["Correct — ALA synthase.","Methionine synthase uses B12 (and folate).","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase uses biotin.","Ferrochelatase inserts iron and doesn't use PLP.","Glycogen synthase doesn't use PLP; glycogen phosphorylase does."]},"fb96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Raw egg white contains avidin, which binds biotin tightly and prevents its absorption → biotin deficiency (dermatitis, alopecia, lethargy).</p><p>Cooking denatures avidin, so cooked eggs don't cause deficiency. Biotin deficiency also follows long-term antibiotics (killing biotin-producing gut bacteria), anticonvulsants, parenteral nutrition without biotin and biotinidase deficiency. Features: periorificial dermatitis, alopecia, conjunctivitis, lethargy, hypotonia and organic aciduria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Riboflavin, destroyed by egg yolk</b> — Eggs don't destroy riboflavin.</li><li><b>Vitamin C, because eggs are acidic</b> — This isn't scurvy, and eggs don't cause vitamin C deficiency.</li><li><b>Niacin, because eggs lack tryptophan</b> — Eggs contain tryptophan, so they don't cause niacin deficiency.</li><li><b>Thiamine, destroyed by egg thiaminase</b> — Eggs aren't the classic cause of thiamine deficiency.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Raw egg whites (avidin) → biotin deficiency.</p>","w":["Eggs don't destroy riboflavin.","This isn't scurvy, and eggs don't cause vitamin C deficiency.","Eggs contain tryptophan, so they don't cause niacin deficiency.","Eggs aren't the classic cause of thiamine deficiency.","Correct — biotin bound by egg-white avidin."]},"fb97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Humans lack L-gulonolactone oxidase, the last enzyme in ascorbic acid synthesis, so vitamin C must come from the diet.</p><p>Most mammals make vitamin C from glucose via the uronic acid pathway. Humans, other primates, guinea pigs and fruit bats lost the functional gene. Vitamin C is a cofactor for hydroxylases (prolyl/lysyl hydroxylase, dopamine β-hydroxylase), enhances non-heme iron absorption (keeps iron Fe²⁺), and is a water-soluble antioxidant.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They lack dihydrofolate reductase</b> — Dihydrofolate reductase reduces folate.</li><li><b>They lack pyridoxal kinase</b> — Pyridoxal kinase activates vitamin B6.</li><li><b>They lack transketolase</b> — Transketolase uses TPP and isn't related to vitamin C synthesis.</li><li><b>They lack intrinsic factor</b> — Intrinsic factor is needed to absorb B12.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vitamin C: collagen hydroxylation, iron absorption, dopamine → norepinephrine.</p>","w":["Dihydrofolate reductase reduces folate.","Pyridoxal kinase activates vitamin B6.","Correct — humans lack L-gulonolactone oxidase.","Transketolase uses TPP and isn't related to vitamin C synthesis.","Intrinsic factor is needed to absorb B12."]},"fb98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vitamin C keeps the iron in prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases reduced (Fe²⁺), enabling hydroxylation of proline and lysine in procollagen.</p><p>Hydroxyproline stabilizes the collagen triple helix through hydrogen bonds; hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and cross-linking. Without vitamin C, unstable collagen is degraded, causing scurvy: swollen bleeding gums, loose teeth, perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing, easy bruising, and anemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cleavage of the procollagen terminal peptides</b> — Procollagen terminal peptides are cleaved by peptidases.</li><li><b>Glycosylation of hydroxylysine</b> — Glycosylation of hydroxylysine doesn't require vitamin C.</li><li><b>Cross-linking by lysyl oxidase using copper</b> — Lysyl oxidase cross-linking requires copper, not vitamin C.</li><li><b>Triple helix formation by disulfide bonds</b> — The triple helix is stabilized by hydrogen bonds; vitamin C acts at hydroxylation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scurvy: corkscrew hairs, perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums.</p>","w":["Procollagen terminal peptides are cleaved by peptidases.","Correct — hydroxylation of proline and lysine.","Glycosylation of hydroxylysine doesn't require vitamin C.","Lysyl oxidase cross-linking requires copper, not vitamin C.","The triple helix is stabilized by hydrogen bonds; vitamin C acts at hydroxylation."]},"fb99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Infantile scurvy: defective collagen weakens vessel walls and loosens periosteal attachment → subperiosteal hemorrhages, bone pain and pseudoparalysis.</p><p>Boiled or heated milk loses vitamin C. Infants and toddlers develop irritability, painful limbs (refusal to move legs — \"frog-leg\" position), swollen limbs from subperiosteal hematomas, and gum bleeding once teeth erupt. X-ray signs: ground-glass bone, Wimberger ring around epiphyses, Frankel line, and scorbutic rosary at costochondral junctions. Bleeding studies (platelets, PT/PTT) are normal because the defect is vascular, not hematologic.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Excess osteoclast activity driven by high PTH levels</b> — Excess PTH-driven osteoclast activity isn't the mechanism.</li><li><b>Platelet deficiency</b> — Platelet counts are normal in scurvy.</li><li><b>Clotting factor deficiency</b> — Clotting factors are normal in scurvy.</li><li><b>Infection of the bone</b> — The bone findings aren't due to infection.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Scurvy bleeding = vascular fragility; coagulation tests normal.</p>","w":["Excess PTH-driven osteoclast activity isn't the mechanism.","Platelet counts are normal in scurvy.","Clotting factors are normal in scurvy.","Correct — loose periosteum and fragile vessels from weak collagen.","The bone findings aren't due to infection."]},"fb100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> B12 deficiency traps folate as N⁵-methyl-THF (folate trap), because methionine synthase can't remove its methyl group to regenerate THF.</p><p>N⁵-methyl-THF is formed irreversibly by MTHFR and can only be demethylated by methionine synthase (B12-dependent), which transfers the methyl to homocysteine (making methionine). Without B12, folate accumulates as N⁵-methyl-THF and other folate forms needed for thymidylate (dTMP) and purine synthesis run low → impaired DNA synthesis → megaloblastic anemia. Giving folate alone can correct the anemia but not the neurologic damage of B12 deficiency — so B12 deficiency must be excluded.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dihydrofolate.</b> — Dihydrofolate is reduced by DHF reductase and isn't the trapped form.</li><li><b>Folic acid.</b> — Folic acid is the dietary/supplement form, not the trapped form.</li><li><b>Formyl tetrahydrofolate</b> — Formyl-THF is used in purine synthesis and isn't what accumulates.</li><li><b>Methenyl tetrahydrofolate</b> — Methenyl-THF isn't the trapped form.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Folate alone masks B12 deficiency anemia but neuropathy progresses.</p>","w":["Dihydrofolate is reduced by DHF reductase and isn't the trapped form.","Folic acid is the dietary/supplement form, not the trapped form.","Formyl-THF is used in purine synthesis and isn't what accumulates.","Methenyl-THF isn't the trapped form.","Correct — N⁵-methyl-tetrahydrofolate."]},"fb101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> B12 is found only in animal products (made by microorganisms); strict vegans/vegetarians risk deficiency → megaloblastic anemia + neurologic signs.</p><p>B12 is needed for methionine synthase (homocysteine → methionine) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (methylmalonyl-CoA → succinyl-CoA). Deficiency raises both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid. Neurologic features: peripheral neuropathy, subacute combined degeneration of dorsal columns (vibration/proprioception loss, ataxia) and lateral corticospinal tracts, and dementia. Liver stores last several years, so dietary deficiency takes years to appear. Other causes: pernicious anemia, gastrectomy, ileal resection/Crohn disease, metformin, PPIs, Diphyllobothrium latum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Iron</b> — Iron deficiency gives microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia.</li><li><b>Vitamin B1</b> — Thiamine deficiency causes neuropathy but not macrocytic anemia.</li><li><b>Copper</b> — Copper deficiency isn't explained by a vegetarian diet and macrocytosis here.</li><li><b>Folic acid</b> — Folate deficiency causes macrocytic anemia without neurologic findings.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑MMA distinguishes B12 from folate deficiency.</p>","w":["Iron deficiency gives microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia.","Thiamine deficiency causes neuropathy but not macrocytic anemia.","Copper deficiency isn't explained by a vegetarian diet and macrocytosis here.","Folate deficiency causes macrocytic anemia without neurologic findings.","Correct — vitamin B12 deficiency."]},"fb102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Folic acid = pteridine + PABA + glutamate(s); active coenzyme = tetrahydrofolate (THF), formed by dihydrofolate reductase.</p><p>Dietary polyglutamates are cleaved to monoglutamates by intestinal conjugase and absorbed in the jejunum. DHFR reduces folate → DHF → THF, which then carries one-carbon units at N5 and/or N10. Bacteria synthesize folate from PABA — sulfonamides block dihydropteroate synthase (bacterial step), and trimethoprim inhibits bacterial DHFR. Humans can't synthesize folate, so it's a dietary requirement (leafy greens; \"foliage\").</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pyrimidine + thiazole; TPP</b> — Pyrimidine + thiazole forming TPP describes thiamine.</li><li><b>Nicotinamide + adenine; NAD+</b> — Nicotinamide + adenine forming NAD⁺ describes niacin.</li><li><b>Isoalloxazine ring + ribitol; FAD</b> — Isoalloxazine ring + ribitol forming FAD describes riboflavin.</li><li><b>Corrin ring + cobalt; methylcobalamin</b> — A corrin ring with cobalt forming methylcobalamin describes B12.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sulfonamides block bacterial folate synthesis; trimethoprim and methotrexate block DHFR.</p>","w":["Correct — pteridine + PABA + glutamate; THF.","Pyrimidine + thiazole forming TPP describes thiamine.","Nicotinamide + adenine forming NAD⁺ describes niacin.","Isoalloxazine ring + ribitol forming FAD describes riboflavin.","A corrin ring with cobalt forming methylcobalamin describes B12."]},"fb103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> THF carries one-carbon units (methyl, methylene, formyl) for purine synthesis, dTMP synthesis (dUMP → dTMP) and amino acid metabolism.</p><p>Serine donates a one-carbon unit to THF (forming N⁵,N¹⁰-methylene-THF and glycine). Thymidylate synthase uses N⁵,N¹⁰-methylene-THF to convert dUMP → dTMP, oxidizing THF to DHF (which DHFR recycles). Purine synthesis uses N¹⁰-formyl-THF for carbons 2 and 8. N⁵-methyl-THF donates its methyl (via B12) to homocysteine. Without folate, dTMP falls and uracil is misincorporated into DNA, causing megaloblastic changes. 5-FU inhibits thymidylate synthase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Synthesis of glycogen</b> — Glycogen synthesis doesn't use THF.</li><li><b>Synthesis of fatty acids</b> — Fatty acid synthesis uses acetyl-CoA, NADPH and biotin, not THF.</li><li><b>Synthesis of collagen</b> — Collagen synthesis needs vitamin C, not THF.</li><li><b>Synthesis of cholesterol</b> — Cholesterol synthesis doesn't use THF.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 5-FU → thymidylate synthase; methotrexate → DHFR.</p>","w":["Glycogen synthesis doesn't use THF.","Fatty acid synthesis uses acetyl-CoA, NADPH and biotin, not THF.","Collagen synthesis needs vitamin C, not THF.","Cholesterol synthesis doesn't use THF.","Correct — purines, dTMP and some amino acids."]},"fb104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Methotrexate competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase → no THF → impaired purine and dTMP synthesis → megaloblastic anemia.</p><p>Rapidly dividing cells (cancer, activated immune cells, marrow, mucosa) depend on DNA synthesis, which explains both therapeutic use (cancers, RA, psoriasis, ectopic pregnancy) and toxicity (myelosuppression, mucositis, megaloblastic anemia, hepatotoxicity, pulmonary fibrosis). Leucovorin (folinic acid) bypasses DHFR to rescue normal cells. Low-dose methotrexate patients take folic acid to reduce side effects.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It inactivates vitamin C</b> — Methotrexate doesn't affect vitamin C.</li><li><b>It inhibits methylmalonyl-CoA mutase</b> — Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs B12 and isn't inhibited by methotrexate.</li><li><b>It binds intrinsic factor</b> — Methotrexate doesn't bind intrinsic factor.</li><li><b>It increases folate excretion in urine</b> — Its main action is enzyme inhibition, not increased folate excretion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Leucovorin rescue bypasses DHFR.</p>","w":["Methotrexate doesn't affect vitamin C.","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs B12 and isn't inhibited by methotrexate.","Correct — methotrexate inhibits DHFR.","Methotrexate doesn't bind intrinsic factor.","Its main action is enzyme inhibition, not increased folate excretion."]},"fb105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Increased folate requirement: pregnancy, infancy, hemolytic anemia (rapid erythropoiesis), and widespread cancer.</p><p>Folate stores last only ~3–4 months, so deficiency develops quickly when demand rises. Classifying causes: decreased intake (poor diet — \"tea and toast\" elderly, alcoholism, malabsorption such as celiac disease), increased need (pregnancy, chronic hemolysis like sickle cell disease, psoriasis), impaired use (methotrexate, trimethoprim, phenytoin). Sickle cell patients and pregnant women receive folate supplements.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Strict vegetarian diet</b> — A strict vegetarian diet is a classic cause of B12, not folate, deficiency (vegetables are rich in folate).</li><li><b>Inadequate diet</b> — An inadequate diet is a cause of decreased intake.</li><li><b>Methotrexate therapy</b> — Methotrexate therapy is impaired use.</li><li><b>Intestinal malabsorption</b> — Intestinal malabsorption is decreased absorption.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Folate stores ~3–4 months; B12 stores ~3–5 years.</p>","w":["A strict vegetarian diet is a classic cause of B12, not folate, deficiency (vegetables are rich in folate).","Correct — pregnancy or hemolytic anemia increases requirement.","An inadequate diet is a cause of decreased intake.","Methotrexate therapy is impaired use.","Intestinal malabsorption is decreased absorption."]},"fb106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Megaloblastic anemia: macro-ovalocytes, hypersegmented neutrophils (≥5 lobes), pancytopenia, low reticulocyte count.</p><p>Impaired DNA synthesis delays nuclear maturation while cytoplasm continues to mature (nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony), producing large megaloblastic precursors in the marrow. Many die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis), raising LDH and indirect bilirubin. MCV is usually &gt;100 fL. Non-megaloblastic macrocytosis (alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, reticulocytosis) lacks hypersegmented neutrophils.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Normal-sized red cells with many schistocytes</b> — Normal-sized cells with schistocytes indicate mechanical (microangiopathic) hemolysis.</li><li><b>Small, pale red cells with target cells</b> — Small, pale red cells with target cells indicate microcytic anemia (e.g., iron deficiency or thalassemia).</li><li><b>Spherocytes and high reticulocyte count</b> — Spherocytes with high reticulocytes indicate hereditary spherocytosis or autoimmune hemolysis.</li><li><b>Sickle cells and Howell-Jolly bodies in red cells</b> — Sickle cells and Howell–Jolly bodies indicate sickle cell disease with autosplenectomy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypersegmented neutrophils = megaloblastic (B12 or folate).</p>","w":["Normal-sized cells with schistocytes indicate mechanical (microangiopathic) hemolysis.","Small, pale red cells with target cells indicate microcytic anemia (e.g., iron deficiency or thalassemia).","Spherocytes with high reticulocytes indicate hereditary spherocytosis or autoimmune hemolysis.","Correct — macro-ovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils.","Sickle cells and Howell–Jolly bodies indicate sickle cell disease with autosplenectomy."]},"fb107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Folate corrects the anemia of B12 deficiency but not the neurologic damage, which continues to progress — always exclude B12 deficiency first.</p><p>Extra folate can bypass the folate trap enough to restore DNA synthesis and correct blood counts, masking B12 deficiency. Meanwhile, methylmalonic acid accumulation and myelin damage continue, potentially causing irreversible subacute combined degeneration. Check B12 (and MMA) before treating megaloblastic anemia with folate alone; if unsure, give both.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>She needed vitamin C instead</b> — Vitamin C isn't the missing treatment.</li><li><b>Folic acid causes neuropathy directly</b> — Folic acid doesn't directly cause neuropathy.</li><li><b>Folic acid was given at too low a daily dose</b> — The dose isn't the problem; the missed B12 deficiency is.</li><li><b>Iron should have been given instead</b> — Iron doesn't treat megaloblastic anemia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Subacute combined degeneration: dorsal columns + lateral corticospinal tracts.</p>","w":["Correct — coexisting B12 deficiency wasn't excluded.","Vitamin C isn't the missing treatment.","Folic acid doesn't directly cause neuropathy.","The dose isn't the problem; the missed B12 deficiency is.","Iron doesn't treat megaloblastic anemia."]},"fb108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Periconceptional folic acid (0.4 mg/day; 4 mg/day for high-risk women) reduces neural tube defects.</p><p>The neural tube closes by day ~28 after conception, often before a woman knows she's pregnant, so supplementation should start before conception. NTDs: anencephaly, spina bifida (occulta, meningocele, myelomeningocele). Risk factors: prior affected pregnancy, maternal diabetes, obesity, valproate or carbamazepine use. Screening: elevated maternal serum AFP and amniotic AFP/acetylcholinesterase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Syndactyly</b> — Syndactyly results from failed interdigital apoptosis.</li><li><b>Congenital heart block</b> — Congenital heart block is linked to maternal anti-Ro antibodies (SLE), not folate.</li><li><b>Hypospadias</b> — Hypospadias isn't the defect linked to folate on the slides.</li><li><b>Cleft palate</b> — Cleft palate isn't the defect the slides link to folate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> NTD prevention: folic acid before conception; ↑AFP in open defects.</p>","w":["Syndactyly results from failed interdigital apoptosis.","Congenital heart block is linked to maternal anti-Ro antibodies (SLE), not folate.","Hypospadias isn't the defect linked to folate on the slides.","Cleft palate isn't the defect the slides link to folate.","Correct — neural tube defects."]},"fb109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> B12 is freed from food by stomach acid/proteases, binds intrinsic factor (from gastric parietal cells), is absorbed in the terminal ileum, and travels on transcobalamin II.</p><p>Steps: stomach acid and pepsin release B12 from food → binds haptocorrin (R-binder) from saliva → pancreatic proteases free it in the duodenum → binds intrinsic factor → IF–B12 complex absorbed by cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum → transcobalamin II carries it to tissues. Large liver stores last 3–5 years. Deficiency causes at each step: achlorhydria/PPIs, pernicious anemia/gastrectomy, pancreatic insufficiency, ileal resection/Crohn disease, bacterial overgrowth, fish tapeworm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Absorbed in the duodenum without any binding protein</b> — Absorption requires intrinsic factor and occurs in the ileum, not the duodenum.</li><li><b>Carried in blood bound only to albumin</b> — B12's main transport protein is transcobalamin II, not albumin.</li><li><b>Made by plants and absorbed along with fiber</b> — B12 is made only by microorganisms; plants don't contain it.</li><li><b>Binds chief-cell intrinsic factor; absorbed in the jejunum</b> — Intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells, and absorption is ileal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pancreatic insufficiency → B12 malabsorption (can't release from R-binder).</p>","w":["Absorption requires intrinsic factor and occurs in the ileum, not the duodenum.","B12's main transport protein is transcobalamin II, not albumin.","Correct — binds parietal-cell intrinsic factor and is absorbed in the ileum.","B12 is made only by microorganisms; plants don't contain it.","Intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells, and absorption is ileal."]},"fb110":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> B12 is required for only two human enzymes: methionine synthase (methylcobalamin) and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (adenosylcobalamin).</p><p>Methionine synthase: homocysteine + N⁵-methyl-THF → methionine + THF (links B12 and folate). Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase: methylmalonyl-CoA → succinyl-CoA, in catabolism of odd-chain fatty acids, valine, isoleucine, methionine and threonine. Deficiency: ↑homocysteine and ↑MMA. Folate deficiency raises only homocysteine.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glutamate decarboxylase and histidine decarboxylase</b> — Glutamate and histidine decarboxylases require PLP (B6).</li><li><b>Prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase</b> — Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases require vitamin C.</li><li><b>Pyruvate DH and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase</b> — PDH and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase require TPP (thiamine).</li><li><b>Pyruvate carboxylase and acetyl-CoA carboxylase</b> — Pyruvate and acetyl-CoA carboxylases require biotin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Methylmalonic acidemia: mutase or B12 metabolism defect → metabolic acidosis in infants.</p>","w":["Glutamate and histidine decarboxylases require PLP (B6).","Correct — methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases require vitamin C.","PDH and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase require TPP (thiamine).","Pyruvate and acetyl-CoA carboxylases require biotin."]},"fb111":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In B12 deficiency, methylmalonyl-CoA accumulates and abnormal (odd-chain/branched) fatty acids are incorporated into myelin, contributing to demyelination.</p><p>Without adenosylcobalamin, methylmalonyl-CoA and propionyl-CoA build up. Propionyl-CoA can substitute for acetyl-CoA in fatty acid synthesis, producing odd-chain fatty acids, and methylmalonyl-CoA forms branched fatty acids; these are incorporated into neuronal membranes, destabilizing myelin. Reduced SAM (from impaired methionine synthase) also impairs myelin methylation. Result: subacute combined degeneration, peripheral neuropathy and cognitive changes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It causes severe hypoglycemia within neurons</b> — Severe neuronal hypoglycemia isn't the proposed mechanism.</li><li><b>It prevents iron uptake by neurons in the cord</b> — Impaired iron uptake by neurons isn't the mechanism.</li><li><b>It blocks activation of thiamine to TPP</b> — Thiamine activation to TPP isn't affected.</li><li><b>Methylmalonic acid directly blocks acetylcholine receptors</b> — Methylmalonic acid doesn't act at acetylcholine receptors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑MMA = B12-specific; drives neurologic damage.</p>","w":["Severe neuronal hypoglycemia isn't the proposed mechanism.","Impaired iron uptake by neurons isn't the mechanism.","Thiamine activation to TPP isn't affected.","Correct — abnormal fatty acids are built into myelin lipids.","Methylmalonic acid doesn't act at acetylcholine receptors."]},"fb112":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pernicious anemia = autoimmune gastritis destroying parietal cells → no intrinsic factor → B12 malabsorption.</p><p>Autoantibodies target parietal cells (H⁺/K⁺-ATPase) and intrinsic factor (more specific). Atrophic gastritis of the body/fundus causes achlorhydria, elevated gastrin and increased risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and carcinoid tumors. Features: megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, neuropathy; associated with other autoimmune diseases (thyroid disease, vitiligo, type 1 diabetes). Treatment: lifelong IM B12 (or high-dose oral B12, which is partly absorbed by passive diffusion).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased requirement from pregnancy</b> — Pregnancy increases requirement, but that isn't her mechanism.</li><li><b>Dietary lack of B12 from a vegan diet</b> — Her diet isn't the issue; the antibodies point to autoimmunity.</li><li><b>Bacterial overgrowth in a blind loop</b> — Blind-loop bacterial overgrowth is another cause, but it doesn't produce anti-parietal antibodies.</li><li><b>Fish tapeworm competing for B12</b> — The fish tapeworm competes for B12 but doesn't cause anti-parietal antibodies.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pernicious anemia: anti-IF antibodies, ↑gastrin, ↑gastric cancer risk.</p>","w":["Correct — lack of intrinsic factor.","Pregnancy increases requirement, but that isn't her mechanism.","Her diet isn't the issue; the antibodies point to autoimmunity.","Blind-loop bacterial overgrowth is another cause, but it doesn't produce anti-parietal antibodies.","The fish tapeworm competes for B12 but doesn't cause anti-parietal antibodies."]},"fc1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cell theory tenet 3 — all cells arise from pre-existing cells (Virchow: \"omnis cellula e cellula\") — refutes spontaneous generation.</p><p>Classic cell theory: (1) all living organisms are made of one or more cells; (2) the cell is the basic (smallest) unit of life; (3) all cells come from pre-existing cells by division. The modern (expanded) theory adds that cells contain hereditary information (DNA) passed on during division, that all cells have basically the same chemical composition, and that energy flow (metabolism) occurs within cells. Pasteur's swan-neck flask experiments disproved spontaneous generation experimentally.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cells contain DNA in their chromosomes</b> — DNA in chromosomes is part of the expanded cell theory, but it doesn't address where new cells come from.</li><li><b>The cell is the smallest unit of life</b> — The cell being the smallest unit of life is true but doesn't address cell origin.</li><li><b>Energy flow occurs within cells</b> — Energy flow within cells is part of the expanded theory, not about cell origin.</li><li><b>All living organisms are made up of one or more cells</b> — Organisms being made of cells is true but concerns composition, not origin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Schleiden (plants) + Schwann (animals) → cells; Virchow → cells from cells.</p>","w":["DNA in chromosomes is part of the expanded cell theory, but it doesn't address where new cells come from.","The cell being the smallest unit of life is true but doesn't address cell origin.","Energy flow within cells is part of the expanded theory, not about cell origin.","Correct — new cells come only from division of existing cells.","Organisms being made of cells is true but concerns composition, not origin."]},"fc2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Prokaryote features: small (0.1–5 µm), nucleoid (no nuclear membrane), no membrane-bound organelles, 70S ribosomes, binary fission — and usually a peptidoglycan cell wall.</p><p>Bacteria have a rigid cell wall containing peptidoglycan (thick in gram-positive, thin with an outer membrane in gram-negative). Many antibiotics exploit prokaryotic differences: β-lactams and vancomycin target peptidoglycan synthesis; aminoglycosides, tetracyclines, macrolides target 70S ribosomes. Eukaryotes (10–100 µm) have a membrane-bound nucleus, organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi), 80S ribosomes, and divide by mitosis/meiosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Division by mitosis</b> — Prokaryotes divide by binary fission, not mitosis.</li><li><b>A Golgi apparatus for protein secretion</b> — Prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles such as the Golgi.</li><li><b>A nuclear envelope with pores</b> — A nuclear envelope is eukaryotic; this organism's DNA is in a nucleoid.</li><li><b>80S ribosomes on rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> — 80S ribosomes and RER are eukaryotic features.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 70S (30S + 50S) = bacteria; 80S (40S + 60S) = eukaryotes; mitochondria have 70S-like ribosomes.</p>","w":["Correct — bacteria typically have a peptidoglycan cell wall.","Prokaryotes divide by binary fission, not mitosis.","Prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles such as the Golgi.","A nuclear envelope is eukaryotic; this organism's DNA is in a nucleoid.","80S ribosomes and RER are eukaryotic features."]},"fc3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Double-membrane organelles: nucleus and mitochondria (also chloroplasts in plants).</p><p>Mitochondria have an outer membrane (porins, permeable to small molecules) and an inner membrane folded into cristae (impermeable, containing the electron transport chain and ATP synthase). Their double membrane reflects endosymbiotic origin from bacteria. The nuclear envelope is two membranes with a perinuclear space, perforated by nuclear pores; the outer membrane is continuous with the RER. Single-membrane organelles: ER, Golgi, lysosomes, peroxisomes, endosomes. No membrane: ribosomes, centrioles, cytoskeleton, nucleolus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroxisome</b> — Peroxisomes have a single membrane.</li><li><b>Ribosome</b> — Ribosomes aren't membrane-bound at all.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — The Golgi is made of single-membrane cisternae.</li><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomes have a single membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mitochondria: own circular DNA, 70S-like ribosomes, maternal inheritance — endosymbiont theory.</p>","w":["Peroxisomes have a single membrane.","Ribosomes aren't membrane-bound at all.","The Golgi is made of single-membrane cisternae.","Lysosomes have a single membrane.","Correct — mitochondria have outer and inner membranes."]},"fc4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The nuclear lamina (lamins A, B, C — type V intermediate filaments) reinforces the inner nuclear membrane and anchors chromatin.</p><p>Lamins form a meshwork under the inner nuclear membrane, giving the nucleus shape and mechanical strength, organizing heterochromatin at the periphery and regulating gene expression and DNA replication. During mitosis, lamin phosphorylation (by CDK1) disassembles the nuclear envelope; dephosphorylation reassembles it. Hutchinson–Gilford progeria is caused by an LMNA mutation producing progerin, an abnormal lamin A that distorts nuclear shape (blebbing), causing premature aging: alopecia, thin skin, short stature, atherosclerosis and early death from cardiovascular disease. LMNA defects also cause some muscular dystrophies (Emery–Dreifuss) and cardiomyopathy (laminopathies).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Synthesizes ribosomal RNA</b> — rRNA is synthesized in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>Studs the outer nuclear membrane with ribosomes</b> — The outer nuclear membrane carries ribosomes and is continuous with the RER — not the lamina's role.</li><li><b>Forms pores that allow bidirectional transport across the nuclear envelope</b> — Nuclear pores, not the lamina, provide transport between nucleus and cytoplasm.</li><li><b>Separates sister chromatids during anaphase</b> — Sister chromatid separation depends on the mitotic spindle (microtubules) and separase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Progeria = LMNA (lamin A) mutation.</p>","w":["rRNA is synthesized in the nucleolus.","The outer nuclear membrane carries ribosomes and is continuous with the RER — not the lamina's role.","Correct — the nuclear lamina reinforces the inner membrane and links it to chromatin.","Nuclear pores, not the lamina, provide transport between nucleus and cytoplasm.","Sister chromatid separation depends on the mitotic spindle (microtubules) and separase."]},"fc5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Nucleolus zones: fibrillar center (rDNA, inactive), dense fibrillar component (rRNA transcription and processing), granular component at the periphery (ribosome subunit assembly).</p><p>The nucleolus forms around nucleolar organizer regions (rDNA on the short arms of acrocentric chromosomes 13, 14, 15, 21, 22). RNA polymerase I transcribes 45S pre-rRNA, which is processed into 28S, 18S and 5.8S rRNA; 5S rRNA comes from RNA polymerase III outside the nucleolus. Ribosomal proteins (made in the cytoplasm) are imported and assembled with rRNA in the granular zone into 40S and 60S subunits, which are exported through nuclear pores. Cells making lots of protein (growing cells, cancer cells, plasma cells) have large, prominent nucleoli.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Translation of mRNA into protein</b> — Translation occurs on ribosomes in the cytoplasm, not in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>DNA replication</b> — DNA replication occurs throughout the nucleus during S phase, not specifically in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>Splicing of mRNA</b> — mRNA splicing occurs in the nucleoplasm (spliceosomes), not the granular zone.</li><li><b>Transcription of rRNA</b> — rRNA transcription occurs in the fibrillar zones toward the center.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RNA Pol I → rRNA (nucleolus); Pol II → mRNA; Pol III → tRNA, 5S rRNA.</p>","w":["Translation occurs on ribosomes in the cytoplasm, not in the nucleolus.","Correct — the peripheral granular zone assembles ribosomal subunits.","DNA replication occurs throughout the nucleus during S phase, not specifically in the nucleolus.","mRNA splicing occurs in the nucleoplasm (spliceosomes), not the granular zone.","rRNA transcription occurs in the fibrillar zones toward the center."]},"fc6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cytosol = the fluid portion of the cytoplasm; cytoplasm = cytosol + organelles + cytoskeleton + inclusions (everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus).</p><p>The cytosol is a crowded aqueous gel containing ions, metabolites, enzymes (glycolysis, fatty acid synthesis, pentose phosphate pathway, protein synthesis on free ribosomes), and proteasomes. Inclusions are non-membrane-bound stored material such as glycogen granules, lipid droplets and pigments (lipofuscin, melanin). Nucleoplasm is the equivalent fluid inside the nucleus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The interior of the nucleus where chromosomes are found</b> — The interior of the nucleus is the nucleoplasm.</li><li><b>The lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum</b> — The ER lumen is a separate compartment enclosed by ER membrane.</li><li><b>The space between the inner and outer mitochondrial membranes</b> — The space between the mitochondrial membranes is the intermembrane space.</li><li><b>All the contents of the cell inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus</b> — Everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus defines the cytoplasm, not the cytosol.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytosolic pathways: glycolysis, fatty acid synthesis, HMP shunt, protein synthesis (free ribosomes).</p>","w":["The interior of the nucleus is the nucleoplasm.","The ER lumen is a separate compartment enclosed by ER membrane.","The space between the mitochondrial membranes is the intermembrane space.","Correct — cytosol is the fluid part of the cytoplasm.","Everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus defines the cytoplasm, not the cytosol."]},"fc7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> RER = interconnected cisternae whose cytoplasmic surface is studded with ribosomes that translate secretory, membrane and lysosomal proteins into the lumen.</p><p>Translation begins on free ribosomes; an N-terminal signal sequence is recognized by the signal recognition particle (SRP), which pauses translation and docks the ribosome on the SRP receptor in the ER membrane. Translation resumes through the translocon (Sec61) into the ER lumen, where the signal peptide is cleaved, N-linked glycosylation begins, disulfide bonds form and chaperones aid folding. Misfolded proteins are retrotranslocated and degraded by proteasomes (ERAD). Plasma cells, pancreatic acinar cells and fibroblasts have abundant RER (basophilic cytoplasm).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Contains proteins that recognize signal peptides on translating proteins</b> — Signal peptides are recognized by SRP in the cytosol; the receptor docks it, but this doesn't define the RER.</li><li><b>Continuous with the Golgi apparatus</b> — The ER isn't continuous with the Golgi; proteins travel in COPII vesicles via the ERGIC.</li><li><b>Made up of noncontinuous cisternae</b> — ER cisternae and tubules are interconnected and share one continuous lumen.</li><li><b>Main function is to detoxify and produce steroid hormones</b> — Detoxification and steroid production are functions of the smooth ER.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nissl bodies in neurons = RER; basophilia = RER-rich cells (plasma cells).</p>","w":["Signal peptides are recognized by SRP in the cytosol; the receptor docks it, but this doesn't define the RER.","The ER isn't continuous with the Golgi; proteins travel in COPII vesicles via the ERGIC.","Correct — membrane-bound ribosomes on its cytoplasmic surface make the RER \"rough.\"","ER cisternae and tubules are interconnected and share one continuous lumen.","Detoxification and steroid production are functions of the smooth ER."]},"fc8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Default pathway: proteins entering the Golgi with no sorting or retention signal are secreted (if soluble) or delivered to the plasma membrane (if membrane-bound).</p><p>Sorting at the trans-Golgi network depends on tags: mannose-6-phosphate → lysosomes; KDEL sequence → returned to the ER; transmembrane retention signals → stay in the Golgi; specific signals and aggregation → regulated secretory granules. Untagged soluble proteins leave in constitutive secretory vesicles and are released when the vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane. This is why secretion is called the \"default\" or \"bulk flow\" pathway.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cytosolic protein</b> — Once a protein has entered the ER/Golgi pathway, it can't return to the cytosol.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus protein</b> — Golgi-resident proteins need a retention signal to stay.</li><li><b>Lysosomal protein</b> — Lysosomal proteins need the mannose-6-phosphate tag.</li><li><b>Membrane protein</b> — A membrane protein would be embedded in the vesicle membrane; this protein is free-floating in the lumen.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> M6P → lysosome; KDEL → ER; no tag → secretion.</p>","w":["Once a protein has entered the ER/Golgi pathway, it can't return to the cytosol.","Golgi-resident proteins need a retention signal to stay.","Lysosomal proteins need the mannose-6-phosphate tag.","A membrane protein would be embedded in the vesicle membrane; this protein is free-floating in the lumen.","Correct — no tag plus soluble means the default pathway, secretion."]},"fc9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proteins move through the Golgi from cis (entry, facing ER) → medial stack (modification) → trans (exit, sorting).</p><p>COPII vesicles bring cargo from the ER via the ERGIC to the cis-Golgi network. As cisternae mature (cisternal maturation model) or vesicles shuttle forward, proteins encounter enzymes for trimming and adding sugars, O-glycosylation, sulfation and phosphorylation (M6P tagging begins in cis-Golgi). The trans-Golgi network sorts cargo into vesicles for lysosomes (clathrin-coated), regulated secretory granules and constitutive secretion. COPI vesicles return escaped ER proteins backward (retrograde).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cis-Golgi to trans-Golgi to Golgi stack</b> — The trans-Golgi is the exit face; proteins don't return to the stack afterward.</li><li><b>Golgi stack to cis-Golgi to trans-Golgi</b> — Proteins enter at the cis face, not the stack.</li><li><b>Golgi stack to trans-Golgi to cis-Golgi</b> — This reverses the direction and would end at the entry face.</li><li><b>Trans-Golgi to cis-Golgi to Golgi stack</b> — Proteins don't enter at the trans face.</li><li><b>Trans-Golgi to Golgi stack to cis-Golgi</b> — This is the exact reverse of the real pathway.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> COPII = anterograde (ER → Golgi); COPI = retrograde (Golgi → ER); clathrin = TGN → lysosome, endocytosis.</p>","w":["Correct — cis → stack → trans.","The trans-Golgi is the exit face; proteins don't return to the stack afterward.","Proteins enter at the cis face, not the stack.","This reverses the direction and would end at the entry face.","Proteins don't enter at the trans face.","This is the exact reverse of the real pathway."]},"fc10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Smooth ER makes lipids and steroid hormones and detoxifies drugs; steroid-producing cells have abundant SER.</p><p>SER contains enzymes for cholesterol and phospholipid synthesis and for converting cholesterol into steroid hormones (several steps also occur in mitochondria). Cells with expanded SER: adrenal cortex, Leydig cells, ovarian theca/granulosa-lutein cells (steroids), hepatocytes (detoxification, lipoproteins, glucose-6-phosphatase) and muscle (sarcoplasmic reticulum for Ca²⁺). In H&amp;E, steroid-producing cells look pale and foamy because of lipid droplets.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Rough ER</b> — RER makes secretory and membrane proteins, not steroids.</li><li><b>Golgi cisternae</b> — The Golgi packages and modifies proteins; it doesn't synthesize steroids.</li><li><b>Lysosomes</b> — Lysosomes digest macromolecules; they aren't expanded for hormone synthesis.</li><li><b>Peroxisomes</b> — Peroxisomes oxidize VLCFAs and help make bile acids and plasmalogens, but steroid synthesis relies on SER.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SER-rich cells: adrenal cortex, Leydig cells, hepatocytes.</p>","w":["RER makes secretory and membrane proteins, not steroids.","Correct — steroid-synthesizing cells have marked SER expansion.","The Golgi packages and modifies proteins; it doesn't synthesize steroids.","Lysosomes digest macromolecules; they aren't expanded for hormone synthesis.","Peroxisomes oxidize VLCFAs and help make bile acids and plasmalogens, but steroid synthesis relies on SER."]},"fc11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proteins entering the Golgi without targeting signals follow the default constitutive secretory pathway.</p><p>Constitutive secretion is continuous and unregulated: vesicles from the trans-Golgi constantly fuse with the plasma membrane, delivering membrane lipids and proteins and releasing soluble contents (e.g., collagen from fibroblasts, albumin and immunoglobulins). Regulated secretion stores products (hormones, neurotransmitters, digestive enzymes) in secretory granules until a signal (often Ca²⁺) triggers release.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosomal degradation</b> — Lysosomal proteins need the mannose-6-phosphate tag; untagged proteins aren't sent there.</li><li><b>Cytosolic retention</b> — Proteins that have entered the Golgi have already left the cytosol and can't be retained there.</li><li><b>Nuclear import</b> — Nuclear import uses a nuclear localization signal on cytosolic proteins, not a Golgi route.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial localization</b> — Mitochondrial proteins are made on free ribosomes with their own targeting sequences.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Constitutive (continuous) vs regulated (stored, triggered) secretion.</p>","w":["Lysosomal proteins need the mannose-6-phosphate tag; untagged proteins aren't sent there.","Proteins that have entered the Golgi have already left the cytosol and can't be retained there.","Nuclear import uses a nuclear localization signal on cytosolic proteins, not a Golgi route.","Correct — untagged proteins follow constitutive secretion.","Mitochondrial proteins are made on free ribosomes with their own targeting sequences."]},"fc12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> RER-bound polysomes make secretory, membrane and lysosomal proteins; free polysomes make cytosolic, nuclear, peroxisomal and most mitochondrial proteins.</p><p>Whether a ribosome becomes membrane-bound depends on the protein it's making: an ER signal sequence brings it to the RER via SRP. Antibodies are secreted, so they're translated on RER-bound polysomes, glycosylated and processed through the Golgi. Catalase (peroxisomal) carries a peroxisomal targeting signal (PTS1, often SKL at the C-terminus) and is translated fully on free polysomes, then imported post-translationally via peroxins (defective in Zellweger syndrome).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Antibody on free polysomes; catalase on RER-bound polysomes</b> — This reverses the two sites.</li><li><b>Both on membrane-bound polysomes of the RER</b> — Peroxisomal proteins are made on free polysomes and imported directly.</li><li><b>Both on free polysomes</b> — Secretory proteins like antibodies must be made on the RER.</li><li><b>Both inside mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria make only 13 of their own proteins — neither antibodies nor catalase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Free ribosomes: cytosol, nucleus, mitochondria, peroxisomes. RER: secretion, membrane, lysosome, ER/Golgi.</p>","w":["Correct — antibodies on RER polysomes; catalase on free polysomes.","This reverses the two sites.","Peroxisomal proteins are made on free polysomes and imported directly.","Secretory proteins like antibodies must be made on the RER.","Mitochondria make only 13 of their own proteins — neither antibodies nor catalase."]},"fc13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Drugs like phenobarbital induce cytochrome P450 enzymes, causing hepatocyte smooth ER to proliferate (enzyme induction).</p><p>Hepatocyte SER contains cytochrome P450 enzymes that oxidize lipophilic drugs (phase I), and UDP-glucuronosyltransferase that conjugates them (phase II), making them more water-soluble for excretion. Chronic exposure to inducers expands the SER and increases metabolism of other drugs (e.g., reducing warfarin or oral contraceptive levels). Inducers: phenobarbital, phenytoin, carbamazepine, rifampin, St John's wort, chronic alcohol. Inhibitors: azole antifungals, macrolides (not azithromycin), grapefruit juice, cimetidine, ritonavir.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Synthesis of ribosomal RNA in the nucleolus</b> — rRNA is synthesized in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>Packaging of proteins for lysosomes</b> — Packaging lysosomal proteins happens in the trans-Golgi (mannose-6-phosphate).</li><li><b>Secretion of albumin</b> — Albumin is a secretory protein made on the RER.</li><li><b>Degradation of very-long-chain fatty acids</b> — VLCFA β-oxidation occurs in peroxisomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> P450 inducers ↓ levels of other drugs (warfarin, OCPs); inhibitors ↑ them.</p>","w":["rRNA is synthesized in the nucleolus.","Packaging lysosomal proteins happens in the trans-Golgi (mannose-6-phosphate).","Albumin is a secretory protein made on the RER.","VLCFA β-oxidation occurs in peroxisomes.","Correct — SER expands for P450 drug detoxification."]},"fc14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glucose-6-phosphatase sits in the ER membrane (active site in the lumen); its deficiency causes von Gierke disease.</p><p>Glucose-6-phosphate from glycogenolysis or gluconeogenesis is transported into the ER by a G6P translocase, where glucose-6-phosphatase removes the phosphate; free glucose then exits the cell via GLUT-2. Type Ia von Gierke disease is phosphatase deficiency; type Ib is translocase deficiency (also neutropenia). Without free glucose release: severe fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hypertriglyceridemia and hepatomegaly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cytosol</b> — Glycolysis and glycogen breakdown to G6P occur in the cytosol, but the phosphatase is in the ER.</li><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomal acid maltase (deficient in Pompe disease) is a different enzyme.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial matrix</b> — The mitochondrial matrix contains TCA and fatty acid oxidation enzymes.</li><li><b>Peroxisome</b> — Peroxisomes oxidize VLCFAs and phytanic acid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microsomal enzymes: glucose-6-phosphatase, cytochrome P450, HMG-CoA reductase.</p>","w":["Glycolysis and glycogen breakdown to G6P occur in the cytosol, but the phosphatase is in the ER.","Lysosomal acid maltase (deficient in Pompe disease) is a different enzyme.","Correct — glucose-6-phosphatase is an ER enzyme.","The mitochondrial matrix contains TCA and fatty acid oxidation enzymes.","Peroxisomes oxidize VLCFAs and phytanic acid."]},"fc15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The sarcoplasmic reticulum is specialized smooth ER that stores Ca²⁺ in muscle and releases it to trigger contraction.</p><p>The SR forms a network around each myofibril with terminal cisternae flanking T-tubules (forming triads at the A–I junction in skeletal muscle). When the action potential travels down the T-tubule, DHPRs mechanically open RyR1 Ca²⁺ release channels. Calsequestrin binds Ca²⁺ inside the SR; SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back for relaxation. Defective RyR1 causes malignant hyperthermia and central core disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomes digest macromolecules.</li><li><b>Mitochondrion</b> — Mitochondria can take up some Ca²⁺ but aren't the sarcoplasmic reticulum.</li><li><b>Rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> — RER makes proteins; the calcium-storing network is ribosome-free SER.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — The Golgi packages proteins and doesn't store contraction Ca²⁺.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Triad (skeletal): T-tubule + 2 terminal cisternae; diad (cardiac).</p>","w":["Lysosomes digest macromolecules.","Correct — the sarcoplasmic reticulum is specialized SER.","Mitochondria can take up some Ca²⁺ but aren't the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","RER makes proteins; the calcium-storing network is ribosome-free SER.","The Golgi packages proteins and doesn't store contraction Ca²⁺."]},"fc16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> COPII-coated vesicles carry cargo forward from the ER exit sites to the ERGIC and cis-Golgi.</p><p>Vesicle coats select cargo and shape vesicles. COPII (Sar1 GTPase, Sec23/24, Sec13/31) → anterograde ER → Golgi. COPI (ARF GTPase) → retrograde Golgi → ER and between Golgi cisternae (returning KDEL-tagged ER proteins). Clathrin (with adaptor proteins and dynamin) → trans-Golgi → endosomes/lysosomes and plasma membrane endocytosis. Microtubules and motors (dynein toward minus end near the centrosome/Golgi; kinesin toward the periphery) move vesicles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caveolin</b> — Caveolin forms caveolae in the plasma membrane.</li><li><b>COPI</b> — COPI carries proteins backward, from Golgi to ER.</li><li><b>Dynein</b> — Dynein is a microtubule motor protein, not a vesicle coat.</li><li><b>Clathrin</b> — Clathrin coats vesicles from the trans-Golgi (e.g., to lysosomes) and the plasma membrane (endocytosis).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"COPII goes forward (to the Golgi) — 2 goes forward; COPI goes back.\"</p>","w":["Caveolin forms caveolae in the plasma membrane.","COPI carries proteins backward, from Golgi to ER.","Dynein is a microtubule motor protein, not a vesicle coat.","Correct — COPII vesicles carry cargo forward from the ER.","Clathrin coats vesicles from the trans-Golgi (e.g., to lysosomes) and the plasma membrane (endocytosis)."]},"fc17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Golgi performs O-linked glycosylation, proteoglycan sulfation, mannose-6-phosphate tagging and final sorting of proteins.</p><p>N-linked glycosylation starts en bloc in the RER and is trimmed and modified in the Golgi; O-linked sugars are added one by one in the Golgi to Ser/Thr hydroxyls. GAG chains are assembled on proteoglycan core proteins and sulfated in the Golgi before secretion. Highly secretory cells (e.g., plasma cells) show a pale perinuclear \"hof\" where a large Golgi sits.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleolus</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA and assembles ribosomes.</li><li><b>Peroxisome</b> — Peroxisomes oxidize fatty acids and degrade H₂O₂.</li><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomes degrade, rather than modify, proteins.</li><li><b>Rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> — The RER starts N-linked glycosylation; O-glycosylation happens in the Golgi.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> N-linked (Asn) begins in RER; O-linked (Ser/Thr) in Golgi.</p>","w":["Correct — O-glycosylation and proteoglycan sulfation occur in the Golgi.","The nucleolus makes rRNA and assembles ribosomes.","Peroxisomes oxidize fatty acids and degrade H₂O₂.","Lysosomes degrade, rather than modify, proteins.","The RER starts N-linked glycosylation; O-glycosylation happens in the Golgi."]},"fc18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Centrosome-nucleated microtubules position the Golgi next to the nucleus; depolymerizing them fragments and disperses the Golgi.</p><p>The Golgi associates with microtubule minus ends near the centrosome (MTOC). Dynein moves membranes toward minus ends, holding Golgi stacks together centrally. Drugs such as colchicine, nocodazole or vinca alkaloids depolymerize microtubules, scattering Golgi elements as ministacks near ER exit sites. Microtubules also carry ERGIC vesicles to the Golgi. When drugs are removed, microtubules regrow and the Golgi reassembles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The Golgi is held in place by peptidoglycan</b> — Peptidoglycan is a bacterial cell wall component.</li><li><b>Actin filaments form the Golgi cisternae</b> — Golgi cisternae are membranes, not actin filaments.</li><li><b>The Golgi is directly continuous with the nuclear envelope</b> — The outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the ER, not the Golgi.</li><li><b>Golgi enzymes require GTP produced by microtubules</b> — Microtubules bind GTP for their own assembly; they don't supply it to Golgi enzymes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microtubule poisons: colchicine (gout), vinca alkaloids and taxanes (cancer).</p>","w":["Peptidoglycan is a bacterial cell wall component.","Golgi cisternae are membranes, not actin filaments.","The outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the ER, not the Golgi.","Microtubules bind GTP for their own assembly; they don't supply it to Golgi enzymes.","Correct — centrosome-nucleated microtubules hold the Golgi in place."]},"fc19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Proinsulin is cleaved into insulin and C-peptide by prohormone convertases in the trans-Golgi/secretory granules; failure → high proinsulin, low insulin and C-peptide.</p><p>Preproinsulin is made on the RER; the signal peptide is cleaved, leaving proinsulin, which folds with three disulfide bonds. In the Golgi and immature secretory granules, prohormone convertases (PC1/3 and PC2) and carboxypeptidase E remove the connecting C-peptide, producing mature insulin and C-peptide in equal amounts. Proinsulin has only ~5–10% of insulin's activity, so the patient behaves as if insulin-deficient. C-peptide is also used clinically: high in insulinoma/sulfonylurea use, low with exogenous insulin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Degradation of insulin by lysosomes</b> — Lysosomal degradation of insulin wouldn't raise proinsulin.</li><li><b>Removal of glucose-6-phosphate's phosphate in the ER</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase isn't involved in insulin processing.</li><li><b>Mannose-6-phosphate tagging in the Golgi</b> — Mannose-6-phosphate tags lysosomal enzymes, not insulin.</li><li><b>Translation of preproinsulin on free ribosomes</b> — Preproinsulin is made on the RER (not free ribosomes), and its synthesis is normal here.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> C-peptide: ↑ in insulinoma/sulfonylureas; ↓ in exogenous insulin (factitious hypoglycemia).</p>","w":["Lysosomal degradation of insulin wouldn't raise proinsulin.","Glucose-6-phosphatase isn't involved in insulin processing.","Correct — the Golgi peptidase that converts proinsulin fails.","Mannose-6-phosphate tags lysosomal enzymes, not insulin.","Preproinsulin is made on the RER (not free ribosomes), and its synthesis is normal here."]},"fc20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> I-cell disease: N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase defect → no mannose-6-phosphate on lysosomal enzymes → enzymes secreted into blood; lysosomes fill with undigested material.</p><p>In the cis-Golgi, the phosphotransferase phosphorylates mannose on lysosomal hydrolases; M6P receptors in the trans-Golgi then send them to lysosomes. Without the tag, enzymes follow the default secretory pathway, so plasma enzyme levels are high while lysosomes accumulate inclusion bodies. Clinically resembles Hurler syndrome but more severe: coarse facies, gingival hyperplasia, restricted joints, skeletal abnormalities, developmental delay, death usually in early childhood.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inappropriate protein targeting to endoplasmic reticulum</b> — Targeting to the ER is normal — the enzymes are made on the RER; the problem is later, in the Golgi.</li><li><b>Inappropriate degradation of lysosomal enzymes</b> — The enzymes aren't degraded — they're secreted, which is why plasma levels are high.</li><li><b>Congenital lack of lysosomal formation</b> — Lysosomes do form; they just lack hydrolases and fill with inclusion bodies.</li><li><b>Misfolding of nuclear proteins</b> — Nuclear proteins aren't involved; the defect is lysosomal enzyme sorting.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> I-cell = ↑plasma lysosomal enzymes; autosomal recessive (GNPTAB).</p>","w":["Targeting to the ER is normal — the enzymes are made on the RER; the problem is later, in the Golgi.","The enzymes aren't degraded — they're secreted, which is why plasma levels are high.","Lysosomes do form; they just lack hydrolases and fill with inclusion bodies.","Correct — failure of mannose phosphorylation.","Nuclear proteins aren't involved; the defect is lysosomal enzyme sorting."]},"fc21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> High plasma lysosomal enzymes + coarse facies + restricted joints = I-cell disease: failed mannose-6-phosphate tagging in the cis-Golgi.</p><p>Lysosomal hydrolases are made on the RER and travel to the Golgi, where N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase begins making the M6P tag. M6P receptors in the trans-Golgi network capture tagged enzymes into clathrin-coated vesicles bound for late endosomes/lysosomes; acidic pH there releases the enzymes and the receptors recycle. Without the tag, enzymes are secreted (default pathway), so lysosomes are empty and fill with inclusions while plasma enzyme levels rise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Protein synthesis on free ribosomes</b> — Free ribosomes make cytosolic proteins; the problem is enzyme targeting, not synthesis.</li><li><b>Acidification of lysosomes</b> — Lysosomes in I-cell disease are acidic but empty of enzymes; acidification isn't the defect.</li><li><b>Autophagosome formation</b> — Autophagosome formation isn't impaired; the enzymes never arrive in the lysosome.</li><li><b>Ubiquitination of cytosolic proteins</b> — Ubiquitination tags cytosolic proteins for the proteasome — unrelated to lysosomal enzyme targeting.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> M6P receptor binds at neutral pH (Golgi) and releases at acidic pH (endosome).</p>","w":["Free ribosomes make cytosolic proteins; the problem is enzyme targeting, not synthesis.","Correct — failed M6P tagging sends lysosomal enzymes into the plasma.","Lysosomes in I-cell disease are acidic but empty of enzymes; acidification isn't the defect.","Autophagosome formation isn't impaired; the enzymes never arrive in the lysosome.","Ubiquitination tags cytosolic proteins for the proteasome — unrelated to lysosomal enzyme targeting."]},"fc22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysosomal acid hydrolases work best at pH ~5 and are much less active at the neutral pH (~7.2) of the cytosol — a built-in safeguard.</p><p>An ATP-dependent proton pump (V-type H⁺-ATPase) in the lysosomal membrane keeps the lumen acidic. The lysosomal membrane is also protected from its own enzymes by heavily glycosylated membrane proteins (LAMPs) lining the inner surface. If a few enzymes leak, the neutral cytosol largely inactivates them. Massive lysosomal rupture (e.g., silica in macrophages) can still cause cell death and inflammation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are inactive until phosphorylated by CDKs</b> — CDKs regulate the cell cycle, not lysosomal enzymes.</li><li><b>They require hydrogen peroxide to function</b> — H₂O₂ is used by peroxisomal catalase, not lysosomal hydrolases.</li><li><b>They are immediately ubiquitinated in the cytosol</b> — Ubiquitination isn't the main protection; pH is.</li><li><b>They are synthesized on free ribosomes in an inactive form</b> — Lysosomal hydrolases are made on ER-bound ribosomes, not free ones.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lysosomal pH ~5 maintained by V-type H⁺-ATPase.</p>","w":["CDKs regulate the cell cycle, not lysosomal enzymes.","Correct — acid hydrolases are active only at the acidic pH inside lysosomes.","H₂O₂ is used by peroxisomal catalase, not lysosomal hydrolases.","Ubiquitination isn't the main protection; pH is.","Lysosomal hydrolases are made on ER-bound ribosomes, not free ones."]},"fc23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Autophagy: the cell encloses its own organelles/cytoplasm in a double-membrane autophagosome, which fuses with a lysosome for digestion.</p><p>Autophagy is upregulated by starvation (inhibition of mTOR), allowing the cell to recycle its components for energy and building blocks. It also removes damaged mitochondria (mitophagy), protein aggregates, and intracellular pathogens. Defective autophagy is implicated in neurodegeneration (Parkinson, Huntington), Crohn disease (ATG16L1) and cancer. Lipofuscin (\"wear-and-tear\" pigment) is undigested residue of autophagy that accumulates with age.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Exocytosis</b> — Exocytosis releases material out of the cell.</li><li><b>Phagocytosis</b> — Phagocytosis engulfs large external particles (e.g., bacteria).</li><li><b>Apoptosis</b> — Apoptosis is programmed death of the whole cell.</li><li><b>Endocytosis</b> — Endocytosis takes in extracellular fluid and macromolecules.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lysosomes receive cargo via phagocytosis, endocytosis and autophagy; mTOR inhibits autophagy.</p>","w":["Exocytosis releases material out of the cell.","Phagocytosis engulfs large external particles (e.g., bacteria).","Apoptosis is programmed death of the whole cell.","Correct — autophagy digests the cell's own organelles via lysosomes.","Endocytosis takes in extracellular fluid and macromolecules."]},"fc24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lysosomal storage disorders: deficiency of one specific lysosomal enzyme → its specific substrate accumulates in lysosomes.</p><p>Tay–Sachs disease is hexosaminidase A deficiency → GM2 ganglioside accumulation in neurons: progressive neurodegeneration, developmental regression, exaggerated startle, cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly; autosomal recessive, increased in Ashkenazi Jews. Other examples: Gaucher (glucocerebrosidase), Niemann–Pick (sphingomyelinase), Fabry (α-galactosidase A, X-linked), Krabbe (galactocerebrosidase), metachromatic leukodystrophy (arylsulfatase A), Hurler/Hunter (MPS), Pompe (acid maltase).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroxisomes fail to form because of PEX gene mutations</b> — PEX gene failure to form peroxisomes describes the Zellweger spectrum.</li><li><b>Proteins fail to enter the rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> — Failure to enter the RER would affect all secretory and membrane proteins, not one lysosomal substrate.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial DNA deletions impair oxidative phosphorylation</b> — mtDNA deletions describe mitochondrial diseases such as Kearns–Sayre syndrome.</li><li><b>Lysosomal enzymes are secreted into the blood because of a missing mannose-6-phosphate tag</b> — Missing M6P with secreted enzymes is I-cell disease, which mis-sorts all hydrolases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cherry-red spot: Tay–Sachs (no HSM) vs Niemann–Pick (with HSM).</p>","w":["Correct — one missing enzyme causes accumulation of its substrate.","PEX gene failure to form peroxisomes describes the Zellweger spectrum.","Failure to enter the RER would affect all secretory and membrane proteins, not one lysosomal substrate.","mtDNA deletions describe mitochondrial diseases such as Kearns–Sayre syndrome.","Missing M6P with secreted enzymes is I-cell disease, which mis-sorts all hydrolases."]},"fc25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> I-cell disease = deficient N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase (Golgi), which creates the mannose-6-phosphate tag.</p><p>The enzyme transfers GlcNAc-1-phosphate onto mannose residues of lysosomal hydrolases; a second enzyme removes GlcNAc, exposing M6P. Without it, hydrolases aren't recognized by M6P receptors and are secreted. Note: some cells (hepatocytes, leukocytes) can use M6P-independent pathways, so their lysosomes are relatively normal, while fibroblasts show inclusion bodies (\"I-cells\").</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Catalase</b> — Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme that degrades H₂O₂.</li><li><b>Hexosaminidase A</b> — Hexosaminidase A is the lysosomal enzyme deficient in Tay–Sachs disease.</li><li><b>Glucose-6-phosphatase</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase is an ER enzyme deficient in von Gierke disease.</li><li><b>Acyl-CoA oxidase</b> — Acyl-CoA oxidase catalyzes the first step of peroxisomal β-oxidation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> I-cell disease: inclusion bodies in fibroblasts; ↑plasma lysosomal enzymes.</p>","w":["Catalase is a peroxisomal enzyme that degrades H₂O₂.","Hexosaminidase A is the lysosomal enzyme deficient in Tay–Sachs disease.","Glucose-6-phosphatase is an ER enzyme deficient in von Gierke disease.","Acyl-CoA oxidase catalyzes the first step of peroxisomal β-oxidation.","Correct — this Golgi enzyme creates the M6P tag."]},"fc26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intrinsic (mitochondrial) apoptosis: Bax/Bak permeabilize the outer membrane → cytochrome c released → apoptosome (Apaf-1 + caspase-9) → caspase-3.</p><p>The Bcl-2 family controls the gate. Anti-apoptotic Bcl-2 and Bcl-xL stabilize the outer membrane; pro-apoptotic Bax and Bak form pores when activated by BH3-only sensors (Bim, Bid, Puma, Noxa) triggered by DNA damage (via p53), growth factor withdrawal or cell stress. Released cytochrome c binds Apaf-1, forming the apoptosome that activates caspase-9, which activates executioner caspases (3, 6, 7). The extrinsic pathway (Fas, TNF receptors) activates caspase-8. Follicular lymphoma overexpresses Bcl-2 via t(14;18).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caspase‑3</b> — Caspase-3 is the executioner caspase, activated downstream in the cytosol — not released from mitochondria.</li><li><b>ATP synthase</b> — ATP synthase is an inner membrane enzyme and isn't released to start apoptosis.</li><li><b>Bcl‑2</b> — Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic and stays in the outer membrane, blocking cytochrome c release.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 is a nuclear transcription factor that responds to DNA damage; it isn't stored in mitochondria.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> t(14;18) → Bcl-2 overexpression → follicular lymphoma (cells resist apoptosis).</p>","w":["Caspase-3 is the executioner caspase, activated downstream in the cytosol — not released from mitochondria.","ATP synthase is an inner membrane enzyme and isn't released to start apoptosis.","Correct — cytochrome c release triggers the apoptosome.","Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic and stays in the outer membrane, blocking cytochrome c release.","p53 is a nuclear transcription factor that responds to DNA damage; it isn't stored in mitochondria."]},"fc27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Outer mitochondrial membrane has porins (permeable to molecules &lt;5000 Da); inner membrane is impermeable to ions, so it can hold the proton gradient.</p><p>Mitochondrial compartments: outer membrane, intermembrane space (where H⁺ accumulates; contains cytochrome c), inner membrane folded into cristae (ETC complexes, ATP synthase, cardiolipin, specific transporters such as the adenine nucleotide translocase and pyruvate carrier), and matrix (TCA cycle, β-oxidation, mtDNA, ribosomes). The inner membrane's impermeability is what allows chemiosmotic coupling; uncouplers make it leaky to H⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Glycocalyx on the inner membrane; nuclear pores in the outer membrane</b> — A glycocalyx and nuclear pores aren't mitochondrial structures.</li><li><b>Ribosomes on the outer membrane; ATP synthase in the matrix</b> — Mitochondria aren't studded with ribosomes, and ATP synthase sits in the inner membrane.</li><li><b>Cholesterol in the inner membrane; caveolae in the outer membrane</b> — Caveolae are plasma membrane structures, not mitochondrial.</li><li><b>Cristae in the outer membrane; porins in the inner membrane</b> — Cristae are folds of the inner membrane, and porins are in the outer membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cardiolipin = inner mitochondrial membrane phospholipid; anti-cardiolipin antibodies in syphilis/APS.</p>","w":["A glycocalyx and nuclear pores aren't mitochondrial structures.","Mitochondria aren't studded with ribosomes, and ATP synthase sits in the inner membrane.","Correct — porins make the outer membrane leaky; the inner membrane holds the gradient.","Caveolae are plasma membrane structures, not mitochondrial.","Cristae are folds of the inner membrane, and porins are in the outer membrane."]},"fc28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> mtDNA: circular, ~16,569 bp, 37 genes (13 ETC proteins, 2 rRNAs, 22 tRNAs), maternally inherited.</p><p>Each cell has hundreds to thousands of mtDNA copies. The 13 proteins are subunits of complexes I, III, IV and V (complex II is entirely nuclear-encoded). All other mitochondrial proteins (~1,000+) are nuclear-encoded, made on free polysomes and imported via TOM/TIM complexes. mtDNA has a higher mutation rate and limited repair. Heteroplasmy (mixture of normal and mutant mtDNA) explains variable severity among relatives.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Linear DNA of about 3 billion base pairs that encodes most mitochondrial proteins</b> — Linear DNA of ~3 billion bp is the nuclear genome; mtDNA encodes few proteins.</li><li><b>Circular DNA of ~16,500 bp that encodes all citric acid cycle enzymes</b> — TCA cycle enzymes are nuclear-encoded and imported.</li><li><b>Single-stranded RNA genome that is inherited from the father</b> — mtDNA is DNA, not RNA, and it's inherited from the mother.</li><li><b>Linear DNA wrapped around histones and located in the nucleolus</b> — Histone-wrapped linear DNA is nuclear chromatin; mtDNA isn't in the nucleolus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Complex II = only ETC complex with no mtDNA-encoded subunits.</p>","w":["Linear DNA of ~3 billion bp is the nuclear genome; mtDNA encodes few proteins.","Correct — circular, ~16.5 kb, 37 genes.","TCA cycle enzymes are nuclear-encoded and imported.","mtDNA is DNA, not RNA, and it's inherited from the mother.","Histone-wrapped linear DNA is nuclear chromatin; mtDNA isn't in the nucleolus."]},"fc29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mitochondrial inheritance: transmitted only through mothers to all children; affected fathers never transmit. LHON = mtDNA point mutation causing bilateral central vision loss in young men.</p><p>Sperm mitochondria are destroyed after fertilization, so all mtDNA comes from the egg. Pedigree clues: affected mothers → potentially all offspring affected; affected fathers → no affected children. Variable expression due to heteroplasmy. LHON mutations (e.g., in ND4 of complex I) damage retinal ganglion cells and optic nerve fibers, causing painless subacute loss of central vision, often sequentially in both eyes. Males are affected more often.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nuclear X chromosome</b> — X-linked disease would be passed from an affected father to all his daughters (as carriers); here affected men never transmit it.</li><li><b>Lysosomal enzyme gene</b> — Lysosomal enzyme deficiencies are typically autosomal recessive storage diseases.</li><li><b>Y chromosome</b> — Y-linked traits pass from father to all sons — the opposite of this pattern.</li><li><b>Peroxisomal PEX gene</b> — PEX gene mutations cause Zellweger spectrum disorders, not isolated optic neuropathy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mitochondrial diseases: LHON, MELAS, MERRF, Kearns–Sayre — ragged red fibers on biopsy (except LHON).</p>","w":["X-linked disease would be passed from an affected father to all his daughters (as carriers); here affected men never transmit it.","Lysosomal enzyme deficiencies are typically autosomal recessive storage diseases.","Y-linked traits pass from father to all sons — the opposite of this pattern.","Correct — a point mutation in mtDNA with maternal inheritance.","PEX gene mutations cause Zellweger spectrum disorders, not isolated optic neuropathy."]},"fc30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kearns–Sayre syndrome = single large mtDNA deletion → onset before 20 with ophthalmoplegia, ptosis, pigmentary retinopathy (± heart block).</p><p>Kearns–Sayre is usually sporadic (the deletion arises in the oocyte or early embryo). Additional features: cardiac conduction defects (complete heart block — may need a pacemaker), cerebellar ataxia, elevated CSF protein, short stature, diabetes. Muscle biopsy shows ragged red fibers (accumulated abnormal mitochondria under the sarcolemma on Gomori trichrome stain). Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO) is a milder related deletion syndrome.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Leber hereditary optic neuropathy</b> — LHON is a point mutation causing optic neuropathy, not ophthalmoplegia.</li><li><b>Refsum disease</b> — Refsum disease is a peroxisomal defect of phytanic acid α-oxidation.</li><li><b>I-cell disease</b> — I-cell disease is a lysosomal enzyme targeting defect.</li><li><b>Zellweger syndrome</b> — Zellweger syndrome is a peroxisome biogenesis disorder of infancy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> KSS triad: onset &lt;20 y, ophthalmoplegia, pigmentary retinopathy (+ heart block).</p>","w":["Correct — Kearns–Sayre syndrome.","LHON is a point mutation causing optic neuropathy, not ophthalmoplegia.","Refsum disease is a peroxisomal defect of phytanic acid α-oxidation.","I-cell disease is a lysosomal enzyme targeting defect.","Zellweger syndrome is a peroxisome biogenesis disorder of infancy."]},"fc31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes β-oxidize very-long-chain fatty acids (&gt;22 carbons) that are too long for mitochondria.</p><p>VLCFAs are activated to acyl-CoA and imported into peroxisomes via the ABCD1 transporter. Peroxisomal β-oxidation shortens them (acyl-CoA oxidase produces H₂O₂), and the resulting medium-chain acyl groups are transferred (as carnitine esters) to mitochondria to be fully oxidized. Peroxisomes also α-oxidize phytanic acid (branched-chain). Defects: X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (ABCD1), Zellweger (no peroxisomes).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cyclin degradation</b> — Cyclins are degraded by the ubiquitin–proteasome system, not in peroxisomes.</li><li><b>Glycolysis</b> — Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol.</li><li><b>Medium-chain fatty acid β-oxidation</b> — Medium-chain fatty acids are oxidized in mitochondria; peroxisomes produce them.</li><li><b>Ubiquitination</b> — Ubiquitination takes place in the cytosol and nucleus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> VLCFA ↑ in plasma → X-ALD or Zellweger.</p>","w":["Cyclins are degraded by the ubiquitin–proteasome system, not in peroxisomes.","Glycolysis occurs in the cytosol.","Medium-chain fatty acids are oxidized in mitochondria; peroxisomes produce them.","Ubiquitination takes place in the cytosol and nucleus.","Correct — VLCFA β-oxidation is peroxisomal."]},"fc32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal oxidases pass electrons directly to O₂, generating H₂O₂, which catalase converts to H₂O + O₂.</p><p>The name \"peroxisome\" comes from this H₂O₂ production. Catalase protects the cell (2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂) or uses H₂O₂ to oxidize toxins (ethanol, formaldehyde, phenols). Peroxisomes are abundant in liver and kidney, reflecting their detoxification role. Catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Nocardia, Candida) are dangerous in chronic granulomatous disease, because they destroy their own H₂O₂ that phagocytes could otherwise use.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Carbon tetrachloride (CCl4)</b> — CCl₄ is an environmental toxin metabolized by P450 in the SER — not a peroxisomal byproduct.</li><li><b>Formaldehyde (CH2O)</b> — Formaldehyde is a compound catalase can oxidize using H₂O₂; it isn't the byproduct.</li><li><b>Hydroxide (OH-)</b> — Hydroxide ions aren't the characteristic peroxisomal byproduct.</li><li><b>Superoxide (O2-)</b> — Superoxide is produced mainly by the mitochondrial ETC and NADPH oxidase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CGD: catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Serratia, Nocardia, Burkholderia).</p>","w":["CCl₄ is an environmental toxin metabolized by P450 in the SER — not a peroxisomal byproduct.","Formaldehyde is a compound catalase can oxidize using H₂O₂; it isn't the byproduct.","Correct — H₂O₂ is the toxic peroxisomal byproduct.","Hydroxide ions aren't the characteristic peroxisomal byproduct.","Superoxide is produced mainly by the mitochondrial ETC and NADPH oxidase."]},"fc33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Defective VLCFA β-oxidation + phytanic acid α-oxidation together → whole-peroxisome defect (Zellweger spectrum).</p><p>Both pathways are housed in peroxisomes. When both fail, the likely problem is that peroxisomes can't be built or import their enzymes (PEX mutations). Infants have severe hypotonia, seizures, poor feeding, liver dysfunction, renal cysts and characteristic facies. Lab: elevated VLCFAs, phytanic acid and pipecolic acid; reduced plasmalogens. An isolated phytanic acid defect is Refsum disease; isolated VLCFA defect is X-ALD.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria oxidize shorter fatty acids, can't take up VLCFAs, and don't α-oxidize phytanic acid.</li><li><b>Rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> — The RER makes secretory and membrane proteins.</li><li><b>Proteasomes</b> — Proteasomes degrade ubiquitinated proteins.</li><li><b>Lysosomes</b> — Lysosomes digest macromolecules with acid hydrolases.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Peroxisomal disorders: Zellweger (all functions), Refsum (phytanic acid), X-ALD (VLCFA).</p>","w":["Mitochondria oxidize shorter fatty acids, can't take up VLCFAs, and don't α-oxidize phytanic acid.","The RER makes secretory and membrane proteins.","Proteasomes degrade ubiquitinated proteins.","Lysosomes digest macromolecules with acid hydrolases.","Correct — the defect is in peroxisomes.","The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins."]},"fc34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> VLCFA β-oxidation and phytanic acid α-oxidation are both peroxisomal → impairment of both points to peroxisome dysfunction.</p><p>Phytanic acid (from dairy, ruminant fat) has a methyl branch at carbon 3 that blocks β-oxidation; peroxisomes remove one carbon by α-oxidation (phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase) to make pristanic acid, which can then be β-oxidized. VLCFAs (&gt;22 C) are β-oxidized in peroxisomes before mitochondria finish the job. An infant with both pathways defective likely has a peroxisome biogenesis disorder.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomes break down macromolecules with acid hydrolases; they don't oxidize VLCFA or phytanic acid.</li><li><b>Mitochondria</b> — Mitochondria handle short-, medium- and long-chain fatty acids, but VLCFAs are too large for them.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins; it doesn't oxidize fatty acids.</li><li><b>Smooth ER</b> — Smooth ER synthesizes lipids and steroids and detoxifies drugs, but doesn't perform VLCFA β-oxidation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Refsum: phytanic acid ↑ → retinitis pigmentosa, neuropathy, ichthyosis; restrict dietary phytanic acid.</p>","w":["Lysosomes break down macromolecules with acid hydrolases; they don't oxidize VLCFA or phytanic acid.","Mitochondria handle short-, medium- and long-chain fatty acids, but VLCFAs are too large for them.","Correct — the peroxisome is the dysfunctional organelle.","The Golgi modifies and sorts proteins; it doesn't oxidize fatty acids.","Smooth ER synthesizes lipids and steroids and detoxifies drugs, but doesn't perform VLCFA β-oxidation."]},"fc35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Zellweger syndrome (cerebrohepatorenal) = PEX gene mutations → peroxins can't import enzymes → no functional peroxisomes.</p><p>Peroxins recognize PTS1 (C-terminal SKL) or PTS2 (N-terminal) signals on cytosolic peroxisomal proteins and import them. Without functional peroxins, peroxisomes are absent (\"ghosts\"), causing accumulation of VLCFAs, phytanic acid, pipecolic acid and bile acid intermediates, and deficiency of plasmalogens (needed for myelin). Features: profound hypotonia, seizures, hepatomegaly/jaundice, renal cortical cysts, high forehead, large fontanelle, flat face, stippled epiphyses; death usually within the first year.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mitochondrial tRNA gene</b> — mtDNA tRNA mutations cause mitochondrial disorders (e.g., MELAS, MERRF).</li><li><b>Dystrophin gene</b> — Dystrophin mutations cause muscular dystrophy.</li><li><b>Gene for glucose-6-phosphatase</b> — Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency causes von Gierke disease.</li><li><b>Gene for N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase</b> — Phosphotransferase deficiency causes I-cell disease.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Zellweger: hypotonia + seizures + hepatomegaly + renal cysts + craniofacial dysmorphism.</p>","w":["mtDNA tRNA mutations cause mitochondrial disorders (e.g., MELAS, MERRF).","Dystrophin mutations cause muscular dystrophy.","Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency causes von Gierke disease.","Phosphotransferase deficiency causes I-cell disease.","Correct — a PEX (peroxin) gene mutation."]},"fc36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal proteins are made on free ribosomes and imported post-translationally via peroxins that recognize a C-terminal tripeptide (PTS1, Ser-Lys-Leu).</p><p>The PTS1 receptor (PEX5) binds the cargo in the cytosol, docks at the peroxisomal membrane, translocates it (even folded proteins can be imported), and recycles to the cytosol. PTS2 cargo uses PEX7. Unlike lysosomal or secretory proteins, peroxisomal enzymes never pass through the ER or Golgi. Peroxisomal membrane proteins use separate peroxins (PEX3, PEX19). Peroxisomes multiply by growth and fission.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleus</b> — Nuclear import uses nuclear localization signals and nuclear pores, not peroxins.</li><li><b>Secretory vesicle</b> — Secretory proteins are made on the RER and pass through the Golgi.</li><li><b>Lysosome</b> — Lysosomal enzymes are made on the RER and sorted by mannose-6-phosphate in the Golgi.</li><li><b>Golgi apparatus</b> — Proteins don't enter the Golgi directly from free ribosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PTS1 = C-terminal SKL; mitochondrial = N-terminal amphipathic signal; nuclear = NLS (Lys/Arg-rich).</p>","w":["Nuclear import uses nuclear localization signals and nuclear pores, not peroxins.","Secretory proteins are made on the RER and pass through the Golgi.","Correct — PTS-tagged enzymes are imported into peroxisomes.","Lysosomal enzymes are made on the RER and sorted by mannose-6-phosphate in the Golgi.","Proteins don't enter the Golgi directly from free ribosomes."]},"fc37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomes help synthesize plasmalogens (ether phospholipids important in myelin), bile acids, cholesterol and DHA.</p><p>The first steps of plasmalogen synthesis occur only in peroxisomes. Plasmalogens make up a large fraction of myelin and cardiac membranes, which helps explain the neurological features of peroxisomal disorders. Peroxisomes also shorten the cholesterol side chain during bile acid synthesis and help synthesize DHA, an ω-3 fatty acid. Catabolic functions include VLCFA β-oxidation, phytanic acid α-oxidation, and D-amino acid and polyamine oxidation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Synthesis of glycogen from glucose-1-phosphate</b> — Glycogen is synthesized in the cytosol.</li><li><b>Synthesis of ribosomal RNA in the nucleolus</b> — rRNA is made in the nucleolus.</li><li><b>Synthesis of secretory proteins for export</b> — Secretory proteins are made on the RER.</li><li><b>Synthesis of ATP by oxidative phosphorylation</b> — Oxidative phosphorylation occurs on the inner mitochondrial membrane; peroxisomal oxidation makes H₂O₂, not ATP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata = plasmalogen synthesis defect (PEX7).</p>","w":["Glycogen is synthesized in the cytosol.","Correct — peroxisomes synthesize plasmalogens and bile salts.","rRNA is made in the nucleolus.","Secretory proteins are made on the RER.","Oxidative phosphorylation occurs on the inner mitochondrial membrane; peroxisomal oxidation makes H₂O₂, not ATP."]},"fc38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peroxisomal β-oxidation: acts on VLCFAs, first step by acyl-CoA oxidase (electrons → O₂ → H₂O₂), not coupled to ATP synthesis, and stops at medium-chain fatty acids for mitochondria to finish.</p><p>In mitochondria, the first step uses acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, which passes electrons via FADH₂ to the ETC, producing ATP. In peroxisomes, acyl-CoA oxidase passes electrons straight to O₂, producing H₂O₂ and heat instead of ATP. Peroxisomal β-oxidation runs only a few cycles; the shortened acyl-CoAs and acetyl-CoA are converted to carnitine esters by peroxisomal carnitine acyltransferases and exported to mitochondria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peroxisomal β-oxidation completely oxidizes fatty acids to acetyl-CoA without mitochondrial help</b> — Peroxisomes only shorten VLCFAs; mitochondria complete oxidation.</li><li><b>Peroxisomal β-oxidation handles only short-chain fatty acids</b> — Peroxisomes handle very-long-chain, not short-chain, fatty acids.</li><li><b>Peroxisomal β-oxidation is coupled to ATP synthesis</b> — Peroxisomal β-oxidation isn't coupled to ATP synthesis.</li><li><b>Peroxisomal β-oxidation begins with acyl-CoA dehydrogenase linked to the electron transport chain</b> — The first peroxisomal step is acyl-CoA oxidase, which uses O₂ (making H₂O₂), not the ETC.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mitochondrial: dehydrogenase → FADH₂ → ATP; peroxisomal: oxidase → H₂O₂ → heat.</p>","w":["Peroxisomes only shorten VLCFAs; mitochondria complete oxidation.","Peroxisomes handle very-long-chain, not short-chain, fatty acids.","Peroxisomal β-oxidation isn't coupled to ATP synthesis.","Correct — electrons go to O₂ forming H₂O₂, and VLCFAs are shortened.","The first peroxisomal step is acyl-CoA oxidase, which uses O₂ (making H₂O₂), not the ETC."]},"fc39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Catalase's peroxidatic reaction uses H₂O₂ to oxidize substrates such as ethanol, formaldehyde, methanol and phenols.</p><p>Catalase has two modes: catalatic (2 H₂O₂ → 2 H₂O + O₂) and peroxidatic (H₂O₂ + RH₂ → R + 2 H₂O). Ethanol is metabolized mainly by alcohol dehydrogenase (cytosol), with contributions from MEOS (cytochrome P450 2E1 in the SER, induced by chronic drinking) and catalase (peroxisomes). All produce acetaldehyde, which aldehyde dehydrogenase converts to acetate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cytochrome c</b> — Cytochrome c is a mitochondrial electron carrier, not a catalase substrate.</li><li><b>ATP</b> — ATP isn't oxidized by catalase.</li><li><b>Cholesterol</b> — Cholesterol isn't a catalase substrate.</li><li><b>Glucose</b> — Glucose isn't oxidized by catalase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ethanol metabolism: ADH (main), CYP2E1/MEOS (chronic), catalase (minor).</p>","w":["Correct — catalase can oxidize ethanol using H₂O₂.","Cytochrome c is a mitochondrial electron carrier, not a catalase substrate.","ATP isn't oxidized by catalase.","Cholesterol isn't a catalase substrate.","Glucose isn't oxidized by catalase."]},"fc40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy = ABCD1 mutation → VLCFAs can't enter peroxisomes → VLCFA buildup damages myelin and adrenal cortex.</p><p>ABCD1 encodes ALDP, an ABC transporter that imports VLCFA-CoA into peroxisomes. Accumulated VLCFAs disrupt membranes and trigger inflammatory demyelination of cerebral white matter (often parieto-occipital on MRI) and adrenal insufficiency (fatigue, hyperpigmentation). The childhood cerebral form presents in boys aged 4–10 with behavioral and school problems progressing to severe disability. Adrenomyeloneuropathy is a milder adult form. Diagnosis: elevated plasma VLCFAs. Early hematopoietic stem cell transplant or gene therapy can halt progression.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase</b> — N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase is the I-cell disease enzyme.</li><li><b>Dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin mutations cause muscular dystrophy.</li><li><b>Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase</b> — Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency causes Refsum disease (phytanic acid buildup), not X-linked VLCFA buildup.</li><li><b>Lamin A</b> — Lamin A mutations cause nuclear envelope disorders like progeria.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> X-ALD: boy + behavioral decline + adrenal insufficiency + ↑VLCFA.</p>","w":["N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase is the I-cell disease enzyme.","Dystrophin mutations cause muscular dystrophy.","Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency causes Refsum disease (phytanic acid buildup), not X-linked VLCFA buildup.","Lamin A mutations cause nuclear envelope disorders like progeria.","Correct — ABCD1 transporter deficiency."]},"fc43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Phospholipids are amphipathic (polar head + nonpolar tails); the two leaflets are held together by weak noncovalent (hydrophobic and van der Waals) interactions between tails.</p><p>In water, phospholipids spontaneously form bilayers: heads face the aqueous ICF and ECF, tails hide in the middle. No covalent bonds join the leaflets — the hydrophobic effect drives assembly, which is why membranes can self-seal, fuse with vesicles and bend. Membrane lipids include glycerophospholipids (PC, PE, PS, PI), sphingolipids (sphingomyelin, glycolipids) and cholesterol.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hydrophilic; hydrogen bonds between fatty acid tails and water</b> — The tails are hydrophobic and avoid water; they don't hydrogen bond with it.</li><li><b>Hydrophobic; ionic bonds between phosphate groups</b> — Phospholipids aren't entirely hydrophobic, and the head groups face away from each other toward water.</li><li><b>Hydrophilic; covalent bonds between head groups</b> — A phospholipid isn't entirely hydrophilic, and the leaflets aren't covalently linked.</li><li><b>Amphipathic; disulfide bonds between leaflets</b> — There are no disulfide bonds in a lipid bilayer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amphipathic molecules: phospholipids, bile salts, detergents.</p>","w":["The tails are hydrophobic and avoid water; they don't hydrogen bond with it.","Phospholipids aren't entirely hydrophobic, and the head groups face away from each other toward water.","Correct — amphipathic, with leaflets held by weak noncovalent tail interactions.","A phospholipid isn't entirely hydrophilic, and the leaflets aren't covalently linked.","There are no disulfide bonds in a lipid bilayer."]},"fc44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane asymmetry: outer leaflet = phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin (plus glycolipids); inner leaflet = phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylinositol.</p><p>Flippases (ATP-dependent) keep PS and PE on the inner leaflet; floppases and scramblases can move lipids outward. PI on the inner leaflet is phosphorylated to PIP₂, which is cleaved by phospholipase C into IP₃ and DAG for signaling. Negatively charged PS on the inner face also helps anchor cytoskeletal and signaling proteins. Loss of asymmetry has consequences: PS exposure triggers phagocytosis and provides a surface for clotting factor assembly on activated platelets.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Phosphatidylserine and phosphatidylinositol</b> — Phosphatidylserine and phosphatidylinositol both face the cytosol.</li><li><b>Phosphatidylethanolamine and phosphatidylserine</b> — PE and PS are both inner-leaflet phospholipids.</li><li><b>Phosphatidylinositol and phosphatidylethanolamine</b> — PI and PE are both inner-leaflet phospholipids.</li><li><b>Cardiolipin and phosphatidylserine</b> — Cardiolipin is a mitochondrial inner membrane lipid, and PS is inner-leaflet.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Choline outside\": PC and sphingomyelin (both contain choline) are outer-leaflet.</p>","w":["Phosphatidylserine and phosphatidylinositol both face the cytosol.","Correct — PC and sphingomyelin are the outer-leaflet phospholipids.","PE and PS are both inner-leaflet phospholipids.","PI and PE are both inner-leaflet phospholipids.","Cardiolipin is a mitochondrial inner membrane lipid, and PS is inner-leaflet."]},"fc45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Phosphatidylserine flipped to the outer leaflet during apoptosis is an \"eat-me\" signal for phagocytes.</p><p>In healthy cells, ATP-dependent flippases keep PS on the inner leaflet. During apoptosis, caspases inactivate flippases and activate scramblases, exposing PS on the cell surface. Macrophages recognize PS (directly or via bridging proteins), engulfing the dying cell before it releases contents — so apoptosis causes no inflammation. Annexin V binds exposed PS and is used to detect apoptotic cells in the lab. PS exposure on activated platelets also supports coagulation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cholesterol</b> — Cholesterol is a sterol, not a phospholipid that flips during apoptosis.</li><li><b>Phosphatidylcholine</b> — Phosphatidylcholine is already on the outer leaflet.</li><li><b>Ganglioside</b> — Gangliosides are glycolipids already on the outer surface.</li><li><b>Sphingomyelin</b> — Sphingomyelin is already on the outer leaflet.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Annexin V binding = PS exposure = early apoptosis marker.</p>","w":["Cholesterol is a sterol, not a phospholipid that flips during apoptosis.","Phosphatidylcholine is already on the outer leaflet.","Gangliosides are glycolipids already on the outer surface.","Correct — externalized phosphatidylserine signals phagocytes.","Sphingomyelin is already on the outer leaflet."]},"fc46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cholesterol inserts parallel to phospholipid tails, with its OH group near the polar heads; at body temperature it stiffens the membrane (decreases fluidity) and decreases permeability.</p><p>The rigid steroid ring fills gaps created by kinks in unsaturated tails, restricting their movement near the head groups. Cholesterol acts as a fluidity buffer: at high temperatures it reduces fluidity, while at low temperatures it prevents tails from packing tightly and crystallizing. It's concentrated in lipid rafts. Spur cells (acanthocytes) in liver disease reflect excess membrane cholesterol.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Perpendicular to the phospholipid tails, spanning both leaflets; increases fluidity</b> — Cholesterol is parallel to the tails, and at body temperature it decreases fluidity.</li><li><b>Restricted to the inner leaflet, where it forms hydrophilic pores</b> — Cholesterol is present in both leaflets and doesn't form pores.</li><li><b>Covalently attached to membrane proteins; increases permeability to ions</b> — Membrane cholesterol isn't covalently bound to proteins.</li><li><b>Buried in the hydrophobic core with its OH group hidden; has no effect on fluidity</b> — Its hydroxyl group interacts with the polar head groups; it isn't buried.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholesterol = fluidity buffer; ↓ permeability to small water-soluble molecules.</p>","w":["Correct — OH at the head groups, ring parallel to tails; stiffens the membrane.","Cholesterol is parallel to the tails, and at body temperature it decreases fluidity.","Cholesterol is present in both leaflets and doesn't form pores.","Membrane cholesterol isn't covalently bound to proteins.","Its hydroxyl group interacts with the polar head groups; it isn't buried."]},"fc47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> More unsaturated fatty acids (cis double bonds → kinks) keep membranes fluid in the cold.</p><p>Saturated tails are straight and pack tightly, making the membrane more rigid (like butter). Cis double bonds introduce kinks that prevent tight packing, increasing fluidity (like oil). Shorter chains also increase fluidity. Organisms adapt to cold by increasing unsaturation and shortening chains (homeoviscous adaptation). In humans, temperature is stable, so cholesterol and lipid composition maintain appropriate fluidity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>More saturated fatty acids (fewer double bonds)</b> — More saturated fatty acids pack tightly and make the membrane more rigid.</li><li><b>Longer fatty acid chains in each phospholipid</b> — Longer chains increase tail interactions and decrease fluidity.</li><li><b>Removal of the kinks from the fatty acid tails</b> — Kinks increase fluidity; removing them would make the membrane stiffer.</li><li><b>Fewer double bonds in the fatty acid tails</b> — Fewer double bonds means more saturation and less fluidity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fluidity ↑ with temperature, unsaturation, shorter chains; cholesterol buffers.</p>","w":["More saturated fatty acids pack tightly and make the membrane more rigid.","Longer chains increase tail interactions and decrease fluidity.","Kinks increase fluidity; removing them would make the membrane stiffer.","Fewer double bonds means more saturation and less fluidity.","Correct — more unsaturated fatty acids increase fluidity."]},"fc48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Glycolipids (e.g., gangliosides) are lipids with sugars that always face outward; they serve as receptors for cholera toxin (GM1) and tetanus toxin (GT1b/GD1b).</p><p>Glycolipids are made in the Golgi and delivered to the outer leaflet, contributing to the glycocalyx. Gangliosides contain sialic acid and are abundant in neurons. Cholera toxin's B subunit binds GM1, allowing the A subunit to enter and activate Gs (↑cAMP → CFTR Cl⁻ secretion → watery diarrhea). Tetanus toxin binds gangliosides on motor nerve terminals and travels retrogradely to inhibit release of GABA and glycine from inhibitory interneurons (spastic paralysis, trismus). ABO antigens are carbohydrates on both glycolipids and glycoproteins.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cholesterol</b> — Cholesterol has no carbohydrate group.</li><li><b>Peripheral membrane proteins</b> — The question describes lipids, not proteins.</li><li><b>Phospholipids</b> — Phospholipids don't carry the carbohydrate groups that serve as toxin receptors.</li><li><b>Integral membrane proteins</b> — The question describes lipids, not proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholera toxin → GM1 ganglioside; Tay–Sachs → GM2 accumulation.</p>","w":["Cholesterol has no carbohydrate group.","The question describes lipids, not proteins.","Correct — glycolipids act as receptors for these toxins.","Phospholipids don't carry the carbohydrate groups that serve as toxin receptors.","The question describes lipids, not proteins."]},"fc49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Receptors that bind extracellular ligands and signal inward span the bilayer — they're integral (transmembrane) proteins.</p><p>Integral proteins have hydrophobic segments (α-helices or β-barrels) embedded in the bilayer and can only be removed with detergents. Examples: receptors (GPCRs with 7 transmembrane helices, receptor tyrosine kinases with one), channels, transporters, adhesion molecules. Peripheral proteins attach noncovalently to integral proteins or lipid head groups; lipid-anchored proteins are covalently attached to lipids on one face.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Peripheral protein</b> — Peripheral proteins don't extend into the bilayer, so they couldn't relay a signal from outside to inside.</li><li><b>Lipid-anchored protein</b> — Lipid-anchored proteins (e.g., G proteins, GPI-anchored proteins) sit on one side and don't span the membrane.</li><li><b>Lipid raft</b> — A lipid raft is a membrane region rich in cholesterol and glycosphingolipids, not a protein.</li><li><b>Glycolipid</b> — A glycolipid is a lipid with a sugar attached; receptors are proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GPCR = 7-pass transmembrane; RTK = single-pass.</p>","w":["Peripheral proteins don't extend into the bilayer, so they couldn't relay a signal from outside to inside.","Lipid-anchored proteins (e.g., G proteins, GPI-anchored proteins) sit on one side and don't span the membrane.","Correct — a ligand receptor is an integral membrane protein.","A lipid raft is a membrane region rich in cholesterol and glycosphingolipids, not a protein.","A glycolipid is a lipid with a sugar attached; receptors are proteins."]},"fc50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Plasma membrane proteins follow the secretory pathway: RER ribosome → inserted into RER membrane → Golgi → vesicle → plasma membrane.</p><p>Membrane proteins contain signal sequences and hydrophobic stop-transfer or signal-anchor sequences that lodge them in the RER membrane during translation, fixing their orientation (topology). They then travel in COPII vesicles to the Golgi for glycosylation and sorting, and vesicles carry them to the plasma membrane. When the vesicle fuses, the part that faced the ER/Golgi lumen ends up facing the extracellular space — which is why glycosylated domains face outward.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleolus → nuclear pore → cytosol → plasma membrane</b> — The nucleolus makes rRNA and ribosomes, not membrane proteins.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial ribosome → Golgi → vesicle → membrane</b> — Mitochondria don't export proteins to the Golgi.</li><li><b>Free ribosome → cytosol → directly into the plasma membrane</b> — Free ribosomes make cytosolic, nuclear, peroxisomal and mitochondrial proteins, not plasma membrane proteins.</li><li><b>Free ribosome → peroxisome → lysosome → membrane</b> — Peroxisomes and lysosomes aren't on the route to the plasma membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lumen of ER/Golgi = topologically equivalent to the cell exterior.</p>","w":["The nucleolus makes rRNA and ribosomes, not membrane proteins.","Correct — RER → Golgi → vesicle → membrane.","Mitochondria don't export proteins to the Golgi.","Free ribosomes make cytosolic, nuclear, peroxisomal and mitochondrial proteins, not plasma membrane proteins.","Peroxisomes and lysosomes aren't on the route to the plasma membrane."]},"fc51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Peripheral membrane proteins attach noncovalently to other proteins or lipid heads and don't enter the bilayer — high salt releases them; integral proteins need detergent.</p><p>Spectrin (with actin, ankyrin and band 4.1) forms the RBC's inner membrane skeleton, giving the cell flexibility and its biconcave shape. Ankyrin links spectrin to band 3 (integral anion exchanger); band 4.1 links to glycophorin. Defects in spectrin, ankyrin, band 3 or protein 4.2 cause hereditary spherocytosis (loss of membrane, spherical cells, increased osmotic fragility, splenomegaly). Defects in spectrin interactions also cause hereditary elliptocytosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are anchored covalently by GPI to the outer leaflet</b> — GPI-anchored proteins are covalently lipid-anchored; a salt wash wouldn't release them.</li><li><b>They are lipids with carbohydrate attached to the head group</b> — Lipids with carbohydrates are glycolipids, not proteins.</li><li><b>They are β-barrel channel proteins that cross the membrane</b> — β-barrel proteins span the membrane and are integral.</li><li><b>They span the bilayer with a hydrophobic transmembrane α-helical domain</b> — Spanning the bilayer with a hydrophobic α-helix describes integral proteins, released only by detergent.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hereditary spherocytosis: spectrin/ankyrin/band 3 defects; EMA binding test, ↑osmotic fragility.</p>","w":["GPI-anchored proteins are covalently lipid-anchored; a salt wash wouldn't release them.","Lipids with carbohydrates are glycolipids, not proteins.","β-barrel proteins span the membrane and are integral.","Correct — peripheral proteins bind noncovalently outside the bilayer.","Spanning the bilayer with a hydrophobic α-helix describes integral proteins, released only by detergent."]},"fc52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GPI-anchored proteins are covalently attached to glycophosphatidylinositol in the outer leaflet and have no transmembrane domain.</p><p>GPI anchors are added in the ER after the protein's hydrophobic C-terminal segment is cleaved. GPI-anchored proteins often cluster in lipid rafts. Clinically important examples: CD55 (DAF) and CD59 (MIRL), which protect RBCs from complement. In paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, a PIGA mutation prevents GPI anchor synthesis, so these proteins are missing and complement lyses RBCs (hemolysis, hemoglobinuria, thrombosis). Flow cytometry for CD55/CD59 or FLAER diagnoses PNH.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Multipass transmembrane protein</b> — A multipass transmembrane protein crosses the membrane multiple times; this protein has no transmembrane domain.</li><li><b>Peripheral membrane protein</b> — Peripheral proteins are held noncovalently by other proteins, not by a covalent lipid anchor.</li><li><b>Glycolipid</b> — A glycolipid is a lipid, not a protein anchored to one.</li><li><b>Channel protein</b> — Channels span the bilayer to form a pore.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PNH = PIGA mutation → no GPI anchors → no CD55/CD59 → complement lysis.</p>","w":["Correct — this is a lipid-anchored (GPI-anchored) protein.","A multipass transmembrane protein crosses the membrane multiple times; this protein has no transmembrane domain.","Peripheral proteins are held noncovalently by other proteins, not by a covalent lipid anchor.","A glycolipid is a lipid, not a protein anchored to one.","Channels span the bilayer to form a pore."]},"fc53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> β-barrel: many β-strands rolled into a cylinder forming a water-filled pore (e.g., porins in bacterial and mitochondrial outer membranes).</p><p>Two ways to cross a bilayer: α-helices (about 20 hydrophobic residues each; single-pass or multipass bundles — most plasma membrane proteins, including receptors and channels) or β-barrels (alternating hydrophobic residues facing lipids and hydrophilic residues lining the pore). β-barrels are found in outer membranes of gram-negative bacteria, mitochondria and chloroplasts — consistent with the endosymbiotic origin of mitochondria.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>α-helical bundle</b> — α-helical bundles are made of α-helices, not β-sheets.</li><li><b>Glycocalyx</b> — The glycocalyx is the carbohydrate coat on the cell surface.</li><li><b>GPI anchor</b> — A GPI anchor is a lipid anchor, not a protein fold.</li><li><b>Lipid raft</b> — A lipid raft is a lipid-ordered membrane domain.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Porins: β-barrels in gram-negative outer membrane and mitochondrial outer membrane.</p>","w":["α-helical bundles are made of α-helices, not β-sheets.","The glycocalyx is the carbohydrate coat on the cell surface.","A GPI anchor is a lipid anchor, not a protein fold.","A lipid raft is a lipid-ordered membrane domain.","Correct — β-sheets rolled into a cylinder form a β-barrel."]},"fc54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ABO blood groups are carbohydrate antigens on RBC surface glycoproteins (and glycolipids); type O lacks the A and B sugars.</p><p>All people make the H antigen (a fucose-containing oligosaccharide). The A allele encodes a glycosyltransferase that adds N-acetylgalactosamine to H (A antigen); the B allele adds galactose (B antigen); the O allele encodes an inactive enzyme, leaving only H. Type O people have anti-A and anti-B IgM antibodies; type O RBCs are the universal donor cells. Bombay phenotype lacks even H antigen.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Phosphatidylserine in the inner leaflet</b> — Phosphatidylserine distribution is normal in all blood types.</li><li><b>Spectrin in the membrane cytoskeleton</b> — Type O red cells have normal spectrin.</li><li><b>The band 3 anion exchanger protein</b> — Band 3 is present on all red cells.</li><li><b>Cholesterol in the bilayer</b> — Cholesterol is present in all red cell membranes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> O = universal RBC donor; AB = universal plasma donor and RBC recipient.</p>","w":["Phosphatidylserine distribution is normal in all blood types.","Type O red cells have normal spectrin.","Correct — type O RBCs lack the A or B carbohydrate antigen.","Band 3 is present on all red cells.","Cholesterol is present in all red cell membranes."]},"fc55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Uniporter = one solute (GLUT); symporter = two solutes same direction (SGLT1); antiporter = two solutes opposite directions (Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger).</p><p>In the enterocyte, SGLT1 on the apical membrane cotransports 2 Na⁺ with each glucose (secondary active). Glucose leaves basolaterally via GLUT-2 (uniport, facilitated diffusion), while the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase removes Na⁺. In cardiac myocytes, NCX extrudes 1 Ca²⁺ for 3 Na⁺ entering (antiport, secondary active). Transporters bind solutes and change conformation; channels form pores.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>SGLT1 symporter; GLUT antiporter; Na+/Ca2+ exchanger uniporter</b> — GLUT moves only glucose (uniport), and the exchanger moves two ions in opposite directions (antiport).</li><li><b>SGLT1 antiporter; GLUT symporter; Na+/Ca2+ exchanger uniporter</b> — SGLT1 moves both solutes the same way (symport), and the exchanger is an antiporter.</li><li><b>All three are channels</b> — These transporters change conformation to move solutes; channels form open pores.</li><li><b>SGLT1 uniporter; GLUT symporter; Na+/Ca2+ exchanger antiporter</b> — SGLT1 moves two solutes, so it isn't a uniporter; GLUT moves only one.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Enterocyte: apical SGLT1 (symport) → basolateral GLUT-2 (uniport) + Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</p>","w":["GLUT moves only glucose (uniport), and the exchanger moves two ions in opposite directions (antiport).","Correct — symporter, uniporter, antiporter.","SGLT1 moves both solutes the same way (symport), and the exchanger is an antiporter.","These transporters change conformation to move solutes; channels form open pores.","SGLT1 moves two solutes, so it isn't a uniporter; GLUT moves only one."]},"fc56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Multidrug resistance (P-glycoprotein/MDR1) is an ABC transporter that uses ATP to pump hydrophobic drugs out of cells.</p><p>ABC (ATP-binding cassette) transporters share conserved ATP-binding domains. MDR1 (P-glycoprotein, ABCB1) exports many structurally unrelated hydrophobic drugs (vinca alkaloids, anthracyclines, taxanes), explaining resistance to several chemotherapy agents at once. P-gp is also normally expressed in the intestine, blood–brain barrier and kidney, limiting drug absorption and CNS penetration. Other ABC transporters: CFTR (Cl⁻ channel), ABCD1 (VLCFA into peroxisomes; X-ALD), ABCA1 (cholesterol efflux; Tangier disease).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lamins</b> — Lamins form the nuclear lamina.</li><li><b>Glucose uniporters</b> — Glucose uniporters move glucose down its gradient.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated ion channels</b> — Ion channels let ions flow down their gradients; they don't pump drugs using ATP.</li><li><b>Caveolins</b> — Caveolins shape caveolae.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ABC transporters: MDR1/P-gp, CFTR, ABCD1 (X-ALD), ABCA1 (Tangier).</p>","w":["Lamins form the nuclear lamina.","Glucose uniporters move glucose down its gradient.","Ion channels let ions flow down their gradients; they don't pump drugs using ATP.","Correct — MDR protein is an ABC transporter.","Caveolins shape caveolae."]},"fc57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Channels form selective hydrophilic pores that let ions flow down their electrochemical gradients — far faster than transporters, which bind and change shape for each molecule.</p><p>Channels conduct millions of ions per second because they don't need to bind and move each ion individually; they open and close by gating. Selectivity filters choose which ion passes based on size, charge and hydration. Transporters (uniporters, symporters, antiporters, pumps) bind solutes, saturate, and move far fewer molecules per second. Only pumps (and secondary active transporters) move solutes against gradients.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are peripheral proteins that do not enter the lipid bilayer</b> — Channels span the membrane; they're integral, not peripheral, proteins.</li><li><b>They bind each solute and change conformation for every molecule moved</b> — Binding each solute and changing conformation describes transporters.</li><li><b>They always hydrolyze ATP to move ions against their electrochemical gradient</b> — Channels don't use ATP; ATP-driven pumping is done by pumps such as ABC transporters.</li><li><b>They move two different solutes in opposite directions at once</b> — Moving two solutes in opposite directions describes antiporters.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Channel = pore, fast, gated, downhill; carrier = binding, slower, saturable.</p>","w":["Correct — channels form selective hydrophilic pores.","Channels span the membrane; they're integral, not peripheral, proteins.","Binding each solute and changing conformation describes transporters.","Channels don't use ATP; ATP-driven pumping is done by pumps such as ABC transporters.","Moving two solutes in opposite directions describes antiporters."]},"fc58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane receptors have three domains: extracellular (ligand binding), transmembrane (crosses bilayer), and intracellular (signals inside).</p><p>Ligand binding changes the receptor's shape, which is transmitted through the transmembrane segment to the intracellular domain. That domain may activate a G protein (GPCRs), have intrinsic kinase activity (receptor tyrosine kinases like the insulin receptor), recruit cytoplasmic kinases (JAK–STAT for cytokine receptors), or form an ion channel (ligand-gated channels). Lipophilic hormones (steroids, thyroid hormone) instead cross the membrane and bind intracellular receptors.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A peripheral domain that never contacts the bilayer</b> — Receptors span the bilayer and contact it.</li><li><b>Two extracellular domains joined by a GPI anchor</b> — GPI-anchored proteins don't span the membrane to relay signals inward this way.</li><li><b>A carbohydrate domain that faces the cytoplasm</b> — Carbohydrates of membrane glycoproteins face outward, not the cytoplasm.</li><li><b>A single cytoplasmic domain with no membrane-spanning part</b> — A cytoplasmic-only protein couldn't bind an extracellular hormone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Water-soluble hormones → surface receptors; lipid-soluble → intracellular receptors.</p>","w":["Receptors span the bilayer and contact it.","GPI-anchored proteins don't span the membrane to relay signals inward this way.","Carbohydrates of membrane glycoproteins face outward, not the cytoplasm.","A cytoplasmic-only protein couldn't bind an extracellular hormone.","Correct — extracellular, transmembrane and intracellular domains."]},"fc59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caveolae are flask-shaped, cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich invaginations (a type of lipid raft) shaped by caveolin proteins.</p><p>Lipid rafts are ordered membrane microdomains enriched in cholesterol, sphingolipids and saturated fatty acids, concentrating signaling molecules and GPI-anchored proteins. Caveolae (50–100 nm) are abundant in endothelial cells, adipocytes and smooth muscle; they function in transcytosis across endothelium, signal transduction, mechanosensing (flattening under stretch) and clathrin-independent endocytosis. Some viruses and toxins use them to enter cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Components of phospholipids</b> — Caveolae are whole membrane domains, not components of a phospholipid molecule.</li><li><b>Intercalated between cholesterol molecules</b> — Cholesterol is concentrated within caveolae; caveolae aren't wedged between cholesterol molecules.</li><li><b>Composed of disorderly phospholipids</b> — Lipid rafts are more ordered (saturated tails, high cholesterol), not disorderly.</li><li><b>Regions with high carbohydrate content</b> — High carbohydrate content describes the glycocalyx.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caveolin = caveolae; clathrin = coated pits (receptor-mediated endocytosis).</p>","w":["Caveolae are whole membrane domains, not components of a phospholipid molecule.","Cholesterol is concentrated within caveolae; caveolae aren't wedged between cholesterol molecules.","Lipid rafts are more ordered (saturated tails, high cholesterol), not disorderly.","High carbohydrate content describes the glycocalyx.","Correct — caveolae are cholesterol-enriched invaginations."]},"fc60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Frye–Edidin cell-fusion experiment showed lateral diffusion of membrane proteins — evidence for the fluid mosaic model (Singer–Nicolson).</p><p>In the fluid mosaic model, the bilayer behaves as a two-dimensional fluid in which lipids and many proteins move laterally. Lipids diffuse laterally quickly and rotate, but rarely flip between leaflets without enzymes. Protein movement can be restricted by the cytoskeleton, tight junctions (fence function separating apical and basolateral domains) or binding to other cells/extracellular matrix. Mixing was temperature-dependent and didn't require ATP or protein synthesis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Proteins are continuously degraded and resynthesized at the surface</b> — Mixing within 40 minutes reflects diffusion, not degradation and resynthesis.</li><li><b>Phospholipids flip-flop rapidly between leaflets</b> — Phospholipids normally don't flip-flop between leaflets; this experiment concerns lateral movement.</li><li><b>Cholesterol forms covalent bonds with proteins</b> — Cholesterol doesn't bind proteins covalently.</li><li><b>Membrane proteins are fixed in place by the glycocalyx</b> — If the glycocalyx fixed proteins in place, they couldn't intermix.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lateral diffusion = fast; transverse flip-flop = rare (needs flippases/scramblases).</p>","w":["Mixing within 40 minutes reflects diffusion, not degradation and resynthesis.","Phospholipids normally don't flip-flop between leaflets; this experiment concerns lateral movement.","Correct — membrane components move laterally within the membrane.","Cholesterol doesn't bind proteins covalently.","If the glycocalyx fixed proteins in place, they couldn't intermix."]},"fc61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cell polarity = distinct apical and basolateral membrane domains with different proteins and functions, maintained by tight junctions.</p><p>Tight junctions (claudins, occludins) seal adjacent cells (barrier function) and prevent apical and basolateral membrane proteins from mixing (fence function). Polarized epithelia use this to perform vectorial transport: e.g., enterocytes have SGLT1 and brush-border enzymes apically, and GLUT-2 and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase basolaterally. Sorting signals in the trans-Golgi network direct proteins to the correct domain.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Phospholipid asymmetry</b> — Phospholipid asymmetry refers to inner vs outer leaflet lipids, not apical vs basal regions.</li><li><b>Membrane fluidity</b> — Membrane fluidity describes how freely membrane components move.</li><li><b>Treadmilling</b> — Treadmilling describes actin filament turnover.</li><li><b>Dynamic instability</b> — Dynamic instability describes microtubule growth and shrinkage.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tight junctions: barrier + fence → epithelial polarity.</p>","w":["Phospholipid asymmetry refers to inner vs outer leaflet lipids, not apical vs basal regions.","Correct — this is cell polarity.","Membrane fluidity describes how freely membrane components move.","Treadmilling describes actin filament turnover.","Dynamic instability describes microtubule growth and shrinkage."]},"fc62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The glycocalyx is the carbohydrate-rich coat on the outer cell surface (proteoglycans, glycoproteins, glycolipids); in vessels it limits fluid and protein leak.</p><p>Endothelial glycocalyx contributes to the vascular barrier (acts as a molecular sieve, effectively part of the reflection coefficient for proteins), reduces leukocyte and platelet adhesion, and senses shear stress (triggering NO release). Sepsis, ischemia–reperfusion and hyperglycemia shed the glycocalyx, increasing capillary permeability, edema and inflammation. On enterocytes, the glycocalyx contains brush-border digestive enzymes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>A layer of peripheral cytoskeletal proteins on the cytosolic face</b> — The glycocalyx is on the outer surface and made of sugars, not cytoskeletal proteins.</li><li><b>A cholesterol-rich lipid raft that actively pumps sodium out</b> — Lipid rafts are cholesterol-rich signaling domains and don't pump sodium.</li><li><b>A caveola, a flask-shaped invagination formed by caveolin</b> — Caveolae are invaginations, not a luminal sugar coat.</li><li><b>The nuclear lamina lining the inner nuclear membrane</b> — The nuclear lamina lines the inner nuclear membrane.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Glycocalyx: cell recognition, protection, vascular barrier, brush-border enzymes.</p>","w":["The glycocalyx is on the outer surface and made of sugars, not cytoskeletal proteins.","Lipid rafts are cholesterol-rich signaling domains and don't pump sodium.","Caveolae are invaginations, not a luminal sugar coat.","Correct — the glycocalyx limits fluid loss from vessels.","The nuclear lamina lines the inner nuclear membrane."]},"fc63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lipid rafts: cholesterol- and sphingolipid-rich domains with saturated tails → more ordered, less fluid platforms that cluster signaling proteins.</p><p>Saturated tails pack tightly, and cholesterol fills gaps, making rafts thicker and more ordered than the surrounding membrane. Rafts concentrate GPI-anchored proteins, receptors and signaling enzymes (e.g., Src-family kinases), increasing the efficiency of signaling. They also participate in membrane trafficking and are used by some viruses (HIV, influenza) for entry and budding. Caveolae are a specialized raft subtype.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It consists of carbohydrate without lipid</b> — Rafts are lipid domains (cholesterol, sphingolipids), not pure carbohydrate.</li><li><b>It lacks proteins entirely</b> — Rafts concentrate signaling proteins rather than lacking proteins.</li><li><b>It is found only in prokaryotes</b> — Lipid rafts are eukaryotic plasma membrane domains.</li><li><b>It is more fluid and less ordered</b> — Saturated tails and extra cholesterol make rafts less, not more, fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lipid raft = cholesterol + sphingolipids + saturated tails + signaling proteins.</p>","w":["Correct — rafts are less fluid and more ordered.","Rafts are lipid domains (cholesterol, sphingolipids), not pure carbohydrate.","Rafts concentrate signaling proteins rather than lacking proteins.","Lipid rafts are eukaryotic plasma membrane domains.","Saturated tails and extra cholesterol make rafts less, not more, fluid."]},"fc64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caveolae are flask-shaped lipid-raft invaginations built by caveolin; abundant in endothelial cells, adipocytes and smooth muscle; absent in neurons.</p><p>Caveolin proteins insert into the inner leaflet, binding cholesterol and bending the membrane; cavins stabilize the coat. Caveolae mediate transcytosis of albumin and other molecules across capillary endothelium, cluster signaling molecules (e.g., eNOS is regulated by caveolin-1), store and sense membrane tension, and mediate clathrin-independent endocytosis. Distinguish from clathrin-coated pits, which mediate receptor-mediated endocytosis (e.g., LDL).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin forms the red cell membrane skeleton.</li><li><b>Lamin</b> — Lamins form the nuclear lamina.</li><li><b>Clathrin</b> — Clathrin coats pits and vesicles for receptor-mediated endocytosis — a different structure.</li><li><b>Dynein</b> — Dynein is a microtubule motor protein.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caveolin-1 regulates eNOS in endothelium.</p>","w":["Spectrin forms the red cell membrane skeleton.","Lamins form the nuclear lamina.","Clathrin coats pits and vesicles for receptor-mediated endocytosis — a different structure.","Dynein is a microtubule motor protein.","Correct — caveolin is the main structural protein of caveolae."]},"fc65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane functions: envelope/structure, protection, selective permeability (transport), and signal transduction — not rRNA synthesis.</p><p>The plasma membrane also mediates cell adhesion (junctions, integrins), cell recognition (glycocalyx, MHC) and endocytosis/exocytosis. rRNA is transcribed by RNA polymerase I in the nucleolus and assembled with ribosomal proteins into subunits that are exported to the cytoplasm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transducing extracellular signals into intracellular events</b> — Transducing extracellular signals via receptors is a membrane function.</li><li><b>Enveloping the cell and maintaining structural integrity</b> — Enveloping the cell and maintaining integrity is a membrane function.</li><li><b>Protecting intracellular organelles from the external environment</b> — Protecting intracellular organelles from the external environment is a membrane function.</li><li><b>Regulating the traffic of ions and macromolecules as a semipermeable barrier</b> — Regulating ion and macromolecule traffic is a membrane function.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nucleolus → rRNA (RNA Pol I).</p>","w":["Transducing extracellular signals via receptors is a membrane function.","Enveloping the cell and maintaining integrity is a membrane function.","Correct — rRNA synthesis isn't a membrane function.","Protecting intracellular organelles from the external environment is a membrane function.","Regulating ion and macromolecule traffic is a membrane function."]},"fc66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Actin filaments polymerize from ATP-bound G-actin and show treadmilling (net addition at the plus end balanced by loss at the minus end).</p><p>G-actin–ATP adds to the plus (barbed) end; within the filament ATP is hydrolyzed to ADP, and ADP-actin dissociates from the minus (pointed) end. At a certain G-actin concentration, the filament length is constant while subunits flux through — treadmilling, which drives lamellipodia at the leading edge of migrating cells. Toxins: phalloidin stabilizes F-actin; cytochalasins block plus-end growth; latrunculin sequesters G-actin. Microtubules show dynamic instability with GTP-tubulin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intermediate filament</b> — Intermediate filaments aren't polar and don't use ATP to polymerize.</li><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is an intermediate filament and doesn't treadmill with ATP.</li><li><b>Microtubule</b> — Microtubules use GTP (bound to tubulin), not ATP.</li><li><b>Tubulin</b> — Tubulin is the GTP-binding subunit of microtubules.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Actin = ATP + treadmilling; microtubules = GTP + dynamic instability.</p>","w":["Correct — ATP-dependent polymerization plus treadmilling identifies actin.","Intermediate filaments aren't polar and don't use ATP to polymerize.","Keratin is an intermediate filament and doesn't treadmill with ATP.","Microtubules use GTP (bound to tubulin), not ATP.","Tubulin is the GTP-binding subunit of microtubules."]},"fc67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dystrophin links cortical actin filaments to the dystroglycan complex and, through it, to laminin in the basal lamina.</p><p>The dystrophin–glycoprotein complex (dystrophin, dystroglycans, sarcoglycans) transmits force from the sarcomere to the extracellular matrix and stabilizes the sarcolemma. Without dystrophin (Duchenne — frameshift deletion in the large DMD gene on Xp21), contraction tears the membrane, Ca²⁺ enters, fibers necrose and are replaced by fat and connective tissue. Features: proximal weakness, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy, high CK, dilated cardiomyopathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intermediate filaments</b> — The intermediate filament of muscle is desmin, which isn't anchored by dystrophin.</li><li><b>Microtubules</b> — Microtubules aren't the component dystrophin connects to the ECM.</li><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin is a motor protein, not a cytoskeletal filament tied to the basal lamina by dystrophin.</li><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is an intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Dystrophin: actin → dystroglycan → laminin (basal lamina).</p>","w":["Correct — actin filaments can't anchor to the basal lamina without dystrophin.","The intermediate filament of muscle is desmin, which isn't anchored by dystrophin.","Microtubules aren't the component dystrophin connects to the ECM.","Myosin is a motor protein, not a cytoskeletal filament tied to the basal lamina by dystrophin.","Vimentin is an intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells."]},"fc68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> RBCs depend on a spectrin–actin membrane skeleton (tethered by ankyrin and band 4.1) to withstand the shear stress of circulation.</p><p>RBCs repeatedly squeeze through capillaries narrower than their diameter and through splenic slits. The spectrin network is flexible, letting the cell deform and spring back. Vertical links (spectrin–ankyrin–band 3) prevent membrane loss; horizontal links (spectrin–actin–band 4.1) maintain the lattice. Defects cause hereditary spherocytosis (vertical) or hereditary elliptocytosis (horizontal).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epithelial cell</b> — Epithelial cells rely on keratin intermediate filaments and junctions.</li><li><b>Muscle cell</b> — Muscle relies on dystrophin and desmin.</li><li><b>Neuron</b> — Neurons rely on neurofilaments and microtubules (though they contain spectrin, they don't depend on it for this purpose in the same way).</li><li><b>White blood cell</b> — White cells don't depend on a spectrin skeleton as RBCs do.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spherocytosis: vertical link defects; elliptocytosis: horizontal link defects.</p>","w":["Epithelial cells rely on keratin intermediate filaments and junctions.","Muscle relies on dystrophin and desmin.","Neurons rely on neurofilaments and microtubules (though they contain spectrin, they don't depend on it for this purpose in the same way).","Correct — red blood cells require spectrin for membrane stability.","White cells don't depend on a spectrin skeleton as RBCs do."]},"fc69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Filament sizes: actin microfilaments ~7 nm &lt; intermediate filaments ~10 nm &lt; microtubules ~25 nm.</p><p>Microfilaments (actin): thin, polar, ATP-dependent; involved in cell shape, motility, cytokinesis and microvilli. Intermediate filaments: rope-like, nonpolar, very strong, tissue-specific (keratin, vimentin, desmin, GFAP, neurofilaments, lamins); provide mechanical strength. Microtubules: hollow tubes of 13 protofilaments of α/β-tubulin; polar, GTP-dependent; involved in intracellular transport, mitotic spindles, cilia and flagella.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intermediate filaments, actin microfilaments, microtubules</b> — Intermediate filaments are larger than actin microfilaments.</li><li><b>Actin microfilaments, microtubules, intermediate filaments</b> — Microtubules (25 nm) are larger than intermediate filaments (10 nm).</li><li><b>Microtubules, actin microfilaments, intermediate filaments</b> — Microtubules are the largest, not the smallest.</li><li><b>Microtubules, intermediate filaments, actin microfilaments</b> — This order is reversed — microtubules are the largest.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Intermediate\" = between actin and microtubules in size.</p>","w":["Intermediate filaments are larger than actin microfilaments.","Correct — actin, intermediate filaments, microtubules.","Microtubules (25 nm) are larger than intermediate filaments (10 nm).","Microtubules are the largest, not the smallest.","This order is reversed — microtubules are the largest."]},"fc70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ATP-bound G-actin adds preferentially at the plus (barbed) end of the polar actin filament.</p><p>Polymerization starts with nucleation (slow, helped by Arp2/3 for branched networks or formins for unbranched filaments). The plus end has a lower critical concentration, so it grows faster. After incorporation, ATP is hydrolyzed to ADP-Pi and then ADP, which destabilizes older parts of the filament; cofilin severs ADP-actin. Profilin promotes ADP→ATP exchange on G-actin, recharging subunits.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both ends equally; no nucleotide required</b> — Actin filaments are polar, and subunits must bind ATP.</li><li><b>Plus end; GTP</b> — GTP is used by tubulin, not actin.</li><li><b>Minus end; ADP</b> — ADP-actin tends to dissociate from the minus end rather than drive growth.</li><li><b>Minus end; GTP</b> — Actin grows mainly at the plus end and binds ATP, not GTP.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Arp2/3 → branched actin (lamellipodia); formins → unbranched (filopodia, stress fibers).</p>","w":["Actin filaments are polar, and subunits must bind ATP.","GTP is used by tubulin, not actin.","ADP-actin tends to dissociate from the minus end rather than drive growth.","Correct — plus end, ATP.","Actin grows mainly at the plus end and binds ATP, not GTP."]},"fc71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The actin–myosin II contractile ring divides the cytoplasm in cytokinesis; without it, nuclear division still occurs, producing binucleate cells.</p><p>During anaphase and telophase, a ring of actin filaments and myosin II assembles beneath the plasma membrane at the cell's equator (positioned by the spindle midzone and RhoA signaling). Myosin pulls actin filaments, constricting the cleavage furrow until the cell is pinched in two. Cytochalasin B inhibits actin polymerization and blocks cytokinesis. Microtubule poisons (colchicine, vinca alkaloids), by contrast, block the spindle and arrest cells in metaphase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Centrosome</b> — The centrosome organizes microtubules, not the contractile ring.</li><li><b>Basal body</b> — Basal bodies anchor cilia and are built from microtubules.</li><li><b>Nuclear lamina</b> — The nuclear lamina is made of lamins (intermediate filaments).</li><li><b>Mitotic spindle</b> — The mitotic spindle is made of microtubules and worked here, since nuclei divided.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytokinesis = actin; karyokinesis = microtubules.</p>","w":["Correct — the actin contractile ring failed to form.","The centrosome organizes microtubules, not the contractile ring.","Basal bodies anchor cilia and are built from microtubules.","The nuclear lamina is made of lamins (intermediate filaments).","The mitotic spindle is made of microtubules and worked here, since nuclei divided."]},"fc72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Stress fibers are contractile bundles of actin and non-muscle myosin II ending at focal adhesions (cell–ECM contacts via integrins).</p><p>At focal adhesions, integrins bind ECM proteins (fibronectin, collagen) outside and link via talin, vinculin and α-actinin to actin inside. Stress fibers generate tension that lets fibroblasts pull on the matrix during migration and wound contraction (myofibroblasts express α-smooth muscle actin). Actin also forms the cortical network under the plasma membrane, microvilli cores and lamellipodia/filopodia at the leading edge.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells and isn't contractile.</li><li><b>Neurofilaments</b> — Neurofilaments are found in neurons.</li><li><b>Nuclear lamins</b> — Nuclear lamins line the inner nuclear membrane.</li><li><b>Microtubules</b> — Microtubules don't form contractile bundles anchored to the ECM.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Focal adhesions: integrin–talin–vinculin–actin; myofibroblasts contract wounds.</p>","w":["Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells and isn't contractile.","Neurofilaments are found in neurons.","Nuclear lamins line the inner nuclear membrane.","Microtubules don't form contractile bundles anchored to the ECM.","Correct — stress fibers are actin microfilaments."]},"fc73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apical constriction by actin–myosin bundles narrows the tops of neural plate cells, folding the epithelium into a tube.</p><p>An actin–myosin belt lies just beneath the apical surface, associated with adherens junctions. Contraction makes cells wedge-shaped, bending the sheet. Similar actin-driven folding forms the neural tube, lens vesicle and other epithelial tubes. Failure of neural tube closure leads to neural tube defects (anencephaly, spina bifida), linked to folate deficiency and other factors.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is an intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells and isn't contractile.</li><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle.</li><li><b>Glial fibrillary acidic protein</b> — GFAP is the intermediate filament of glial cells.</li><li><b>Lamin A</b> — Lamin A supports the nuclear envelope.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Neural tube closes by end of week 4; folate reduces NTD risk.</p>","w":["Vimentin is an intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells and isn't contractile.","Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle.","Correct — apical actin contraction folds epithelia into tubes.","GFAP is the intermediate filament of glial cells.","Lamin A supports the nuclear envelope."]},"fc74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hereditary spherocytosis: defect in RBC membrane skeleton proteins (spectrin, ankyrin, band 3, protein 4.2) → loss of membrane → spherocytes → splenic destruction.</p><p>Without stable vertical links, bits of lipid membrane bleb off, so the cell loses surface area relative to volume and becomes spherical, losing central pallor. Spherocytes are rigid and trapped in splenic cords, where macrophages remove them. Features: anemia, jaundice, splenomegaly, pigment gallstones, aplastic crises with parvovirus B19. Labs: increased MCHC, reticulocytosis, increased osmotic fragility, negative Coombs test, reduced EMA binding. Usually autosomal dominant. Splenectomy improves anemia (Howell–Jolly bodies appear afterward).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is the muscle intermediate filament.</li><li><b>Dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin defects cause muscular dystrophy.</li><li><b>Tubulin</b> — Tubulin forms microtubules.</li><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is an epithelial intermediate filament.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spherocytosis: ↑MCHC, ↑osmotic fragility, Coombs negative, splenomegaly.</p>","w":["Desmin is the muscle intermediate filament.","Correct — a spectrin defect causes spherocytosis.","Dystrophin defects cause muscular dystrophy.","Tubulin forms microtubules.","Keratin is an epithelial intermediate filament."]},"fc75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Rigid spherocytes are trapped and destroyed in the spleen (extravascular hemolysis).</p><p>The red pulp's cords and narrow endothelial slits act as a filter; deformable normal RBCs pass, but spherocytes can't. Splenic macrophages phagocytose them. That's why patients develop splenomegaly and why splenectomy (ideally after age 5, with vaccination against encapsulated organisms) corrects the anemia even though spherocytes persist. Intravascular hemolysis (e.g., complement in PNH, mechanical valves) instead causes hemoglobinuria and low haptoglobin.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Liver sinusoids</b> — The spleen, not the liver, is the main site of spherocyte destruction.</li><li><b>Within the circulation by complement</b> — Complement-mediated intravascular lysis isn't the mechanism in hereditary spherocytosis.</li><li><b>Bone marrow</b> — The bone marrow makes RBCs; it doesn't destroy spherocytes.</li><li><b>Kidney glomeruli</b> — The kidney isn't the site of RBC destruction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Extravascular hemolysis: ↑unconjugated bilirubin, splenomegaly; intravascular: hemoglobinuria, ↓haptoglobin.</p>","w":["The spleen, not the liver, is the main site of spherocyte destruction.","Complement-mediated intravascular lysis isn't the mechanism in hereditary spherocytosis.","The bone marrow makes RBCs; it doesn't destroy spherocytes.","Correct — spherocytes are destroyed in the spleen.","The kidney isn't the site of RBC destruction."]},"fc76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ankyrin anchors the spectrin–actin network to the RBC membrane by binding band 3 (the Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger).</p><p>Vertical connections: spectrin ↔ ankyrin ↔ band 3 (with protein 4.2). Horizontal connections: spectrin tetramers ↔ short actin filaments ↔ band 4.1 ↔ glycophorin C. Ankyrin mutations are the most common cause of hereditary spherocytosis. Ankyrins also anchor ion channels in neurons (e.g., at nodes of Ranvier) and cardiomyocytes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dynein</b> — Dynein is a minus-end-directed microtubule motor.</li><li><b>Kinesin</b> — Kinesin is a plus-end-directed microtubule motor.</li><li><b>Caveolin</b> — Caveolin forms caveolae.</li><li><b>Lamin B</b> — Lamin B is part of the nuclear lamina.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ankyrin = most commonly mutated protein in hereditary spherocytosis.</p>","w":["Correct — ankyrin tethers the spectrin–actin network to the membrane.","Dynein is a minus-end-directed microtubule motor.","Kinesin is a plus-end-directed microtubule motor.","Caveolin forms caveolae.","Lamin B is part of the nuclear lamina."]},"fc77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Becker muscular dystrophy: in-frame DMD mutation → reduced or truncated but partially functional dystrophin → later onset and slower progression than Duchenne.</p><p>Reading-frame rule: out-of-frame (frameshift) deletions abolish dystrophin (Duchenne, onset ~2–5 years, wheelchair by ~12); in-frame deletions produce a shorter, partly functional protein (Becker, onset in adolescence or later, ambulation often into adulthood). Both are X-linked recessive, show elevated CK, calf pseudohypertrophy and risk of dilated cardiomyopathy. Diagnosis: genetic testing; muscle biopsy shows reduced/abnormal (Becker) vs absent (Duchenne) dystrophin staining.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hereditary spherocytosis</b> — Hereditary spherocytosis is a red cell membrane disorder.</li><li><b>Kartagener syndrome</b> — Kartagener syndrome is a ciliary dynein defect.</li><li><b>Myasthenia gravis</b> — Myasthenia gravis is autoimmune against acetylcholine receptors; dystrophin would be normal.</li><li><b>Duchenne muscular dystrophy</b> — Duchenne involves absent dystrophin, early childhood onset and faster progression.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Duchenne = frameshift (no dystrophin); Becker = in-frame (some dystrophin).</p>","w":["Hereditary spherocytosis is a red cell membrane disorder.","Kartagener syndrome is a ciliary dynein defect.","Myasthenia gravis is autoimmune against acetylcholine receptors; dystrophin would be normal.","Duchenne involves absent dystrophin, early childhood onset and faster progression.","Correct — partially functional dystrophin indicates Becker muscular dystrophy."]},"fc78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intermediate filaments are tissue-specific, so immunostaining identifies tumor origin: cytokeratin → epithelium (carcinoma).</p><p>Common markers: cytokeratin (epithelial cells — carcinomas, mesothelioma); vimentin (mesenchymal cells — sarcomas, also many others); desmin (muscle — rhabdomyosarcoma, leiomyosarcoma); GFAP (astrocytes and other glia — astrocytoma, glioblastoma); neurofilaments (neurons — neuroblastoma, neuroendocrine tumors); lamins (nuclear lamina — all nucleated cells). A metastatic, poorly differentiated cytokeratin-positive tumor is a carcinoma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Connective tissue</b> — Connective tissue (mesenchymal) tumors express vimentin.</li><li><b>Glial cells</b> — Glial tumors express GFAP.</li><li><b>Muscle</b> — Muscle tumors express desmin.</li><li><b>Neuron</b> — Neuronal tumors express neurofilaments.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytokeratin = carcinoma; vimentin = sarcoma.</p>","w":["Connective tissue (mesenchymal) tumors express vimentin.","Correct — cytokeratin indicates epithelial origin.","Glial tumors express GFAP.","Muscle tumors express desmin.","Neuronal tumors express neurofilaments."]},"fc79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Desmin is the intermediate filament of skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle → desmin-positive tumor = muscle origin.</p><p>Desmin connects Z discs of adjacent myofibrils and links them to the sarcolemma, nucleus and mitochondria, keeping sarcomeres aligned. Rhabdomyosarcoma (skeletal muscle, most common soft tissue sarcoma in children; also myogenin/MyoD1 positive) and leiomyosarcoma (smooth muscle; also smooth muscle actin positive) stain for desmin. Desmin mutations cause desmin-related myopathy and cardiomyopathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Neurons</b> — Neuronal tumors stain for neurofilaments.</li><li><b>Fibroblasts</b> — Fibroblasts and other mesenchymal cells stain for vimentin.</li><li><b>Neuroglia</b> — Glial tumors stain for GFAP.</li><li><b>Epithelium</b> — Epithelial tumors stain for cytokeratin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rhabdomyosarcoma: desmin, myogenin, MyoD1.</p>","w":["Neuronal tumors stain for neurofilaments.","Fibroblasts and other mesenchymal cells stain for vimentin.","Correct — desmin positivity indicates muscle origin.","Glial tumors stain for GFAP.","Epithelial tumors stain for cytokeratin."]},"fc80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> GFAP is the intermediate filament of glial cells (classically astrocytes) → GFAP-positive tumor = glioma (astrocytoma, glioblastoma).</p><p>Astrocytes support neurons, form part of the blood–brain barrier and respond to injury with GFAP-rich reactive gliosis (scar). Glioblastoma (grade 4 astrocytoma) is the most common primary malignant brain tumor in adults: GFAP-positive, with pseudopalisading necrosis and vascular proliferation, often crossing the corpus callosum (\"butterfly glioma\"). Pilocytic astrocytoma is the common benign GFAP-positive tumor of children (Rosenthal fibers).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epithelial cells</b> — Epithelial cells contain cytokeratin.</li><li><b>Neurons</b> — Neurons contain neurofilaments, not GFAP.</li><li><b>Endothelial cells</b> — Endothelial cells contain vimentin.</li><li><b>Fibroblasts</b> — Fibroblasts contain vimentin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GFAP: astrocytes, astrocytomas, glioblastoma, reactive gliosis.</p>","w":["Epithelial cells contain cytokeratin.","Correct — GFAP marks astrocytes.","Neurons contain neurofilaments, not GFAP.","Endothelial cells contain vimentin.","Fibroblasts contain vimentin."]},"fc81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vimentin is the intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells (fibroblasts, endothelium, chondroblasts, leukocytes) and forms a cage around the nucleus.</p><p>Vimentin helps position organelles and the nucleus and provides resilience to mechanical stress. It's the marker of sarcomas (connective tissue tumors), but many tumor types co-express it, so it's less specific. Other intermediate filaments are tissue-specific (keratin, desmin, GFAP, neurofilaments). Lamins are nuclear intermediate filaments located inside the nucleus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is found in muscle cells.</li><li><b>Neurofilament</b> — Neurofilaments are found in neurons.</li><li><b>Lamin</b> — Lamins line the inner nuclear membrane inside the nucleus, not around it in the cytoplasm.</li><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is found in epithelial cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vimentin = mesenchymal/sarcoma marker.</p>","w":["Desmin is found in muscle cells.","Neurofilaments are found in neurons.","Lamins line the inner nuclear membrane inside the nucleus, not around it in the cytoplasm.","Correct — vimentin forms a perinuclear cage in mesenchymal cells.","Keratin is found in epithelial cells."]},"fc82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Keratin tonofilaments insert into desmosomes (cell–cell) and hemidesmosomes (cell–basement membrane), giving epithelia tensile strength.</p><p>Keratins come in acidic (type I) and basic (type II) pairs, e.g., K5/K14 in basal keratinocytes and K1/K10 in suprabasal cells. Mutations weaken cells: K5/K14 → epidermolysis bullosa simplex (blisters splitting within basal cells); K1/K10 → epidermolytic hyperkeratosis. Desmosomal proteins are targets in pemphigus vulgaris (desmoglein 3), and hemidesmosomal proteins in bullous pemphigoid (BP180/BP230).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Neurofilament</b> — Neurofilaments are found in neurons.</li><li><b>Glial fibrillary acidic protein</b> — GFAP is found in glial cells.</li><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is found in mesenchymal cells.</li><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is found in muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pemphigus = desmosome (intraepidermal); pemphigoid = hemidesmosome (subepidermal).</p>","w":["Correct — keratin tonofilaments anchor at desmosomes and hemidesmosomes.","Neurofilaments are found in neurons.","GFAP is found in glial cells.","Vimentin is found in mesenchymal cells.","Desmin is found in muscle."]},"fc83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Nuclear lamins (A, B, C) form the nuclear lamina inside the inner nuclear membrane of every nucleated cell.</p><p>The lamina gives the nucleus shape and strength, anchors nuclear pore complexes, tethers heterochromatin at the periphery and helps regulate DNA replication and transcription. It disassembles in prophase when CDK1 phosphorylates lamins and reassembles in telophase. LMNA mutations cause laminopathies: progeria, Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy, dilated cardiomyopathy and familial partial lipodystrophy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is cytoplasmic and limited to epithelia.</li><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is limited to muscle.</li><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is cytoplasmic and limited to mesenchymal cells.</li><li><b>Neurofilaments</b> — Neurofilaments are limited to neurons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lamins = the only nuclear intermediate filaments.</p>","w":["Keratin is cytoplasmic and limited to epithelia.","Desmin is limited to muscle.","Vimentin is cytoplasmic and limited to mesenchymal cells.","Neurofilaments are limited to neurons.","Correct — nuclear lamins line the inner nuclear membrane."]},"fc84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Neurofilaments are the intermediate filaments of neurons; they support axons and determine axon diameter (and thus conduction velocity).</p><p>Neurofilaments are abundant in large myelinated axons. Their accumulation is seen in neurodegenerative disease (e.g., ALS), and serum neurofilament light chain is a biomarker of axonal damage. Neuronal tumors (neuroblastoma, ganglioneuroma) and neuroendocrine tumors stain for neurofilaments.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Keratin</b> — Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells.</li><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is the intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells.</li><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not an intermediate filament.</li><li><b>Desmin</b> — Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Neurofilament light chain = blood marker of axonal injury.</p>","w":["Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells.","Vimentin is the intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells.","Correct — neurofilaments support axons.","Spectrin is an actin-binding protein, not an intermediate filament.","Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle."]},"fc85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intermediate filament types are fairly tissue-specific and tumors usually keep them, so immunostaining reveals a metastasis's tissue of origin.</p><p>When a tumor is poorly differentiated, histology alone may not show where it came from. Staining for cytokeratin (carcinoma), vimentin (sarcoma), desmin (muscle), GFAP (glial) or neurofilaments (neuronal) narrows the diagnosis and guides treatment. In practice, panels add other markers (e.g., CK7/CK20, TTF-1, S-100, CD45).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intermediate filaments are made only in dividing cells</b> — Intermediate filaments are present in non-dividing cells too (e.g., neurons).</li><li><b>Intermediate filaments are identical in all cell types</b> — Intermediate filaments differ by tissue — that's what makes them useful.</li><li><b>Intermediate filaments are the only proteins visible by light microscopy</b> — They're identified by immunostaining, not because they're uniquely visible by light microscopy.</li><li><b>Intermediate filaments are lost in all malignant cells</b> — Tumors generally keep the intermediate filament of their tissue of origin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> IF markers: keratin–epithelium, vimentin–mesenchyme, desmin–muscle, GFAP–glia, NF–neurons.</p>","w":["Intermediate filaments are present in non-dividing cells too (e.g., neurons).","Correct — subtypes are fairly tissue-specific.","Intermediate filaments differ by tissue — that's what makes them useful.","They're identified by immunostaining, not because they're uniquely visible by light microscopy.","Tumors generally keep the intermediate filament of their tissue of origin."]},"fc86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Microtubules are tracks for kinesin and dynein; they also form the spindle, cilia/flagella and maintain cell shape.</p><p>Distinguish functions by filament. Microtubules: intracellular transport, mitotic spindle and chromosome segregation, cilia and flagella, cell polarity, Golgi positioning. Actin: cortical support, anchoring membrane proteins, microvilli, cell–ECM contacts (focal adhesions), stress fibers, contractile ring, cell migration, epithelial folding. Intermediate filaments: mechanical strength.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anchoring of membrane proteins</b> — Anchoring membrane proteins is an actin microfilament function.</li><li><b>Creating the contractile ring during cytokinesis</b> — The contractile ring of cytokinesis is made of actin with myosin.</li><li><b>Establishing contacts between cells and the extracellular matrix</b> — Cell–ECM contacts are established by actin microfilaments.</li><li><b>Folding epithelia during development</b> — Folding epithelia during development is done by actin microfilaments.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microtubule drugs: colchicine, vinca alkaloids, taxanes, griseofulvin, mebendazole.</p>","w":["Correct — microtubules act as railway tracks for intracellular transport.","Anchoring membrane proteins is an actin microfilament function.","The contractile ring of cytokinesis is made of actin with myosin.","Cell–ECM contacts are established by actin microfilaments.","Folding epithelia during development is done by actin microfilaments."]},"fc87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kinesin (anterograde, toward + end) and dynein (retrograde, toward − end) are the ATP-powered microtubule motors.</p><p>Motor proteins have heads that bind the track and hydrolyze ATP, plus tails that bind cargo. Kinesin moves vesicles from the cell body toward the periphery (e.g., synaptic vesicle precursors down axons). Dynein moves cargo toward the cell center and also powers ciliary beating. Myosins move along actin filaments (e.g., myosin V transports vesicles near the membrane).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dystrophin</b> — Dystrophin is an actin-binding protein linking actin to the ECM.</li><li><b>Myosin</b> — Myosin moves along actin filaments, not microtubules.</li><li><b>Spectrin</b> — Spectrin is an actin-binding protein of the RBC cytoskeleton.</li><li><b>Vimentin</b> — Vimentin is an intermediate filament, not a motor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Kinesin = anterograde; dynein = retrograde (both ATP).</p>","w":["Dystrophin is an actin-binding protein linking actin to the ECM.","Correct — kinesin is a microtubule motor protein.","Myosin moves along actin filaments, not microtubules.","Spectrin is an actin-binding protein of the RBC cytoskeleton.","Vimentin is an intermediate filament, not a motor."]},"fc88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A microtubule is a 25-nm hollow tube of 13 protofilaments, each a chain of α/β-tubulin heterodimers.</p><p>α/β-tubulin dimers bind GTP and add head-to-tail, creating polarity: β-tubulin faces the plus end. Thirteen protofilaments arranged side by side form the wall. Microtubules nucleate from γ-tubulin ring complexes at the centrosome. In cilia, microtubules are arranged as 9 doublets + 2 central singlets (axoneme); centrioles and basal bodies have 9 triplets.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>2 protofilaments of G-actin</b> — Actin filaments are two-stranded helices of G-actin, not hollow tubes.</li><li><b>13 protofilaments of keratin</b> — Keratin forms intermediate filaments.</li><li><b>9 protofilaments of actin</b> — Microtubules are made of tubulin, not actin.</li><li><b>9 protofilaments of α/β-tubulin plus 2 central singlets</b> — 9 + 2 describes the arrangement of microtubules in a motile cilium.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Microtubule = 13 protofilaments; centriole = 9 triplets; motile cilium = 9 doublets + 2.</p>","w":["Actin filaments are two-stranded helices of G-actin, not hollow tubes.","Keratin forms intermediate filaments.","Microtubules are made of tubulin, not actin.","Correct — 13 protofilaments of α/β-tubulin.","9 + 2 describes the arrangement of microtubules in a motile cilium."]},"fc89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Microtubule minus ends are anchored at the centrosome (MTOC) near the nucleus; plus ends grow toward the cell periphery.</p><p>The centrosome contains two centrioles surrounded by pericentriolar material with γ-tubulin, which nucleates microtubules. Because plus ends point outward, kinesin carries cargo out to the periphery and dynein brings it back to the center. In axons, all microtubules point plus-end-out; in dendrites, orientation is mixed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both ends at the plasma membrane</b> — One end is anchored at the centrosome, not at the plasma membrane.</li><li><b>Minus ends in the nucleus; plus ends in mitochondria</b> — Microtubules don't anchor in the nucleus or mitochondria.</li><li><b>Minus ends at the cell periphery; plus ends at the centrosome</b> — This is reversed.</li><li><b>Microtubules have no polarity</b> — Microtubules are polar, like actin filaments.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> γ-tubulin nucleates microtubules at the centrosome.</p>","w":["Correct — minus ends at the centrosome, plus ends at the periphery.","One end is anchored at the centrosome, not at the plasma membrane.","Microtubules don't anchor in the nucleus or mitochondria.","This is reversed.","Microtubules are polar, like actin filaments."]},"fc90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dynamic instability: microtubule plus ends switch rapidly between growth and catastrophic shrinkage.</p><p>GTP-tubulin adds to the plus end and forms a stabilizing \"GTP cap.\" GTP is hydrolyzed after incorporation; if growth slows and the cap is lost, GDP-tubulin protofilaments peel apart and the microtubule rapidly shrinks (catastrophe). It may then regrow (rescue). This lets spindle microtubules search for and capture kinetochores during mitosis. Drugs that block (vinca) or freeze (taxanes) dynamics stop mitosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nucleation at the centrosome</b> — Nucleation starts microtubules; it isn't the switching behavior.</li><li><b>Dynamic instability at the minus end</b> — The minus end is anchored at the centrosome.</li><li><b>Treadmilling at the plus end only</b> — Treadmilling describes balanced plus-end addition and minus-end loss, not rapid switching.</li><li><b>Treadmilling at the minus end</b> — Treadmilling is classically described for actin, and the minus end is anchored here.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> GTP cap → stable; loss of cap → catastrophe.</p>","w":["Nucleation starts microtubules; it isn't the switching behavior.","The minus end is anchored at the centrosome.","Treadmilling describes balanced plus-end addition and minus-end loss, not rapid switching.","Treadmilling is classically described for actin, and the minus end is anchored here.","Correct — dynamic instability at the plus end."]},"fc91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine) bind tubulin and inhibit microtubule assembly → no spindle (M-phase arrest) and impaired axonal transport (neuropathy).</p><p>Vincristine is used in ALL, lymphomas and pediatric solid tumors. Its dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral neuropathy (paresthesias, areflexia, constipation from autonomic neuropathy), because long axons depend on microtubule-based transport. Vinblastine causes more bone marrow suppression (\"vinBLASTine BLASTs the marrow\"). Taxanes do the opposite (stabilize microtubules) but also cause neuropathy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Stabilization of microtubules, preventing disassembly</b> — Stabilizing microtubules and preventing disassembly is paclitaxel's mechanism.</li><li><b>Inhibition of intermediate filament phosphorylation</b> — Vincristine doesn't target intermediate filaments.</li><li><b>Inhibition of actin polymerization at the plus end</b> — Vincristine doesn't inhibit actin polymerization.</li><li><b>Cross-linking of DNA strands during S phase</b> — DNA cross-linking is the mechanism of alkylating agents (e.g., cyclophosphamide, cisplatin).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vincristine → neuropathy; vinblastine → myelosuppression.</p>","w":["Stabilizing microtubules and preventing disassembly is paclitaxel's mechanism.","Vincristine doesn't target intermediate filaments.","Correct — vincristine inhibits microtubule assembly.","Vincristine doesn't inhibit actin polymerization.","DNA cross-linking is the mechanism of alkylating agents (e.g., cyclophosphamide, cisplatin)."]},"fc92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Paclitaxel (taxanes) stabilizes microtubules by inhibiting disassembly, freezing the spindle and arresting cells in mitosis.</p><p>Taxanes bind β-tubulin on the inside of the microtubule, preventing depolymerization. Without dynamic instability, chromosomes can't be properly aligned and separated, so the spindle checkpoint keeps cells in M phase until they die. Used for breast, ovarian and lung cancers. Toxicities: myelosuppression, peripheral neuropathy, hypersensitivity (from the solvent).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It blocks actin treadmilling</b> — Paclitaxel targets microtubules, not actin.</li><li><b>It inhibits microtubule assembly</b> — Inhibiting microtubule assembly is the vinca alkaloid mechanism.</li><li><b>It inhibits kinesin ATPase activity</b> — Paclitaxel doesn't directly inhibit kinesin.</li><li><b>It degrades tubulin</b> — Paclitaxel doesn't degrade tubulin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Taxes stabilize society\" — taxanes stabilize microtubules.</p>","w":["Paclitaxel targets microtubules, not actin.","Correct — paclitaxel inhibits microtubule disassembly.","Inhibiting microtubule assembly is the vinca alkaloid mechanism.","Paclitaxel doesn't directly inhibit kinesin.","Paclitaxel doesn't degrade tubulin."]},"fc93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dynein carries cargo retrograde (plus → minus end), from axon terminals back to the cell body — the route used by neurotropic viruses and toxins.</p><p>Rabies virus, herpes simplex virus, varicella-zoster virus, poliovirus and tetanus toxin exploit retrograde dynein transport to reach the neuronal cell body or CNS. HSV and VZV then remain latent in sensory ganglia and travel anterograde (kinesin) back to the skin during reactivation. Retrograde transport also returns worn-out organelles and growth factor signals (e.g., NGF) to the cell body.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Dynein, anterograde (minus to plus end)</b> — Dynein moves toward the minus end, not the plus end.</li><li><b>Myosin, along actin</b> — Myosin moves along actin, not long distances along axonal microtubules.</li><li><b>Kinesin, retrograde (plus to minus end)</b> — Kinesin moves toward the plus end (anterograde).</li><li><b>Kinesin, anterograde (minus to plus end)</b> — Kinesin moves cargo away from the cell body.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Retrograde (dynein): rabies, HSV, VZV, polio, tetanus toxin.</p>","w":["Dynein moves toward the minus end, not the plus end.","Myosin moves along actin, not long distances along axonal microtubules.","Kinesin moves toward the plus end (anterograde).","Correct — dynein mediates retrograde transport.","Kinesin moves cargo away from the cell body."]},"fc94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kinesin carries cargo anterograde (minus → plus end), from the neuronal cell body to the axon terminal, using ATP.</p><p>Synaptic vesicle precursors, mitochondria and membrane proteins are made in the cell body and must travel up to a meter down the axon. Fast axonal transport (~200–400 mm/day) by kinesin moves membrane-bound organelles; slow transport moves cytoskeletal and soluble proteins. Defective kinesin-based transport is implicated in some hereditary neuropathies (e.g., Charcot–Marie–Tooth type 2A, KIF1B) and spastic paraplegias.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Myosin carries the vesicles along intermediate filaments</b> — Myosin moves on actin, not intermediate filaments.</li><li><b>Spectrin carries the vesicles along actin</b> — Spectrin isn't a motor protein.</li><li><b>Dynein carries the vesicles toward the minus end using GTP</b> — Dynein moves cargo toward the cell body, and motors use ATP, not GTP.</li><li><b>The vesicles diffuse passively down the axon</b> — Passive diffusion would be far too slow over the length of an axon.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fast anterograde transport = kinesin; retrograde = dynein.</p>","w":["Correct — kinesin carries vesicles toward the plus end using ATP.","Myosin moves on actin, not intermediate filaments.","Spectrin isn't a motor protein.","Dynein moves cargo toward the cell body, and motors use ATP, not GTP.","Passive diffusion would be far too slow over the length of an axon."]},"fc95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia): defective dynein arms → immotile cilia and sperm flagella.</p><p>Autosomal recessive mutations in dynein arm proteins (e.g., DNAH5, DNAI1) stop the ATP-driven sliding of microtubule doublets. Consequences: impaired mucociliary clearance (chronic sinusitis, otitis media with conductive hearing loss, bronchiectasis, recurrent pneumonia), male infertility (immotile sperm with normal count), reduced female fertility and ectopic pregnancy risk, and situs inversus in about half (Kartagener triad: situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis). Diagnosis: low nasal nitric oxide, electron microscopy of cilia, genetic testing.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nuclear lamina</b> — Lamin defects affect the nucleus.</li><li><b>Desmin framework of myofibrils</b> — Desmin defects affect muscle.</li><li><b>Spectrin–actin network</b> — Spectrin defects cause hereditary spherocytosis.</li><li><b>Keratin tonofilaments</b> — Keratin defects affect epithelial integrity (skin), not cilia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Kartagener triad: situs inversus + sinusitis + bronchiectasis (+ infertility).</p>","w":["Lamin defects affect the nucleus.","Desmin defects affect muscle.","Spectrin defects cause hereditary spherocytosis.","Keratin defects affect epithelial integrity (skin), not cilia.","Correct — defective dynein arms cause immotile cilia and sperm."]},"fc96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fallopian tube cilia sweep the egg/embryo toward the uterus; immotile cilia delay transit and raise ectopic pregnancy risk.</p><p>The ampulla is the usual site of fertilization. The embryo normally reaches the uterus around day 4–5 and implants around day 6–7. If tubal transport is slow (ciliary dyskinesia, prior salpingitis from chlamydia/gonorrhea, tubal surgery, endometriosis), the blastocyst may implant in the tube — an ectopic pregnancy, which can rupture and cause life-threatening bleeding. Presentation: amenorrhea, abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding; β-hCG and transvaginal ultrasound diagnose it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sperm transport through her tubes is accelerated</b> — Ciliary dyskinesia slows, rather than speeds, tubal transport.</li><li><b>Her ovaries fail to release oocytes at ovulation</b> — Ovulation itself isn't impaired by ciliary defects.</li><li><b>Her fallopian tube epithelium lacks absorptive microvilli</b> — Tubal transport depends on cilia, not microvilli.</li><li><b>Her endometrium cannot support normal implantation</b> — The endometrium is normal in primary ciliary dyskinesia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ectopic pregnancy risk factors: PID, prior ectopic, tubal surgery, IUD, ciliary dysfunction.</p>","w":["Ciliary dyskinesia slows, rather than speeds, tubal transport.","Ovulation itself isn't impaired by ciliary defects.","Correct — immotile tubal cilia slow the egg's movement.","Tubal transport depends on cilia, not microvilli.","The endometrium is normal in primary ciliary dyskinesia."]},"fc97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Motile cilia have a 9 + 2 axoneme; dynein arms on doublets slide them past each other (ATP), bending the cilium.</p><p>Nexin links connect adjacent doublets, converting sliding into bending. Radial spokes connect the doublets to the central pair, coordinating the beat. Airway cilia beat in coordinated waves (metachronal), moving mucus up toward the pharynx at several mm/min — the mucociliary escalator. Smoking paralyzes and destroys cilia, impairing clearance. The axoneme grows from a basal body (9 triplets).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>9 + 2 arrangement; spectrin</b> — Spectrin isn't part of cilia.</li><li><b>9 + 0 arrangement; kinesin</b> — 9 + 0 is the nonmotile (primary) cilium, and kinesin isn't the ciliary motor.</li><li><b>13 protofilaments in a single tube; myosin</b> — 13 protofilaments describe a single microtubule, and myosin works on actin.</li><li><b>9 triplets with no central pair; actin</b> — 9 triplets with no central pair describe centrioles/basal bodies, not motile cilia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Motile cilium 9 + 2 (axoneme); basal body/centriole 9 × 3.</p>","w":["Spectrin isn't part of cilia.","Correct — 9 + 2 axoneme, with dynein arms generating sliding.","9 + 0 is the nonmotile (primary) cilium, and kinesin isn't the ciliary motor.","13 protofilaments describe a single microtubule, and myosin works on actin.","9 triplets with no central pair describe centrioles/basal bodies, not motile cilia."]},"fc98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Primary (nonmotile, sensory) cilia have a 9 + 0 arrangement — 9 doublets without a central pair.</p><p>Most cells have a single primary cilium acting as an antenna. In the kidney, primary cilia sense urine flow via polycystin-1 and polycystin-2 (a Ca²⁺ channel). Mutations cause autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (PKD1/PKD2) and other ciliopathies (nephronophthisis). Primary cilia also mediate Hedgehog signaling, photoreceptor function (connecting cilium) and olfaction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>3 + 9</b> — 3 + 9 isn't a ciliary arrangement.</li><li><b>2 + 9</b> — 2 + 9 isn't a ciliary arrangement.</li><li><b>13 + 0</b> — 13 is the number of protofilaments in one microtubule.</li><li><b>9 + 2</b> — 9 + 2 is the motile cilium.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ADPKD: polycystin-1/2 defects in primary cilia.</p>","w":["3 + 9 isn't a ciliary arrangement.","2 + 9 isn't a ciliary arrangement.","13 is the number of protofilaments in one microtubule.","Correct — nonmotile sensory cilia have a 9 + 0 arrangement.","9 + 2 is the motile cilium."]},"fc99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Per the slides, nonmotile (9 + 0) sensory cilia detect signaling gradients that establish left–right body patterning; defects can cause situs inversus.</p><p>At the embryonic node, cilia create a leftward flow of fluid carrying signaling molecules, which is sensed by cilia at the edge of the node, activating left-sided genes (Nodal, Lefty, Pitx2). Disrupted ciliary function randomizes laterality, producing situs inversus or heterotaxy. This explains why about half of Kartagener patients have situs inversus. (Node cilia include a motile 9 + 0 type that generates flow, but the slides emphasize their sensory role.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are actin-based microvilli</b> — Microvilli are actin-based and don't set body laterality.</li><li><b>They are intermediate filament bundles</b> — Cilia are microtubule-based, not intermediate filament bundles.</li><li><b>They are flagella of sperm</b> — Sperm flagella move sperm; they don't pattern the embryo.</li><li><b>They are motile 9 + 2 cilia that move mucus</b> — Mucus-clearing 9 + 2 cilia line the airways and aren't described for this patterning role on the slides.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nodal cilia defects → situs inversus (Kartagener ~50%).</p>","w":["Correct — nonmotile 9 + 0 cilia with a sensory role.","Microvilli are actin-based and don't set body laterality.","Cilia are microtubule-based, not intermediate filament bundles.","Sperm flagella move sperm; they don't pattern the embryo.","Mucus-clearing 9 + 2 cilia line the airways and aren't described for this patterning role on the slides."]},"fc100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chromosome distribution to daughter cells (mitotic spindle) is a microtubule function.</p><p>Spindle microtubules attach to kinetochores, align chromosomes at the metaphase plate and pull sister chromatids apart in anaphase (kinetochore microtubules shorten; polar microtubules push poles apart with motor proteins). Errors cause aneuploidy. The spindle assembly checkpoint (Mad2, BubR1) prevents anaphase until all chromosomes are attached.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Providing tensile strength at desmosomes in the skin</b> — Keratin intermediate filaments provide tensile strength at desmosomes.</li><li><b>Stabilizing the red blood cell membrane</b> — The spectrin–actin network stabilizes the RBC membrane.</li><li><b>Forming the contractile ring in cytokinesis</b> — The contractile ring in cytokinesis is made of actin.</li><li><b>Forming stress fibers in fibroblasts</b> — Stress fibers are made of actin.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spindle checkpoint protects against aneuploidy.</p>","w":["Keratin intermediate filaments provide tensile strength at desmosomes.","The spectrin–actin network stabilizes the RBC membrane.","The contractile ring in cytokinesis is made of actin.","Stress fibers are made of actin.","Correct — microtubules distribute chromosomes to daughter cells."]},"fc101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> G1 = growth phase: the cell enlarges and duplicates its organelles before committing to DNA replication.</p><p>Cell cycle: G1 (growth, organelle duplication, checks nutrients and DNA damage) → S (DNA and histone synthesis, centrosome duplication) → G2 (continued growth, prepares for mitosis, checks replication) → M (mitosis + cytokinesis). The restriction point late in G1 (controlled by cyclin D–CDK4/6 phosphorylating Rb) commits the cell to divide. G1 length varies the most between cell types; quiescent cells exit to G0.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>G2 phase</b> — G2 continues growth and prepares for mitosis, but major organelle duplication occurs in G1.</li><li><b>Prophase</b> — Prophase is part of mitosis (chromosome condensation), not growth.</li><li><b>S phase</b> — S phase is devoted to DNA (and histone) replication.</li><li><b>Telophase</b> — Telophase is the end of mitosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> G1 is the most variable phase; Rb phosphorylation releases E2F at the restriction point.</p>","w":["Correct — growth and organelle duplication occur in G1.","G2 continues growth and prepares for mitosis, but major organelle duplication occurs in G1.","Prophase is part of mitosis (chromosome condensation), not growth.","S phase is devoted to DNA (and histone) replication.","Telophase is the end of mitosis."]},"fc102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Permanent (postmitotic) cells stay in G0 and don't divide: neurons, skeletal muscle and cardiac muscle.</p><p>Regenerative capacity determines how tissues heal. Labile cells (marrow, skin and GI epithelium) constantly divide and regenerate readily. Stable cells (hepatocytes, renal tubular cells) rest in G0 but re-enter G1 when stimulated. Permanent cells can't divide, so injury heals by scarring (e.g., myocardial infarction → fibrosis; brain injury → gliosis). Skeletal muscle has satellite cells that permit limited repair.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Embryonic stem cells</b> — Embryonic stem cells divide actively.</li><li><b>Hematopoietic cells</b> — Hematopoietic cells in the marrow divide actively (labile).</li><li><b>Hepatocytes</b> — Hepatocytes are stable cells that can re-enter G1 for regeneration.</li><li><b>Intestinal epithelial cells</b> — Intestinal epithelium is labile and divides rapidly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Permanent: neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle (and RBCs, lens fibers — non-nucleated/terminal).</p>","w":["Embryonic stem cells divide actively.","Hematopoietic cells in the marrow divide actively (labile).","Hepatocytes are stable cells that can re-enter G1 for regeneration.","Intestinal epithelium is labile and divides rapidly.","Correct — neurons are permanently in G0."]},"fc103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Count chromosomes by centromeres: after S phase a somatic cell still has 46 chromosomes, but each has 2 sister chromatids → 92 chromatids (4c DNA).</p><p>Notation: n = sets of chromosomes, c = DNA content. G1: 46 chromosomes, 46 chromatids (2n, 2c). After S/G2: 46 chromosomes, 92 chromatids (2n, 4c). After mitosis each daughter returns to 2n, 2c. Gametes are 23 chromosomes, 1n, 1c. Chromosome number changes only when centromeres separate (anaphase) or cells divide.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>23 chromosomes, 46 chromatids</b> — 23 chromosomes is the haploid number of a gamete.</li><li><b>92 chromosomes, 92 chromatids</b> — Attached sister chromatids still count as one chromosome, so the count stays 46.</li><li><b>46 chromosomes, 46 chromatids</b> — 46 chromatids describes G1, before DNA replication.</li><li><b>92 chromosomes, 184 chromatids</b> — Chromosome number doesn't double in S phase; only DNA content does.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> G2 = 2n, 4c; gamete = 1n, 1c.</p>","w":["23 chromosomes is the haploid number of a gamete.","Attached sister chromatids still count as one chromosome, so the count stays 46.","Correct — 46 chromosomes and 92 chromatids.","46 chromatids describes G1, before DNA replication.","Chromosome number doesn't double in S phase; only DNA content does."]},"fc104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cohesin rings hold sister chromatids together (especially at the centromere) from S phase until anaphase.</p><p>At anaphase onset, the anaphase-promoting complex (APC/C) ubiquitinates securin, freeing separase, which cleaves cohesin so sister chromatids can separate. Premature or failed cohesin cleavage causes aneuploidy. Kinetochores assemble on centromeres and attach spindle microtubules. Age-related loss of cohesin in oocytes contributes to maternal-age-related nondisjunction (e.g., trisomy 21).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cyclins at the centrosome</b> — Cyclins activate CDKs, and the centrosome organizes microtubules.</li><li><b>Lamins at the telomere</b> — Lamins form the nuclear lamina, not chromatid cohesion at telomeres.</li><li><b>Tubulins at the kinetochore</b> — Tubulin forms spindle microtubules that attach at kinetochores but doesn't hold chromatids together.</li><li><b>Histones at the nucleolus</b> — Histones package DNA; the nucleolus makes ribosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anaphase: APC/C → securin degraded → separase cleaves cohesin.</p>","w":["Cyclins activate CDKs, and the centrosome organizes microtubules.","Correct — cohesins at the centromere.","Lamins form the nuclear lamina, not chromatid cohesion at telomeres.","Tubulin forms spindle microtubules that attach at kinetochores but doesn't hold chromatids together.","Histones package DNA; the nucleolus makes ribosomes."]},"fc105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> G2: continued growth, preparation for mitosis and checking that DNA replication is complete and error-free (G2/M checkpoint).</p><p>During G2 the cell synthesizes proteins needed for mitosis (e.g., tubulin), and cyclin B accumulates with CDK1. ATM/ATR kinases detect DNA damage or incomplete replication and activate Chk1/Chk2, which inhibit Cdc25 phosphatase, keeping CDK1 inactive until problems are fixed. Once cleared, Cdc25 activates cyclin B–CDK1 (maturation/M-phase promoting factor) and mitosis begins.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Its sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles</b> — Sister chromatid separation occurs in anaphase.</li><li><b>It replicates its DNA and duplicates its histones</b> — DNA replication and histone duplication occur in S phase.</li><li><b>Its cytoplasm divides to form two daughter cells</b> — Cytoplasmic division is cytokinesis.</li><li><b>It exits the cycle and stays quiescent until stimulated</b> — Exiting the cycle into quiescence is G0.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MPF = cyclin B–CDK1; activated by Cdc25 at G2/M.</p>","w":["Sister chromatid separation occurs in anaphase.","DNA replication and histone duplication occur in S phase.","Cytoplasmic division is cytokinesis.","Correct — G2 is growth, mitotic preparation and DNA checking.","Exiting the cycle into quiescence is G0."]},"fc106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hepatocytes are stable (quiescent) cells: they sit in G0 and re-enter G1 when stimulated by growth factors (e.g., HGF, EGF, IL-6, TNF after partial hepatectomy).</p><p>After partial hepatectomy, cytokines prime hepatocytes to leave G0, and growth factors push them through the restriction point. Hepatocytes divide one or two rounds until liver mass is restored (~1–2 weeks), then return to quiescence. If hepatocyte proliferation is impaired (e.g., chronic injury), liver progenitor cells contribute. This is compensatory hyperplasia — remaining cells restore mass, not original shape.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hepatocytes are labile cells that never stop dividing</b> — Labile cells divide continuously; hepatocytes are normally quiescent.</li><li><b>Hepatocytes skip G1 and enter S phase directly</b> — Cells re-enter at G1 and must pass the restriction point; they don't skip to S.</li><li><b>Hepatocytes are permanent cells and the liver regenerates from stem cells only</b> — Hepatocytes themselves divide; they aren't permanent cells.</li><li><b>Hepatocytes divide by meiosis</b> — Somatic cells divide by mitosis, not meiosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Stable cells: hepatocytes, renal tubular cells, fibroblasts, smooth muscle, endothelium.</p>","w":["Correct — stable hepatocytes leave G0 and re-enter G1.","Labile cells divide continuously; hepatocytes are normally quiescent.","Cells re-enter at G1 and must pass the restriction point; they don't skip to S.","Hepatocytes themselves divide; they aren't permanent cells.","Somatic cells divide by mitosis, not meiosis."]},"fc107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cardiac myocytes are permanent cells; lost myocardium is replaced by fibrous scar, not new muscle.</p><p>After MI: coagulative necrosis (hours–days), neutrophils (1–3 d), macrophages (3–7 d; risk of free-wall rupture), granulation tissue (1–2 wk), and dense collagen scar (by ~2 months). The scar doesn't contract, reducing pump function and potentially causing aneurysm, heart failure or arrhythmias. Neurons and skeletal muscle are also permanent (skeletal muscle has limited satellite-cell repair).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They are labile cells that divide continuously</b> — Labile cells divide continuously; cardiac myocytes don't.</li><li><b>They undergo meiosis</b> — Meiosis occurs only in germ cells.</li><li><b>They are stable cells that quickly re-enter G1</b> — Stable cells regenerate after re-entering G1; myocytes can't.</li><li><b>They are arrested in S phase</b> — Myocytes aren't stuck in S phase; they've left the cycle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Permanent tissue injury → scar (MI → fibrosis; CNS → gliosis).</p>","w":["Labile cells divide continuously; cardiac myocytes don't.","Meiosis occurs only in germ cells.","Stable cells regenerate after re-entering G1; myocytes can't.","Myocytes aren't stuck in S phase; they've left the cycle.","Correct — cardiac myocytes are permanent, non-dividing cells."]},"fc108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Without growth factors, cells can't pass the G1 restriction point — they arrest and enter G0.</p><p>Growth factors bind receptor tyrosine kinases → Ras–MAPK signaling → cyclin D synthesis. Cyclin D–CDK4/6 begins phosphorylating Rb; once enough Rb is phosphorylated (later reinforced by cyclin E–CDK2), E2F is released and the cell is committed to S phase even if growth factors are withdrawn. Before that point, withdrawing serum sends cells into G0. Cancer cells often bypass this requirement (oncogenic Ras, cyclin D amplification, Rb or p16 loss).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>They skip to M phase</b> — Cells can't skip S and G2 to enter M.</li><li><b>They become permanent cells</b> — Fibroblasts are stable cells that can be stimulated to divide again.</li><li><b>They pass the restriction point and complete S phase</b> — Without growth factors, the cell can't pass the restriction point.</li><li><b>They undergo immediate apoptosis</b> — Lack of growth factors pauses the cycle; it doesn't immediately cause apoptosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Restriction point = growth-factor-dependent commitment; after it, the cycle proceeds autonomously.</p>","w":["Cells can't skip S and G2 to enter M.","Fibroblasts are stable cells that can be stimulated to divide again.","Correct — cells arrest at the restriction point and enter G0.","Without growth factors, the cell can't pass the restriction point.","Lack of growth factors pauses the cycle; it doesn't immediately cause apoptosis."]},"fc109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cyclin D is the growth-factor-dependent, limiting activator of CDK4/6; constitutive cyclin D drives cells through G1/S.</p><p>Cyclin D–CDK4/6 phosphorylates Rb, releasing E2F, which transcribes cyclin E, cyclin A and DNA synthesis genes. Overexpression of cyclin D (e.g., t(11;14) in mantle cell lymphoma, amplification in breast cancer) pushes cells into S phase without proper signals. CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib) treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>CDK4</b> — CDK4 is already constitutively expressed; extra CDK4 without more cyclin D has little effect.</li><li><b>Centromere</b> — The centromere is a chromosomal structure, not a regulator.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 halts the cycle in response to DNA damage.</li><li><b>Rb protein</b> — Hypophosphorylated Rb holds E2F and blocks G1→S.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> t(11;14) → cyclin D1 overexpression → mantle cell lymphoma.</p>","w":["CDK4 is already constitutively expressed; extra CDK4 without more cyclin D has little effect.","The centromere is a chromosomal structure, not a regulator.","Correct — constitutive cyclin D speeds G1/S progression.","p53 halts the cycle in response to DNA damage.","Hypophosphorylated Rb holds E2F and blocks G1→S."]},"fc110":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> DNA damage in G1 → p53 stabilized → transcribes p21 → p21 inhibits cyclin E/CDK2 (and other CDKs) → G1 arrest.</p><p>Normally MDM2 ubiquitinates p53, keeping its levels low. DNA damage activates ATM/ATR and Chk kinases, which phosphorylate p53 so it escapes MDM2. Stabilized p53 induces p21 (CDK inhibitor), GADD45 (DNA repair) and, if damage persists, pro-apoptotic BAX/PUMA. Li–Fraumeni syndrome (germline TP53 mutation) predisposes to sarcomas, breast cancer, brain tumors, leukemia and adrenocortical carcinoma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>25C phosphatase</b> — Cdc25 phosphatase activates CDKs and promotes progression rather than arrest.</li><li><b>Cyclin D</b> — Cyclin D drives G1 progression.</li><li><b>CDK2</b> — CDK2 promotes the G1/S transition; it's the target that p21 inhibits.</li><li><b>E2F</b> — E2F activates S-phase genes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> p53 → p21 → CDK inhibition → G1 arrest; Li–Fraumeni = germline TP53.</p>","w":["Cdc25 phosphatase activates CDKs and promotes progression rather than arrest.","Cyclin D drives G1 progression.","CDK2 promotes the G1/S transition; it's the target that p21 inhibits.","E2F activates S-phase genes.","Correct — p21 arrests the cell cycle."]},"fc111":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In S phase, cyclin E/CDK2 then cyclin A/CDK2 are active; p53 and p21 are low and Rb is phosphorylated (inactive).</p><p>CDK2 activity initiates DNA replication (firing of origins, with prevention of re-replication) and drives histone synthesis. In an undamaged cell, MDM2 keeps p53 low, so p21 isn't induced. Rb is hyperphosphorylated from late G1 through mitosis and dephosphorylated again as cells exit mitosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>BRCA1</b> — BRCA1 participates in DNA repair but isn't the S-phase cell-cycle driver.</li><li><b>p21</b> — p21 is induced by p53 only after DNA damage.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 is quickly degraded in normal, undamaged cells.</li><li><b>RB</b> — By S phase, Rb is phosphorylated and inactive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rb phosphorylated = inactive = cell cycling.</p>","w":["BRCA1 participates in DNA repair but isn't the S-phase cell-cycle driver.","Correct — CDK2 is active in S phase.","p21 is induced by p53 only after DNA damage.","p53 is quickly degraded in normal, undamaged cells.","By S phase, Rb is phosphorylated and inactive."]},"fc112":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> CDK levels are constant; activity is controlled by phase-specific, unstable cyclins (and by CDK inhibitors and phosphorylation).</p><p>Each cyclin is synthesized at a particular phase and then degraded by ubiquitin–proteasome pathways (SCF for G1/S cyclins, APC/C for mitotic cyclins). CDKs also need activating phosphorylation (by CAK) and removal of inhibitory phosphates (by Cdc25; added by Wee1). CDK inhibitors (p21/p27/p57 family; INK4 family including p16) brake the cycle.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cyclins are constant while CDK levels rise and fall</b> — This reverses the two: cyclins rise and fall while CDKs stay constant.</li><li><b>CDKs are rapidly degraded at the end of each phase</b> — Cyclins, not CDKs, are degraded after each phase.</li><li><b>CDKs are synthesized only during M phase and then degraded</b> — CDK levels stay constant throughout the cycle.</li><li><b>Cyclins are kinases that phosphorylate and activate CDKs</b> — CDKs are the kinases; cyclins are the activating subunits.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CKIs: INK4 (p16) inhibits CDK4/6; CIP/KIP (p21, p27) inhibit broadly.</p>","w":["This reverses the two: cyclins rise and fall while CDKs stay constant.","Correct — CDKs are always present but need a phase-specific cyclin.","Cyclins, not CDKs, are degraded after each phase.","CDK levels stay constant throughout the cycle.","CDKs are the kinases; cyclins are the activating subunits."]},"fc113":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cyclin B/CDK1 drives the G2→M transition; inhibiting it arrests cells at G2/M.</p><p>Cyclin–CDK pairs by phase: cyclin D–CDK4/6 (G1 restriction point), cyclin E–CDK2 (G1/S), cyclin A–CDK2 (S), cyclin A/B–CDK1 (G2/M). Cyclin B–CDK1 phosphorylates lamins (nuclear envelope breakdown), condensins (chromosome condensation) and microtubule proteins (spindle formation). APC/C degrades cyclin B in anaphase, allowing mitotic exit.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>During early S phase</b> — Early S phase depends on cyclin E (and A)/CDK2.</li><li><b>In G0</b> — G0 results from a lack of growth factors, not CDK1 inhibition.</li><li><b>At the G1 restriction point</b> — The restriction point depends on cyclin D/CDK4/6.</li><li><b>During cytokinesis</b> — Cytokinesis comes after mitosis begins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cyclin B–CDK1 = MPF; its destruction allows mitotic exit.</p>","w":["Early S phase depends on cyclin E (and A)/CDK2.","G0 results from a lack of growth factors, not CDK1 inhibition.","The restriction point depends on cyclin D/CDK4/6.","Correct — cells arrest at G2/M.","Cytokinesis comes after mitosis begins."]},"fc114":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cyclin A works in both S and G2 (with CDK2 and CDK1) and stimulates production of cyclin B.</p><p>Cascade: growth factors → cyclin D → Rb phosphorylation → E2F → cyclin E → G1/S → cyclin A → S and G2 proteins + cyclin B → cyclin B–CDK1 → mitosis. Each cyclin helps induce the next and is then degraded, giving the cycle its forward direction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cyclin E</b> — Cyclin E completes the G1→S transition and induces cyclin A.</li><li><b>Cyclin D</b> — Cyclin D works at the G1/S restriction point.</li><li><b>Cyclin B</b> — Cyclin B drives mitosis; it's the product, not the inducer.</li><li><b>Cyclin C</b> — Cyclin C isn't part of this lecture's sequence.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Order: D → E → A → B.</p>","w":["Correct — cyclin A covers S and G2 and induces cyclin B.","Cyclin E completes the G1→S transition and induces cyclin A.","Cyclin D works at the G1/S restriction point.","Cyclin B drives mitosis; it's the product, not the inducer.","Cyclin C isn't part of this lecture's sequence."]},"fc115":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Retinoblastoma = loss of both RB1 copies → E2F permanently free → unchecked G1/S transition.</p><p>Knudson's two-hit hypothesis: hereditary retinoblastoma inherits one mutant RB1 (germline) and acquires a second hit in retinal cells — bilateral, early-onset tumors, plus later risk of osteosarcoma. Sporadic cases need two somatic hits (usually unilateral, later). Leukocoria (white reflex) is the classic sign. Without Rb, E2F continuously transcribes S-phase genes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>p21</b> — p21 is a CDK inhibitor induced by p53; it doesn't drive the cycle.</li><li><b>Cyclin B</b> — Cyclin B controls G2/M, not the G1/S checkpoint that Rb guards.</li><li><b>p16</b> — p16 inhibits CDK4 and doesn't drive the cycle.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 is a separate tumor suppressor; losing Rb doesn't free p53 to drive the cycle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hereditary Rb: bilateral retinoblastoma + osteosarcoma; RB1 on chromosome 13q14.</p>","w":["p21 is a CDK inhibitor induced by p53; it doesn't drive the cycle.","Cyclin B controls G2/M, not the G1/S checkpoint that Rb guards.","p16 inhibits CDK4 and doesn't drive the cycle.","p53 is a separate tumor suppressor; losing Rb doesn't free p53 to drive the cycle.","Correct — E2F is free to drive the cycle."]},"fc116":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> p16 (INK4a) inhibits CDK4/6; losing p16 → active cyclin D/CDK4 → Rb phosphorylated → E2F released → faster G1/S.</p><p>CDKN2A (encoding p16) is deleted or silenced in many cancers — melanoma (familial melanoma syndrome), pancreatic cancer, gliomas and others. p16 also induces senescence in stressed cells. Conversely, p16 overexpression is used as a marker of HPV-driven cancers (HPV E7 inactivates Rb, causing compensatory p16 rise).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased stabilization of p53 and induction of p21</b> — p16 loss doesn't stabilize p53 or induce p21.</li><li><b>Arrest of the cells at the G2/M checkpoint</b> — p16 acts at the G1/S checkpoint, not G2/M.</li><li><b>Decreased phosphorylation of Rb by cyclin D/CDK4</b> — Losing p16 increases, not decreases, Rb phosphorylation.</li><li><b>Increased binding of Rb to the transcription factor E2F</b> — Phosphorylated Rb releases rather than binds E2F.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> p16 IHC positivity = HPV-associated cervical/oropharyngeal cancer.</p>","w":["p16 loss doesn't stabilize p53 or induce p21.","p16 acts at the G1/S checkpoint, not G2/M.","Correct — loss of p16 increases Rb phosphorylation and speeds G1/S.","Losing p16 increases, not decreases, Rb phosphorylation.","Phosphorylated Rb releases rather than binds E2F."]},"fc117":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Prolonged p53 stabilization (irreparable DNA damage) triggers apoptosis via BAX, PUMA and NOXA.</p><p>p53 is the \"guardian of the genome.\" Its first response to damage is arrest (p21) and repair (GADD45). If repair fails, sustained p53 activity shifts to transcription of pro-apoptotic genes, activating the mitochondrial pathway (cytochrome c release, caspase-9). Loss of p53 lets damaged cells survive and accumulate mutations — which is why TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancer. HPV E6 degrades p53.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Conversion to labile cells</b> — Cell type (labile, stable, permanent) doesn't change.</li><li><b>Permanent G1 arrest with continued division</b> — Arrested cells don't continue dividing.</li><li><b>Increased cyclin D expression</b> — p53 doesn't increase cyclin D expression.</li><li><b>Rapid passage through the G1/S checkpoint</b> — p53 blocks, rather than accelerates, the G1/S transition.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> HPV E6 → p53 degradation; E7 → Rb inactivation.</p>","w":["Cell type (labile, stable, permanent) doesn't change.","Correct — prolonged p53 activates apoptosis.","Arrested cells don't continue dividing.","p53 doesn't increase cyclin D expression.","p53 blocks, rather than accelerates, the G1/S transition."]},"fc118":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The G2/M checkpoint (just before mitosis) ensures all DNA has been replicated (and damage repaired) before chromosomes are segregated.</p><p>Unreplicated DNA is sensed by ATR → Chk1 → inhibition of Cdc25, keeping cyclin B–CDK1 inactive. This prevents daughter cells from inheriting incomplete chromosomes. The three main checkpoints: G1/S (DNA damage, environment), G2/M (complete replication), and metaphase–anaphase/spindle assembly (attachment).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>G0 checkpoint</b> — There's no \"G0 checkpoint\" among the three major checkpoints.</li><li><b>G1/S checkpoint</b> — The G1/S checkpoint checks for DNA damage before replication starts.</li><li><b>Restriction point in early G1</b> — The restriction point checks for growth factors and the environment.</li><li><b>Metaphase–anaphase (spindle) checkpoint</b> — The spindle checkpoint checks chromosome attachment to the spindle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> G2/M: ATR–Chk1 → ↓Cdc25 → CDK1 stays off.</p>","w":["There's no \"G0 checkpoint\" among the three major checkpoints.","The G1/S checkpoint checks for DNA damage before replication starts.","The restriction point checks for growth factors and the environment.","Correct — the G2/M checkpoint prevents mitosis with unreplicated DNA.","The spindle checkpoint checks chromosome attachment to the spindle."]},"fc119":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The spindle assembly (metaphase-to-anaphase) checkpoint halts mitosis until every kinetochore is properly attached to the spindle.</p><p>Unattached kinetochores generate a \"wait\" signal (Mad2, BubR1) that inhibits APC/C–Cdc20. Without APC/C activity, securin isn't degraded, separase stays inactive, and cohesin holds sister chromatids together. Once all kinetochores are attached under tension, the signal stops, APC/C activates and anaphase begins. Defects lead to chromosome missegregation and aneuploidy, common in cancer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>S-phase DNA replication checkpoint</b> — S-phase replication checks don't verify spindle attachment.</li><li><b>Restriction point</b> — The restriction point is in G1.</li><li><b>G2/M checkpoint</b> — The G2/M checkpoint checks that replication is complete.</li><li><b>G1/S checkpoint</b> — The G1/S checkpoint checks for DNA damage before replication.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Spindle checkpoint: Mad2/BubR1 inhibit APC/C–Cdc20.</p>","w":["Correct — the metaphase-to-anaphase checkpoint.","S-phase replication checks don't verify spindle attachment.","The restriction point is in G1.","The G2/M checkpoint checks that replication is complete.","The G1/S checkpoint checks for DNA damage before replication."]},"fc120":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Metaphase: condensed chromosomes align at the equatorial (metaphase) plate, each kinetochore attached to microtubules from opposite poles.</p><p>Phases (PPMAT): prophase (chromatin condenses, centrosomes move apart, spindle forms), prometaphase (nuclear envelope breaks down, kinetochores capture microtubules), metaphase (alignment), anaphase (sister chromatids separate), telophase (nuclear envelopes re-form, chromosomes decondense), then cytokinesis. Metaphase chromosomes are maximally condensed, which is why karyotypes are prepared from metaphase-arrested cells (colchicine).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Telophase</b> — In telophase, chromosomes are at the poles and nuclei re-form.</li><li><b>Prometaphase</b> — In prometaphase, microtubules attach to kinetochores but alignment isn't complete.</li><li><b>Prophase</b> — In prophase, chromosomes condense but aren't aligned.</li><li><b>Anaphase</b> — In anaphase, chromatids move apart.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Karyotypes use colchicine-arrested metaphase cells.</p>","w":["In telophase, chromosomes are at the poles and nuclei re-form.","In prometaphase, microtubules attach to kinetochores but alignment isn't complete.","In prophase, chromosomes condense but aren't aligned.","In anaphase, chromatids move apart.","Correct — alignment at the equator is metaphase."]},"fc121":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Anaphase = sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles.</p><p>Separase cleaves cohesin; kinetochore microtubules shorten (anaphase A) pulling chromatids to the poles, and polar microtubules slide apart while astral microtubules pull the poles outward (anaphase B), elongating the cell. Nondisjunction (failure of separation) produces aneuploid cells; in meiosis this underlies trisomies and monosomies.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Prophase</b> — Prophase is chromosome condensation.</li><li><b>Telophase</b> — In telophase, nuclei re-form at the poles.</li><li><b>Interphase</b> — Interphase (G1, S, G2) isn't part of mitosis.</li><li><b>Metaphase</b> — Metaphase is alignment at the equator.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anaphase A = chromatids to poles; anaphase B = poles move apart.</p>","w":["Prophase is chromosome condensation.","In telophase, nuclei re-form at the poles.","Correct — anaphase.","Interphase (G1, S, G2) isn't part of mitosis.","Metaphase is alignment at the equator."]},"fc122":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mitosis → 2 genetically identical diploid daughter cells; meiosis → 4 genetically different haploid gametes.</p><p>Mitosis: one division after one round of DNA replication; used for growth, repair and asexual replacement in somatic cells. Meiosis: one replication followed by two divisions (meiosis I separates homologs — reductional; meiosis II separates sister chromatids — equational). Genetic variation arises from crossing over (prophase I) and independent assortment (metaphase I). In females, meiosis yields one ovum and polar bodies; in males, four sperm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mitosis produces four haploid, genetically different cells; meiosis produces two identical diploid cells</b> — This reverses mitosis and meiosis.</li><li><b>Mitosis occurs only in germ cells</b> — Mitosis occurs in somatic cells; meiosis occurs in germ cells.</li><li><b>Both produce two diploid cells</b> — Only mitosis produces two diploid cells.</li><li><b>Both produce four haploid cells</b> — Only meiosis produces four haploid cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Meiosis I = reductional (homologs separate); meiosis II = equational (sisters separate).</p>","w":["This reverses mitosis and meiosis.","Correct — mitosis → 2 identical diploid; meiosis → 4 different haploid.","Mitosis occurs in somatic cells; meiosis occurs in germ cells.","Only mitosis produces two diploid cells.","Only meiosis produces four haploid cells."]},"fc123":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cell cycle order: G1 → S → G2 → M (mitosis) → cytokinesis; G1, S and G2 together are interphase.</p><p>Interphase takes up most of the cycle (~23 of 24 hours in a typical human cell). Cells may leave from G1 into G0 (quiescence) and re-enter G1 when stimulated. Checkpoints guard the transitions: restriction point and G1/S, G2/M, and the spindle checkpoint in metaphase.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>M → S → G1 → G2 → cytokinesis</b> — S doesn't follow M directly; G1 comes first.</li><li><b>G1 → G2 → S → M → cytokinesis</b> — S comes before G2, not after it.</li><li><b>S → G1 → G2 → M → cytokinesis</b> — G1 comes before S.</li><li><b>G0 → S → M → G1 → G2</b> — G0 is a resting exit, and S must follow G1.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Interphase = G1 + S + G2.</p>","w":["S doesn't follow M directly; G1 comes first.","S comes before G2, not after it.","G1 comes before S.","Correct — G1 → S → G2 → M → cytokinesis.","G0 is a resting exit, and S must follow G1."]},"fc132":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis morphology: single scattered cells, shrunken with intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm and condensed/fragmented nuclei (pyknosis, karyorrhexis), with no inflammation.</p><p>In the liver, apoptotic hepatocytes appear as eosinophilic \"Councilman bodies\" (acidophil bodies), classically in viral hepatitis. Key distinctions from necrosis: apoptosis affects individual cells, the cell shrinks, the membrane stays intact, apoptotic bodies are phagocytosed, and there's no inflammation. Necrosis affects groups of cells, cells swell, membranes rupture, contents leak, and inflammation follows.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Fatty change</b> — Fatty change shows clear lipid vacuoles in hepatocyte cytoplasm, not shrunken eosinophilic cells.</li><li><b>Cellular swelling</b> — Cellular swelling makes cells larger and paler — the opposite of shrinkage.</li><li><b>Liquefactive necrosis</b> — Liquefactive necrosis digests tissue into liquid with abundant inflammatory cells (abscess, brain infarct).</li><li><b>Coagulative necrosis</b> — Coagulative necrosis affects sheets of contiguous cells and draws neutrophils.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Councilman bodies = apoptotic hepatocytes (viral hepatitis, yellow fever).</p>","w":["Correct — this is apoptosis.","Fatty change shows clear lipid vacuoles in hepatocyte cytoplasm, not shrunken eosinophilic cells.","Cellular swelling makes cells larger and paler — the opposite of shrinkage.","Liquefactive necrosis digests tissue into liquid with abundant inflammatory cells (abscess, brain infarct).","Coagulative necrosis affects sheets of contiguous cells and draws neutrophils."]},"fc133":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptotic bodies are ingested by macrophages and neighboring cells, so apoptosis provokes no inflammation.</p><p>Apoptotic cells shrink, bleb and fragment into membrane-bound apoptotic bodies containing cytoplasm and nuclear fragments. \"Eat-me\" signals (phosphatidylserine on the outer leaflet, thrombospondin) and \"find-me\" signals attract phagocytes, which remove the bodies quickly. Necrotic cells lose membrane integrity, release contents (DAMPs, enzymes) and recruit neutrophils.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Organelle swelling and lysosomal leakage</b> — Organelle swelling and lysosomal leakage are necrotic features (population 1).</li><li><b>Recruitment of neutrophils to the area</b> — Neutrophil recruitment is a response to necrosis, not apoptosis.</li><li><b>Loss of plasma membrane integrity</b> — Apoptotic cells keep their membrane integrity.</li><li><b>Random, diffuse degradation of the DNA</b> — Necrosis degrades DNA randomly; apoptosis cleaves it between nucleosomes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apoptosis: shrinkage, blebs, apoptotic bodies, phagocytosis, no inflammation.</p>","w":["Organelle swelling and lysosomal leakage are necrotic features (population 1).","Neutrophil recruitment is a response to necrosis, not apoptosis.","Apoptotic cells keep their membrane integrity.","Necrosis degrades DNA randomly; apoptosis cleaves it between nucleosomes.","Correct — apoptotic fragments are ingested by macrophages."]},"fc134":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis is an active, ATP-dependent program; without ATP, a death stimulus produces necrosis instead.</p><p>Steps needing energy include apoptosome assembly (dATP/ATP binding by Apaf-1), caspase cascades, chromatin condensation, cytoskeletal reorganization for blebbing and phosphatidylserine exposure. If ATP is depleted (mitochondrial poison, severe ischemia), Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase fails, cells swell, and membranes rupture — necrosis. Thus the same stimulus can cause apoptosis or necrosis depending on ATP levels.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It can occur only in cells that are actively dividing</b> — Non-dividing cells (e.g., neurons) can also undergo apoptosis.</li><li><b>It requires an inflammatory response to be completed</b> — Apoptosis characteristically occurs without inflammation.</li><li><b>It requires lysosomal rupture to proceed</b> — Lysosomal leakage is a necrotic feature, not a requirement of apoptosis.</li><li><b>It depends on random DNA degradation by necrotic enzymes</b> — Apoptosis cleaves DNA in a controlled way; random degradation is necrotic.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ATP present → apoptosis; ATP depleted → necrosis.</p>","w":["Non-dividing cells (e.g., neurons) can also undergo apoptosis.","Apoptosis characteristically occurs without inflammation.","Correct — apoptosis is energy-dependent and actively regulated.","Lysosomal leakage is a necrotic feature, not a requirement of apoptosis.","Apoptosis cleaves DNA in a controlled way; random degradation is necrotic."]},"fc135":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Macrophages that engulf apoptotic cells release anti-inflammatory IL-10 and TGF-β, keeping apoptotic clearance silent.</p><p>Efferocytosis (clearance of apoptotic cells) actively suppresses inflammation: macrophages reduce production of TNF, IL-1 and IL-12 and increase IL-10 and TGF-β. This prevents immune responses against self-antigens from dying cells. Impaired clearance of apoptotic debris (e.g., complement C1q deficiency) is associated with SLE.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>To promote fibrosis and scar formation</b> — TGF-β can promote fibrosis elsewhere, but here the purpose is to keep apoptotic clearance non-inflammatory.</li><li><b>To activate caspases in neighboring cells</b> — These cytokines don't trigger apoptosis in neighbors; apoptosis spares adjacent cells.</li><li><b>To induce fever through the hypothalamus</b> — Fever is caused by pyrogenic cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF), not IL-10/TGF-β.</li><li><b>To stimulate neutrophil recruitment</b> — Neutrophil recruitment would cause inflammation — the opposite of what IL-10 and TGF-β do.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> IL-10 and TGF-β = anti-inflammatory cytokines.</p>","w":["TGF-β can promote fibrosis elsewhere, but here the purpose is to keep apoptotic clearance non-inflammatory.","Correct — these cytokines inhibit inflammation.","These cytokines don't trigger apoptosis in neighbors; apoptosis spares adjacent cells.","Fever is caused by pyrogenic cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF), not IL-10/TGF-β.","Neutrophil recruitment would cause inflammation — the opposite of what IL-10 and TGF-β do."]},"fc136":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptotic endonucleases cut DNA in the linker regions between nucleosomes, giving a \"DNA ladder\" of ~180-bp multiples.</p><p>Caspase-3 cleaves ICAD (inhibitor of caspase-activated DNase), releasing CAD, which cuts linker DNA between nucleosomes (each nucleosome protects ~146 bp; with linker ~180–200 bp). Gel electrophoresis shows discrete bands (a ladder). Necrosis produces random DNA degradation by lysosomal enzymes, giving a smear. TUNEL staining detects these free DNA ends in tissue.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Shortening of telomeres in senescent cells</b> — Telomere shortening affects chromosome ends and doesn't produce a ladder.</li><li><b>Failure of DNA replication during S phase</b> — Failed replication doesn't create regularly spaced DNA fragments.</li><li><b>Random degradation of DNA after loss of membrane integrity</b> — Random degradation with a smear is the necrotic pattern (culture B).</li><li><b>Cleavage of DNA by lysosomal enzymes released during necrosis</b> — Lysosomal enzymes in necrosis digest DNA randomly — a smear, not a ladder.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ladder = apoptosis; smear = necrosis.</p>","w":["Telomere shortening affects chromosome ends and doesn't produce a ladder.","Failed replication doesn't create regularly spaced DNA fragments.","Random degradation with a smear is the necrotic pattern (culture B).","Correct — regulated internucleosomal cleavage during apoptosis.","Lysosomal enzymes in necrosis digest DNA randomly — a smear, not a ladder."]},"fc137":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis of interdigital cells separates the fingers; its failure leaves soft-tissue webbing (syndactyly).</p><p>Around weeks 6–8, BMP signaling triggers apoptosis in the interdigital mesenchyme of the hand and foot plates. Simple syndactyly involves only soft tissue (as here — bones normal); complex syndactyly includes bony fusion. Syndactyly between the 3rd and 4th fingers is most common. Other examples of developmental apoptosis: sculpting of joints, removal of the Müllerian or Wolffian ducts, involution of the tadpole tail, elimination of excess neurons.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Proliferation of cells in the limb bud mesoderm</b> — Limb bud proliferation forms the limb, but it doesn't separate the digits.</li><li><b>Necrosis of interdigital tissue caused by ischemia</b> — Removal of the webbing is programmed, physiological apoptosis, not ischemic necrosis.</li><li><b>Fusion of the digital rays</b> — The digital rays (future bones) are normal; the problem is persistent tissue between them.</li><li><b>Migration of neural crest cells into the limb</b> — Neural crest cells don't form limb soft tissue or separate the digits.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Syndactyly = failed interdigital apoptosis.</p>","w":["Correct — failure of interdigital apoptosis.","Limb bud proliferation forms the limb, but it doesn't separate the digits.","Removal of the webbing is programmed, physiological apoptosis, not ischemic necrosis.","The digital rays (future bones) are normal; the problem is persistent tissue between them.","Neural crest cells don't form limb soft tissue or separate the digits."]},"fc138":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis removes structures needed only at earlier developmental stages (e.g., the tadpole tail at metamorphosis) without inflammation.</p><p>Thyroid hormone triggers apoptosis of tail cells during metamorphosis, a classic model of coordinated developmental cell death. Human parallels: involution of the Müllerian ducts in males (anti-Müllerian hormone) or Wolffian ducts in females, removal of the interdigital web, pruning of excess neurons and lymphocytes, and endometrial shedding with hormone withdrawal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Atrophy caused by loss of blood supply</b> — Ischemic loss of blood supply would produce necrosis and inflammation, not orderly removal.</li><li><b>Coagulative necrosis of the tail muscle</b> — Coagulative necrosis would cause inflammation and damage surrounding tissue.</li><li><b>Hypertrophy of hind limbs absorbing the tail</b> — Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size; it doesn't remove the tail.</li><li><b>Autolysis after death of the organism</b> — Autolysis occurs after an organism dies; the tadpole is alive and developing.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Physiologic apoptosis: development, hormone withdrawal, immune selection, cell turnover.</p>","w":["Ischemic loss of blood supply would produce necrosis and inflammation, not orderly removal.","Coagulative necrosis would cause inflammation and damage surrounding tissue.","Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size; it doesn't remove the tail.","Autolysis occurs after an organism dies; the tadpole is alive and developing.","Correct — apoptosis removes the larval structure."]},"fc139":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosis deletes self-reactive lymphocytes during development (central tolerance); defects allow autoimmunity such as SLE.</p><p>In the thymus, T cells that bind self-peptide–MHC too strongly undergo apoptosis (negative selection); those that can't bind MHC at all die by neglect (failed positive selection). B cells that recognize self-antigen in the bone marrow undergo receptor editing or apoptosis. Peripherally, activated lymphocytes are removed via Fas–FasL. Failure of these mechanisms lets autoreactive cells persist, producing autoantibodies (e.g., anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith in SLE).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Complement-mediated lysis of B cells</b> — Complement-mediated lysis isn't the mechanism of lymphocyte selection.</li><li><b>Necrosis of lymphocytes in the bone marrow</b> — Necrosis is pathologic and inflammatory; it isn't how the immune system selects cells.</li><li><b>Neutrophil-mediated killing in lymph nodes</b> — Neutrophils don't select developing lymphocytes.</li><li><b>Phagocytosis of antibodies by macrophages</b> — Macrophages clear apoptotic cells, but phagocytosing antibodies doesn't delete self-reactive cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Negative selection (thymic medulla, AIRE) → apoptosis of self-reactive T cells.</p>","w":["Complement-mediated lysis isn't the mechanism of lymphocyte selection.","Necrosis is pathologic and inflammatory; it isn't how the immune system selects cells.","Correct — apoptosis acts as quality control.","Neutrophils don't select developing lymphocytes.","Macrophages clear apoptotic cells, but phagocytosing antibodies doesn't delete self-reactive cells."]},"fc140":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> CD8⁺ cytotoxic T cells kill virus-infected cells by inducing apoptosis (perforin/granzyme and Fas ligand–Fas).</p><p>CTLs recognize viral peptides on MHC class I. They kill by (1) releasing perforin, which forms pores, and granzymes, which enter the target and activate caspases directly (and cleave Bid); and (2) Fas ligand binding Fas on the target, activating caspase-8. The infected hepatocytes become shrunken eosinophilic apoptotic bodies (Councilman bodies). NK cells use similar mechanisms against cells lacking MHC class I.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ischemic coagulative necrosis</b> — Ischemia causes coagulative necrosis of groups of cells, not single cells next to lymphocytes.</li><li><b>Hypertrophy followed by atrophy</b> — Hypertrophy and atrophy are adaptations in cell size, not cell killing.</li><li><b>Liquefactive necrosis by neutrophil enzymes</b> — Liquefactive necrosis by neutrophils forms an abscess, not single shrunken cells.</li><li><b>Antibody-dependent complement lysis</b> — Complement lysis causes membrane rupture, not orderly shrinkage with condensed nuclei.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CTL killing: perforin + granzyme B, Fas–FasL.</p>","w":["Ischemia causes coagulative necrosis of groups of cells, not single cells next to lymphocytes.","Correct — cytotoxic T lymphocyte–induced apoptosis.","Hypertrophy and atrophy are adaptations in cell size, not cell killing.","Liquefactive necrosis by neutrophils forms an abscess, not single shrunken cells.","Complement lysis causes membrane rupture, not orderly shrinkage with condensed nuclei."]},"fc141":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> \"Sunburn cells\" are keratinocytes undergoing p53-driven apoptosis after UV-induced DNA damage.</p><p>UVB forms pyrimidine (thymine) dimers in DNA. p53 stabilizes, inducing p21 (arrest for repair by nucleotide excision repair) and, if damage is excessive, Bax/PUMA-mediated apoptosis. Removing badly damaged keratinocytes prevents propagation of mutations. Defective NER (xeroderma pigmentosum) or loss of p53 increases skin cancer risk (squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, melanoma).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Accumulation of melanin in damaged cells</b> — Melanin protects against UV but isn't what makes these cells shrunken and eosinophilic.</li><li><b>Necrosis caused by heat coagulation of proteins</b> — Sunburn isn't a thermal burn; scattered single shrunken cells are apoptotic, not necrotic.</li><li><b>Hyperplasia of the basal layer</b> — Hyperplasia increases cell number rather than producing shrunken eosinophilic cells.</li><li><b>Neutrophil-mediated destruction of keratinocytes</b> — There's no neutrophil-mediated destruction; apoptosis is non-inflammatory.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Xeroderma pigmentosum: defective NER of thymine dimers → early skin cancers.</p>","w":["Melanin protects against UV but isn't what makes these cells shrunken and eosinophilic.","Sunburn isn't a thermal burn; scattered single shrunken cells are apoptotic, not necrotic.","Hyperplasia increases cell number rather than producing shrunken eosinophilic cells.","Correct — apoptosis of UV-damaged keratinocytes.","There's no neutrophil-mediated destruction; apoptosis is non-inflammatory."]},"fc152":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> p53 induces Bax, which (with Bak) oligomerizes in the outer mitochondrial membrane, forming pores that release cytochrome c.</p><p>Intrinsic pathway regulators: BH3-only sensors (Bid, Bim, Bad, PUMA, NOXA) detect stress; effectors Bax/Bak form pores; anti-apoptotic Bcl-2, Bcl-xL and Mcl-1 bind and restrain Bax/Bak. p53 tips the balance toward death by inducing Bax, PUMA and NOXA. Mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization releases cytochrome c and SMAC/DIABLO (which neutralizes IAPs).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caspase-8</b> — Caspase-8 is the initiator caspase of the extrinsic pathway, not a pore-forming protein.</li><li><b>Bcl-xL</b> — Bcl-xL is anti-apoptotic and keeps the outer membrane intact.</li><li><b>Bcl-2</b> — Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic, prevents pore formation and is decreased by p53.</li><li><b>Survivin</b> — Survivin is an inhibitor of apoptosis protein that blocks caspases; it doesn't form pores.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pro-apoptotic: Bax, Bak, Bid, Bim, PUMA, NOXA. Anti-apoptotic: Bcl-2, Bcl-xL, Mcl-1.</p>","w":["Correct — Bax forms the pores.","Caspase-8 is the initiator caspase of the extrinsic pathway, not a pore-forming protein.","Bcl-xL is anti-apoptotic and keeps the outer membrane intact.","Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic, prevents pore formation and is decreased by p53.","Survivin is an inhibitor of apoptosis protein that blocks caspases; it doesn't form pores."]},"fc153":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Apoptosome = cytochrome c + Apaf-1 (+ dATP) assembled into a heptameric wheel that recruits and activates procaspase-9.</p><p>Released cytochrome c binds Apaf-1, which changes shape and oligomerizes. The caspase recruitment domains (CARDs) of Apaf-1 recruit procaspase-9, bringing molecules close enough to activate each other. Active caspase-9 cleaves and activates executioner caspases-3 and -7, which dismantle the cell (cleaving PARP, lamins, ICAD, cytoskeletal proteins).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>p21</b> — p21 is a CDK inhibitor that halts the cell cycle.</li><li><b>Fas-associated death domain (FADD)</b> — FADD is an adaptor in the extrinsic pathway's DISC that activates caspase-8.</li><li><b>Bid</b> — Bid links the extrinsic and intrinsic pathways at the mitochondrion; it isn't part of the apoptosome.</li><li><b>Poly ADP-ribose polymerase (PARP)</b> — PARP is a DNA repair enzyme cleaved by caspase-3 — a marker of apoptosis, not an apoptosome component.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Intrinsic: cytochrome c → Apaf-1 → caspase-9 → caspase-3.</p>","w":["p21 is a CDK inhibitor that halts the cell cycle.","FADD is an adaptor in the extrinsic pathway's DISC that activates caspase-8.","Bid links the extrinsic and intrinsic pathways at the mitochondrion; it isn't part of the apoptosome.","PARP is a DNA repair enzyme cleaved by caspase-3 — a marker of apoptosis, not an apoptosome component.","Correct — cytochrome c binds Apaf-1."]},"fc154":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Extrinsic pathway: FasL binds Fas → FADD recruited → DISC forms → procaspase-8 activated → executioner caspases.</p><p>Death receptors (Fas/CD95, TNFR1, TRAIL receptors) have cytoplasmic death domains. Ligand binding clusters receptors; FADD binds via its death domain and recruits procaspase-8 via its death effector domain, forming the death-inducing signaling complex. c-FLIP can inhibit this step. Caspase-8 activates caspase-3 directly and cleaves Bid to engage the mitochondrial pathway. Without FADD, the DISC can't form.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Transcription of Bax induced by p53</b> — p53-driven Bax transcription belongs to the intrinsic (DNA damage) pathway.</li><li><b>Formation of the apoptosome</b> — The apoptosome (Apaf-1 + cytochrome c + caspase-9) is the intrinsic pathway complex.</li><li><b>Phosphatidylserine flipping to the outer leaflet</b> — Phosphatidylserine exposure is a late event in both pathways, not directly controlled by FADD.</li><li><b>Release of cytochrome c from mitochondria</b> — Cytochrome c release is part of the intrinsic pathway; FADD acts upstream of caspase-8.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Extrinsic: FasL → Fas → FADD → caspase-8.</p>","w":["p53-driven Bax transcription belongs to the intrinsic (DNA damage) pathway.","The apoptosome (Apaf-1 + cytochrome c + caspase-9) is the intrinsic pathway complex.","Correct — FADD is needed for DISC formation and caspase-8 activation.","Phosphatidylserine exposure is a late event in both pathways, not directly controlled by FADD.","Cytochrome c release is part of the intrinsic pathway; FADD acts upstream of caspase-8."]},"fc155":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caspase-8 cleaves Bid to truncated Bid (tBid), which activates Bax/Bak at mitochondria — linking extrinsic to intrinsic pathways.</p><p>In some cells (type II cells, e.g., hepatocytes), extrinsic signaling alone generates too little caspase-8 to kill; amplification through tBid and mitochondrial cytochrome c release is required. In type I cells (e.g., lymphocytes), caspase-8 directly activates enough caspase-3. Bcl-2 overexpression therefore protects type II cells from Fas-mediated death.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Apaf-1</b> — Apaf-1 binds cytochrome c after release; caspase-8 doesn't cleave it.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 responds to DNA damage; it isn't a caspase-8 substrate linking the pathways.</li><li><b>Bcl-2</b> — Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic and would block, not trigger, cytochrome c release.</li><li><b>Survivin</b> — Survivin blocks caspases; it doesn't transmit the signal to mitochondria.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> tBid = extrinsic → intrinsic crosstalk.</p>","w":["Apaf-1 binds cytochrome c after release; caspase-8 doesn't cleave it.","Correct — Bid links the pathways.","p53 responds to DNA damage; it isn't a caspase-8 substrate linking the pathways.","Bcl-2 is anti-apoptotic and would block, not trigger, cytochrome c release.","Survivin blocks caspases; it doesn't transmit the signal to mitochondria."]},"fc156":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caspases are cysteine-dependent, aspartate-specific proteases: an active-site cysteine cleaves substrates after aspartic acid.</p><p>Caspases are made as inactive zymogens (procaspases). Initiators (8, 9, 10, 2) are activated by proximity in complexes (DISC, apoptosome); they activate executioners (3, 6, 7) by cleavage. Executioners cleave hundreds of substrates. Caspase inhibitor peptides (e.g., Z-VAD-FMK) mimic Asp-containing cleavage sites and bind the catalytic cysteine; IAPs (XIAP, survivin) inhibit caspases in vivo.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Threonine proteases that cleave after arginine</b> — Threonine proteases are found in the proteasome, not the caspase family.</li><li><b>Metalloproteinases that cleave after glycine</b> — Metalloproteinases use zinc and degrade extracellular matrix.</li><li><b>Serine proteases that cleave after lysine</b> — Serine proteases (e.g., trypsin) use serine; caspases use cysteine.</li><li><b>Aspartyl proteases that cleave after cysteine</b> — This reverses them — the active-site residue is cysteine and cleavage follows aspartate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Caspase = Cysteine ASPartate protease.</p>","w":["Threonine proteases are found in the proteasome, not the caspase family.","Metalloproteinases use zinc and degrade extracellular matrix.","Serine proteases (e.g., trypsin) use serine; caspases use cysteine.","Correct — cysteine proteases that cleave after aspartic acid.","This reverses them — the active-site residue is cysteine and cleavage follows aspartate."]},"fc157":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caspase-9 is required for the intrinsic (DNA damage) pathway, while Fas ligand acts via caspase-8 (extrinsic), which can bypass caspase-9.</p><p>Without caspase-9, cytochrome c release can't activate executioner caspases efficiently, so radiation-damaged cells resist apoptosis. FasL still activates caspase-8, which directly cleaves caspase-3 (in type I cells), so these cells still die. This experiment demonstrates that the two pathways have separate initiator caspases but converge on shared executioner caspases.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both groups undergo apoptosis normally</b> — Group 1 depends on caspase-9, so apoptosis can't proceed normally.</li><li><b>Both groups die by necrosis with inflammation</b> — Loss of caspase-9 doesn't convert both groups to necrosis; the extrinsic route remains intact.</li><li><b>Group 2 resists apoptosis, but group 1 still undergoes apoptosis</b> — This is reversed — Fas ligand doesn't need caspase-9, while DNA damage does.</li><li><b>Neither group can undergo apoptosis</b> — The extrinsic pathway (caspase-8 → caspase-3) still works in group 2.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Intrinsic initiator = caspase-9; extrinsic initiator = caspase-8; both → caspase-3.</p>","w":["Correct — group 1 resists, group 2 still undergoes apoptosis.","Group 1 depends on caspase-9, so apoptosis can't proceed normally.","Loss of caspase-9 doesn't convert both groups to necrosis; the extrinsic route remains intact.","This is reversed — Fas ligand doesn't need caspase-9, while DNA damage does.","The extrinsic pathway (caspase-8 → caspase-3) still works in group 2."]},"fc158":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Caspase-1 (activated in the inflammasome) processes pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 into active cytokines — inflammation, not apoptosis.</p><p>The NLRP3 inflammasome senses danger signals (urate crystals, ATP, pathogens) and activates caspase-1, which cleaves pro-IL-1β and pro-IL-18 and gasdermin D, causing pyroptosis (inflammatory cell death). Overactive NLRP3 causes cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes, treated with IL-1 blockers (anakinra). Caspase-4/5 (human) also mediate inflammatory responses to intracellular LPS.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caspase-8</b> — Caspase-8 is the initiator caspase of the extrinsic pathway.</li><li><b>Caspase-3</b> — Caspase-3 is the main executioner caspase of apoptosis.</li><li><b>Caspase-9</b> — Caspase-9 is the initiator caspase of the intrinsic pathway.</li><li><b>Caspase-7</b> — Caspase-7 is an effector (executioner) caspase like caspase-3.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1β/IL-18 + pyroptosis (gout crystals activate NLRP3).</p>","w":["Caspase-8 is the initiator caspase of the extrinsic pathway.","Caspase-3 is the main executioner caspase of apoptosis.","Caspase-9 is the initiator caspase of the intrinsic pathway.","Caspase-7 is an effector (executioner) caspase like caspase-3.","Correct — caspase-1 performs cytokine maturation."]},"fc159":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Growth factor/nutrient deprivation is an internal stress that activates the intrinsic pathway → caspase-9 first.</p><p>Growth factors normally maintain anti-apoptotic Bcl-2 family proteins (via PI3K–Akt, which also inactivates pro-apoptotic Bad). Withdrawal lowers Bcl-2/Bcl-xL and activates BH3-only proteins (Bim, Bad), freeing Bax/Bak to permeabilize mitochondria. Cytochrome c release leads to the apoptosome and caspase-9 activation, then caspase-3. Other intrinsic triggers: DNA damage, oxidative stress, misfolded proteins, hypoxia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caspase-3</b> — Caspase-3 is the executioner caspase, activated after an initiator.</li><li><b>Caspase-8</b> — Caspase-8 initiates the extrinsic pathway triggered by death ligands (FasL, TNF).</li><li><b>Caspase-14</b> — Caspase-14 is involved in skin development, not stress-induced apoptosis.</li><li><b>Caspase-1</b> — Caspase-1 handles cytokine maturation (inflammation), not apoptosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Growth factor withdrawal → ↓Akt → active Bad/Bim → intrinsic pathway.</p>","w":["Caspase-3 is the executioner caspase, activated after an initiator.","Caspase-8 initiates the extrinsic pathway triggered by death ligands (FasL, TNF).","Correct — caspase-9 is activated first.","Caspase-14 is involved in skin development, not stress-induced apoptosis.","Caspase-1 handles cytokine maturation (inflammation), not apoptosis."]},"fc160":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Defective Fas (CD95) → failed extrinsic apoptosis of activated lymphocytes → autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome (ALPS).</p><p>After an immune response, activated lymphocytes express Fas and FasL and are removed by activation-induced cell death. Without it, lymphocytes accumulate (chronic lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly), double-negative (CD4⁻CD8⁻) T cells increase, and autoimmune cytopenias develop (autoimmune hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia). Risk of lymphoma is increased. ALPS illustrates how too little apoptosis causes autoimmunity and lymphoproliferation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased activity of caspase-9</b> — Increased caspase-9 activity would increase apoptosis, not cause lymphocyte accumulation.</li><li><b>Loss of the anti-apoptotic protein Bcl-2</b> — Loss of Bcl-2 would make cells die more easily rather than accumulate.</li><li><b>Overproduction of cytochrome c by mitochondria</b> — Cytochrome c isn't overproduced; the defect is at the death receptor.</li><li><b>Excessive apoptosis of lymphocytes through the intrinsic pathway</b> — The problem is too little apoptosis, not too much.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ALPS: Fas mutation, ↑double-negative T cells, lymphadenopathy, autoimmune cytopenias.</p>","w":["Increased caspase-9 activity would increase apoptosis, not cause lymphocyte accumulation.","Correct — failure of death receptor–mediated apoptosis.","Loss of Bcl-2 would make cells die more easily rather than accumulate.","Cytochrome c isn't overproduced; the defect is at the death receptor.","The problem is too little apoptosis, not too much."]},"fc161":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Survivin is an inhibitor of apoptosis protein (IAP) that blocks caspases; overexpression helps tumors resist chemotherapy.</p><p>IAPs (XIAP, cIAP1/2, survivin) bind caspases-3, -7 and -9, preventing their activity. Survivin also participates in mitosis (chromosomal passenger complex). It's highly expressed in many cancers but low in most normal adult tissues, making it a marker of poor prognosis and a therapeutic target. Mitochondrial SMAC/DIABLO is the natural antagonist of IAPs, and SMAC mimetics are being studied as cancer drugs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Bax</b> — Bax is pro-apoptotic and forms pores in the outer mitochondrial membrane.</li><li><b>Apaf-1</b> — Apaf-1 helps activate caspase-9 in the apoptosome.</li><li><b>p53</b> — p53 promotes apoptosis in response to DNA damage; it doesn't block caspases.</li><li><b>Cytochrome c</b> — Cytochrome c activates the apoptosome and promotes caspase activity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> IAPs (survivin, XIAP) block caspases; SMAC/DIABLO neutralizes IAPs.</p>","w":["Bax is pro-apoptotic and forms pores in the outer mitochondrial membrane.","Apaf-1 helps activate caspase-9 in the apoptosome.","p53 promotes apoptosis in response to DNA damage; it doesn't block caspases.","Correct — survivin is an IAP that blocks caspases.","Cytochrome c activates the apoptosome and promotes caspase activity."]},"fc162":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Loss of functional p53 → DNA-damaged tumor cells can't induce Bax (or p21), so they resist chemotherapy-induced apoptosis.</p><p>Many chemotherapy drugs and radiation kill cells by damaging DNA and relying on p53 to trigger apoptosis. TP53-mutant tumors therefore tend to be more resistant and carry worse prognoses. Without p53, cells also fail to arrest the cycle (no p21), accumulate further mutations, and become genetically unstable. TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Enhanced expression of p21 and cell-cycle arrest</b> — p21 induction also requires p53, so it would be reduced rather than enhanced.</li><li><b>Increased transcription of Bax and enhanced apoptosis</b> — Increased Bax transcription is what functional p53 does; a loss-of-function mutation prevents it.</li><li><b>Increased activation of caspase-8 by the death receptor</b> — Caspase-8 belongs to the death receptor pathway, which p53 loss doesn't increase.</li><li><b>Increased formation of the apoptosome</b> — Less, not more, cytochrome c release and apoptosome formation occurs without p53.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> p53 loss → genomic instability + chemoresistance.</p>","w":["Correct — failure to induce Bax reduces apoptosis.","p21 induction also requires p53, so it would be reduced rather than enhanced.","Increased Bax transcription is what functional p53 does; a loss-of-function mutation prevents it.","Caspase-8 belongs to the death receptor pathway, which p53 loss doesn't increase.","Less, not more, cytochrome c release and apoptosome formation occurs without p53."]},"fc164":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chemotherapy kills tumor cells mainly by inducing apoptosis — individual-cell death with nuclear and cytoplasmic fragmentation and no inflammation.</p><p>DNA-damaging drugs (alkylating agents, antimetabolites, topoisomerase inhibitors) and antimitotic drugs activate p53 and the intrinsic pathway. Rapidly dividing normal (labile) cells — marrow, GI mucosa, hair follicles — are also affected, explaining myelosuppression, mucositis and alopecia. Tumor regression on imaging reflects apoptotic loss of cells and their silent clearance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Coagulative necrosis</b> — Coagulative necrosis kills groups of cells, preserves outlines and causes inflammation.</li><li><b>Mitochondrial poisoning</b> — Mitochondrial poisoning causes ATP depletion and necrosis, not targeted single-cell death.</li><li><b>Phagocytosis</b> — Phagocytosis clears apoptotic bodies afterward but isn't the killing mechanism.</li><li><b>Acute inflammation</b> — Acute inflammation accompanies necrosis; apoptosis produces none.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Chemotherapy side effects reflect apoptosis of labile cells.</p>","w":["Coagulative necrosis kills groups of cells, preserves outlines and causes inflammation.","Mitochondrial poisoning causes ATP depletion and necrosis, not targeted single-cell death.","Phagocytosis clears apoptotic bodies afterward but isn't the killing mechanism.","Acute inflammation accompanies necrosis; apoptosis produces none.","Correct — the neoplasm responded through apoptosis."]},"fc165":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bcl-2 overexpression blocks Bax/Bak pore formation, preventing cytochrome c release — tumor cells survive (they don't divide faster, they just don't die).</p><p>Follicular lymphoma is the classic example: t(14;18) moves BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy-chain promoter, causing constitutive Bcl-2 overexpression. Tumor cells accumulate slowly (indolent course) and resist chemotherapy. Bcl-2 overexpression is also seen in CLL. Bcl-2 inhibitors (venetoclax) restore apoptosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It activates caspase-3 in neighboring cells</b> — Bcl-2 inhibits rather than activates the caspase cascade.</li><li><b>It promotes phagocytosis of tumor cells by macrophages</b> — Bcl-2 prevents apoptosis, so fewer cells are cleared by phagocytosis.</li><li><b>It stimulates DNA replication in S phase</b> — Bcl-2 doesn't drive proliferation; it blocks cell death.</li><li><b>It increases expression of Fas on tumor cells</b> — Bcl-2 doesn't increase death receptor (Fas) expression.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> t(14;18) → Bcl-2 → follicular lymphoma.</p>","w":["Bcl-2 inhibits rather than activates the caspase cascade.","Bcl-2 prevents apoptosis, so fewer cells are cleared by phagocytosis.","Bcl-2 doesn't drive proliferation; it blocks cell death.","Bcl-2 doesn't increase death receptor (Fas) expression.","Correct — Bcl-2 prevents cytochrome c release."]},"fc166":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bcl-2 inhibition (venetoclax) frees Bax/Bak → outer mitochondrial membrane permeabilized → cytochrome c released → caspase-9 → caspase-3 → apoptosis.</p><p>BH3 mimetics like venetoclax occupy the BH3-binding groove of Bcl-2, displacing pro-apoptotic proteins that then activate Bax/Bak. CLL cells depend heavily on Bcl-2, so venetoclax kills them rapidly — so rapidly that tumor lysis syndrome (hyperkalemia, hyperuricemia, hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia) is a major risk, requiring gradual dose ramp-up and prophylaxis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased expression of survivin</b> — Survivin induction would counteract the drug's goal and isn't expected.</li><li><b>Decreased activity of caspase-3</b> — Caspase-3 activity rises as the cells undergo apoptosis.</li><li><b>Increased binding of Bcl-2 to Bax</b> — The drug blocks Bcl-2, so it can no longer bind and hold back Bax.</li><li><b>Decreased permeability of the outer mitochondrial membrane</b> — Blocking Bcl-2 increases, not decreases, outer membrane permeability.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Venetoclax (BH3 mimetic) → CLL; watch for tumor lysis syndrome.</p>","w":["Survivin induction would counteract the drug's goal and isn't expected.","Caspase-3 activity rises as the cells undergo apoptosis.","Correct — cytochrome c rises in the cytosol and caspase-9 activates.","The drug blocks Bcl-2, so it can no longer bind and hold back Bax.","Blocking Bcl-2 increases, not decreases, outer membrane permeability."]},"fc167":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Excessive neuronal apoptosis contributes to neurodegenerative diseases (Parkinson, Alzheimer, Huntington, ALS).</p><p>In Parkinson disease, dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta are progressively lost, with α-synuclein Lewy bodies and mitochondrial dysfunction (PINK1/parkin mutations impair mitophagy). Oxidative stress and misfolded proteins trigger intrinsic apoptosis. Too much apoptosis = degenerative diseases and ischemic injury; too little = cancer, autoimmunity and developmental anomalies (syndactyly).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Syndactyly</b> — Syndactyly results from failed (too little) interdigital apoptosis.</li><li><b>Chemotherapy-resistant carcinoma</b> — Chemoresistance reflects resistance to apoptosis.</li><li><b>Follicular lymphoma</b> — Follicular lymphoma is caused by too little apoptosis (Bcl-2 overexpression).</li><li><b>Systemic lupus erythematosus</b> — SLE involves defective apoptosis/clearance that lets autoreactive lymphocytes survive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑Apoptosis: neurodegeneration, ischemia, AIDS (CD4 loss). ↓Apoptosis: cancer, autoimmunity.</p>","w":["Syndactyly results from failed (too little) interdigital apoptosis.","Correct — Parkinson disease.","Chemoresistance reflects resistance to apoptosis.","Follicular lymphoma is caused by too little apoptosis (Bcl-2 overexpression).","SLE involves defective apoptosis/clearance that lets autoreactive lymphocytes survive."]},"fc168":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> TUNEL assay labels free 3′-OH DNA ends produced by apoptotic DNA fragmentation, identifying apoptotic nuclei in tissue.</p><p>Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase adds labeled dUTP to DNA strand breaks. Other apoptosis markers: annexin V binding (phosphatidylserine exposure, flow cytometry), cleaved caspase-3 immunostaining, cleaved PARP on Western blot, and DNA laddering on gels. Ki-67 is a proliferation marker.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ki-67 immunostaining</b> — Ki-67 marks proliferating cells, not dying ones.</li><li><b>Gram stain</b> — Gram stain identifies bacteria.</li><li><b>Periodic acid–Schiff stain</b> — PAS stains glycogen, mucins and basement membranes.</li><li><b>Northern blot</b> — A Northern blot detects RNA, not DNA breaks in tissue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Apoptosis assays: TUNEL, annexin V, cleaved caspase-3, cleaved PARP, DNA ladder.</p>","w":["Ki-67 marks proliferating cells, not dying ones.","Gram stain identifies bacteria.","PAS stains glycogen, mucins and basement membranes.","Correct — the TUNEL assay.","A Northern blot detects RNA, not DNA breaks in tissue."]},"fc169":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> PARP cleavage into an inactive fragment is a hallmark of executioner caspase (caspase-3/7) activity during apoptosis.</p><p>PARP-1 senses DNA strand breaks and recruits repair proteins using NAD⁺. During apoptosis, caspase-3 cleaves PARP (116 kDa → 89 + 24 kDa fragments), stopping DNA repair and preserving cellular NAD⁺/ATP for the energy-dependent death program. PARP inhibitors (olaparib) exploit synthetic lethality in BRCA1/2-mutant cancers, which already lack homologous recombination repair.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Caspase-1</b> — Caspase-1 processes inflammatory cytokines; PARP cleavage reflects executioner caspase activity.</li><li><b>Lysosomal acid hydrolase</b> — Lysosomal hydrolases act in necrosis/autophagy and don't produce the specific PARP fragment.</li><li><b>Cyclin-dependent kinase 4</b> — CDK4 phosphorylates Rb to drive the G1/S transition.</li><li><b>Telomerase</b> — Telomerase lengthens telomeres; it doesn't cleave proteins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PARP inhibitors → BRCA-mutant breast/ovarian cancer (synthetic lethality).</p>","w":["Correct — caspase-3 cleaves PARP.","Caspase-1 processes inflammatory cytokines; PARP cleavage reflects executioner caspase activity.","Lysosomal hydrolases act in necrosis/autophagy and don't produce the specific PARP fragment.","CDK4 phosphorylates Rb to drive the G1/S transition.","Telomerase lengthens telomeres; it doesn't cleave proteins."]},"fc170":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tissue size reflects balance: too little apoptosis → cells accumulate (cancer, autoimmunity); too much → tissue loss (degeneration).</p><p>Normal tissues maintain constant cell numbers by matching proliferation with apoptosis (e.g., intestinal epithelium renewing every few days). Shift toward survival: tumors (Bcl-2 overexpression, p53 loss), autoimmune disease (Fas defects). Shift toward death: neurodegenerative disease, ischemic injury, HIV-related CD4 depletion, some anemias.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Apoptosis and proliferation are controlled by the same single gene</b> — Many genes (Bcl-2 family, p53, caspases) control apoptosis and proliferation.</li><li><b>Apoptosis only occurs during embryonic development</b> — Apoptosis continues throughout life, not only during embryonic development.</li><li><b>Apoptosis always causes inflammation that damages tissues</b> — Apoptosis characteristically doesn't cause inflammation.</li><li><b>Necrosis replaces apoptosis in adult tissues</b> — Apoptosis remains the main physiological form of cell death in adults.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Homeostasis = proliferation ≈ apoptosis.</p>","w":["Many genes (Bcl-2 family, p53, caspases) control apoptosis and proliferation.","Apoptosis continues throughout life, not only during embryonic development.","Apoptosis characteristically doesn't cause inflammation.","Apoptosis remains the main physiological form of cell death in adults.","Correct — too little apoptosis causes accumulation; too much causes tissue loss."]},"fe1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Crossing over happens in pachytene, when homologous chromosomes are fully paired (synapsed) via the synaptonemal complex.</p><p>Prophase I substages (professor's LZPD): leptotene — chromosomes condense into thin threads; zygotene — homologs begin to pair (synapsis starts); pachytene — synapsis complete, tetrads (bivalents) form and crossing over exchanges segments between non-sister chromatids; diplotene — homologs begin to separate but stay joined at chiasmata (crossover points). Textbooks add diakinesis (maximal condensation). Primary oocytes arrest in diplotene from fetal life until ovulation. Crossing over creates genetic diversity and helps homologs segregate properly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Leptotene</b> — Leptotene is when chromosomes first condense into thin threads.</li><li><b>Zygotene</b> — Zygotene is when homologs begin to pair, before crossing over.</li><li><b>Diplotene</b> — Diplotene is when homologs start to separate after crossing over (chiasmata visible).</li><li><b>Diakinesis</b> — Diakinesis isn't part of the professor's LZPD sequence, and crossing over is already complete.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oocytes arrest in diplotene of prophase I until ovulation.</p>","w":["Leptotene is when chromosomes first condense into thin threads.","Zygotene is when homologs begin to pair, before crossing over.","Diplotene is when homologs start to separate after crossing over (chiasmata visible).","Correct — pachytene.","Diakinesis isn't part of the professor's LZPD sequence, and crossing over is already complete."]},"fe2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> n = chromosome sets; c = DNA copies. S phase doubles c but not n: 2n, 2c → 2n, 4c.</p><p>A human somatic cell in G1 has 46 chromosomes (2n) and 46 DNA molecules (2c). After replication each chromosome has two sister chromatids joined at the centromere — still 46 chromosomes (2n) but 92 DNA molecules (4c). Chromosome number changes only when centromeres split or cells divide.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1n, 2c</b> — 1n, 2c describes the cell at the end of meiosis I.</li><li><b>1n, 1c</b> — 1n, 1c describes a mature gamete after meiosis II.</li><li><b>2n, 2c</b> — 2n, 2c describes the cell before S phase (or a new zygote).</li><li><b>4n, 4c</b> — Chromosome number doesn't double in S phase.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> G1: 2n2c; G2: 2n4c; after MI: 1n2c; after MII: 1n1c.</p>","w":["Correct — 2n, 4c.","1n, 2c describes the cell at the end of meiosis I.","1n, 1c describes a mature gamete after meiosis II.","2n, 2c describes the cell before S phase (or a new zygote).","Chromosome number doesn't double in S phase."]},"fe3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After meiosis I, homologs have separated: each cell is haploid (1n) but chromosomes still have two chromatids (2c).</p><p>Meiosis I is the reductional division — homologous chromosomes go to opposite poles, halving chromosome number (46 → 23). Meiosis II is equational — sister chromatids separate (like mitosis), halving DNA content. In spermatogenesis, primary spermatocytes (2n, 4c) → secondary spermatocytes (1n, 2c) → spermatids (1n, 1c). Nondisjunction in meiosis I produces gametes with both homologs; in meiosis II, with two identical sister chromatids.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>2n, 4c</b> — 2n, 4c describes the cell entering meiosis I (primary spermatocyte).</li><li><b>4n, 4c</b> — 4n, 4c isn't a normal stage; four 1n, 1c cells appear only after meiosis II.</li><li><b>2n, 2c</b> — 2n, 2c is a normal diploid cell before S phase.</li><li><b>1n, 1c</b> — 1n, 1c is the state after meiosis II.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> MI halves n (reductional); MII halves c (equational).</p>","w":["2n, 4c describes the cell entering meiosis I (primary spermatocyte).","4n, 4c isn't a normal stage; four 1n, 1c cells appear only after meiosis II.","2n, 2c is a normal diploid cell before S phase.","1n, 1c is the state after meiosis II.","Correct — 1n, 2c."]},"fe4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mitosis → two identical 2n, 2c cells; meiosis → four genetically different 1n, 1c gametes.</p><p>Meiosis has one DNA replication followed by two divisions, so the chromosome number is halved; fertilization restores 46. Genetic variation comes from crossing over (pachytene) and independent assortment of homologs (metaphase I). Mitosis maintains chromosome number for growth and repair.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mitosis halves the chromosome number</b> — Mitosis keeps chromosome number constant.</li><li><b>Meiosis occurs in all somatic cells</b> — Meiosis occurs only in germ cells.</li><li><b>Both produce haploid cells</b> — Only meiosis produces haploid cells.</li><li><b>Meiosis involves only one division, with no reduction in DNA</b> — Meiosis has two divisions and reduces chromosome number.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Variation in meiosis: crossing over + independent assortment.</p>","w":["Mitosis keeps chromosome number constant.","Meiosis occurs only in germ cells.","Correct — mitosis gives two 2n, 2c cells; meiosis gives four 1n, 1c cells.","Only meiosis produces haploid cells.","Meiosis has two divisions and reduces chromosome number."]},"fe5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Primordial germ cells arise in the wall of the yolk sac (near the allantois) and migrate along the dorsal mesentery of the hindgut to the developing gonads.</p><p>PGCs appear around week 3–4 (originating from epiblast) and migrate by amoeboid movement, arriving at the genital ridges by week 6. They induce gonad development; if they don't arrive, gonads don't develop. PGCs that stray along the migration path can give rise to extragonadal germ cell tumors (e.g., sacrococcygeal teratoma — most common tumor in newborns, and mediastinal germ cell tumors).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>In the neural crest; it migrates to the adrenal medulla</b> — Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla, not germ cells.</li><li><b>In the notochord; it migrates to the somites</b> — The notochord forms the axis, and somites form bone, muscle and dermis.</li><li><b>In the gonad; it migrates to the yolk sac</b> — This reverses the path: PGCs move from the yolk sac to the gonads.</li><li><b>In the trophoblast; it migrates to the placenta</b> — The trophoblast forms the placenta, not germ cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Misplaced PGCs → sacrococcygeal teratoma.</p>","w":["Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla, not germ cells.","Correct — yolk sac wall → developing gonads.","The notochord forms the axis, and somites form bone, muscle and dermis.","This reverses the path: PGCs move from the yolk sac to the gonads.","The trophoblast forms the placenta, not germ cells."]},"fe6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Nondisjunction = failure of homologs (meiosis I) or sister chromatids (meiosis II) to separate → gametes with n+1 or n−1 → trisomy or monosomy after fertilization.</p><p>Maternal meiosis I nondisjunction is the most common cause of trisomy 21, and risk rises with maternal age (oocytes remain arrested for decades and cohesion weakens). Most aneuploidies cause early miscarriage; viable autosomal trisomies are 21, 18 and 13. Monosomy X (Turner) is the only viable monosomy. Robertsonian translocation (e.g., 14;21) is another cause of Down syndrome.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Polyspermy</b> — Polyspermy is entry of more than one sperm into an oocyte.</li><li><b>Translocation</b> — A translocation moves a chromosome segment to another chromosome.</li><li><b>Capacitation</b> — Capacitation is activation of sperm in the female tract.</li><li><b>Crossing over</b> — Crossing over is normal exchange between homologs in pachytene.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Most common cause of Down syndrome: maternal meiosis I nondisjunction.</p>","w":["Polyspermy is entry of more than one sperm into an oocyte.","A translocation moves a chromosome segment to another chromosome.","Capacitation is activation of sperm in the female tract.","Correct — nondisjunction.","Crossing over is normal exchange between homologs in pachytene."]},"fe7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Trisomy 18 = Edwards syndrome: rocker-bottom feet, clenched fists with overlapping fingers, micrognathia, prominent occiput, low-set ears, heart defects; most die within the first year.</p><p>Professor's order: 21 · 18 · 13 = Down · Edwards · Patau. Edwards (\"Election age 18\"): severe intellectual disability, VSD, horseshoe kidney. Patau (\"Puberty 13\"): holoprosencephaly, cleft lip/palate, polydactyly, microphthalmia, cutis aplasia. Down (\"Drinking age 21\"): flat face, upslanting palpebral fissures, single palmar crease, endocardial cushion defect, duodenal atresia, early Alzheimer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Patau syndrome</b> — Patau syndrome is trisomy 13.</li><li><b>Down syndrome</b> — Down syndrome is trisomy 21.</li><li><b>Turner syndrome</b> — Turner syndrome is 45,X (monosomy).</li><li><b>Klinefelter syndrome</b> — Klinefelter syndrome is 47,XXY.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Edwards: clenched fists + rocker-bottom feet + micrognathia.</p>","w":["Correct — Edwards syndrome.","Patau syndrome is trisomy 13.","Down syndrome is trisomy 21.","Turner syndrome is 45,X (monosomy).","Klinefelter syndrome is 47,XXY."]},"fe8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Patau syndrome = trisomy 13: holoprosencephaly, cleft lip/palate, polydactyly, microphthalmia, cutis aplasia, congenital heart disease.</p><p>Many features reflect midline forebrain defects (holoprosencephaly → cyclopia spectrum, single nostril). Severe intellectual disability; most die within the first year. Quad screen findings are usually less informative; ultrasound shows structural anomalies. Remember 21 · 18 · 13 = Down · Edwards · Patau.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Trisomy 21</b> — Trisomy 21 causes Down syndrome.</li><li><b>Trisomy 16</b> — Trisomy 16 is common in miscarriages but isn't Patau syndrome.</li><li><b>Trisomy X</b> — Trisomy X is triple X syndrome.</li><li><b>Trisomy 18</b> — Trisomy 18 causes Edwards syndrome.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Trisomy 16 = most common trisomy in miscarriages.</p>","w":["Trisomy 21 causes Down syndrome.","Trisomy 16 is common in miscarriages but isn't Patau syndrome.","Trisomy X is triple X syndrome.","Trisomy 18 causes Edwards syndrome.","Correct — trisomy 13."]},"fe9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In the testis, sex (medullary) cords that canalize become seminiferous tubules; non-canalized cords form the interstitium (professor's notes).</p><p>In males, SRY on the Y chromosome triggers the testis pathway: medullary cords develop into seminiferous cords (later tubules after canalization at puberty), with Sertoli cells derived from the cords and Leydig cells in the interstitium. The cords connect to the rete testis. In females, without SRY, medullary cords regress and cortical cords give granulosa cells around oocytes in follicles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Granulosa cells; ovarian follicles</b> — Granulosa cells and ovarian follicles are the female pattern.</li><li><b>Vas deferens; prostate</b> — The vas deferens and prostate are downstream ducts and glands, not cord derivatives.</li><li><b>Leydig cells; Sertoli cells only</b> — Leydig and Sertoli cells are cells within the interstitium and tubules, not the fates of canalized vs non-canalized cords as described.</li><li><b>Rete testis; epididymis</b> — The rete testis collects from the tubules; it isn't the fate of the cords as described.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SRY (Y chromosome) → testis-determining factor.</p>","w":["Granulosa cells and ovarian follicles are the female pattern.","The vas deferens and prostate are downstream ducts and glands, not cord derivatives.","Correct — seminiferous tubules; interstitium.","Leydig and Sertoli cells are cells within the interstitium and tubules, not the fates of canalized vs non-canalized cords as described.","The rete testis collects from the tubules; it isn't the fate of the cords as described."]},"fe10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Leydig cells in the interstitial tissue between seminiferous tubules secrete testosterone in response to LH.</p><p>In the fetus, Leydig cell testosterone (stimulated by placental hCG) drives Wolffian duct development into epididymis, vas deferens and seminal vesicles; DHT (via 5α-reductase) masculinizes external genitalia and the prostate. At puberty, LH stimulates Leydig cells again. Memory hook: LH → Leydig; FSH → Sertoli. Leydig cell tumors may contain Reinke crystals and produce androgens or estrogens.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Theca interna cells, between follicles</b> — Theca interna cells are in the ovary.</li><li><b>Sertoli cells, on the tubule wall</b> — Sertoli cells line the tubules and make MIF (AMH), not testosterone.</li><li><b>Granulosa cells, around the oocyte</b> — Granulosa cells surround the oocyte in the ovary.</li><li><b>Spermatogonia, in the lumen</b> — Spermatogonia are germ cells at the tubule base, not hormone-producing cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> LH → Leydig (testosterone); FSH → Sertoli (ABP, inhibin).</p>","w":["Theca interna cells are in the ovary.","Correct — Leydig cells in the interstitial tissue.","Sertoli cells line the tubules and make MIF (AMH), not testosterone.","Granulosa cells surround the oocyte in the ovary.","Spermatogonia are germ cells at the tubule base, not hormone-producing cells."]},"fe11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's wording: Sertoli cells (on the tubule wall) maintain the blood–testis barrier and create MIF (anti-Müllerian hormone).</p><p>MIF/AMH causes regression of the Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts in males, preventing uterus and fallopian tube formation. Sertoli cells also nurse developing sperm, secrete androgen-binding protein (keeps testosterone high in tubules) and inhibin (inhibits FSH), and respond to FSH. Their tight junctions form the blood–testis barrier, isolating haploid germ cells from the immune system (textbooks say they form it; the professor says \"maintain, not create\" — use his wording on his exam).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Make testosterone and secrete LH into blood</b> — Leydig cells make testosterone, and LH comes from the anterior pituitary.</li><li><b>Produce estrogen and progesterone in puberty</b> — Estrogen and progesterone are ovarian hormones.</li><li><b>Form the acrosome and the midpiece</b> — The acrosome comes from the Golgi and the midpiece from mitochondria.</li><li><b>Form the rete testis and the epididymis</b> — The rete testis and epididymis are ducts, not Sertoli cell products.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> No AMH → persistent Müllerian duct syndrome (male with uterus).</p>","w":["Leydig cells make testosterone, and LH comes from the anterior pituitary.","Estrogen and progesterone are ovarian hormones.","The acrosome comes from the Golgi and the midpiece from mitochondria.","Correct — maintain the blood–testis barrier and create MIF.","The rete testis and epididymis are ducts, not Sertoli cell products."]},"fe12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sperm pathway: seminiferous tubules → rete testis → (efferent ductules) → epididymis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → (prostatic) urethra.</p><p>Mnemonic \"SEVEN UP\": Seminiferous tubules, Epididymis, Vas deferens, Ejaculatory duct, (Nothing), Urethra, Penis — with the rete testis between tubules and epididymis. Seminal vesicles contribute ~60–70% of semen (fructose for energy), prostate ~20–30% (alkaline fluid, PSA), and bulbourethral glands add pre-ejaculate mucus. The ejaculatory duct forms where the vas deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct, opening into the prostatic urethra.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epididymis → tubules → rete testis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra</b> — Sperm are made in the tubules, not the epididymis.</li><li><b>Tubules → epididymis → rete testis → vas deferens → ejaculatory duct → urethra</b> — The rete testis comes before the epididymis.</li><li><b>Tubules → vas deferens → rete testis → epididymis → ejaculatory duct → urethra</b> — The epididymis comes before the vas deferens.</li><li><b>Rete testis → tubules → vas deferens → epididymis → ejaculatory duct → urethra</b> — Sperm start in the seminiferous tubules, not the rete testis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Seminal vesicles provide most semen volume (fructose).</p>","w":["Correct — this is the full pathway.","Sperm are made in the tubules, not the epididymis.","The rete testis comes before the epididymis.","The epididymis comes before the vas deferens.","Sperm start in the seminiferous tubules, not the rete testis."]},"fe13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The tail of the epididymis continues as the vas (ductus) deferens.</p><p>The vas deferens travels in the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal, crosses over the ureter (\"water under the bridge\"), dilates into the ampulla behind the bladder, and joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct inside the prostate. Vasectomy cuts the vas deferens. Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens is associated with cystic fibrosis (CFTR mutations).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Seminal vesicle</b> — The seminal vesicle adds fluid; it isn't the continuation of the epididymis.</li><li><b>Rete testis</b> — The rete testis comes before the epididymis.</li><li><b>Ejaculatory duct</b> — The ejaculatory duct comes after the vas deferens.</li><li><b>Prostatic urethra</b> — The prostatic urethra is further along the pathway.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CF → congenital bilateral absence of vas deferens → male infertility.</p>","w":["The seminal vesicle adds fluid; it isn't the continuation of the epididymis.","The rete testis comes before the epididymis.","The ejaculatory duct comes after the vas deferens.","The prostatic urethra is further along the pathway.","Correct — the epididymis continues as the vas deferens."]},"fe14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Spermatogenesis takes ~64 days; the cell first becomes haploid as a secondary spermatocyte (after meiosis I).</p><p>Sequence: spermatogonia (2n, mitotic stem cells at the tubule base) → primary spermatocytes (2n, 4c; longest stage in prophase I) → meiosis I → secondary spermatocytes (1n, 2c; short-lived) → meiosis II → spermatids (1n, 1c) → spermiogenesis → spermatozoa. Sperm then spend ~2 weeks maturing in the epididymis. Unlike oogenesis, it begins at puberty and continues throughout life, producing millions of sperm daily.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>9 months; sperm</b> — Spermatogenesis takes about 64 days, not 9 months, and the cell is haploid before the sperm stage.</li><li><b>14 days; primary spermatocyte</b> — Primary spermatocytes are diploid, and the process takes longer than 14 days.</li><li><b>64 days; spermatid</b> — The cell is already haploid before the spermatid stage.</li><li><b>28 days; spermatogonium</b> — The process takes 64 days, and spermatogonia are diploid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Primary spermatocyte = 2n4c; secondary = 1n2c; spermatid = 1n1c.</p>","w":["Spermatogenesis takes about 64 days, not 9 months, and the cell is haploid before the sperm stage.","Primary spermatocytes are diploid, and the process takes longer than 14 days.","Correct — 64 days; haploid as a secondary spermatocyte.","The cell is already haploid before the spermatid stage.","The process takes 64 days, and spermatogonia are diploid."]},"fe15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Spermiogenesis: Golgi apparatus → acrosomal cap; mitochondria → midpiece sheath (energy for the tail); centriole → flagellum (axoneme).</p><p>Spermiogenesis transforms round spermatids into sperm without division: nucleus condenses (protamines replace histones), acrosome forms from Golgi vesicles over the anterior nucleus, centrioles form the flagellum, mitochondria spiral around the midpiece, and excess cytoplasm is shed as residual bodies phagocytosed by Sertoli cells. The acrosome contains enzymes (hyaluronidase, acrosin) released during the acrosome reaction to penetrate the zona pellucida.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both from the endoplasmic reticulum</b> — The endoplasmic reticulum isn't the source of either structure.</li><li><b>Acrosome from mitochondria; midpiece from Golgi</b> — This reverses the sources.</li><li><b>Acrosome from lysosomes; midpiece from ribosomes</b> — Lysosomes and ribosomes aren't the professor's answer.</li><li><b>Acrosome from the nucleus; midpiece from centrioles</b> — The nucleus is enclosed by the head and doesn't form the acrosome; centrioles form the tail, not the midpiece mitochondrial bundle.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Acrosome (Golgi) enzymes: hyaluronidase, acrosin.</p>","w":["The endoplasmic reticulum isn't the source of either structure.","Correct — acrosome from Golgi; midpiece from mitochondria.","This reverses the sources.","Lysosomes and ribosomes aren't the professor's answer.","The nucleus is enclosed by the head and doesn't form the acrosome; centrioles form the tail, not the midpiece mitochondrial bundle."]},"fe16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Capacitation (final activation enabling fertilization) occurs in the female genital tract; maturation occurs in the epididymis.</p><p>During capacitation (~7 hours, mainly in the uterus and tube), glycoprotein coat and seminal plasma proteins are removed from the sperm membrane, cholesterol is lost, and sperm become hyperactivated and able to undergo the acrosome reaction on contact with the zona pellucida. Only capacitated sperm can fertilize — relevant to IVF, where sperm are capacitated in culture medium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Prostate gland</b> — The prostate adds fluid; capacitation doesn't occur there.</li><li><b>Seminiferous tubules</b> — Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules, not capacitated there.</li><li><b>Epididymis</b> — The epididymis is where sperm mature, not where they're capacitated.</li><li><b>Vas deferens</b> — The vas deferens transports sperm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Maturation = epididymis (male); capacitation = female tract.</p>","w":["The prostate adds fluid; capacitation doesn't occur there.","Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules, not capacitated there.","The epididymis is where sperm mature, not where they're capacitated.","Correct — the female genital tract.","The vas deferens transports sperm."]},"fe17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Sperm maturation (gaining motility and fertilizing potential) occurs in the epididymis.</p><p>Sperm leaving the testis are immotile and can't fertilize. During ~2 weeks in the epididymis, they acquire forward motility, membrane changes and coating proteins. The tail of the epididymis stores mature sperm. Capacitation later happens in the female tract.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Rete testis</b> — The rete testis is a collecting network.</li><li><b>Ejaculatory duct</b> — The ejaculatory duct is only a passageway.</li><li><b>Female genital tract</b> — The female genital tract is where capacitation happens.</li><li><b>Ampulla of the tube</b> — The ampulla of the uterine tube is where fertilization happens.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epididymis: maturation + storage.</p>","w":["Correct — epididymis.","The rete testis is a collecting network.","The ejaculatory duct is only a passageway.","The female genital tract is where capacitation happens.","The ampulla of the uterine tube is where fertilization happens."]},"fe18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's starred fact: normal minimum sperm count ≈ 20 million/mL; below this = oligospermia.</p><p>Semen analysis parameters (classically): volume 2–5 mL, concentration ≥20 million/mL (current WHO lower reference ~15 million/mL), motility and normal morphology also assessed. Semen is alkaline to neutralize vaginal acidity. Use the professor's 20 million/mL figure on his exam.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>About 64 million per mL</b> — 64 is the number of days of spermatogenesis.</li><li><b>About 480 per mL</b> — 480 is the approximate lifetime number of ovulated oocytes.</li><li><b>About 200 million per mL</b> — 200 million isn't the professor's number.</li><li><b>About 2 million per mL</b> — 2 million per mL is far too low.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oligospermia: &lt;20 million/mL (professor).</p>","w":["64 is the number of days of spermatogenesis.","480 is the approximate lifetime number of ovulated oocytes.","200 million isn't the professor's number.","2 million per mL is far too low.","Correct — about 20 million per mL."]},"fe19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Oligospermia = sperm count below ~20 million/mL (\"oligo\" = few).</p><p>Here the count is 8 million/mL. Causes include varicocele, hormonal problems (hypogonadism, hyperprolactinemia), cryptorchidism, infections (mumps orchitis), drugs (anabolic steroids, chemotherapy), heat, smoking and genetic conditions (Klinefelter, Y chromosome microdeletions). Related terms: asthenospermia (poor motility), teratospermia (abnormal morphology).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Polyspermy</b> — Polyspermy is entry of more than one sperm into an oocyte.</li><li><b>Aspermia</b> — Aspermia means no semen at all.</li><li><b>Azoospermia</b> — Azoospermia means no sperm in the semen.</li><li><b>Normal semen</b> — Below 20 million/mL is abnormal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anabolic steroids → ↓LH/FSH → testicular atrophy, oligospermia.</p>","w":["Polyspermy is entry of more than one sperm into an oocyte.","Aspermia means no semen at all.","Correct — oligospermia.","Azoospermia means no sperm in the semen.","Below 20 million/mL is abnormal."]},"fe20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Azoospermia = semen present but no sperm; aspermia = no semen at all.</p><p>Azoospermia may be obstructive (blocked ducts — congenital absence of vas deferens in CF, vasectomy, infection) or non-obstructive (testicular failure — Klinefelter syndrome, Y microdeletions, chemotherapy). Measuring FSH and testosterone and testicular size helps distinguish them: high FSH with small testes suggests testicular failure.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Oligospermia</b> — Oligospermia means a low count, not zero.</li><li><b>Normospermia</b> — Normospermia means normal semen, which this isn't.</li><li><b>Asthenospermia</b> — Asthenospermia refers to poor sperm motility.</li><li><b>Aspermia</b> — Aspermia means no semen at all.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Obstructive (normal FSH) vs non-obstructive (↑FSH) azoospermia.</p>","w":["Oligospermia means a low count, not zero.","Correct — azoospermia.","Normospermia means normal semen, which this isn't.","Asthenospermia refers to poor sperm motility.","Aspermia means no semen at all."]},"fe21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Normal semen (professor): volume 2–5 mL, count ≥ ~20 million/mL, alkaline pH.</p><p>The alkaline pH (~7.2–8) comes mainly from prostatic and seminal vesicle secretions and neutralizes the acidic vagina (pH ~4), which would otherwise immobilize sperm. Seminal vesicles also supply fructose (energy) and prostaglandins; the prostate adds PSA, which liquefies the coagulated semen.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Semen is acidic to match the vaginal pH</b> — Semen is alkaline to neutralize the acidic vagina, not acidic.</li><li><b>Sperm count must exceed 200 million/mL</b> — The cutoff is about 20 million/mL, not 200 million.</li><li><b>Volume 10–20 mL and acidic pH</b> — 10–20 mL is far too large, and the pH isn't acidic.</li><li><b>Volume 0.5 mL and neutral pH</b> — 0.5 mL is too small (hypospermia).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Seminal vesicle = fructose; prostate = PSA, alkaline fluid.</p>","w":["Semen is alkaline to neutralize the acidic vagina, not acidic.","The cutoff is about 20 million/mL, not 200 million.","10–20 mL is far too large, and the pH isn't acidic.","Correct — 2–5 mL and alkaline.","0.5 mL is too small (hypospermia)."]},"fe22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The ovarian cortex contains follicles at all stages simultaneously (primordial through Graafian, plus corpora lutea/albicans); the medulla holds vessels and nerves.</p><p>At any time, many primordial follicles rest while a cohort grows each cycle. Most growing follicles undergo atresia; normally one becomes dominant and ovulates. Biopsy of a reproductive-age ovary therefore shows mixed stages. After menopause, follicles are depleted and the cortex becomes fibrous.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Only primordial follicles</b> — All follicle stages are present together, not only primordial follicles.</li><li><b>Seminiferous tubules</b> — Seminiferous tubules are in the testis.</li><li><b>Only Graafian follicles</b> — Follicles at all stages are seen, not only Graafian follicles.</li><li><b>No follicles; they are in the medulla</b> — Follicles are in the cortex, not the medulla.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cortex = follicles; medulla = vessels.</p>","w":["Correct — follicles at all developing stages.","All follicle stages are present together, not only primordial follicles.","Seminiferous tubules are in the testis.","Follicles at all stages are seen, not only Graafian follicles.","Follicles are in the cortex, not the medulla."]},"fe23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The zona pellucida is the glycoprotein coat around the oocyte (ZP3 binds sperm); it hardens after fertilization to block polyspermy.</p><p>Sperm first pass through the corona radiata (granulosa cells, using hyaluronidase), then bind ZP3, triggering the acrosome reaction that digests a path through the zona. Fusion triggers the cortical reaction: cortical granules release enzymes that alter the zona (zona reaction), blocking additional sperm. The embryo stays within the zona until the blastocyst hatches (~day 5) — premature hatching can cause ectopic implantation in the tube.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Theca externa</b> — The theca externa lies outside the follicle.</li><li><b>Decidua basalis</b> — The decidua basalis is part of the pregnant uterine lining.</li><li><b>Corona radiata</b> — The corona radiata is a layer of granulosa cells, not the glycoprotein coat.</li><li><b>Chorion laeve</b> — The chorion laeve is the smooth chorion of the fetal membranes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cortical (zona) reaction = slow block to polyspermy.</p>","w":["The theca externa lies outside the follicle.","The decidua basalis is part of the pregnant uterine lining.","The corona radiata is a layer of granulosa cells, not the glycoprotein coat.","The chorion laeve is the smooth chorion of the fetal membranes.","Correct — zona pellucida."]},"fe24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Follicle = an oocyte surrounded by granulosa (follicular) cells.</p><p>In the female gonad, sex cords break down and surround primordial germ cells with flattened follicular cells, forming primordial follicles (professor's version). As follicles grow, granulosa cells proliferate and produce estrogen (from theca-derived androgens), and theca cells form from surrounding stroma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The corpus luteum</b> — The corpus luteum forms from the follicle after ovulation.</li><li><b>Granulosa cells alone</b> — Granulosa cells without an oocyte are just granulosa cells.</li><li><b>Theca cells + a spermatid</b> — Spermatids are male germ cells.</li><li><b>A cavity without cells</b> — A follicle consists of cells, not an empty cavity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Follicle = oocyte + granulosa cells.</p>","w":["The corpus luteum forms from the follicle after ovulation.","Granulosa cells without an oocyte are just granulosa cells.","Correct — granulosa cells + an oocyte.","Spermatids are male germ cells.","A follicle consists of cells, not an empty cavity."]},"fe25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Primary follicle: single layer of cuboidal granulosa cells around the oocyte, no antrum.</p><p>Follicle stages: primordial (single flat layer) → primary (single cuboidal layer; zona pellucida appears) → secondary (multiple layers, small antrum begins — professor's \"Small, Second = 2\") → Graafian/tertiary (large antrum, cumulus oophorus, about to ovulate — \"Giant\").</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Corpus luteum</b> — The corpus luteum forms after ovulation.</li><li><b>Primordial</b> — Primordial follicle cells are flat, not cuboidal.</li><li><b>Secondary</b> — Secondary follicles have two or more layers and a small cavity.</li><li><b>Graafian</b> — Graafian follicles have a large cavity and many layers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Flat → cuboidal → multilayer + small antrum → large antrum.</p>","w":["The corpus luteum forms after ovulation.","Correct — primary follicle.","Primordial follicle cells are flat, not cuboidal.","Secondary follicles have two or more layers and a small cavity.","Graafian follicles have a large cavity and many layers."]},"fe26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary follicle: two or more layers of granulosa cells with a small cavity (antrum) — \"Secondary = Small cavity, Second = 2 layers.\"</p><p>As granulosa cells proliferate, fluid-filled spaces appear and coalesce into the antrum. Theca interna and externa become distinct around the follicle. FSH drives this growth; the follicle increasingly produces estrogen.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Primary</b> — A primary follicle has a single cuboidal layer.</li><li><b>Graafian</b> — A Graafian follicle has a large cavity.</li><li><b>Primordial</b> — A primordial follicle has a single flat layer.</li><li><b>Corpus albicans</b> — The corpus albicans is a white fibrous scar.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Antrum first appears in the secondary follicle.</p>","w":["A primary follicle has a single cuboidal layer.","A Graafian follicle has a large cavity.","A primordial follicle has a single flat layer.","Correct — secondary follicle.","The corpus albicans is a white fibrous scar."]},"fe27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Graafian (tertiary/mature) follicle: large antrum, many layers, oocyte on the cumulus oophorus — ready to ovulate (\"Graafian = Giant\").</p><p>Usually one dominant follicle per cycle reaches this stage (~2 cm) by about day 13–14. The LH surge triggers completion of meiosis I (secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II) and rupture. The oocyte leaves surrounded by the corona radiata.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Atretic</b> — Atretic follicles degenerate and don't ovulate.</li><li><b>Primordial</b> — A primordial follicle has a flat single layer.</li><li><b>Secondary</b> — A secondary follicle has only a small cavity.</li><li><b>Primary</b> — A primary follicle has a cuboidal single layer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> LH surge → ovulation of Graafian follicle.</p>","w":["Correct — Graafian follicle.","Atretic follicles degenerate and don't ovulate.","A primordial follicle has a flat single layer.","A secondary follicle has only a small cavity.","A primary follicle has a cuboidal single layer."]},"fe28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's answer: theca interna makes estrogen. (Textbook two-cell model: theca interna makes androgens under LH; granulosa cells aromatize them to estrogen under FSH.)</p><p>Theca interna is the inner, vascular, hormone-producing layer; theca externa is outer, fibrous connective tissue. In the textbook two-cell, two-gonadotropin model: LH → theca cells → androstenedione; FSH → granulosa aromatase → estradiol. For this professor's exam, choose theca interna.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Zona pellucida</b> — The zona pellucida is a glycoprotein coat, not hormone-producing.</li><li><b>Granulosa layer</b> — In the textbook version granulosa cells convert androgens to estrogen, but the professor's answer is theca interna.</li><li><b>Corpus albicans</b> — The corpus albicans is a fibrous scar.</li><li><b>Theca externa</b> — Theca externa is the outer, thinner fibrous layer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Two-cell model: theca (LH, androgens) + granulosa (FSH, aromatase).</p>","w":["The zona pellucida is a glycoprotein coat, not hormone-producing.","In the textbook version granulosa cells convert androgens to estrogen, but the professor's answer is theca interna.","The corpus albicans is a fibrous scar.","Theca externa is the outer, thinner fibrous layer.","Correct — theca interna (professor's answer)."]},"fe29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Oogenesis begins in fetal life (≈3rd–7th month); spermatogenesis begins at puberty.</p><p>Oogonia multiply by mitosis to a peak of ~7 million around month 5, then many degenerate. Those remaining enter meiosis I as primary oocytes and arrest in prophase I (diplotene) before birth. About 1–2 million are present at birth and ~40,000 by puberty (professor's figure). No new oocytes form after birth, so oocyte number steadily declines until menopause.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>At birth</b> — Oogenesis is already underway before birth.</li><li><b>At menarche, with the first LH surge</b> — Menarche/LH surges only resume meiosis in some follicles; they don't begin oogenesis.</li><li><b>At puberty</b> — Spermatogenesis, not oogenesis, starts at puberty.</li><li><b>At fertilization</b> — Fertilization completes meiosis II; it doesn't begin oogenesis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oogonia peak ~7 million at 5 months fetal life.</p>","w":["Oogenesis is already underway before birth.","Menarche/LH surges only resume meiosis in some follicles; they don't begin oogenesis.","Correct — fetal life, 3rd–7th month.","Spermatogenesis, not oogenesis, starts at puberty.","Fertilization completes meiosis II; it doesn't begin oogenesis."]},"fe30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> At birth all oocytes are primary oocytes (diploid) arrested in prophase I (\"Primary = Prophase\").</p><p>Arrest is maintained by follicle signals (high cAMP in the oocyte). The first arrest lasts from fetal life until each oocyte's ovulation — up to ~50 years — which contributes to age-related nondisjunction. At each cycle after puberty, the selected oocyte completes meiosis I just before ovulation and arrests again in metaphase II.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Anaphase I</b> — Anaphase I isn't the arrest point.</li><li><b>Metaphase II, as secondary oocytes</b> — Metaphase II arrest occurs after puberty, in the secondary oocyte at ovulation.</li><li><b>Telophase II</b> — Telophase II isn't an arrest point.</li><li><b>They have not begun meiosis</b> — Oocytes have already begun meiosis I by birth.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Two arrests: prophase I (until ovulation), metaphase II (until fertilization).</p>","w":["Anaphase I isn't the arrest point.","Correct — prophase I, diploid primary oocytes.","Metaphase II arrest occurs after puberty, in the secondary oocyte at ovulation.","Telophase II isn't an arrest point.","Oocytes have already begun meiosis I by birth."]},"fe31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor: an FSH surge at puberty wakes some dormant follicles so the primary oocyte completes meiosis I → secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II.</p><p>FSH stimulates follicle growth and granulosa proliferation each cycle. (Textbooks emphasize that the LH surge triggers completion of meiosis I immediately before ovulation, with the first polar body extruded.) For this professor's question, the answer is the FSH surge. The secondary oocyte (1n, 2c) then arrests at metaphase II.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>An LH surge</b> — In the professor's framework, the LH surge triggers ovulation rather than waking follicles.</li><li><b>Progesterone from the corpus luteum</b> — Progesterone is secreted after ovulation by the corpus luteum.</li><li><b>MIF from Sertoli cells</b> — MIF is secreted by Sertoli cells in males.</li><li><b>hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast</b> — hCG is produced by the syncytiotrophoblast in pregnancy.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Textbook: LH surge → meiosis I completed + ovulation.</p>","w":["In the professor's framework, the LH surge triggers ovulation rather than waking follicles.","Progesterone is secreted after ovulation by the corpus luteum.","MIF is secreted by Sertoli cells in males.","Correct — an FSH surge (professor's answer).","hCG is produced by the syncytiotrophoblast in pregnancy."]},"fe32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The secondary oocyte remains arrested in metaphase II and completes meiosis II only if fertilized.</p><p>Sperm entry triggers Ca²⁺ oscillations in the oocyte, which release the metaphase II arrest. The oocyte then completes meiosis II, extrudes the second polar body, and forms the female pronucleus. If not fertilized within ~24 hours of ovulation, the oocyte degenerates.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Never; it stays arrested in prophase I</b> — After puberty, the ovulated oocyte is arrested in metaphase II, not prophase I.</li><li><b>At the next menstruation</b> — An unfertilized oocyte is discarded still arrested.</li><li><b>At puberty</b> — Puberty resumes meiosis I, not meiosis II.</li><li><b>Immediately at ovulation</b> — It stays arrested in metaphase II after ovulation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fertilization → completion of meiosis II → second polar body.</p>","w":["Correct — only if it is fertilized.","After puberty, the ovulated oocyte is arrested in metaphase II, not prophase I.","An unfertilized oocyte is discarded still arrested.","Puberty resumes meiosis I, not meiosis II.","It stays arrested in metaphase II after ovulation."]},"fe33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor: ~480 secondary oocytes ovulated per lifetime (12/year × ~40 years).</p><p>Of ~40,000 primary oocytes present at puberty (professor's figure), only ~480 are ovulated; the rest undergo atresia. Each cycle recruits a cohort, but usually one becomes dominant. Menopause occurs when follicles are nearly exhausted (~age 51), raising FSH because inhibin and estrogen fall.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>40,000</b> — 40,000 is the number of primary oocytes at puberty.</li><li><b>400,000</b> — 400,000 is too many.</li><li><b>40</b> — 40 is too few.</li><li><b>4,000</b> — 4,000 isn't the professor's figure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Menopause: ↑FSH (lost inhibin/estrogen feedback).</p>","w":["40,000 is the number of primary oocytes at puberty.","400,000 is too many.","40 is too few.","4,000 isn't the professor's figure.","Correct — about 480."]},"fe34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After ovulation, remaining granulosa (and theca) cells form the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone (and estrogen).</p><p>LH causes luteinization — cells accumulate lipid and lutein pigment (yellow). Progesterone converts the endometrium to the secretory phase, preparing for implantation, and raises basal body temperature. Without pregnancy, LH support wanes and it degenerates into the corpus albicans; with pregnancy, hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast maintains it.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Leydig cells; testosterone</b> — Leydig cells and testosterone belong to the testis.</li><li><b>Oogonia; FSH</b> — Oogonia are germ cells, and FSH comes from the pituitary.</li><li><b>Theca externa cells; estrogen</b> — Theca externa and estrogen aren't the professor's answer for the corpus luteum.</li><li><b>Syncytiotrophoblast; hCG</b> — The syncytiotrophoblast is embryonic and secretes hCG.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Corpus luteum → progesterone; hCG rescues it in pregnancy.</p>","w":["Leydig cells and testosterone belong to the testis.","Oogonia are germ cells, and FSH comes from the pituitary.","Correct — granulosa cells; progesterone.","Theca externa and estrogen aren't the professor's answer for the corpus luteum.","The syncytiotrophoblast is embryonic and secretes hCG."]},"fe35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Without pregnancy, the corpus luteum lasts ~14 days; with pregnancy, hCG maintains it for ~2–3 months until the placenta takes over progesterone production.</p><p>The luteal phase is fairly constant (~14 days), so variation in cycle length mostly reflects the follicular phase. The luteal–placental shift occurs around weeks 8–10. Removing the corpus luteum before then can cause miscarriage unless progesterone is supplemented.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>2–3 months; 14 days</b> — This reverses the two durations.</li><li><b>28 days; 9 months</b> — 28 days and 9 months aren't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>3 days; 1 month</b> — 3 days and 1 month aren't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>7 days; 14 days</b> — 7 and 14 days are too short.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Luteal phase ≈ 14 days; luteal–placental shift ~8–10 weeks.</p>","w":["This reverses the two durations.","Correct — 14 days; 2–3 months.","28 days and 9 months aren't the professor's numbers.","3 days and 1 month aren't the professor's numbers.","7 and 14 days are too short."]},"fe36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> FSH and LH are released by the anterior pituitary (gonadotropes) in response to hypothalamic GnRH.</p><p>Hypothalamus → pulsatile GnRH → anterior pituitary → FSH (follicle growth, granulosa aromatase) and LH (theca androgens, ovulation, corpus luteum). Estrogen normally gives negative feedback, but sustained high estrogen near mid-cycle gives positive feedback, triggering the LH surge. Continuous (non-pulsatile) GnRH agonist therapy suppresses FSH/LH — used in prostate cancer and endometriosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypothalamus</b> — The hypothalamus releases GnRH, not FSH and LH.</li><li><b>Ovary</b> — The ovary makes estrogen and progesterone.</li><li><b>Adrenal cortex</b> — The adrenal cortex makes corticosteroids and adrenal androgens.</li><li><b>Posterior pituitary</b> — The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and ADH.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pulsatile GnRH stimulates; continuous GnRH suppresses.</p>","w":["The hypothalamus releases GnRH, not FSH and LH.","The ovary makes estrogen and progesterone.","The adrenal cortex makes corticosteroids and adrenal androgens.","Correct — the anterior pituitary.","The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin and ADH."]},"fe37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Estrogen builds the endometrium (proliferative phase); progesterone maintains it and makes it secretory (luteal phase).</p><p>Estrogen stimulates proliferation of glands and stroma. After ovulation, progesterone stops proliferation, causes coiled glands to secrete glycogen-rich fluid, makes spiral arteries more coiled and prepares for implantation. Falling progesterone (corpus luteum regression) causes spiral artery constriction, ischemia and shedding — menstruation. Unopposed estrogen (anovulation, obesity) causes endometrial hyperplasia and cancer risk.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>hCG thickens; estrogen maintains</b> — hCG maintains the corpus luteum, not the endometrium directly, and estrogen builds rather than maintains the lining.</li><li><b>Progesterone thickens; estrogen maintains</b> — This reverses the two hormones' roles.</li><li><b>FSH thickens; LH maintains</b> — FSH and LH act on the ovary, not directly on the endometrium.</li><li><b>LH thickens; FSH maintains</b> — LH and FSH don't directly thicken or maintain the endometrium.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Unopposed estrogen → endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma.</p>","w":["Correct — estrogen thickens; progesterone maintains.","hCG maintains the corpus luteum, not the endometrium directly, and estrogen builds rather than maintains the lining.","This reverses the two hormones' roles.","FSH and LH act on the ovary, not directly on the endometrium.","LH and FSH don't directly thicken or maintain the endometrium."]},"fe38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ovulation occurs on day 14 of a 28-day cycle, triggered by the LH surge (exam favorite).</p><p>Rising estrogen from the dominant follicle switches to positive feedback, causing the LH surge; ovulation follows ~24–36 hours later. Signs: mittelschmerz (mid-cycle pain), clear stretchy cervical mucus, rise in basal body temperature after ovulation (progesterone). Ovulation predictor kits detect urinary LH.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Day 1; FSH</b> — Day 1 is the start of menstruation; FSH doesn't trigger ovulation.</li><li><b>Day 28; hCG</b> — Day 28 is the end of the cycle; hCG is a pregnancy hormone.</li><li><b>Day 7; estrogen</b> — Day 7 is mid-follicular phase.</li><li><b>Day 21; progesterone</b> — Day 21 is around implantation time, and progesterone doesn't trigger ovulation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ovulation = day 14 (luteal phase constant at 14 days).</p>","w":["Day 1 is the start of menstruation; FSH doesn't trigger ovulation.","Day 28 is the end of the cycle; hCG is a pregnancy hormone.","Day 7 is mid-follicular phase.","Day 21 is around implantation time, and progesterone doesn't trigger ovulation.","Correct — day 14, LH."]},"fe39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Days 1–14 = follicular (ovary) = estrogenic (hormone) = proliferative (endometrium).</p><p>Match each name to its organ: the ovary's follicles grow (follicular), they secrete estrogen (estrogenic), and the endometrium regenerates and thickens (proliferative). Days 1–5 are menstruation within this half. Days 14–28 = luteal = progestational = secretory.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Menstrual, ischemic, secretory</b> — \"Menstrual, ischemic, secretory\" isn't the professor's naming.</li><li><b>Ovulatory, LH, secretory</b> — Ovulation is a single day, not a phase name for days 1–14.</li><li><b>Follicular, progestational, secretory</b> — These names mix the two halves of the cycle.</li><li><b>Luteal, progestational, secretory</b> — Luteal, progestational and secretory describe days 14–28.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ovary/hormone/endometrium naming for each half.</p>","w":["\"Menstrual, ischemic, secretory\" isn't the professor's naming.","Ovulation is a single day, not a phase name for days 1–14.","Correct — follicular, estrogenic, proliferative.","These names mix the two halves of the cycle.","Luteal, progestational and secretory describe days 14–28."]},"fe40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Days 14–28: the corpus luteum secretes progesterone (progestational), which makes endometrial glands secrete nutrients (secretory) to nourish an implanting embryo.</p><p>Under progesterone, glands become coiled and secrete glycogen and mucus (\"uterine milk\"), and the stroma becomes edematous and decidualizes, creating a receptive window for implantation around days 20–24. If implantation occurs, hCG preserves the corpus luteum; if not, progesterone falls and the lining sheds.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>hCG causes the endometrial glands to shed</b> — hCG maintains the corpus luteum (and so the lining); it doesn't cause shedding.</li><li><b>Estrogen makes the endometrial glands grow longer</b> — Estrogen-driven gland growth occurs in the proliferative phase.</li><li><b>LH causes the endometrial glands to secrete milk</b> — LH doesn't make endometrial glands secrete milk.</li><li><b>The corpus albicans secretes mucus</b> — The corpus albicans is a scar and doesn't secrete mucus.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Implantation window ≈ days 20–24.</p>","w":["hCG maintains the corpus luteum (and so the lining); it doesn't cause shedding.","Correct — progesterone makes the glands secrete nutrients.","Estrogen-driven gland growth occurs in the proliferative phase.","LH doesn't make endometrial glands secrete milk.","The corpus albicans is a scar and doesn't secrete mucus."]},"fe41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Without fertilization, the corpus luteum regresses around day 28 (luteolysis), progesterone and estrogen fall, menstruation begins, and the remnant becomes the corpus albicans.</p><p>Falling progesterone causes spiral arteries to constrict (prostaglandin-mediated), making the functional layer of the endometrium ischemic so it sheds; the basal layer remains to regenerate. The drop in estrogen and progesterone also lifts negative feedback, allowing FSH to rise and recruit the next cohort of follicles.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It migrates through the tube to the uterus</b> — The corpus luteum stays in the ovary; it doesn't migrate.</li><li><b>It turns back into a mature Graafian follicle</b> — It can't revert to a follicle.</li><li><b>It secretes hCG</b> — hCG is made by the syncytiotrophoblast, not the corpus luteum.</li><li><b>It becomes the corpus luteum of pregnancy</b> — The corpus luteum of pregnancy forms only when hCG is present.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Progesterone withdrawal → menstruation.</p>","w":["The corpus luteum stays in the ovary; it doesn't migrate.","It can't revert to a follicle.","hCG is made by the syncytiotrophoblast, not the corpus luteum.","Correct — it stops secreting and becomes the corpus albicans.","The corpus luteum of pregnancy forms only when hCG is present."]},"fe42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Corpus albicans = white fibrous scar from a degenerated corpus luteum (\"albicans\" = white).</p><p>The luteal cells are replaced by dense collagen over weeks to months. Scars accumulate in the ovarian cortex with age. A corpus luteum cyst can form if it fails to regress, occasionally causing pain or rupture with hemorrhage.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Corpus luteum of pregnancy</b> — The corpus luteum of pregnancy is active, not scar tissue.</li><li><b>Graafian follicle</b> — A Graafian follicle is a mature follicle before ovulation.</li><li><b>Decidua basalis</b> — The decidua basalis is the uterine lining at the implantation site.</li><li><b>Theca externa</b> — The theca externa is a follicle layer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Corpus luteum (yellow) → corpus albicans (white).</p>","w":["Correct — corpus albicans.","The corpus luteum of pregnancy is active, not scar tissue.","A Graafian follicle is a mature follicle before ovulation.","The decidua basalis is the uterine lining at the implantation site.","The theca externa is a follicle layer."]},"fe43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> hCG from the syncytiotrophoblast rescues the corpus luteum (acting like LH), maintaining progesterone so the endometrium isn't shed.</p><p>hCG shares an α subunit with LH, FSH and TSH; its β subunit is unique (measured in tests). It binds LH receptors on the corpus luteum, keeping it alive as the corpus luteum of pregnancy until the placenta makes enough progesterone (~weeks 8–12). Very high hCG (molar pregnancy, choriocarcinoma, twins) can cause hyperemesis and even hyperthyroidism via TSH-receptor cross-reactivity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>FSH from the anterior pituitary</b> — FSH grows follicles; it doesn't maintain the corpus luteum.</li><li><b>Prolactin</b> — Prolactin isn't the rescue signal.</li><li><b>LH surge on day 14</b> — The LH surge triggers ovulation earlier in the cycle.</li><li><b>Estrogen from theca cells</b> — Estrogen doesn't keep the corpus luteum alive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> hCG ≈ LH; molar pregnancy → very high hCG.</p>","w":["FSH grows follicles; it doesn't maintain the corpus luteum.","Prolactin isn't the rescue signal.","The LH surge triggers ovulation earlier in the cycle.","Estrogen doesn't keep the corpus luteum alive.","Correct — hCG from the implanting embryo."]},"fe44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pregnancy tests detect hCG (β-subunit), produced by the syncytiotrophoblast.</p><p>hCG appears in maternal blood ~8–10 days after fertilization (shortly after implantation) and in urine a few days later; levels double every ~48 hours in early normal pregnancy, peaking around weeks 8–10. Serum quantitative hCG helps evaluate ectopic pregnancy (suboptimal rise) and gestational trophoblastic disease (very high), and is a tumor marker for choriocarcinoma and some testicular germ cell tumors.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>FSH</b> — FSH isn't the pregnancy marker.</li><li><b>Estrogen</b> — Estrogen isn't measured in a pregnancy test.</li><li><b>Alpha-fetoprotein</b> — Alpha-fetoprotein screens for neural tube defects and some tumors.</li><li><b>Progesterone</b> — Progesterone isn't measured in standard pregnancy tests.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> hCG doubles every ~48 h early on; slow rise → suspect ectopic.</p>","w":["FSH isn't the pregnancy marker.","Estrogen isn't measured in a pregnancy test.","Correct — hCG.","Alpha-fetoprotein screens for neural tube defects and some tumors.","Progesterone isn't measured in standard pregnancy tests."]},"fe45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After about the third month, the placenta (syncytiotrophoblast) takes over progesterone production from the corpus luteum.</p><p>Placental progesterone keeps the myometrium quiet and maintains the decidua throughout pregnancy. The placenta also makes estrogens (estriol, using fetal adrenal DHEA-S — a marker of fetal well-being), hCG and human placental lactogen. The corpus luteum regresses after the luteal–placental shift.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Corpus albicans</b> — The corpus albicans is inactive scar tissue.</li><li><b>Corpus luteum</b> — The corpus luteum supports only roughly the first 3 months.</li><li><b>Theca interna</b> — Theca interna makes estrogen in the ovary, not pregnancy progesterone.</li><li><b>Anterior pituitary</b> — The anterior pituitary doesn't produce progesterone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Estriol requires fetal adrenal + liver + placenta (fetoplacental unit).</p>","w":["The corpus albicans is inactive scar tissue.","Correct — the placenta.","The corpus luteum supports only roughly the first 3 months.","Theca interna makes estrogen in the ovary, not pregnancy progesterone.","The anterior pituitary doesn't produce progesterone."]},"fe46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's timeline: LMP May 1 → ovulation/fertilization ~May 14–15 → implantation ~May 21 → missed period ~May 28.</p><p>The embryo travels down the tube for ~3–4 days, reaches the uterus as a blastocyst ~day 4–5 after fertilization, and implants ~day 6–7. hCG then rescues the corpus luteum, so the expected menses on day 28 doesn't occur. Gestational age is counted from the LMP (so at the missed period, she's \"4 weeks pregnant\" though the embryo is ~2 weeks old).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Implantation June 1; missed period June 28</b> — June 1 and June 28 are too late.</li><li><b>Implantation May 7; missed period May 14</b> — May 7 and May 14 are too early (May 14 is ovulation).</li><li><b>Implantation May 28; missed period June 14</b> — May 28 and June 14 are too late.</li><li><b>Implantation May 14; missed period May 21</b> — May 14 is ovulation, not implantation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gestational age (from LMP) = embryonic age + 2 weeks.</p>","w":["June 1 and June 28 are too late.","May 7 and May 14 are too early (May 14 is ovulation).","May 28 and June 14 are too late.","Correct — implantation ~May 21; missed period May 28.","May 14 is ovulation, not implantation."]},"fe47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Combined oral contraceptives (estrogen + progestin) mainly suppress FSH/LH via negative feedback → no follicle maturation, no LH surge, no ovulation.</p><p>Estrogen suppresses FSH (preventing follicle selection); progestin suppresses LH (preventing the surge). Progestin also thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium, reducing sperm penetration and implantation. Risks: venous thromboembolism (estrogen increases clotting factors), especially in smokers over 35; contraindicated with migraine with aura and estrogen-dependent cancers. Benefits: reduced ovarian and endometrial cancer risk.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It makes the endometrium proliferate</b> — Causing endometrial proliferation isn't its mechanism.</li><li><b>It kills sperm directly in the vaginal canal</b> — It isn't a spermicide.</li><li><b>It blocks hCG</b> — It doesn't block hCG.</li><li><b>It makes the corpus luteum persist for months</b> — Making the corpus luteum persist isn't its mechanism.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> COC: ↓ovarian + endometrial cancer; ↑VTE (smokers &gt;35).</p>","w":["Correct — it suppresses FSH, preventing ovulation.","Causing endometrial proliferation isn't its mechanism.","It isn't a spermicide.","It doesn't block hCG.","Making the corpus luteum persist isn't its mechanism."]},"fe48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor: oocyte viable ~24 hours after ovulation; sperm survive up to ~72 hours in the female tract.</p><p>So the fertile window spans several days before ovulation to about a day after. With ovulation on day 14, intercourse on days 11–13 can lead to pregnancy (sperm waiting in the tube), and the egg remains fertilizable into day 15. (Some sources give sperm survival up to 5 days.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>12 hours; 12 hours</b> — 12 hours for both isn't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>72 hours; 24 hours</b> — This reverses the two durations.</li><li><b>7 days; 2 days</b> — 7 days and 2 days aren't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>14 days; 3 days</b> — 14 days and 3 days aren't the professor's numbers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fertile window ≈ day 11 to day 15.</p>","w":["12 hours for both isn't the professor's numbers.","This reverses the two durations.","7 days and 2 days aren't the professor's numbers.","14 days and 3 days aren't the professor's numbers.","Correct — egg 24 hours; sperm 72 hours."]},"fe49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Uterine wall inner → outer: endometrium (mucosa), myometrium (smooth muscle), perimetrium (serosa).</p><p>The endometrium has a functional layer (shed each cycle, responsive to hormones) and a basal layer (regenerates it). In pregnancy it becomes the decidua. The myometrium contracts during labor (oxytocin) and is the site of leiomyomas (fibroids). Ectopic endometrium in the myometrium = adenomyosis; outside the uterus = endometriosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decidua, chorion, amnion</b> — Decidua, chorion and amnion are pregnancy lining and membranes.</li><li><b>Mucosa, submucosa, serosa</b> — Mucosa, submucosa, serosa aren't the standard uterine terms.</li><li><b>Perimetrium, myometrium, endometrium</b> — This lists the layers from outer to inner.</li><li><b>Stroma, glands, vessels</b> — Stroma, glands and vessels are components of the endometrium.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Adenomyosis = endometrium in myometrium; endometriosis = outside uterus.</p>","w":["Decidua, chorion and amnion are pregnancy lining and membranes.","Mucosa, submucosa, serosa aren't the standard uterine terms.","Correct — endometrium, myometrium, perimetrium.","This lists the layers from outer to inner.","Stroma, glands and vessels are components of the endometrium."]},"fe50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla of the uterine tube.</p><p>Fimbriae sweep the ovulated oocyte into the tube; peristalsis and cilia move it to the ampulla (the widest, longest segment), where capacitated sperm meet it within ~24 hours. The ampulla is also the most common site of ectopic pregnancy. The embryo then moves through the isthmus to the uterus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cervix</b> — The cervix is a passageway, not the fertilization site.</li><li><b>Ovary</b> — The oocyte leaves the ovary at ovulation.</li><li><b>Isthmus of the uterus near the cervix</b> — The uterine isthmus near the cervix isn't the fertilization site.</li><li><b>Uterine cavity</b> — The uterine cavity is where implantation, not fertilization, normally occurs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Ampulla: fertilization and most ectopic pregnancies.</p>","w":["The cervix is a passageway, not the fertilization site.","Correct — the ampulla of the tube.","The oocyte leaves the ovary at ovulation.","The uterine isthmus near the cervix isn't the fertilization site.","The uterine cavity is where implantation, not fertilization, normally occurs."]},"fe51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Once one sperm enters, the zona reaction (from cortical granule release) closes the zona pellucida — the block to polyspermy.</p><p>Fast block: membrane depolarization of the oocyte. Slow block: cortical granules release enzymes that modify ZP2/ZP3, so other sperm can't bind or penetrate. Polyspermy (e.g., two sperm) produces a triploid zygote (69 chromosomes), which usually miscarries or forms a partial hydatidiform mole.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>To complete meiosis I</b> — Meiosis I was completed before ovulation; fertilization completes meiosis II.</li><li><b>To start implantation</b> — Implantation occurs about a week later.</li><li><b>To release hCG</b> — hCG comes later from the syncytiotrophoblast.</li><li><b>To begin capacitation</b> — Capacitation occurs before fertilization in the female tract.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Partial mole = triploid (two sperm + egg).</p>","w":["Meiosis I was completed before ovulation; fertilization completes meiosis II.","Implantation occurs about a week later.","hCG comes later from the syncytiotrophoblast.","Correct — to block polyspermy.","Capacitation occurs before fertilization in the female tract."]},"fe52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> During cleavage, blastomeres stay inside the zona pellucida as the embryo moves down the tube; the blastocyst hatches before implanting.</p><p>Cleavage divisions produce smaller cells without overall growth. The zona prevents the embryo from sticking to the tubal wall (protects against tubal implantation) and keeps blastomeres together. Hatching occurs around day 5 in the uterus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Nothing; they are bare cells</b> — The blastomeres are still enclosed by the zona.</li><li><b>Amniotic membrane</b> — The amnion forms in week 2, after implantation.</li><li><b>Decidua basalis</b> — The decidua is the maternal uterine lining.</li><li><b>Chorion frondosum villi</b> — Chorionic villi form later in development.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Zona prevents premature (tubal) implantation.</p>","w":["Correct — the zona pellucida.","The blastomeres are still enclosed by the zona.","The amnion forms in week 2, after implantation.","The decidua is the maternal uterine lining.","Chorionic villi form later in development."]},"fe53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fertilization: (1) restores diploidy, (2) completes meiosis II, (3) determines sex, (4) initiates cleavage. Implantation is a later event.</p><p>Fusion of male and female pronuclei restores 46 chromosomes. The second polar body is extruded as meiosis II finishes. The sperm's X or Y determines genetic sex. Mitotic cleavage begins within ~24 hours. Implantation starts ~day 6–7 after fertilization.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Meiosis II completed</b> — Completion of meiosis II is a result of fertilization.</li><li><b>Diploid number re-established</b> — Re-establishing the diploid number is a result of fertilization.</li><li><b>Cleavage initiated</b> — Initiating cleavage is a result of fertilization.</li><li><b>Sex determined</b> — Determining sex is a result of fertilization.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Four results: diploid, meiosis II done, sex set, cleavage begins.</p>","w":["Completion of meiosis II is a result of fertilization.","Re-establishing the diploid number is a result of fertilization.","Initiating cleavage is a result of fertilization.","Determining sex is a result of fertilization.","Correct — implantation isn't a result of fertilization."]},"fe54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Genetic sex is determined at fertilization by the sperm: X-bearing → XX (female); Y-bearing → XY (male).</p><p>All oocytes carry an X; sperm carry X or Y in roughly equal numbers. SRY on the Y chromosome triggers testis development; without it, ovaries develop. Phenotypic sex can differ from chromosomal sex in conditions such as androgen insensitivity (XY, female external phenotype) or 5α-reductase deficiency.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The FSH level</b> — FSH level doesn't decide genetic sex.</li><li><b>The mother's age</b> — Maternal age doesn't decide sex.</li><li><b>The position of implantation in the uterus</b> — Implantation position doesn't decide sex.</li><li><b>The oocyte's X chromosome alone</b> — Every oocyte carries an X, so it can't determine sex alone.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SRY → testis; androgen insensitivity = XY with female phenotype.</p>","w":["FSH level doesn't decide genetic sex.","Maternal age doesn't decide sex.","Correct — the sperm's X or Y chromosome.","Implantation position doesn't decide sex.","Every oocyte carries an X, so it can't determine sex alone."]},"fe55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor: at ~16 cells, the embryo is a morula (\"mulberry\") — a solid ball of blastomeres (~day 3).</p><p>Sequence: zygote → 2-cell (~30 h) → 4-cell → 8-cell → compaction → morula (16 cells, day 3) → blastocyst (day 4–5) → hatching → implantation (day 6–7). Compaction makes outer cells form tight junctions, beginning the inner/outer cell distinction (ICM vs trophoblast).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>8</b> — 8 cells is too early.</li><li><b>4</b> — 4 cells is too early.</li><li><b>32</b> — 32 cells isn't the professor's number.</li><li><b>64</b> — 64 cells isn't the professor's number.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Morula = 16 cells, day 3.</p>","w":["8 cells is too early.","Correct — 16 cells.","4 cells is too early.","32 cells isn't the professor's number.","64 cells isn't the professor's number."]},"fe56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fluid enters the morula and forms a cavity (blastocoel), creating the blastocyst with an inner cell mass and outer trophoblast.</p><p>Trophoblast cells pump Na⁺ into the interior and water follows, forming the blastocoel. The inner cell mass (embryoblast) gathers at one pole (embryonic pole) and will form the embryo; the trophoblast forms the placenta's fetal part. The blastocyst reaches the uterus at ~day 4–5.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The zona pellucida thickens around it</b> — The zona pellucida is shed (hatching), not thickened.</li><li><b>The neural tube closes in the embryo</b> — Neural tube closure occurs much later (week 4).</li><li><b>Its outer cells fuse into a syncytium</b> — Outer cells fuse into a syncytium only at implantation.</li><li><b>Mesoderm forms between two layers</b> — Mesoderm forms between layers in week 3 (gastrulation).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Blastocyst = ICM + trophoblast + blastocoel.</p>","w":["The zona pellucida is shed (hatching), not thickened.","Neural tube closure occurs much later (week 4).","Outer cells fuse into a syncytium only at implantation.","Correct — fluid enters and forms a cavity.","Mesoderm forms between layers in week 3 (gastrulation)."]},"fe57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Implantation begins ~day 6–7 after fertilization (≈ cycle day 20–21).</p><p>The blastocyst hatches from the zona, attaches to the endometrium (usually posterior wall of the upper uterine body) at its embryonic pole, and the trophoblast differentiates into cytotrophoblast and invasive syncytiotrophoblast. Implantation is complete by about day 10–12. Light spotting at implantation may be mistaken for a period.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Day 14</b> — Day 14 is ovulation in the cycle.</li><li><b>Day 3–4</b> — The blastocyst only reaches the uterus around day 4–5.</li><li><b>Day 21</b> — Day 21 is the cycle day, not the number of days after fertilization.</li><li><b>Day 1–2</b> — On days 1–2 the embryo is still cleaving in the tube.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Normal site: posterior wall of upper uterine body.</p>","w":["Correct — day 6–7.","Day 14 is ovulation in the cycle.","The blastocyst only reaches the uterus around day 4–5.","Day 21 is the cycle day, not the number of days after fertilization.","On days 1–2 the embryo is still cleaving in the tube."]},"fe58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Inner cell mass → embryo; trophoblast → fetal part of the placenta (\"ICM = infant; trophoblast = feeding\").</p><p>The ICM forms the bilaminar disc (epiblast and hypoblast) in week 2, then all three germ layers through gastrulation (from epiblast). The trophoblast becomes cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast, forming chorionic villi and producing hCG. Extraembryonic mesoderm and the amnion and yolk sac also develop from ICM derivatives.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypoblast → embryo; epiblast → placenta</b> — Both epiblast and hypoblast come from the ICM; the epiblast forms the embryo.</li><li><b>Trophoblast → embryo; inner cell mass → placenta</b> — This reverses ICM and trophoblast.</li><li><b>Zona pellucida → placenta</b> — The zona pellucida is shed and doesn't form the placenta.</li><li><b>Yolk sac → embryo</b> — The yolk sac isn't the embryo.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Epiblast → all three germ layers.</p>","w":["Both epiblast and hypoblast come from the ICM; the epiblast forms the embryo.","This reverses ICM and trophoblast.","The zona pellucida is shed and doesn't form the placenta.","The yolk sac isn't the embryo.","Correct — ICM → embryo; trophoblast → placenta."]},"fe59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hatching from the zona pellucida exposes the trophoblast, allowing it to adhere to and invade the endometrium.</p><p>The blastocyst escapes through a small breach in the zona (helped by trophoblast enzymes). The exposed trophoblast attaches via adhesion molecules (e.g., integrins, L-selectin) to a receptive, progesterone-prepared endometrium. Assisted hatching is sometimes used in IVF.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The neural tube closes in the embryo</b> — Neural tube closure occurs much later.</li><li><b>The corpus luteum degenerates into a scar</b> — Corpus luteum regression would cause menstruation, preventing implantation.</li><li><b>The amniotic cavity forms above the epiblast</b> — The amniotic cavity forms in week 2, after implantation begins.</li><li><b>The morula forms in the fallopian tube</b> — Morula formation occurs earlier, in the tube.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Receptive endometrium requires progesterone.</p>","w":["Neural tube closure occurs much later.","Corpus luteum regression would cause menstruation, preventing implantation.","Correct — hatching exposes the sticky trophoblast.","The amniotic cavity forms in week 2, after implantation begins.","Morula formation occurs earlier, in the tube."]},"fe60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In pregnancy, the endometrium is called the decidua; the part beneath the implantation site is the decidua basalis (forms the maternal part of the placenta).</p><p>Decidual regions: basalis (beneath the embryo — maternal placenta), capsularis (over the embryo, facing the uterine cavity), parietalis (rest of the uterus). As the fetus grows, the capsularis fuses with the parietalis. Decidual cells are large, glycogen- and lipid-rich stromal cells. Absence of decidua basalis allows villi to invade the myometrium (placenta accreta spectrum), often over prior cesarean scars.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Perimetrium; stratum basalis</b> — Perimetrium and stratum basalis aren't the names used for the pregnant lining and implantation site.</li><li><b>Endometrium; myometrium</b> — Before implantation it's the endometrium; the myometrium is muscle.</li><li><b>Chorion; chorion laeve</b> — The chorion and chorion laeve are fetal membranes.</li><li><b>Amnion; amniotic cavity</b> — The amnion and amniotic cavity are fetal structures.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Defective decidua basalis → placenta accreta.</p>","w":["Perimetrium and stratum basalis aren't the names used for the pregnant lining and implantation site.","Correct — decidua; decidua basalis.","Before implantation it's the endometrium; the myometrium is muscle.","The chorion and chorion laeve are fetal membranes.","The amnion and amniotic cavity are fetal structures."]},"fe61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Implantation (~day 21 of the cycle) occurs in the secretory (luteal/progestational) phase, when progesterone has prepared a glandular, nutrient-rich endometrium.</p><p>The corpus luteum's progesterone peaks about a week after ovulation, exactly when the blastocyst arrives. This \"window of implantation\" (~days 20–24) features pinopodes on the epithelial surface and secretion of glycogen and mucins. An inadequate luteal phase (low progesterone) can impair implantation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ovulatory</b> — Ovulation occurs on day 14, not at implantation.</li><li><b>Postmenopausal</b> — The postmenopausal state isn't relevant.</li><li><b>Proliferative (estrogenic)</b> — One line in the notes says proliferative, but the starred recap says secretory — secretory is correct.</li><li><b>Menstrual</b> — The lining is shed in the menstrual phase, so implantation can't occur then.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Implantation window ≈ days 20–24 (secretory phase).</p>","w":["Ovulation occurs on day 14, not at implantation.","The postmenopausal state isn't relevant.","One line in the notes says proliferative, but the starred recap says secretory — secretory is correct.","Correct — the secretory (luteal) phase.","The lining is shed in the menstrual phase, so implantation can't occur then."]},"fe62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Trophoblast differentiates into an inner cellular cytotrophoblast and an outer multinucleated syncytiotrophoblast, which invades the endometrium and secretes hCG.</p><p>The syncytiotrophoblast forms by fusion of cytotrophoblast cells (losing their membranes). It erodes maternal tissue and vessels, creating lacunae filled with maternal blood (beginning uteroplacental circulation). It produces hCG (detectable in blood ~day 8–10), and later human placental lactogen, progesterone and estrogen. The cytotrophoblast is the proliferating stem layer.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decidua; prolactin</b> — The decidua is maternal tissue; prolactin isn't the hormone described here.</li><li><b>Cytotrophoblast; progesterone</b> — The cytotrophoblast keeps its membranes and isn't the hCG source in this context.</li><li><b>Hypoblast; estrogen</b> — The hypoblast is part of the embryonic disc.</li><li><b>Epiblast; FSH</b> — The epiblast is embryonic, and FSH comes from the pituitary.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Syncytiotrophoblast: hCG, hPL; lacks MHC class I (less immune attack).</p>","w":["Correct — syncytiotrophoblast; hCG.","The decidua is maternal tissue; prolactin isn't the hormone described here.","The cytotrophoblast keeps its membranes and isn't the hCG source in this context.","The hypoblast is part of the embryonic disc.","The epiblast is embryonic, and FSH comes from the pituitary."]},"fe63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Week 2 = \"week of twos\": the inner cell mass forms a bilaminar disc of epiblast (upper/dorsal) and hypoblast (lower/ventral).</p><p>Other \"twos\" in week 2: two trophoblast layers (cyto- and syncytiotrophoblast), two cavities (amniotic cavity and yolk sac), two layers of extraembryonic mesoderm. The epiblast later gives rise to all three germ layers during gastrulation (week 3); the hypoblast is displaced by definitive endoderm and contributes to the yolk sac.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chorion and amnion</b> — Chorion and amnion are membranes, not the disc layers.</li><li><b>Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast</b> — Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast are trophoblast layers.</li><li><b>Sclerotome and dermatome</b> — Sclerotome and dermatome are somite parts that form later.</li><li><b>Ectoderm and mesoderm</b> — Mesoderm forms in week 3.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Week 2 = rule of twos.</p>","w":["Chorion and amnion are membranes, not the disc layers.","Cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast are trophoblast layers.","Sclerotome and dermatome are somite parts that form later.","Mesoderm forms in week 3.","Correct — epiblast and hypoblast."]},"fe64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The amniotic cavity forms within/above the epiblast; the primary yolk sac forms below the hypoblast (lined by Heuser's membrane).</p><p>Amnioblasts from the epiblast line the amniotic cavity, which eventually surrounds the whole embryo and fills with amniotic fluid. Hypoblast-derived cells line the primary (exocoelomic) yolk sac, later replaced by the smaller secondary yolk sac. The yolk sac is the first site of hematopoiesis and the origin of primordial germ cells.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypoblast → amniotic cavity; epiblast → yolk sac</b> — This reverses the epiblast and hypoblast derivatives.</li><li><b>Trophoblast → both</b> — The trophoblast builds extraembryonic mesoderm and the chorionic cavity, not these two cavities.</li><li><b>Mesoderm → both</b> — Mesoderm doesn't exist yet in week 2.</li><li><b>Syncytiotrophoblast → amniotic cavity; cytotrophoblast → yolk sac</b> — Syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblast don't form these cavities.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Yolk sac: first blood cells and primordial germ cells.</p>","w":["This reverses the epiblast and hypoblast derivatives.","The trophoblast builds extraembryonic mesoderm and the chorionic cavity, not these two cavities.","Correct — epiblast → amniotic cavity; hypoblast → primary yolk sac.","Mesoderm doesn't exist yet in week 2.","Syncytiotrophoblast and cytotrophoblast don't form these cavities."]},"fe65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Heuser's (exocoelomic) membrane lines the primary yolk sac.</p><p>Around day 9, hypoblast-derived cells spread to line the blastocoel, forming Heuser's membrane and converting the cavity into the primary (exocoelomic) yolk sac. Extraembryonic mesoderm then forms between this membrane and the cytotrophoblast.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Zona pellucida</b> — The zona pellucida surrounds the oocyte and early embryo.</li><li><b>Chorion laeve</b> — The chorion laeve is the smooth, villus-free chorion.</li><li><b>Decidua capsularis</b> — Decidua capsularis isn't the term used for the yolk sac lining.</li><li><b>Wharton's jelly</b> — Wharton's jelly is the connective tissue of the umbilical cord.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heuser's membrane = lining of primary yolk sac.</p>","w":["The zona pellucida surrounds the oocyte and early embryo.","Correct — Heuser's membrane.","The chorion laeve is the smooth, villus-free chorion.","Decidua capsularis isn't the term used for the yolk sac lining.","Wharton's jelly is the connective tissue of the umbilical cord."]},"fe66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The chorionic (extraembryonic coelomic) cavity forms within extraembryonic mesoderm and is eventually obliterated as the amniotic cavity expands.</p><p>The chorionic cavity surrounds the embryo, amnion and yolk sac, which hang from the connecting stalk (future umbilical cord). As the amnion grows, it fuses with the chorion (amniochorionic membrane, ~week 12), eliminating the chorionic cavity. The intraembryonic coelom, by contrast, forms within lateral plate mesoderm and becomes the pericardial, pleural and peritoneal cavities.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Primary yolk sac; it becomes the gut</b> — The primary yolk sac is a separate cavity contributing to the gut.</li><li><b>Intraembryonic coelom; it becomes the body cavities</b> — The intraembryonic coelom is a different cavity inside the embryo that becomes the body cavities.</li><li><b>Amniotic cavity; it persists</b> — The amniotic cavity enlarges and persists; it isn't the cavity splitting the extraembryonic mesoderm.</li><li><b>Intervillous space; it fills with maternal blood</b> — The intervillous space lies between villi and fills with maternal blood.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amnion + chorion fuse → amniochorionic membrane.</p>","w":["The primary yolk sac is a separate cavity contributing to the gut.","The intraembryonic coelom is a different cavity inside the embryo that becomes the body cavities.","The amniotic cavity enlarges and persists; it isn't the cavity splitting the extraembryonic mesoderm.","Correct — chorionic cavity, later obliterated.","The intervillous space lies between villi and fills with maternal blood."]},"fe67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Extraembryonic mesoderm splits into an outer parietal (somatopleuric) layer and an inner visceral (splanchnopleuric) layer.</p><p>Extraembryonic somatic mesoderm lines the trophoblast and covers the amnion; extraembryonic splanchnic mesoderm covers the yolk sac. The same split occurs within the embryo's lateral plate mesoderm: somatic (parietal) layer with ectoderm forms the body wall; splanchnic (visceral) layer with endoderm forms gut wall musculature and the heart.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Both layers are called splanchnopleuric</b> — The two layers have different names.</li><li><b>Outer = amnion; inner = chorion</b> — The amnion and chorion are membranes, not mesoderm layers.</li><li><b>Outer = visceral/splanchnopleuric; inner = parietal/somatopleuric</b> — This reverses the outer and inner layers.</li><li><b>Outer = endoderm; inner = ectoderm</b> — Endoderm and ectoderm are germ layers, not mesoderm layers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Somatic = body wall; splanchnic = viscera (heart, gut wall).</p>","w":["Correct — outer parietal/somatopleuric; inner visceral/splanchnopleuric.","The two layers have different names.","The amnion and chorion are membranes, not mesoderm layers.","This reverses the outer and inner layers.","Endoderm and ectoderm are germ layers, not mesoderm layers."]},"fe68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Tertiary villi contain fetal capillaries within their mesodermal core, enabling maternal–fetal exchange.</p><p>Villus progression: primary (cytotrophoblast core covered by syncytiotrophoblast, ~end of week 2), secondary (mesoderm core added, ~week 3), tertiary (capillaries appear in mesoderm, end of week 3), connecting with vessels developing in the embryo so blood circulates when the heart begins beating (~day 22).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Chorion laeve</b> — The chorion laeve has lost its villi.</li><li><b>Stem villus without mesoderm</b> — A stem villus without mesoderm isn't the answer.</li><li><b>Secondary villus</b> — Secondary villi have mesoderm but no capillaries yet.</li><li><b>Primary villus</b> — Primary villi consist only of trophoblast layers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Villi: primary (trophoblast) → secondary (+mesoderm) → tertiary (+capillaries).</p>","w":["The chorion laeve has lost its villi.","A stem villus without mesoderm isn't the answer.","Secondary villi have mesoderm but no capillaries yet.","Primary villi consist only of trophoblast layers.","Correct — tertiary villus."]},"fe69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary villus = syncytiotrophoblast + cytotrophoblast + extraembryonic mesoderm core (no capillaries yet).</p><p>Each stage adds one layer: primary (two trophoblast layers), secondary (+ mesoderm core), tertiary (+ capillaries). The placental barrier in mature villi is very thin (syncytiotrophoblast, thin cytotrophoblast remnants, basement membrane, endothelium), facilitating exchange.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Syncytiotrophoblast + cytotrophoblast</b> — Syncytio- and cytotrophoblast alone form the primary villus.</li><li><b>Decidua basalis + myometrium + vessels</b> — Decidua basalis, myometrium and vessels are maternal tissues.</li><li><b>Syncytio- + cytotrophoblast + mesoderm + capillaries</b> — Adding capillaries makes it a tertiary villus.</li><li><b>Syncytiotrophoblast only</b> — Syncytiotrophoblast alone is incomplete.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Placental barrier thins over pregnancy.</p>","w":["Syncytio- and cytotrophoblast alone form the primary villus.","Decidua basalis, myometrium and vessels are maternal tissues.","Correct — syncytio- + cytotrophoblast + mesoderm.","Adding capillaries makes it a tertiary villus.","Syncytiotrophoblast alone is incomplete."]},"fe70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Chorion frondosum (bushy villous chorion facing decidua basalis) + decidua basalis = placenta; elsewhere villi degenerate, leaving the smooth chorion laeve.</p><p>Villi facing the decidua capsularis are compressed and lose blood supply, so they degenerate (chorion laeve). The chorion frondosum (fetal part) and decidua basalis (maternal part) form the discoid placenta. The maternal surface is divided into 15–20 cotyledons by decidual septa.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cotyledons; the yolk sac</b> — Cotyledons are lobes of the placenta, and they don't form the yolk sac.</li><li><b>Chorion laeve; amnion</b> — The chorion laeve is the smooth, villus-free chorion.</li><li><b>Decidua capsularis; the cord</b> — The decidua capsularis covers the embryo and doesn't form the cord.</li><li><b>Wharton's jelly; the placenta</b> — Wharton's jelly is the umbilical cord connective tissue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fetal placenta = chorion frondosum; maternal = decidua basalis.</p>","w":["Cotyledons are lobes of the placenta, and they don't form the yolk sac.","Correct — chorion frondosum forms the placenta.","The chorion laeve is the smooth, villus-free chorion.","The decidua capsularis covers the embryo and doesn't form the cord.","Wharton's jelly is the umbilical cord connective tissue."]},"fe71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The intervillous space is filled with maternal blood, which bathes the villi; fetal blood stays inside villus capillaries.</p><p>Spiral arteries of the decidua (remodeled by invading cytotrophoblast into low-resistance vessels) spurt maternal blood into the intervillous space; it drains through endometrial veins. Gases, nutrients and wastes cross the placental barrier. Inadequate spiral artery remodeling is linked to preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Wharton's jelly</b> — Wharton's jelly is in the umbilical cord.</li><li><b>Amniotic fluid</b> — Amniotic fluid is within the amniotic cavity.</li><li><b>Fetal blood</b> — Fetal blood stays inside villus capillaries.</li><li><b>Cerebrospinal fluid</b> — CSF isn't relevant to the placenta.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Poor spiral artery remodeling → preeclampsia.</p>","w":["Wharton's jelly is in the umbilical cord.","Amniotic fluid is within the amniotic cavity.","Fetal blood stays inside villus capillaries.","Correct — maternal blood.","CSF isn't relevant to the placenta."]},"fe72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Artificial rupture of membranes (ARM, amniotomy) cuts the amniotic and chorionic (amniochorionic) membranes to release amniotic fluid and help labor progress.</p><p>The amnion and chorion fuse by ~week 12 into one membrane surrounding the fetus. Normally they rupture spontaneously during labor. ARM is used to augment or induce labor and to allow fetal scalp monitoring. Risks: cord prolapse (if the head isn't engaged) and infection. Premature rupture of membranes before labor raises risks of chorioamnionitis and preterm birth.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Episiotomy; the perineal skin and muscle</b> — An episiotomy cuts the perineal skin and muscle.</li><li><b>Caesarean section; the myometrium</b> — A cesarean section is abdominal delivery through the myometrium.</li><li><b>Chorionic villus sampling; chorion frondosum</b> — Chorionic villus sampling takes villi for genetic testing (~10–13 weeks).</li><li><b>Amniocentesis; the amnion only, by needle</b> — Amniocentesis aspirates fluid with a needle through the abdominal wall.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ARM risk: cord prolapse.</p>","w":["Correct — ARM cutting amniotic and chorionic membranes.","An episiotomy cuts the perineal skin and muscle.","A cesarean section is abdominal delivery through the myometrium.","Chorionic villus sampling takes villi for genetic testing (~10–13 weeks).","Amniocentesis aspirates fluid with a needle through the abdominal wall."]},"fe73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's sequence: amnion enlarges → chorionic cavity obliterated → yolk sac reduced to secondary yolk sac → it invaginates into the endoderm → primitive gut.</p><p>Textbook framing: during week 4, embryonic folding (cephalocaudal and lateral) incorporates the dorsal part of the yolk sac into the embryo as the primitive gut (foregut, midgut, hindgut), while the remainder stays outside as the vitelline duct and yolk sac. The midgut connects to the yolk sac via the vitelline duct; failure of its obliteration causes Meckel diverticulum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Trophoblast invaginates into the endoderm → primitive gut</b> — The trophoblast forms the placenta, not the gut.</li><li><b>Notochord invaginates → neural plate → neural tube → gut</b> — Notochord-induced neural plate and tube formation describes neurulation.</li><li><b>Chorionic cavity enlarges → amnion obliterated → yolk sac → gut</b> — This reverses the sequence; the amnion expands and the chorionic cavity disappears.</li><li><b>Intermediate mesoderm hollows out → tube → primitive gut</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys and gonads.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Persistent vitelline duct → Meckel diverticulum (\"rule of 2s\").</p>","w":["The trophoblast forms the placenta, not the gut.","Notochord-induced neural plate and tube formation describes neurulation.","This reverses the sequence; the amnion expands and the chorionic cavity disappears.","Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys and gonads.","Correct — amnion enlarges, chorionic cavity lost, secondary yolk sac invaginates → gut."]},"fe74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Normal umbilical cord: 2 umbilical arteries + 1 umbilical vein, embedded in Wharton's jelly.</p><p>Arteries carry deoxygenated blood from fetal internal iliac arteries to the placenta; the single vein carries oxygenated blood to the fetus (via the ductus venosus to the IVC). After birth: vein → ligamentum teres; arteries → medial umbilical ligaments. A single umbilical artery (2-vessel cord) is associated with renal and cardiac anomalies and aneuploidy. The allantois/urachus also passes through the cord region; a patent urachus leaks urine at the umbilicus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1 artery and 1 vein</b> — One artery and one vein is too few vessels (single umbilical artery is abnormal).</li><li><b>2 arteries and 2 veins</b> — Two veins is too many; the right umbilical vein normally regresses.</li><li><b>3 arteries</b> — Three arteries isn't normal.</li><li><b>2 veins and 1 artery in Wharton's jelly</b> — This reverses the numbers of arteries and veins.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 2-vessel cord → screen for renal/cardiac anomalies.</p>","w":["One artery and one vein is too few vessels (single umbilical artery is abnormal).","Two veins is too many; the right umbilical vein normally regresses.","Correct — 2 arteries and 1 vein in Wharton's jelly.","Three arteries isn't normal.","This reverses the numbers of arteries and veins."]},"fe75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Wharton's jelly is mucoid connective tissue derived from extraembryonic mesoderm, cushioning the umbilical vessels.</p><p>It's rich in hyaluronic acid and contains mesenchymal stem cells. The jelly protects the vessels from compression and kinking. The connecting stalk (extraembryonic mesoderm) forms the cord, incorporating the yolk sac stalk (vitelline duct) and allantois.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest isn't the source of Wharton's jelly.</li><li><b>Trophoblast</b> — The trophoblast isn't the source.</li><li><b>Yolk sac endoderm</b> — Yolk sac endoderm isn't the source.</li><li><b>Amniotic fluid</b> — Amniotic fluid isn't the source.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Wharton's jelly = mucous connective tissue (hyaluronic acid, MSCs).</p>","w":["Neural crest isn't the source of Wharton's jelly.","Correct — extraembryonic mesoderm.","The trophoblast isn't the source.","Yolk sac endoderm isn't the source.","Amniotic fluid isn't the source."]},"fe76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Nuchal cord = umbilical cord wrapped around the fetal neck; more likely with long cords (normal ~50–60 cm).</p><p>Nuchal cords are common (~20–30% of deliveries) and usually harmless, but tight or multiple loops can compress the cord, causing fetal heart rate decelerations and, rarely, hypoxia. Long cords also predispose to true knots and cord prolapse; short cords can cause placental abruption or limit descent.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Velamentous insertion</b> — Velamentous insertion means cord vessels run through the membranes before reaching the placenta.</li><li><b>Succenturiate lobe</b> — A succenturiate lobe is an accessory placental lobe.</li><li><b>Circumvallate placenta</b> — A circumvallate placenta involves the membranes folding back at the placental edge.</li><li><b>Placenta previa</b> — Placenta previa is implantation over or near the cervix.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Velamentous insertion → vasa previa risk (fetal hemorrhage).</p>","w":["Velamentous insertion means cord vessels run through the membranes before reaching the placenta.","A succenturiate lobe is an accessory placental lobe.","A circumvallate placenta involves the membranes folding back at the placental edge.","Correct — nuchal cord.","Placenta previa is implantation over or near the cervix."]},"fe77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The prochordal plate (thickened hypoblast/endoderm) marks the cranial (head) end and becomes the oropharyngeal membrane (future mouth).</p><p>Establishing the cranial–caudal axis early is essential. At the cranial end, the prochordal plate fuses with ectoderm to form the oropharyngeal (buccopharyngeal) membrane, which ruptures in week 4 to open the mouth. At the caudal end, the cloacal membrane forms, later becoming the anal and urogenital openings. These are the only two places without mesoderm between ectoderm and endoderm.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cloaca</b> — The cloaca is at the tail end.</li><li><b>Primitive streak</b> — The primitive streak marks the tail end.</li><li><b>Notochordal process</b> — The notochordal process is a midline rod.</li><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest forms melanocytes, adrenal medulla and peripheral neurons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Oropharyngeal and cloacal membranes: ectoderm + endoderm with no mesoderm.</p>","w":["Correct — prochordal plate.","The cloaca is at the tail end.","The primitive streak marks the tail end.","The notochordal process is a midline rod.","Neural crest forms melanocytes, adrenal medulla and peripheral neurons."]},"fe78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The primitive streak forms on the epiblast (ectodermal) side in the caudal midline, marking the tail end and the body axis.</p><p>Appearing at the start of week 3, the primitive streak establishes cranial–caudal, dorsal–ventral and left–right axes. Its cranial end is the primitive node (pit). Epiblast cells migrate through the streak (gastrulation) to form endoderm and mesoderm. Remnants persisting in the sacrococcygeal region can cause sacrococcygeal teratoma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Yolk sac; gut end</b> — The yolk sac isn't where the primitive streak forms.</li><li><b>Amnion; dorsal side</b> — The amnion isn't where the primitive streak forms.</li><li><b>Trophoblast; placental side</b> — The trophoblast isn't where the primitive streak forms.</li><li><b>Hypoblast side; head end</b> — The hypoblast side at the head end describes the prochordal plate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Persistent primitive streak → sacrococcygeal teratoma.</p>","w":["The yolk sac isn't where the primitive streak forms.","The amnion isn't where the primitive streak forms.","The trophoblast isn't where the primitive streak forms.","The hypoblast side at the head end describes the prochordal plate.","Correct — ectodermal side, tail end."]},"fe79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gastrulation forms all three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm) from the epiblast and is complete by the end of week 3.</p><p>Epiblast cells move through the primitive streak: the first displace the hypoblast to form definitive endoderm; the next form mesoderm between the layers; remaining epiblast becomes ectoderm. Mesoderm then organizes into paraxial (somites), intermediate (urogenital) and lateral plate mesoderm. Week 3 is a sensitive teratogenic period; e.g., alcohol at this stage can cause holoprosencephaly. Caudal dysgenesis (sirenomelia) relates to insufficient mesoderm (maternal diabetes).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Implantation of the blastocyst; day 7</b> — Implantation of the blastocyst occurs at day ~7.</li><li><b>Formation of the placenta; end of month 3</b> — Placental takeover occurs at the end of month 3.</li><li><b>Formation of the neural tube; end of week 4</b> — Neural tube formation (neurulation) completes in week 4.</li><li><b>Division of the zygote into blastomeres; day 1</b> — Cleavage of the zygote into blastomeres begins on day 1.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Week 3 = week of threes (three germ layers).</p>","w":["Implantation of the blastocyst occurs at day ~7.","Placental takeover occurs at the end of month 3.","Correct — formation of all three germ layers, end of week 3.","Neural tube formation (neurulation) completes in week 4.","Cleavage of the zygote into blastomeres begins on day 1."]},"fe80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The notochord (from the notochordal process) is the midline axis around which vertebrae form; its adult remnant is the nucleus pulposus.</p><p>The notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate (neurulation) via signals like Sonic hedgehog, and organizes somite sclerotomes into vertebral bodies. It degenerates within vertebral bodies but persists in intervertebral discs as the nucleus pulposus. A chordoma is a rare malignant tumor of notochord remnants, usually at the clivus or sacrum.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Primitive gut; esophagus</b> — The primitive gut forms from endoderm, not the notochord.</li><li><b>Allantois; urachus</b> — The allantois becomes the urachus, but it isn't this midline cord.</li><li><b>Septum transversum; diaphragm</b> — The septum transversum (central tendon of the diaphragm) is mesodermal but not the notochord.</li><li><b>Neural tube; spinal cord</b> — The neural tube comes from ectoderm, induced by the notochord.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Notochord → nucleus pulposus; chordoma at clivus/sacrum.</p>","w":["The primitive gut forms from endoderm, not the notochord.","Correct — notochord; nucleus pulposus.","The allantois becomes the urachus, but it isn't this midline cord.","The septum transversum (central tendon of the diaphragm) is mesodermal but not the notochord.","The neural tube comes from ectoderm, induced by the notochord."]},"fe81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mesoderm forms muscle, bone/cartilage, connective tissue, the cardiovascular system (heart, vessels, blood), dermis, kidneys/gonads and serous linings.</p><p>Germ layer summary — ectoderm: epidermis and its appendages (hair, nails, sweat, sebaceous and mammary glands), nervous system, lens, inner ear; neural crest: melanocytes, adrenal medulla, PNS ganglia, facial bones. Mesoderm: muscle, bone, CVS, blood, dermis, kidneys, gonads, spleen, adrenal cortex. Endoderm: epithelial lining of GI and respiratory tracts, liver, pancreas, thyroid, thymus, bladder epithelium.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Mammary, sebaceous and sweat glands</b> — Mammary, sebaceous and sweat glands are ectodermal.</li><li><b>Lungs, liver and the GI lining</b> — Lungs, liver and the GI lining are endodermal.</li><li><b>Epidermis, hair and the nervous system</b> — Epidermis, hair and the nervous system are ectodermal.</li><li><b>Melanocytes and the adrenal medulla</b> — Melanocytes and the adrenal medulla derive from neural crest (ectoderm).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Adrenal cortex = mesoderm; adrenal medulla = neural crest.</p>","w":["Mammary, sebaceous and sweat glands are ectodermal.","Lungs, liver and the GI lining are endodermal.","Epidermis, hair and the nervous system are ectodermal.","Correct — muscle, bone, vessels and dermis.","Melanocytes and the adrenal medulla derive from neural crest (ectoderm)."]},"fe82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Epidermis (and appendages) from surface ectoderm; dermis from mesoderm (dermatome of somites on the back; lateral plate mesoderm elsewhere).</p><p>This dual origin explains why skin has an epithelial layer (keratinocytes) and a connective tissue layer (collagen, vessels, nerves). Melanocytes migrate from neural crest into the epidermis; Langerhans cells come from bone marrow.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Epidermis from mesoderm; dermis from ectoderm</b> — This reverses the origins.</li><li><b>Epidermis from neural crest; dermis from endoderm</b> — The epidermis isn't from neural crest, and the dermis isn't from endoderm.</li><li><b>Epidermis and dermis both from ectoderm</b> — The dermis is mesodermal, not ectodermal.</li><li><b>Both from endoderm</b> — Neither skin layer comes from endoderm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Melanocytes = neural crest; dermis = mesoderm.</p>","w":["Correct — epidermis from ectoderm; dermis from mesoderm.","This reverses the origins.","The epidermis isn't from neural crest, and the dermis isn't from endoderm.","The dermis is mesodermal, not ectodermal.","Neither skin layer comes from endoderm."]},"fe83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ectoderm forms the epidermis, hair, nails, skin glands (sweat, sebaceous, mammary) and the entire nervous system.</p><p>Surface ectoderm: epidermis, hair, nails, glands, lens, enamel, anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), inner ear epithelium. Neuroectoderm (neural tube): brain, spinal cord, retina, posterior pituitary. Neural crest: PNS, melanocytes, adrenal medulla, Schwann cells, craniofacial bones and cartilage.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Muscle, bone and the dermis of skin</b> — Muscle, bone and dermis are mesodermal.</li><li><b>Kidneys and ureters</b> — Kidneys and ureters come from intermediate mesoderm.</li><li><b>Heart and blood vessels</b> — The heart and blood vessels are mesodermal (splanchnic layer).</li><li><b>Liver, lungs and the GI tract lining</b> — Liver, lungs and GI lining are endodermal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anterior pituitary = surface ectoderm; posterior = neuroectoderm.</p>","w":["Muscle, bone and dermis are mesodermal.","Kidneys and ureters come from intermediate mesoderm.","The heart and blood vessels are mesodermal (splanchnic layer).","Liver, lungs and GI lining are endodermal.","Correct — epidermis, hair, glands and nervous system."]},"fe84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the GI tract and respiratory tract, plus the liver and pancreas (and thyroid, thymus, parathyroids, bladder epithelium).</p><p>Endoderm forms only epithelium and glands; the surrounding muscle and connective tissue come from splanchnic mesoderm. The primitive gut (foregut, midgut, hindgut) gives rise to lung buds (from the foregut), liver and pancreatic buds.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vertebrae</b> — Vertebrae come from sclerotome (mesoderm).</li><li><b>Heart and blood vessels</b> — The heart and blood vessels are mesodermal.</li><li><b>Brain and spinal cord</b> — The brain and spinal cord are ectodermal (neural tube).</li><li><b>Kidneys and ureters</b> — Kidneys and ureters come from intermediate mesoderm.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Endoderm = lining epithelium; mesoderm = gut wall muscle.</p>","w":["Vertebrae come from sclerotome (mesoderm).","The heart and blood vessels are mesodermal.","Correct — GI tract, lungs and liver.","The brain and spinal cord are ectodermal (neural tube).","Kidneys and ureters come from intermediate mesoderm."]},"fe85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Weeks 4–8 (organogenesis/embryonic period) are when most major congenital malformations arise — the most teratogen-sensitive window.</p><p>Weeks 1–2: \"all-or-none\" period — teratogens either kill the embryo or cause no defect (cells are totipotent and replaceable). Weeks 3–8: organ primordia form, so insults cause major structural defects. Weeks 9–38 (fetal period): growth and maturation; insults cause growth restriction or functional/minor defects (brain remains vulnerable throughout).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Weeks 1–2</b> — Weeks 1–2 are the all-or-none period (cleavage, implantation, bilaminar disc).</li><li><b>Weeks 12–16</b> — By weeks 12–16 organs are already formed.</li><li><b>Weeks 20–24</b> — Weeks 20–24 are mainly growth.</li><li><b>Weeks 36–40</b> — Weeks 36–40 are mainly growth and maturation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Weeks 1–2: all-or-none; weeks 3–8: malformations.</p>","w":["Weeks 1–2 are the all-or-none period (cleavage, implantation, bilaminar disc).","Correct — weeks 4–8.","By weeks 12–16 organs are already formed.","Weeks 20–24 are mainly growth.","Weeks 36–40 are mainly growth and maturation."]},"fe86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> TORCH = Toxoplasmosis, Other (syphilis, VZV, parvovirus B19, HIV, Zika), Rubella, CMV, Herpes — most damaging during organogenesis (weeks 4–8).</p><p>Common features of congenital TORCH infections: growth restriction, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, thrombocytopenia (\"blueberry muffin\" rash), microcephaly, chorioretinitis, hearing loss. Classic distinguishers: toxoplasmosis — hydrocephalus, diffuse intracranial calcifications, chorioretinitis (cat feces, undercooked meat); rubella — cataracts, deafness, PDA; CMV — periventricular calcifications, sensorineural hearing loss (most common); HSV-2 — vesicles, encephalitis (usually acquired at delivery).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>TB, Rabies, Cholera, HIV; third trimester</b> — TB, rabies, cholera and HIV aren't the TORCH list.</li><li><b>Toxo, RSV, Coxsackie, HIV; postpartum</b> — Toxo, RSV, Coxsackie and HIV aren't the TORCH list, and the timing is wrong.</li><li><b>Typhoid, Rubeola, Chlamydia, HPV; week 20</b> — Typhoid, rubeola, chlamydia and HPV aren't the TORCH list.</li><li><b>Tetanus, Rotavirus, Candida, Hepatitis; at delivery</b> — Tetanus, rotavirus, candida and hepatitis aren't the TORCH list.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Most common congenital infection = CMV.</p>","w":["TB, rabies, cholera and HIV aren't the TORCH list.","Toxo, RSV, Coxsackie and HIV aren't the TORCH list, and the timing is wrong.","Typhoid, rubeola, chlamydia and HPV aren't the TORCH list.","Correct — Toxo, Rubella, CMV, Herpes; weeks 4–8.","Tetanus, rotavirus, candida and hepatitis aren't the TORCH list."]},"fe87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Congenital rubella triad: cataracts, sensorineural deafness, congenital heart disease (PDA, pulmonary artery stenosis).</p><p>Infection in the first trimester (especially before 12 weeks) carries the highest risk. Other findings: \"blueberry muffin\" rash (extramedullary hematopoiesis), microcephaly, intellectual disability, hepatosplenomegaly. Prevented by MMR vaccination before pregnancy (live vaccine — contraindicated during pregnancy).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Herpes simplex</b> — HSV causes skin, eye and mouth lesions or encephalitis.</li><li><b>Syphilis</b> — Syphilis isn't part of TORCH as taught here and causes rhinitis, rash, bone changes.</li><li><b>Toxoplasmosis</b> — Toxoplasmosis classically causes chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus and calcifications.</li><li><b>Cytomegalovirus</b> — CMV classically causes periventricular calcifications and hearing loss.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Rubella: cataracts + deafness + PDA; MMR is live (not in pregnancy).</p>","w":["Correct — rubella.","HSV causes skin, eye and mouth lesions or encephalitis.","Syphilis isn't part of TORCH as taught here and causes rhinitis, rash, bone changes.","Toxoplasmosis classically causes chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus and calcifications.","CMV classically causes periventricular calcifications and hearing loss."]},"fe88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Intraembryonic mesoderm medial → lateral (PIL): paraxial (somites), intermediate (urogenital system), lateral plate (body cavities' linings, heart, limbs).</p><p>Paraxial mesoderm segments into somites (sclerotome, myotome, dermatome) and forms skull parts (somitomeres). Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys, gonads and their ducts. Lateral plate splits (by the intraembryonic coelom) into somatic layer (body wall, limb bones/connective tissue) and splanchnic layer (heart, gut wall, blood vessels).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Lateral → somites; intermediate → kidneys; paraxial → limbs</b> — Paraxial mesoderm forms the somites, and lateral plate forms limb skeleton/cavities; this reverses them.</li><li><b>All three form somites</b> — Each column has a different fate.</li><li><b>Intermediate → somites; paraxial → kidneys; lateral → gut</b> — This mixes up the columns.</li><li><b>Paraxial → kidneys; intermediate → somites; lateral → heart</b> — This reverses paraxial and intermediate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PIL = Paraxial, Intermediate, Lateral.</p>","w":["Paraxial mesoderm forms the somites, and lateral plate forms limb skeleton/cavities; this reverses them.","Each column has a different fate.","This mixes up the columns.","This reverses paraxial and intermediate.","Correct — paraxial → somites; intermediate → kidneys; lateral → cavities."]},"fe89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The kidneys develop from intermediate mesoderm (pronephros → mesonephros → metanephros).</p><p>The metanephros (permanent kidney) arises from the ureteric bud (outgrowth of the mesonephric duct → collecting system) and the metanephric blastema (→ nephrons). Bilateral renal agenesis (failure of ureteric bud) causes oligohydramnios and Potter sequence: pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities — incompatible with life.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Septum transversum</b> — The septum transversum forms the liver and diaphragm.</li><li><b>Prochordal plate</b> — The prochordal plate marks the head end.</li><li><b>Paraxial</b> — Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.</li><li><b>Lateral plate</b> — Lateral plate mesoderm forms body cavities and limbs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bilateral renal agenesis → oligohydramnios → Potter sequence.</p>","w":["The septum transversum forms the liver and diaphragm.","The prochordal plate marks the head end.","Correct — intermediate mesoderm.","Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.","Lateral plate mesoderm forms body cavities and limbs."]},"fe90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The septum transversum (a block of mesoderm in front of the pericardial cavity) contributes the central tendon of the diaphragm and the stroma in which the liver develops.</p><p>The diaphragm forms from four parts: septum transversum (central tendon), pleuroperitoneal membranes, body wall muscle, and dorsal esophageal mesentery (crura). Failure of pleuroperitoneal membranes to close (usually left, Bochdalek hernia) causes congenital diaphragmatic hernia with pulmonary hypoplasia. The liver bud (endoderm) grows into the septum transversum, which provides its connective tissue and Kupffer-independent stroma.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intermediate mesoderm</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys.</li><li><b>Paraxial mesoderm</b> — Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.</li><li><b>Notochord</b> — The notochord leaves the nucleus pulposus.</li><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Diaphragm innervation C3–C5 (phrenic) reflects cervical origin of the septum transversum.</p>","w":["Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys.","Correct — septum transversum.","Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.","The notochord leaves the nucleus pulposus.","Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla."]},"fe91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Horseshoe-shaped intraembryonic coelom: apex → pericardial cavity; sides → pleural cavities; ends (continuous with extraembryonic coelom) → peritoneal cavity.</p><p>Embryonic folding brings the cranial apex ventral to the foregut, forming the pericardial cavity. Pericardioperitoneal canals (future pleural cavities) are later separated by pleuropericardial and pleuroperitoneal membranes. Each cavity is lined by parietal (somatic) and visceral (splanchnic) serous membranes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The chorionic cavity</b> — The chorionic cavity is extraembryonic and is obliterated.</li><li><b>The primary yolk sac</b> — The yolk sac isn't a body cavity.</li><li><b>The two sides of the arch</b> — The two sides become the pleural cavities.</li><li><b>The ends of the arch</b> — The ends become the peritoneal cavity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Coelom → pericardial, pleural, peritoneal cavities.</p>","w":["The chorionic cavity is extraembryonic and is obliterated.","The yolk sac isn't a body cavity.","The two sides become the pleural cavities.","Correct — the apex of the arch.","The ends become the peritoneal cavity."]},"fe92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The heart forms in the visceral (splanchnic) layer of lateral plate mesoderm — the cardiogenic area cranial to the prochordal plate.</p><p>Splanchnic mesoderm lies next to endoderm, which induces heart formation. Paired endocardial tubes fuse into a single heart tube (week 3), which begins beating around day 22 — the first functioning organ. Folding brings the heart into the thorax. The somatic (parietal) layer lies next to ectoderm and forms the body wall and parietal serous linings.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest contributes to the outflow tract septum but isn't the heart's main origin.</li><li><b>Intermediate mesoderm</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys.</li><li><b>Paraxial mesoderm</b> — Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.</li><li><b>Parietal (somatopleuric), toward the ectoderm</b> — The parietal layer lines the body wall.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Heart beats ~day 22; first functional organ.</p>","w":["Correct — visceral (splanchnopleuric) layer.","Neural crest contributes to the outflow tract septum but isn't the heart's main origin.","Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys.","Paraxial mesoderm forms somites.","The parietal layer lines the body wall."]},"fe93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Somite divisions: sclerotome → vertebrae and ribs; myotome → skeletal muscle; dermatome → dermis of back skin.</p><p>The sclerotome migrates around the notochord and neural tube; each vertebra forms from the caudal half of one sclerotome and the cranial half of the next (resegmentation), so spinal nerves exit between vertebrae. Myotome cells split into epimere (deep back muscles, dorsal rami) and hypomere (body wall and limb muscles, ventral rami). Each segment keeps its spinal nerve, producing dermatomes and myotomes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sclerotome → nucleus pulposus; myotome → dermis; dermatome → bone</b> — The nucleus pulposus comes from the notochord, and the other fates are mixed up.</li><li><b>All three form muscle</b> — Only the myotome forms muscle.</li><li><b>Sclerotome → dermis; myotome → bone; dermatome → muscle</b> — This mixes up the fates.</li><li><b>Sclerotome → kidney; myotome → heart; dermatome → epidermis</b> — Kidneys, heart and epidermis don't come from somites.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Resegmentation → spinal nerves exit between vertebrae.</p>","w":["The nucleus pulposus comes from the notochord, and the other fates are mixed up.","Only the myotome forms muscle.","This mixes up the fates.","Kidneys, heart and epidermis don't come from somites.","Correct — sclerotome bone, myotome muscle, dermatome dermis."]},"fe94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The nucleus pulposus is the adult remnant of the notochord; the annulus fibrosus comes from sclerotome.</p><p>Disc herniation usually occurs posterolaterally (where the posterior longitudinal ligament is thinnest), compressing the nerve root below the disc in the lumbar spine (e.g., L4–L5 disc → L5 root). Symptoms: radicular pain (sciatica), sensory loss, weakness, reflex changes; positive straight leg raise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Sclerotome</b> — Sclerotome forms the vertebral bodies and annulus fibrosus around the notochord.</li><li><b>Neural crest</b> — Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla.</li><li><b>Dermatome</b> — Dermatome forms the dermis.</li><li><b>Intermediate mesoderm</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> L4–L5 disc → L5 root; L5–S1 → S1 root.</p>","w":["Sclerotome forms the vertebral bodies and annulus fibrosus around the notochord.","Neural crest forms melanocytes and the adrenal medulla.","Correct — the notochordal process.","Dermatome forms the dermis.","Intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys."]},"fe95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cranial–caudal and lateral folding in week 4 converts the flat trilaminar disc into a \"tube within a tube.\"</p><p>Head and tail folding bring the heart and septum transversum ventrally and form the foregut and hindgut; lateral folding closes the ventral body wall and forms the midgut. Endoderm becomes the inner gut tube, mesoderm surrounds it, and ectoderm covers the outside. Failures of lateral folding cause ventral body wall defects: gastroschisis (no sac, right of umbilicus) and omphalocele (sac-covered, midline — failure of gut return, associated with aneuploidy).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Only cranial–caudal folding of the disc</b> — Lateral folding also occurs, not only cranial–caudal.</li><li><b>Only lateral folding of the trophoblast</b> — The trophoblast isn't part of the embryo's body folding.</li><li><b>Splitting of the notochord into two rods</b> — The notochord doesn't split into two rods.</li><li><b>Fusion of the amnion with the chorion</b> — Fusion of amnion and chorion doesn't form the body tube.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gastroschisis (no sac) vs omphalocele (sac, associated anomalies).</p>","w":["Lateral folding also occurs, not only cranial–caudal.","Correct — cranial–caudal plus lateral folding.","The trophoblast isn't part of the embryo's body folding.","The notochord doesn't split into two rods.","Fusion of amnion and chorion doesn't form the body tube."]},"fe96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Neurulation: notochord induces overlying ectoderm → neural plate → neural groove with neural folds → folds fuse into the neural tube (completed by end of week 4).</p><p>Fusion begins in the cervical region and proceeds both ways; the cranial neuropore closes ~day 25 and the caudal neuropore ~day 28. Failure: cranial → anencephaly (↑AFP, polyhydramnios); caudal → spina bifida. The neural tube forms the CNS; neural crest cells leave the fold edges.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Endoderm invaginates → groove → neural tube</b> — The nervous system comes from ectoderm, not endoderm.</li><li><b>Notochord → neural groove → spinal cord</b> — The notochord is mesodermal and induces neurulation; it doesn't itself form the neural groove.</li><li><b>Neural tube → neural plate → neural groove and folds</b> — This sequence is reversed.</li><li><b>Paraxial mesoderm folds → neural tube → brain</b> — Paraxial mesoderm doesn't fold to form the neural tube.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cranial neuropore closes day 25; caudal day 28.</p>","w":["The nervous system comes from ectoderm, not endoderm.","The notochord is mesodermal and induces neurulation; it doesn't itself form the neural groove.","This sequence is reversed.","Correct — neural plate → groove and folds → tube.","Paraxial mesoderm doesn't fold to form the neural tube."]},"fe97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The adrenal medulla (chromaffin cells) and melanocytes both derive from neural crest.</p><p>Neural crest derivatives: PNS (dorsal root and autonomic ganglia), Schwann cells, adrenal medulla, melanocytes, enterochromaffin cells, parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid, pia/arachnoid, craniofacial bones and cartilage, odontoblasts, aorticopulmonary septum. Neural crest tumors: pheochromocytoma (catecholamines — episodic hypertension), neuroblastoma (children), melanoma. Disorders of migration: Hirschsprung disease (no enteric ganglia).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Endoderm; hepatocytes</b> — Endoderm forms hepatocytes, not the adrenal medulla.</li><li><b>Mesoderm; osteoblasts</b> — Mesoderm forms osteoblasts and the adrenal cortex, not the medulla.</li><li><b>Notochord; nucleus pulposus</b> — The notochord forms the nucleus pulposus.</li><li><b>Intermediate mesoderm; nephrons</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms nephrons.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hirschsprung = failed neural crest migration to colon.</p>","w":["Correct — neural crest; melanocytes.","Endoderm forms hepatocytes, not the adrenal medulla.","Mesoderm forms osteoblasts and the adrenal cortex, not the medulla.","The notochord forms the nucleus pulposus.","Intermediate mesoderm forms nephrons."]},"fe98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Neural crest cells arise at the edges (crests) of the neural folds and migrate widely as the neural tube closes.</p><p>They undergo epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition and travel along defined pathways. Neurocristopathies include Hirschsprung disease, DiGeorge syndrome (pharyngeal arch/aorticopulmonary septum problems), Treacher Collins syndrome (first arch), Waardenburg syndrome (pigmentation, deafness) and neurofibromatosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The prochordal plate</b> — The prochordal plate marks the head end.</li><li><b>The hypoblast</b> — The hypoblast is early endoderm.</li><li><b>The trophoblast</b> — The trophoblast forms the placenta.</li><li><b>The intermediate mesoderm</b> — Intermediate mesoderm forms kidneys.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Neurocristopathies: Hirschsprung, DiGeorge, Treacher Collins, Waardenburg.</p>","w":["The prochordal plate marks the head end.","The hypoblast is early endoderm.","The trophoblast forms the placenta.","Intermediate mesoderm forms kidneys.","Correct — edges of the neural folds."]},"fe99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Mature placenta: disc-shaped, ~500 g (about 1/6 of fetal weight), central cord insertion, maternal surface with cotyledons, smooth shiny fetal surface; maternal and fetal blood don't mix.</p><p>Fetal surface is covered by amnion with chorionic vessels radiating from the cord; maternal surface is dull red, divided by septa into 15–20 cotyledons. After delivery, the placenta is inspected for completeness — missing cotyledons can mean retained tissue causing postpartum hemorrhage or infection.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>~500 g; fetal side has cotyledons; edge cord</b> — Cotyledons are on the maternal surface, and the cord normally inserts centrally.</li><li><b>~5 kg; both sides smooth with a central cord</b> — About 5 kg is wrong, and the maternal side isn't smooth.</li><li><b>~500 g; maternal and fetal blood mix inside</b> — Maternal and fetal blood never mix.</li><li><b>~500 g; smooth maternal side; cord at the edge</b> — The maternal surface has cotyledons, and the cord normally inserts centrally.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Missing cotyledon → retained placenta → postpartum hemorrhage.</p>","w":["Cotyledons are on the maternal surface, and the cord normally inserts centrally.","About 5 kg is wrong, and the maternal side isn't smooth.","Correct — ~500 g, maternal cotyledons, central cord.","Maternal and fetal blood never mix.","The maternal surface has cotyledons, and the cord normally inserts centrally."]},"fe100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> IgG is the only immunoglobulin that crosses the placenta (via neonatal Fc receptors), giving passive immunity for the first months of life.</p><p>Transfer increases in the third trimester, so preterm babies get less. Maternal IgG can also harm the fetus: Rh alloimmunization (hemolytic disease of the newborn), neonatal Graves (TSI), neonatal myasthenia, neonatal lupus/heart block (anti-Ro). IgM (pentamer) can't cross; fetal IgM in cord blood indicates congenital infection. IgA is transferred in breast milk.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>IgA</b> — IgA is transferred in breast milk, not across the placenta.</li><li><b>IgE</b> — IgE doesn't cross the placenta.</li><li><b>IgD</b> — IgD doesn't cross the placenta.</li><li><b>IgM</b> — IgM is too large to cross.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Elevated cord-blood IgM = congenital infection.</p>","w":["IgA is transferred in breast milk, not across the placenta.","Correct — IgG.","IgE doesn't cross the placenta.","IgD doesn't cross the placenta.","IgM is too large to cross."]},"fe101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> After the baby is delivered, the placenta separates within ~15–20 minutes (third stage of labor), and uterine contraction constricts the spiral vessels to limit blood loss.</p><p>The myometrium's interlacing fibers act as \"living ligatures,\" clamping vessels as the uterus contracts. Signs of separation: gush of blood, lengthening of the cord, firm rising uterus. Uterine atony (failure to contract) is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage; oxytocin is given routinely to prevent it. A third stage lasting &gt;30 minutes suggests retained placenta.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>About 24 hours; the corpus luteum</b> — The corpus luteum isn't involved; 24 hours is far too long.</li><li><b>It never separates normally</b> — The placenta normally does separate.</li><li><b>About 2 minutes; fetal clotting factors</b> — About 2 minutes is too fast, and fetal clotting factors don't control maternal bleeding.</li><li><b>About 2 hours; oxytocin from the baby</b> — About 2 hours is too slow, and the oxytocin comes from the mother.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Uterine atony = #1 cause of postpartum hemorrhage.</p>","w":["The corpus luteum isn't involved; 24 hours is far too long.","The placenta normally does separate.","About 2 minutes is too fast, and fetal clotting factors don't control maternal bleeding.","Correct — 15–20 minutes; uterine vessels constrict.","About 2 hours is too slow, and the oxytocin comes from the mother."]},"fe102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Velamentous insertion: the umbilical vessels branch and run through the membranes, unprotected by Wharton's jelly, before reaching the placenta.</p><p>Exposed vessels can be compressed or torn. If they cross the internal os (vasa previa), membrane rupture can tear them, causing rapid fetal hemorrhage (painless bleeding with sudden fetal distress/bradycardia). Diagnosed by color Doppler ultrasound; planned C-section prevents catastrophe. Common in twin and IVF pregnancies.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Succenturiate</b> — A succenturiate lobe is an accessory placental lobe.</li><li><b>Fenestrated</b> — A fenestrated placenta has a hole in it.</li><li><b>Bilobed</b> — A bilobed placenta consists of two placental lobes.</li><li><b>Circumvallate</b> — A circumvallate placenta has membranes gripping the edge too tightly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vasa previa: painless bleeding + fetal bradycardia (fetal blood).</p>","w":["Correct — velamentous insertion.","A succenturiate lobe is an accessory placental lobe.","A fenestrated placenta has a hole in it.","A bilobed placenta consists of two placental lobes.","A circumvallate placenta has membranes gripping the edge too tightly."]},"fe103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Succenturiate lobe: an accessory placental lobe some distance from the main placenta, connected by vessels in the membranes.</p><p>Clinical importance: the accessory lobe may be retained after delivery (causing postpartum hemorrhage or infection), and its connecting vessels can form vasa previa. Inspect the membranes for torn vessels running to a missing lobe. A bilobed placenta has two nearly equal lobes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Circumvallate placenta</b> — A circumvallate placenta has tight membranes at the edge.</li><li><b>Placenta percreta</b> — Placenta percreta invades through the uterine wall.</li><li><b>Fenestrated placenta</b> — A fenestrated placenta has a hole.</li><li><b>Bilobed placenta</b> — A bilobed placenta has two equal lobes side by side.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Retained succenturiate lobe → postpartum hemorrhage.</p>","w":["A circumvallate placenta has tight membranes at the edge.","Placenta percreta invades through the uterine wall.","A fenestrated placenta has a hole.","A bilobed placenta has two equal lobes side by side.","Correct — succenturiate lobe."]},"fe104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Circumvallate placenta: the chorionic plate is smaller than the basal plate, so membranes fold back and grip the edge — associated with bleeding, abruption and difficult separation (professor's description).</p><p>The thickened, folded membrane ring at the placental edge can trap blood. It's associated with antepartum bleeding, preterm labor, abruption and growth restriction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Bilobed</b> — A bilobed placenta consists of two lobes.</li><li><b>Abruptio placentae</b> — Abruptio placentae is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta.</li><li><b>Velamentous</b> — Velamentous insertion means vessels run through the membranes.</li><li><b>Fenestrated</b> — A fenestrated placenta has a hole.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Circumvallate → antepartum bleeding, abruption.</p>","w":["A bilobed placenta consists of two lobes.","Abruptio placentae is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta.","Correct — circumvallate placenta.","Velamentous insertion means vessels run through the membranes.","A fenestrated placenta has a hole."]},"fe105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Placental abruption = premature separation of a normally implanted placenta before delivery.</p><p>Classic presentation: painful vaginal bleeding (may be concealed) with a tender, firm uterus and fetal distress. Risk factors: hypertension/preeclampsia, cocaine, smoking, trauma, previous abruption. Complications: hemorrhagic shock, DIC, fetal hypoxia/death. Contrast placenta previa: painless bright red bleeding with a soft uterus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Implantation over the internal os</b> — Implantation over the internal os is placenta previa.</li><li><b>A hole in the placenta</b> — A hole in the placenta is a fenestrated placenta.</li><li><b>An accessory lobe</b> — An accessory lobe is a succenturiate lobe.</li><li><b>Villi invading the myometrium</b> — Villi invading the myometrium is placenta increta.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Abruption = painful bleeding; previa = painless.</p>","w":["Implantation over the internal os is placenta previa.","Correct — premature separation of the placenta.","A hole in the placenta is a fenestrated placenta.","An accessory lobe is a succenturiate lobe.","Villi invading the myometrium is placenta increta."]},"fe106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Placenta increta: villi invade INTO the myometrium (but not through it). Accreta = attaches to myometrium; percreta = penetrates through to serosa/adjacent organs.</p><p>Placenta accreta spectrum results from a deficient decidua basalis (no Nitabuch layer), usually over a prior cesarean scar or with previa. The placenta can't separate normally, causing massive hemorrhage at delivery; management is often planned cesarean hysterectomy. Risk rises with the number of prior cesareans, especially combined with placenta previa.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Circumvallate placenta</b> — Circumvallate is a membrane–edge problem.</li><li><b>Placenta accreta</b> — Accreta only attaches to the myometrium without invading it.</li><li><b>Placenta previa</b> — Previa refers to low location, not depth of invasion.</li><li><b>Placenta percreta</b> — Percreta penetrates the whole wall (through the perimetrium).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Accreta (attach) → increta (into) → percreta (through); risk: prior C-section + previa.</p>","w":["Circumvallate is a membrane–edge problem.","Accreta only attaches to the myometrium without invading it.","Previa refers to low location, not depth of invasion.","Correct — placenta increta.","Percreta penetrates the whole wall (through the perimetrium)."]},"fe107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Placenta percreta: villi penetrate through the myometrium and perimetrium (serosa), possibly into the bladder or other organs.</p><p>It's the most severe form of the accreta spectrum. Percreta into the bladder can cause hematuria. Delivery requires a multidisciplinary team, blood products and usually hysterectomy.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increta</b> — Increta invades into the muscle only.</li><li><b>Accreta</b> — Accreta only attaches to the myometrium.</li><li><b>Abruptio</b> — Abruptio is premature separation of the placenta.</li><li><b>Velamentous</b> — Velamentous refers to vessels running in the membranes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Percreta → can invade bladder (hematuria).</p>","w":["Correct — percreta.","Increta invades into the muscle only.","Accreta only attaches to the myometrium.","Abruptio is premature separation of the placenta.","Velamentous refers to vessels running in the membranes."]},"fe108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Placenta previa = placenta implanted in the lower uterine segment near or over the internal os; complete coverage = 4th degree (complete/total previa).</p><p>Classic presentation: painless, bright red bleeding in the third trimester (soft, non-tender uterus). Diagnosed by ultrasound; digital vaginal examination is avoided because it can provoke hemorrhage. Risk factors: prior cesarean, multiparity, advanced maternal age, smoking, multiple gestation. Low-lying placentas detected early often \"migrate\" upward as the lower segment grows.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abruptio placentae</b> — Abruptio placentae is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta (usually painful).</li><li><b>Circumvallate placenta</b> — Circumvallate placenta is a membrane problem.</li><li><b>Placenta accreta</b> — Placenta accreta refers to abnormal depth of attachment, not location.</li><li><b>Placenta previa, 1st degree</b> — First-degree previa only approaches the os, without covering it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Previa: painless bleeding; no digital vaginal exam.</p>","w":["Abruptio placentae is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta (usually painful).","Circumvallate placenta is a membrane problem.","Placenta accreta refers to abnormal depth of attachment, not location.","First-degree previa only approaches the os, without covering it.","Correct — placenta previa, 4th degree."]},"fe109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In placenta previa, the placenta lies over the os, so it separates and delivers before the fetus, causing hemorrhage.</p><p>As the cervix dilates and the lower segment thins, placental attachments tear, opening maternal sinuses. The lower segment contracts poorly, so bleeding is hard to control. Complete previa requires cesarean delivery.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The villi penetrate through the perimetrium</b> — Villi penetrating the perimetrium describes placenta percreta.</li><li><b>The umbilical cord is too long and tears</b> — An overly long cord isn't the mechanism of previa bleeding.</li><li><b>The fetus has bilateral renal agenesis</b> — Fetal renal agenesis isn't related.</li><li><b>The corpus luteum fails early in pregnancy</b> — Early corpus luteum failure isn't related.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Complete previa = C-section.</p>","w":["Villi penetrating the perimetrium describes placenta percreta.","An overly long cord isn't the mechanism of previa bleeding.","Correct — the placenta lies over the os and comes out first.","Fetal renal agenesis isn't related.","Early corpus luteum failure isn't related."]},"fe110":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amniocentesis aspirates amniotic fluid under ultrasound guidance; fetal cells are karyotyped (professor: FISH, ~12 weeks).</p><p>Advanced maternal age (≥35 at delivery) increases aneuploidy risk. Options: noninvasive screening (cell-free fetal DNA, first-trimester combined screen, quad screen) and diagnostic tests — CVS (~10–13 weeks, placental tissue) and amniocentesis (standard ~15–20 weeks). FISH gives rapid results for common aneuploidies; full karyotype or microarray follows. (Use the professor's timing on his exam.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cordocentesis with a fetal blood culture</b> — Cordocentesis with blood culture isn't the procedure described.</li><li><b>Chorionic villus sampling with a Gram stain</b> — CVS with Gram stain isn't what the professor describes for karyotyping.</li><li><b>Maternal urine hCG measured at 12 weeks</b> — Maternal urine hCG is the pregnancy test, not a chromosomal test.</li><li><b>Biopsy of the decidua for histology</b> — Decidual biopsy for histology isn't described.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Standard: CVS 10–13 wk; amniocentesis 15–20 wk.</p>","w":["Cordocentesis with blood culture isn't the procedure described.","Correct — amniocentesis with karyotyping (FISH).","CVS with Gram stain isn't what the professor describes for karyotyping.","Maternal urine hCG is the pregnancy test, not a chromosomal test.","Decidual biopsy for histology isn't described."]},"fe111":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amniocentesis can introduce infection (chorioamnionitis); inflamed membranes may form fibrous amniotic bands that wrap fetal parts.</p><p>Chorioamnionitis presents with maternal fever, uterine tenderness, foul-smelling fluid and fetal tachycardia; it risks preterm labor and neonatal sepsis. Amniotic band sequence (from amnion rupture) causes constriction rings, limb amputations and facial clefts. Procedure-related miscarriage risk is low (~0.1–0.5%).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Abruptio placentae</b> — Abruptio placentae is premature placental separation.</li><li><b>Renal agenesis</b> — Renal agenesis isn't related to the procedure.</li><li><b>Velamentous insertion</b> — Velamentous insertion is a cord insertion variant.</li><li><b>Placenta previa</b> — Placenta previa is a placental location problem.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Amniotic bands → limb amputations, constriction rings.</p>","w":["Abruptio placentae is premature placental separation.","Renal agenesis isn't related to the procedure.","Velamentous insertion is a cord insertion variant.","Correct — amniochorionitis leading to amniotic bands.","Placenta previa is a placental location problem."]},"fe112":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the marker for open neural tube defects; periconceptional folic acid prevents them.</p><p>AFP is made by the fetal liver and yolk sac and leaks into amniotic fluid and maternal serum through open defects (anencephaly, open spina bifida) and ventral wall defects. Amniotic acetylcholinesterase confirms an open NTD. Low AFP is associated with Down syndrome (quad screen: ↓AFP, ↑hCG, ↓estriol, ↑inhibin A). (Professor's version: diagnose by week 8 via ultrasound + CVS; standard practice uses maternal serum AFP at 16–18 weeks and ultrasound.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>hCG; iron supplements</b> — hCG is the pregnancy marker, and iron doesn't prevent NTDs.</li><li><b>Estrogen; vitamin B12</b> — Estrogen isn't the NTD marker, and B12 isn't the preventive.</li><li><b>FSH; calcium</b> — FSH and calcium aren't related to NTD screening or prevention.</li><li><b>Progesterone; vitamin C</b> — Progesterone and vitamin C aren't related to NTDs.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑AFP: NTDs, abdominal wall defects, twins; ↓AFP: Down syndrome.</p>","w":["Correct — alpha-fetoprotein; folic acid.","hCG is the pregnancy marker, and iron doesn't prevent NTDs.","Estrogen isn't the NTD marker, and B12 isn't the preventive.","FSH and calcium aren't related to NTD screening or prevention.","Progesterone and vitamin C aren't related to NTDs."]},"fe113":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Amniotic fluid ~1000–1200 mL near term (professor), kept constant by fetal swallowing → GI absorption → renal filtration → urination back into the cavity.</p><p>Early in pregnancy, fluid is mostly transudate from maternal plasma across membranes; after ~16 weeks, fetal urine is the main source. Fetal lungs also secrete fluid. Functions: cushions the fetus, allows movement (muscle and limb development), prevents adhesions, supports lung development, maintains temperature. Disrupted swallowing → polyhydramnios; absent urine → oligohydramnios.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>~3000 mL; leaked from the umbilical vein</b> — About 3000 mL is too much (polyhydramnios).</li><li><b>~100 mL; secreted only by the placenta</b> — About 100 mL is too little, and the placenta isn't the only source.</li><li><b>~50 mL; made by the yolk sac</b> — About 50 mL is too little, and the yolk sac doesn't produce it.</li><li><b>~5 L; produced by the mother's kidneys</b> — About 5 L is far too much, and the mother's kidneys don't produce it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> After 16 weeks, fetal urine = main source of amniotic fluid.</p>","w":["About 3000 mL is too much (polyhydramnios).","About 100 mL is too little, and the placenta isn't the only source.","About 50 mL is too little, and the yolk sac doesn't produce it.","About 5 L is far too much, and the mother's kidneys don't produce it.","Correct — ~1000–1200 mL, maintained by the swallow–absorb–filter–urinate cycle."]},"fe114":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Polyhydramnios (&gt;1500 mL) results when the fetus can't swallow or absorb fluid — e.g., esophageal atresia/tracheoesophageal fistula or duodenal atresia.</p><p>Esophageal atresia with TEF: newborn drools, chokes or coughs with feeds; a feeding tube can't pass into the stomach. Duodenal atresia (associated with Down syndrome) shows a \"double bubble\" sign. Other causes of polyhydramnios: anencephaly (impaired swallowing), maternal diabetes (fetal polyuria), twin–twin transfusion (recipient), fetal anemia/hydrops.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Renal agenesis</b> — Renal agenesis causes too little fluid (oligohydramnios).</li><li><b>Excess fetal urine (diabetes insipidus)</b> — Excess fetal urine can cause polyhydramnios, but it isn't the cause indicated here.</li><li><b>Placenta previa</b> — Placenta previa doesn't affect amniotic fluid volume.</li><li><b>Neural tube closure</b> — Normal neural tube closure doesn't cause polyhydramnios.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Polyhydramnios: GI obstruction, anencephaly, maternal diabetes.</p>","w":["Renal agenesis causes too little fluid (oligohydramnios).","Excess fetal urine can cause polyhydramnios, but it isn't the cause indicated here.","Correct — upper GI obstruction such as TEF.","Placenta previa doesn't affect amniotic fluid volume.","Normal neural tube closure doesn't cause polyhydramnios."]},"fe115":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bilateral renal agenesis → no fetal urine → oligohydramnios (&lt;700 mL) → Potter sequence: flat face, limb deformities (club foot), pulmonary hypoplasia.</p><p>Potter sequence (\"POTTER\"): Pulmonary hypoplasia, Oligohydramnios (trigger), Twisted face (flattened nose, recessed chin, low-set ears), Twisted skin, Extremity defects (club feet), Renal failure (in utero). Pulmonary hypoplasia is usually the cause of death. Other causes of oligohydramnios: posterior urethral valves, ARPKD, placental insufficiency, rupture of membranes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Polyhydramnios; neural tube defect</b> — Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios, not polyhydramnios, and isn't linked to neural tube defects.</li><li><b>Polyhydramnios; macrosomia</b> — Fluid is reduced, not increased, and macrosomia isn't a consequence.</li><li><b>Normal fluid; no consequences</b> — Fluid volume is markedly reduced, with serious consequences.</li><li><b>Oligohydramnios; tracheo-esophageal fistula</b> — TEF causes polyhydramnios.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Potter sequence: oligohydramnios → pulmonary hypoplasia (cause of death).</p>","w":["Renal agenesis causes oligohydramnios, not polyhydramnios, and isn't linked to neural tube defects.","Correct — oligohydramnios with flat face and club foot.","Fluid is reduced, not increased, and macrosomia isn't a consequence.","Fluid volume is markedly reduced, with serious consequences.","TEF causes polyhydramnios."]},"fe116":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Professor's thresholds: polyhydramnios &gt;1500 mL; oligohydramnios &lt;700 mL (normal ~1000–1200 mL near term).</p><p>Clinically, amniotic fluid is estimated by ultrasound: amniotic fluid index (AFI) — oligohydramnios &lt;5 cm, polyhydramnios &gt;24–25 cm; or single deepest pocket (&lt;2 cm or &gt;8 cm). Either abnormality warrants evaluation for fetal anomalies and maternal conditions.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>&gt;3000 mL; &lt;100 mL</b> — &gt;3000 and &lt;100 mL aren't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>&gt;1200 mL; &lt;1000 mL</b> — &gt;1200 and &lt;1000 mL aren't the professor's numbers.</li><li><b>&gt;700 mL; &lt;1500 mL</b> — This reverses the cutoffs.</li><li><b>&gt;1000 mL; &lt;500 mL</b> — &gt;1000 and &lt;500 mL aren't the professor's numbers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> AFI: &lt;5 cm oligo; &gt;24 cm poly.</p>","w":[">3000 and <100 mL aren't the professor's numbers.",">1200 and <1000 mL aren't the professor's numbers.","This reverses the cutoffs.","Correct — >1500 mL and <700 mL.",">1000 and <500 mL aren't the professor's numbers."]},"fe117":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Monozygotic twins: one zygote splits — genetically identical, same sex (~1/3 of twins). Dizygotic twins: two oocytes fertilized by two sperm — like ordinary siblings (~2/3).</p><p>Dizygotic twinning rate varies with maternal age, family history (hereditary hyperovulation), ethnicity and fertility treatment; monozygotic twinning occurs at a fairly constant rate worldwide. Monochorionic twins (always monozygotic) risk twin–twin transfusion syndrome through shared placental vessels.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>DZ twins always share one chorion</b> — Dizygotic twins are always dichorionic, not sharing one chorion.</li><li><b>MZ twins make up about 2/3 of all twins</b> — Monozygotic twins make up about 1/3 of twins, not 2/3.</li><li><b>DZ twins come from one zygote that splits</b> — One zygote that splits describes monozygotic twins.</li><li><b>MZ twins can be of different sexes</b> — Monozygotic twins are always the same sex.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Monochorionic = monozygotic; risk of twin–twin transfusion.</p>","w":["Correct — MZ from one splitting zygote; DZ from two zygotes.","Dizygotic twins are always dichorionic, not sharing one chorion.","Monozygotic twins make up about 1/3 of twins, not 2/3.","One zygote that splits describes monozygotic twins.","Monozygotic twins are always the same sex."]},"fe118":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Dizygotic twins always have their own chorion and amnion (dichorionic, diamniotic), though their placentas may fuse.</p><p>Each zygote implants separately and develops its own trophoblast (chorion) and amnion. Even if two placentas grow together, the vascular systems usually remain separate. On ultrasound, a thick dividing membrane with a \"twin peak\" (lambda) sign suggests dichorionic twins; a thin membrane with a \"T sign\" suggests monochorionic diamniotic.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Conjoined</b> — Conjoined twins result only from very late monozygotic splitting.</li><li><b>Dichorionic, monoamniotic</b> — Dichorionic monoamniotic isn't a standard arrangement for dizygotic twins.</li><li><b>Monochorionic, diamniotic</b> — Monochorionic diamniotic results only from monozygotic splitting at the blastocyst stage.</li><li><b>Monochorionic, monoamniotic</b> — Monochorionic monoamniotic results only from late monozygotic splitting.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lambda sign = dichorionic; T sign = monochorionic.</p>","w":["Conjoined twins result only from very late monozygotic splitting.","Dichorionic monoamniotic isn't a standard arrangement for dizygotic twins.","Monochorionic diamniotic results only from monozygotic splitting at the blastocyst stage.","Monochorionic monoamniotic results only from late monozygotic splitting.","Correct — dichorionic, diamniotic."]},"fe119":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Monozygotic split at days 4–8 (inner cell mass divides after the chorion forms) → monochorionic, diamniotic (most common MZ type).</p><p>Timing rule for monozygotic twins — later split = more sharing: days 0–3 (morula) → di/di; days 4–8 (blastocyst) → mono/di (~70% of MZ twins); days 8–13 (after amnion forms) → mono/mono (risk of cord entanglement); &gt;13 days → conjoined. Monochorionic twins share placental circulation, risking twin–twin transfusion syndrome (donor: anemic, oligohydramnios; recipient: polycythemic, polyhydramnios). (The professor's table lists an extra uncommon category; know his table for his exam.)</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Monochorionic, monoamniotic</b> — Monochorionic monoamniotic results from a split after the amnion forms (days 8–13).</li><li><b>Dichorionic, diamniotic</b> — Dichorionic diamniotic results from an early (morula) split.</li><li><b>Dichorionic, monoamniotic</b> — Dichorionic monoamniotic isn't the standard outcome.</li><li><b>Conjoined</b> — Conjoined twins result from a split at days 13–15.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mono/di = most common MZ arrangement; TTTS risk.</p>","w":["Monochorionic monoamniotic results from a split after the amnion forms (days 8–13).","Dichorionic diamniotic results from an early (morula) split.","Correct — monochorionic, diamniotic.","Dichorionic monoamniotic isn't the standard outcome.","Conjoined twins result from a split at days 13–15."]},"fe120":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Monozygotic split at days 1–3 (morula stage), before trophoblast differentiation → dichorionic, diamniotic.</p><p>Each half develops its own trophoblast (chorion) and amnion, so the arrangement looks like dizygotic twins; only genetic testing (or same sex with identical markers) can confirm monozygosity. About 25–30% of monozygotic twins are di/di.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Monochorionic, diamniotic</b> — Monochorionic diamniotic results from a blastocyst-stage split.</li><li><b>Monochorionic, monoamniotic</b> — Monochorionic monoamniotic results from a late split after the amnion forms.</li><li><b>A single fetus</b> — Twins do form; a single fetus would mean no split.</li><li><b>Conjoined twins</b> — Conjoined twins result from the latest split.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Di/di twins can be MZ or DZ.</p>","w":["Monochorionic diamniotic results from a blastocyst-stage split.","Correct — dichorionic, diamniotic.","Monochorionic monoamniotic results from a late split after the amnion forms.","Twins do form; a single fetus would mean no split.","Conjoined twins result from the latest split."]},"fe121":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Thoracopagus = conjoined twins joined at the chest (the most common type, often sharing a heart).</p><p>Conjoined twins arise from incomplete splitting of the embryonic disc after day 13; they're always monochorionic, monoamniotic and same sex. Named by site of fusion (-pagus = fastened): craniopagus (head), thoracopagus (chest), omphalopagus (abdomen), ischiopagus (pelvis), pygopagus (sacrum/buttocks). Surgical separation depends on shared organs (shared heart in thoracopagus often precludes it).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pygopagus</b> — Pygopagus (joined at the sacrum) isn't in the professor's list.</li><li><b>Ischiopagus</b> — Ischiopagus twins are joined at the pelvis.</li><li><b>Omphalopagus</b> — Omphalopagus twins are joined at the abdomen/umbilicus.</li><li><b>Craniopagus</b> — Craniopagus twins are joined at the head.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Thoracopagus = most common; shared heart.</p>","w":["Pygopagus (joined at the sacrum) isn't in the professor's list.","Ischiopagus twins are joined at the pelvis.","Omphalopagus twins are joined at the abdomen/umbilicus.","Correct — thoracopagus.","Craniopagus twins are joined at the head."]},"fe122":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Omphalopagus = conjoined twins joined at the abdomen/umbilicus (omphalos = navel), often sharing a liver.</p><p>Omphalopagus twins usually have separate hearts and may share liver tissue and sometimes bowel, making them relatively good candidates for surgical separation. Conjoined twins form from the latest monozygotic splitting (~days 13–15), when separation of the inner cell mass/embryonic disc is incomplete.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Craniopagus</b> — Craniopagus twins are joined at the head.</li><li><b>Ischiopagus</b> — Ischiopagus twins are joined at the pelvis.</li><li><b>Thoracopagus</b> — Thoracopagus twins are joined at the chest.</li><li><b>Cephalopagus</b> — Cephalopagus isn't in the professor's list.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Omphalopagus: shared liver, often separable.</p>","w":["Correct — omphalopagus.","Craniopagus twins are joined at the head.","Ischiopagus twins are joined at the pelvis.","Thoracopagus twins are joined at the chest.","Cephalopagus isn't in the professor's list."]},"fp1":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Homeostasis works mainly by negative feedback: the response pushes the regulated variable back toward its set point, opposing the disturbance.</p><p>Every feedback loop has a sensor (carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors), a control center (medullary cardiovascular centers), and effectors (heart and blood vessels). Here, hemorrhage lowers pressure → baroreceptors fire less → sympathetic outflow rises and vagal tone falls → tachycardia, increased contractility and vasoconstriction (cool, clammy skin) → pressure rises back toward normal. The response is in the opposite direction to the change, which is the defining feature of negative feedback.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The response amplifies the initial disturbance</b> — Amplifying the disturbance is positive feedback — the opposite of homeostasis.</li><li><b>The response eliminates all physiologic variation</b> — Homeostasis keeps variables within a normal range; it never removes all variation.</li><li><b>The response establishes a permanently altered set point</b> — The set point isn't permanently changed; the reflex restores pressure toward the existing set point.</li><li><b>The response functions independently of sensory information</b> — The reflex depends on sensory input from the baroreceptors.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Negative feedback = response opposite in direction to the stimulus.</p>","w":["Amplifying the disturbance is positive feedback — the opposite of homeostasis.","Correct — negative feedback opposes the deviation and pushes pressure back toward normal.","Homeostasis keeps variables within a normal range; it never removes all variation.","The set point isn't permanently changed; the reflex restores pressure toward the existing set point.","The reflex depends on sensory input from the baroreceptors."]},"fp2":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pathologic positive feedback = a \"vicious cycle\" in which the result of a disturbance worsens the disturbance itself.</p><p>Kidneys normally control long-term blood pressure by excreting salt and water (pressure natriuresis). In chronic kidney disease, this ability is impaired, so pressure rises. High pressure then damages renal arterioles and glomeruli (hypertensive nephrosclerosis), further reducing renal function, which raises pressure more. Each step reinforces the last. Physiologic positive feedback exists (oxytocin in labor, the LH surge, clotting cascade, Na⁺ channel opening in an action potential), but it is self-limited; uncontrolled positive feedback causes progressive deterioration.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Physiologic negative feedback</b> — Negative feedback would oppose the disturbance; here each step worsens it.</li><li><b>Feed-forward motor control</b> — Feed-forward control anticipates a disturbance (e.g., rapid movement), not a worsening cycle.</li><li><b>Adaptive learning</b> — Adaptive learning refines future responses (e.g., the cerebellum); it isn't a vicious cycle.</li><li><b>Steady-state regulation</b> — Steady state implies stability, but this condition progressively deteriorates.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Physiologic positive feedback: labor (oxytocin), ovulation (LH surge), clotting, action potential upstroke.</p>","w":["Negative feedback would oppose the disturbance; here each step worsens it.","Feed-forward control anticipates a disturbance (e.g., rapid movement), not a worsening cycle.","Adaptive learning refines future responses (e.g., the cerebellum); it isn't a vicious cycle.","Correct — hypertension damages the kidney, which worsens hypertension: a self-reinforcing cycle.","Steady state implies stability, but this condition progressively deteriorates."]},"fp3":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gain = correction ÷ remaining error; a negative sign means the system opposes the disturbance.</p><p>Step 1 — find what would have happened with no control: 175 mm Hg. Step 2 — find what actually happened: 125 mm Hg. Step 3 — correction = actual − uncorrected = 125 − 175 = −50. Step 4 — remaining error = actual − baseline = 125 − 100 = +25. Gain = −50 / +25 = −2. The baroreceptor system has a gain of about −2 (moderate); temperature control systems can have gains of about −33, meaning they correct far more completely. The larger the magnitude, the smaller the leftover error.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>+0.33</b> — +0.33 has the wrong sign and value; a positive gain would mean the response adds to the error.</li><li><b>+2.00</b> — +2.00 has the right magnitude but the wrong sign; negative feedback has a negative gain.</li><li><b>−25.00</b> — −25 confuses the remaining error (25 mm Hg) with the gain.</li><li><b>−50.00</b> — −50 is the correction alone, not correction ÷ error.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Gain = correction/error; baroreceptor ≈ −2, thermoregulation ≈ −33.</p>","w":["+0.33 has the wrong sign and value; a positive gain would mean the response adds to the error.","+2.00 has the right magnitude but the wrong sign; negative feedback has a negative gain.","Correct — correction (−50) ÷ remaining error (+25) = −2.","−25 confuses the remaining error (25 mm Hg) with the gain.","−50 is the correction alone, not correction ÷ error."]},"fp4":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Negative feedback means the response counteracts the original change, even if the response itself is an increase (here, ventilation increases to lower CO₂).</p><p>Central chemoreceptors in the medulla sense rising PCO₂ (via the resulting fall in CSF pH); peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies also respond. The respiratory centers increase rate and depth of breathing, which blows off CO₂ and lowers it back toward 40 mm Hg. Students often mistake \"ventilation goes up\" for positive feedback — but the variable being regulated is CO₂, and the response drives CO₂ down.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Positive feedback because ventilation increases</b> — Ventilation rises, but it lowers CO₂; the response opposes the stimulus, so it isn't positive feedback.</li><li><b>Feed-forward control because ventilation precedes the CO₂ increase</b> — The response follows the CO₂ rise; feed-forward acts before a disturbance occurs.</li><li><b>Adaptive control because the response is learned</b> — This is a built-in reflex, not a learned response.</li><li><b>Open-loop control because no sensor is involved</b> — Chemoreceptors sense CO₂, so this is a closed loop with a sensor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Judge feedback by what happens to the regulated variable, not to the response.</p>","w":["Ventilation rises, but it lowers CO₂; the response opposes the stimulus, so it isn't positive feedback.","Correct — increased ventilation removes CO₂, opposing the original rise.","The response follows the CO₂ rise; feed-forward acts before a disturbance occurs.","This is a built-in reflex, not a learned response.","Chemoreceptors sense CO₂, so this is a closed loop with a sensor."]},"fp5":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Effectors carry out the corrective response; sensors detect the change; the control center integrates and commands.</p><p>In thermoregulation: sensors = peripheral (skin) and central (hypothalamic) thermoreceptors; control center = preoptic/anterior hypothalamus; efferent pathway = sympathetic cholinergic fibers; effectors = sweat glands (evaporative heat loss) and cutaneous blood vessels (vasodilation). Only the effector physically changes the regulated variable (body temperature).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypothalamic temperature sensor</b> — The hypothalamus integrates temperature information (sensor/control center) but doesn't produce the corrective response itself.</li><li><b>Cerebral cortex</b> — The cerebral cortex isn't the effector in thermoregulation.</li><li><b>Cutaneous thermoreceptors</b> — Cutaneous thermoreceptors are sensors that detect temperature.</li><li><b>Peripheral sensory nerves</b> — Sensory nerves carry information to the control center (afferent limb).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sweat glands are sympathetic but cholinergic (muscarinic M₃) — anticholinergics cause hyperthermia.</p>","w":["The hypothalamus integrates temperature information (sensor/control center) but doesn't produce the corrective response itself.","The cerebral cortex isn't the effector in thermoregulation.","Correct — sweat glands are the effectors that carry out heat loss.","Cutaneous thermoreceptors are sensors that detect temperature.","Sensory nerves carry information to the control center (afferent limb)."]},"fp6":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (primary active transport) maintains high intracellular K⁺ and low intracellular Na⁺ against their gradients.</p><p>Passive processes (diffusion, channels) always move ions down their electrochemical gradients, which would tend to equalize concentrations. Only energy-consuming pumps can keep ICF K⁺ (~140 mEq/L) far above ECF K⁺ (~4 mEq/L). Even in hyperkalemia from renal failure, the pump keeps intracellular K⁺ much higher. Insulin and β₂-agonists stimulate the pump, which is why they're used to shift K⁺ into cells in acute hyperkalemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Simple diffusion through the lipid bilayer</b> — K⁺ is charged and can't cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion.</li><li><b>Facilitated diffusion through potassium channels</b> — K⁺ channels let K⁺ move down its gradient — they dissipate, not maintain, the gradient.</li><li><b>Osmosis through aquaporins</b> — Aquaporins move water, not K⁺.</li><li><b>Vesicular transport through the plasma membrane</b> — Vesicular transport moves large molecules, not ion gradients.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Acute hyperkalemia: insulin + glucose and β₂-agonists drive K⁺ into cells via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</p>","w":["K⁺ is charged and can't cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion.","K⁺ channels let K⁺ move down its gradient — they dissipate, not maintain, the gradient.","Correct — the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase uses ATP to keep K⁺ high inside the cell.","Aquaporins move water, not K⁺.","Vesicular transport moves large molecules, not ion gradients."]},"fp7":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> When ATP runs out, pumps stop but leak channels keep working, so ion gradients run down (Na⁺ in, K⁺ out) and the cell depolarizes and swells.</p><p>Ion gradients are a dynamic balance between continuous passive leak and active pumping. Remove the ATP and only the leak remains: Na⁺ enters, K⁺ leaves, the resting membrane potential decays toward zero (depolarization), and water follows Na⁺ into the cell, causing swelling. In neurons, depolarization also opens voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels and triggers glutamate release, contributing to excitotoxic injury. Toxins like cyanide (complex IV inhibitor) cause exactly this.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased maintenance of intracellular potassium</b> — Without ATP, the cell loses intracellular K⁺; it can't maintain it.</li><li><b>Increased maintenance of extracellular sodium</b> — Extracellular Na⁺ can't be maintained — Na⁺ leaks into the cell.</li><li><b>Increased osmotic equilibrium without cellular swelling</b> — Na⁺ entry with water causes swelling; osmotic balance isn't preserved.</li><li><b>Enhanced transport of ions against their gradients</b> — Uphill transport is exactly what fails when ATP runs out.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Reversible cell injury: ATP depletion → Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase failure → cellular swelling (first sign).</p>","w":["Without ATP, the cell loses intracellular K⁺; it can't maintain it.","Extracellular Na⁺ can't be maintained — Na⁺ leaks into the cell.","Correct — passive leaks continue while active transport stops, so gradients run down.","Na⁺ entry with water causes swelling; osmotic balance isn't preserved.","Uphill transport is exactly what fails when ATP runs out."]},"fp8":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Positive feedback is recognized when the output makes the original change bigger — e.g., failing heart → less coronary flow → weaker heart.</p><p>In cardiogenic shock, falling cardiac output lowers aortic pressure, the driving force for coronary perfusion (coronary flow occurs mostly in diastole). The ischemic myocardium contracts less, output falls further, and pressure drops more — the cycle accelerates toward death unless broken (revascularization, inotropes, mechanical support). Mild disturbances are usually corrected by negative feedback; positive feedback takes over only when the disturbance exceeds the system's ability to compensate.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The response reduces the original disturbance</b> — Reducing the original disturbance describes negative feedback.</li><li><b>The response restores cardiac output toward baseline</b> — Restoring cardiac output toward baseline describes a corrective (negative feedback) response.</li><li><b>The response eliminates all variation in cardiac output</b> — No feedback system eliminates all variation.</li><li><b>The response depends primarily on hormonal signaling</b> — The cycle here is hemodynamic, not hormonal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Mild shock → compensated by negative feedback; severe shock → positive feedback → irreversible.</p>","w":["Reducing the original disturbance describes negative feedback.","Restoring cardiac output toward baseline describes a corrective (negative feedback) response.","Correct — low output → less coronary flow → weaker heart → even lower output.","No feedback system eliminates all variation.","The cycle here is hemodynamic, not hormonal."]},"fp9":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Feed-forward control sends preplanned motor commands before feedback can arrive; adaptive control uses later feedback to improve future performance.</p><p>Some movements happen too fast for sensory feedback to correct them mid-movement (signals take tens of milliseconds to travel to the brain and back). The brain therefore sends a preprogrammed command (feed-forward). Afterward, the cerebellum compares the actual sensory outcome with the intended movement and adjusts future commands — motor learning. This combination explains why practice makes fast, complex movements more precise.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Pure negative feedback</b> — Pure negative feedback is too slow for rapid movements because sensory signals arrive after the movement.</li><li><b>Positive feedback</b> — Positive feedback amplifies a disturbance; it doesn't plan movements.</li><li><b>Hormonal feedback control</b> — Hormones are far too slow for rapid motor control.</li><li><b>Steady-state regulation</b> — Steady-state regulation keeps a variable stable; it doesn't coordinate rapid movements.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cerebellum = comparator for feed-forward + adaptive motor learning.</p>","w":["Pure negative feedback is too slow for rapid movements because sensory signals arrive after the movement.","Positive feedback amplifies a disturbance; it doesn't plan movements.","Correct — preprogrammed commands plus delayed sensory evaluation improve later performance.","Hormones are far too slow for rapid motor control.","Steady-state regulation keeps a variable stable; it doesn't coordinate rapid movements."]},"fp10":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Total body water (~60% of body weight in young adult men) falls with age and with higher body fat, because fat holds little water while muscle holds a lot.</p><p>Lean tissue is ~70–75% water; adipose tissue is only ~10–20% water. Women and older adults have relatively more fat and less muscle, so TBW is about 50% of body weight (or lower in the elderly). Clinical relevance: water-soluble drugs (e.g., aminoglycosides, lithium, ethanol) have a smaller volume of distribution in older adults, so the same dose produces higher plasma concentrations; fat-soluble drugs (e.g., diazepam) have a larger volume of distribution and longer half-life.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Aging increases total body water because fat contains abundant intracellular water</b> — Adipose tissue contains little water, so more fat lowers total body water.</li><li><b>Aging increases total body water because extracellular fluid expands progressively</b> — Extracellular fluid doesn't progressively expand with age.</li><li><b>Aging leaves total body water unchanged because homeostasis prevents body- composition changes</b> — Body composition clearly changes with age; homeostasis doesn't prevent that.</li><li><b>Aging decreases total body water because plasma volume becomes negligible</b> — Plasma volume remains a meaningful compartment in older adults.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> TBW 60% → ICF 40%, ECF 20% (interstitial 15%, plasma 5%) — the 60-40-20 rule.</p>","w":["Adipose tissue contains little water, so more fat lowers total body water.","Extracellular fluid doesn't progressively expand with age.","Correct — water-rich muscle is replaced by water-poor fat, so TBW falls with age.","Body composition clearly changes with age; homeostasis doesn't prevent that.","Plasma volume remains a meaningful compartment in older adults."]},"fp11":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Fick's law: diffusion ∝ (area × diffusion coefficient × concentration gradient) ÷ thickness. Fibrosis increases thickness → less O₂ transfer.</p><p>Gases cross the alveolar–capillary membrane by simple diffusion. Interstitial fibrosis deposits collagen in the alveolar walls, increasing the diffusion distance and lowering DLCO. Because O₂ diffuses slower than CO₂, hypoxemia appears first, especially during exercise when RBC transit time shortens. Emphysema impairs diffusion differently — by reducing surface area. Supplemental O₂ helps diffusion-limited hypoxemia by increasing the gradient.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased oxygen concentration gradient</b> — The alveolar–capillary O₂ gradient isn't the primary problem described.</li><li><b>Decreased diffusion coefficient of oxygen</b> — O₂'s diffusion coefficient is a physical property and isn't changed by fibrosis.</li><li><b>Increased membrane surface area</b> — Surface area is stated to be normal, and an increase would improve diffusion.</li><li><b>Increased oxygen solubility in the membrane</b> — Increased solubility would improve, not impair, diffusion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↓DLCO: fibrosis (thickness), emphysema (area), anemia, pulmonary vascular disease.</p>","w":["The alveolar–capillary O₂ gradient isn't the primary problem described.","O₂'s diffusion coefficient is a physical property and isn't changed by fibrosis.","Surface area is stated to be normal, and an increase would improve diffusion.","Correct — Fick's law: flux falls as membrane thickness rises.","Increased solubility would improve, not impair, diffusion."]},"fp12":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Carrier-mediated transport saturates (reaches Tm or Vmax) because there's a limited number of transporters/binding sites.</p><p>At low glucose, most GLUT carriers are empty, so adding glucose increases transport almost linearly. As glucose rises, more carriers are occupied until essentially all are busy; then transport plateaus. This follows Michaelis–Menten-like kinetics, and Km is the concentration at half-maximal transport. Simple diffusion, in contrast, rises linearly with concentration without a plateau. A clinical example of transporter saturation: glucosuria occurs when filtered glucose exceeds the renal SGLT transport maximum (blood glucose ~180–200 mg/dL).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Direct ATP hydrolysis by the transporter</b> — Glucose carriers in skeletal muscle (GLUTs) don't hydrolyze ATP; that's primary active transport.</li><li><b>Movement of glucose against its concentration gradient</b> — Facilitated diffusion moves glucose down its concentration gradient.</li><li><b>Absence of specific binding sites</b> — Carriers do have specific binding sites, which is why they saturate.</li><li><b>Dependence exclusively on membrane lipid solubility</b> — Lipid solubility governs simple diffusion, which doesn't saturate.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Carrier features: saturation, specificity (stereospecificity), competition.</p>","w":["Glucose carriers in skeletal muscle (GLUTs) don't hydrolyze ATP; that's primary active transport.","Facilitated diffusion moves glucose down its concentration gradient.","Carriers do have specific binding sites, which is why they saturate.","Correct — with a finite number of carriers, transport plateaus at Vmax.","Lipid solubility governs simple diffusion, which doesn't saturate."]},"fp13":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Stereospecificity: carriers recognize the 3-D shape of a substrate, so they can transport D-glucose but not its mirror-image L-glucose.</p><p>D- and L-glucose have the same formula and chemical groups but are enantiomers (mirror images). The binding pocket of GLUT transporters fits only D-glucose, the form found in nature and metabolized by cells. Simple diffusion depends only on size and lipid solubility, so it can't distinguish enantiomers; only protein-mediated transport shows stereospecificity. This is one of three hallmarks of carrier-mediated transport.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Competitive inhibition</b> — Competitive inhibition is two similar substrates competing for one carrier (e.g., galactose vs glucose).</li><li><b>Electrochemical coupling</b> — Electrochemical coupling links transport to an ion gradient (e.g., SGLT).</li><li><b>Membrane electrogenicity</b> — Electrogenic transport moves net charge across the membrane.</li><li><b>Transport saturation</b> — Saturation is the plateau at high concentrations, not isomer selectivity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Carrier transport hallmarks: saturation, stereospecificity, competition.</p>","w":["Competitive inhibition is two similar substrates competing for one carrier (e.g., galactose vs glucose).","Electrochemical coupling links transport to an ion gradient (e.g., SGLT).","Electrogenic transport moves net charge across the membrane.","Correct — the carrier recognizes D-glucose but not its mirror image, L-glucose.","Saturation is the plateau at high concentrations, not isomer selectivity."]},"fp14":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Oral rehydration therapy uses SGLT1: Na⁺ and glucose are absorbed together, and water follows osmotically — even when Cl⁻ secretion is stimulated.</p><p>Cholera toxin permanently activates Gs → high cAMP → CFTR opens → massive Cl⁻ and water secretion into the lumen. However, the SGLT1 cotransporter on the apical membrane is unaffected. Giving both Na⁺ and glucose lets SGLT1 move them into enterocytes (secondary active transport driven by the Na⁺ gradient from the basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase). Glucose exits via GLUT-2, Na⁺ via the pump, and water follows the absorbed solutes, restoring intravascular volume.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Passive diffusion of glucose through the lipid bilayer</b> — Glucose is polar and doesn't cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion.</li><li><b>Primary active transport of glucose using ATP</b> — Intestinal glucose uptake isn't directly ATP-powered; it uses the Na⁺ gradient.</li><li><b>Sodium movement down its gradient through a chloride channel</b> — Na⁺ doesn't travel through chloride channels.</li><li><b>Receptor-mediated endocytosis of sodium and glucose</b> — Endocytosis isn't how Na⁺ and glucose are absorbed.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cholera: Gs ↑ → cAMP ↑ → CFTR Cl⁻ secretion → \"rice-water\" diarrhea; treat with ORS.</p>","w":["Glucose is polar and doesn't cross the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion.","Intestinal glucose uptake isn't directly ATP-powered; it uses the Na⁺ gradient.","Na⁺ doesn't travel through chloride channels.","Endocytosis isn't how Na⁺ and glucose are absorbed.","Correct — SGLT1 cotransport brings Na⁺ and glucose in, and water follows."]},"fp15":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ischemia → ATP loss → Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase fails → Na⁺ accumulates inside the cell → water follows → cellular swelling.</p><p>Cellular swelling (hydropic change) is the earliest, reversible sign of cell injury. Reduced ATP also shifts metabolism to anaerobic glycolysis (lactic acid, ↓pH), detaches ribosomes from the RER and impairs Ca²⁺ pumps. If ischemia persists, Ca²⁺ influx activates enzymes and membranes are damaged, causing irreversible injury (necrosis). Note that choices A and D are identical in the source; A is keyed.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased activity of sodium-glucose cotransport</b> — SGLT activity doesn't increase in hypoxia, and it isn't the trigger for swelling.</li><li><b>Increased potassium influx through voltage-gated channels</b> — K⁺ normally leaves cells; K⁺ influx isn't the mechanism.</li><li><b>Failure of ATP-dependent Na⁺ extrusion</b> — This is identical to choice A in the source document; A is the keyed answer.</li><li><b>Increased receptor-mediated endocytosis</b> — Endocytosis doesn't cause ischemic cell swelling.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Reversible injury: swelling, membrane blebs, ER dilation; irreversible: membrane damage, nuclear changes.</p>","w":["Correct — ATP loss stops the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Na⁺ builds up inside, and water follows.","SGLT activity doesn't increase in hypoxia, and it isn't the trigger for swelling.","K⁺ normally leaves cells; K⁺ influx isn't the mechanism.","This is identical to choice A in the source document; A is the keyed answer.","Endocytosis doesn't cause ischemic cell swelling."]},"fp16":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP; blocking it → intracellular Na⁺ rises and intracellular K⁺ falls.</p><p>With the pump inhibited, the normal leak of Na⁺ into and K⁺ out of the cell is no longer counterbalanced. The result: higher [Na⁺]i, lower [K⁺]i, slow depolarization, and cell swelling. Higher [Na⁺]i also reduces the driving force for Na⁺-coupled secondary active transport (e.g., NCX). Digoxin and ouabain inhibit this pump; in the heart, the reduced Na⁺ gradient slows NCX Ca²⁺ extrusion, increasing intracellular Ca²⁺ and contractility.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased intracellular Na⁺ and increased intracellular K⁺</b> — K⁺ is no longer pumped in, so intracellular K⁺ falls rather than rises.</li><li><b>Decreased intracellular Na⁺ and decreased intracellular K⁺</b> — Na⁺ is no longer pumped out, so intracellular Na⁺ rises.</li><li><b>Decreased intracellular Na⁺ and increased intracellular K⁺</b> — This is the opposite of what happens.</li><li><b>No change in intracellular Na⁺ or K⁺ concentrations</b> — Ion concentrations change progressively once the pump stops.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Digoxin: inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑[Na⁺]i → ↓NCX → ↑[Ca²⁺]i → ↑contractility; toxicity worsened by hypokalemia.</p>","w":["K⁺ is no longer pumped in, so intracellular K⁺ falls rather than rises.","Na⁺ is no longer pumped out, so intracellular Na⁺ rises.","Correct — blocking the pump raises intracellular Na⁺ and lowers intracellular K⁺.","This is the opposite of what happens.","Ion concentrations change progressively once the pump stops."]},"fp17":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger (NCX) is a secondary active antiporter: 3 Na⁺ in, 1 Ca²⁺ out.</p><p>NCX uses the inward Na⁺ gradient (maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase) to push Ca²⁺ out against a ~10,000-fold gradient. Because 3 positive charges enter while 2 leave, it is electrogenic. In cardiac myocytes, NCX removes much of the Ca²⁺ that enters during each beat, together with SERCA (back into the SR) and PMCA. Antiport = opposite directions; symport (cotransport) = same direction.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Primary active transport of Ca²⁺ using ATP</b> — Primary active Ca²⁺ transport is performed by PMCA and SERCA, not NCX.</li><li><b>Facilitated diffusion of Ca²⁺ through a lipid-soluble pathway</b> — Ca²⁺ doesn't pass through a lipid-soluble pathway.</li><li><b>Symport of Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ in the same direction</b> — NCX is an antiporter — the ions move in opposite directions.</li><li><b>Simple diffusion of Ca²⁺ down its concentration gradient</b> — Ca²⁺ is pumped out against its gradient, not diffusing down it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Antiport: NCX, Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger, Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger. Symport: SGLT, NKCC.</p>","w":["Primary active Ca²⁺ transport is performed by PMCA and SERCA, not NCX.","Ca²⁺ doesn't pass through a lipid-soluble pathway.","NCX is an antiporter — the ions move in opposite directions.","Correct — 3 Na⁺ in, 1 Ca²⁺ out, powered by the Na⁺ gradient.","Ca²⁺ is pumped out against its gradient, not diffusing down it."]},"fp18":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR → less Cl⁻ secretion and more ENaC-mediated Na⁺ (and water) absorption → dehydrated, thick airway mucus.</p><p>CFTR is an ATP-gated Cl⁻ channel (activated by cAMP/PKA) in epithelial apical membranes. ΔF508, the commonest mutation, causes misfolding and ER retention, so CFTR doesn't reach the membrane. In airways, reduced Cl⁻ secretion plus loss of CFTR's normal inhibition of ENaC causes excessive Na⁺ and water absorption, depleting the airway surface liquid. Cilia can't clear the thick mucus → chronic infection (S. aureus, Pseudomonas), bronchiectasis. In sweat ducts, CFTR normally reabsorbs Cl⁻, so CF sweat is salty (sweat Cl⁻ &gt;60 mmol/L is diagnostic). Pancreatic duct obstruction causes exocrine insufficiency and fat malabsorption.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased chloride secretion into the airway lumen</b> — CFTR defects decrease, not increase, Cl⁻ secretion.</li><li><b>Increased water secretion into the airway lumen</b> — Less water reaches the lumen, which is why secretions thicken.</li><li><b>Decreased sodium absorption from airway epithelial cells</b> — Na⁺ absorption increases in CF airways.</li><li><b>Increased receptor-mediated endocytosis of chloride</b> — Chloride isn't moved by endocytosis.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Sweat: CFTR absorbs Cl⁻ → high sweat Cl⁻ in CF. Airways/pancreas: CFTR secretes Cl⁻ → thick secretions.</p>","w":["CFTR defects decrease, not increase, Cl⁻ secretion.","Correct — less Cl⁻ secretion plus more Na⁺ and water absorption dehydrates the mucus.","Less water reaches the lumen, which is why secretions thicken.","Na⁺ absorption increases in CF airways.","Chloride isn't moved by endocytosis."]},"fp19":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> LDL enters cells by receptor-mediated endocytosis: LDL receptors cluster in clathrin-coated pits and are internalized.</p><p>The LDL receptor binds apoB-100 on LDL. Receptors gather in clathrin-coated pits (via the adaptor protein ARH/AP-2), which invaginate and pinch off (dynamin), forming coated vesicles. In endosomes, low pH releases LDL; receptors recycle to the surface, and LDL is degraded in lysosomes, releasing cholesterol that suppresses HMG-CoA reductase. In familial hypercholesterolemia (autosomal dominant), receptor defects — absent, can't bind, or can't cluster in coated pits (internalization-defective, e.g., the \"JD\" mutation) — raise LDL, causing tendon xanthomas, corneal arcus and premature coronary disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Simple diffusion of LDL across the lipid bilayer</b> — LDL is far too large to diffuse across the membrane.</li><li><b>Facilitated diffusion of LDL through a membrane carrier</b> — LDL has no carrier protein for facilitated diffusion.</li><li><b>Primary active transport of LDL using ATP</b> — LDL isn't moved by an ATP pump.</li><li><b>Pinocytosis of extracellular fluid</b> — Pinocytosis is nonspecific fluid uptake, not LDL receptor dependent.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Familial hypercholesterolemia: LDLR (or apoB/PCSK9) defects; Achilles tendon xanthomas.</p>","w":["LDL is far too large to diffuse across the membrane.","LDL has no carrier protein for facilitated diffusion.","Correct — LDL receptors cluster in clathrin-coated pits for receptor-mediated endocytosis.","LDL isn't moved by an ATP pump.","Pinocytosis is nonspecific fluid uptake, not LDL receptor dependent."]},"fp20":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Voltage-gated channels open in response to a change in membrane potential (e.g., depolarization from −70 toward −55 mV, the threshold).</p><p>Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels have a voltage sensor (the S4 segment with positive charges) that moves when the membrane depolarizes, opening the channel. When threshold is reached, rapid Na⁺ influx produces the action potential upstroke; the channels then inactivate within a millisecond. Classify channels by what opens them: voltage (voltage-gated Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺ channels), ligand (nicotinic ACh receptor, GABA-A), or mechanical stretch (Piezo, hair cell channels).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ligand-gated channel</b> — Ligand-gated channels open when a chemical binds (e.g., ACh at the NMJ).</li><li><b>Mechanically gated channel</b> — Mechanically gated channels open with stretch or deformation.</li><li><b>Aquaporin water channel</b> — Aquaporins conduct water and aren't voltage-gated.</li><li><b>Receptor-mediated endocytic channel</b> — Endocytosis isn't a channel.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Na⁺ channel: closed → open (threshold) → inactivated (absolute refractory period).</p>","w":["Ligand-gated channels open when a chemical binds (e.g., ACh at the NMJ).","Mechanically gated channels open with stretch or deformation.","Aquaporins conduct water and aren't voltage-gated.","Endocytosis isn't a channel.","Correct — opening in response to depolarization defines voltage gating."]},"fp21":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isotonic fluid loss (diarrhea, hemorrhage) = isosmotic volume contraction: only the ECF shrinks; osmolarity and ICF volume don't change.</p><p>Use the 3-step approach: (1) What was gained or lost? Isotonic fluid. (2) What happens to ECF volume and osmolarity? Volume falls; osmolarity is unchanged. (3) Does water shift between ECF and ICF? Only if osmolarity changed — here it didn't, so ICF is unchanged. Because plasma water is lost while RBCs and proteins stay, hematocrit and plasma protein concentration rise (hemoconcentration). Clinically, this gives hypovolemia (orthostasis, low BP) with normal serum Na⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased ECF volume, increased ICF volume, decreased ECF osmolarity</b> — ICF increasing with low osmolarity is hyposmotic contraction (e.g., adrenal insufficiency).</li><li><b>Decreased ECF volume, decreased ICF volume, increased ECF osmolarity</b> — Both compartments shrinking with high osmolarity is hyperosmotic contraction (water deprivation, sweating).</li><li><b>Increased ECF volume, unchanged ICF volume, unchanged ECF osmolarity</b> — Diarrhea decreases, not increases, ECF volume.</li><li><b>Increased ECF volume, increased ICF volume, decreased ECF osmolarity</b> — Both compartments expanding with low osmolarity is hyposmotic expansion (SIADH).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Darrow–Yannet: water moves only when ECF osmolarity changes.</p>","w":["Correct — isotonic loss shrinks only the ECF; with no osmotic gradient, the ICF is unchanged.","ICF increasing with low osmolarity is hyposmotic contraction (e.g., adrenal insufficiency).","Both compartments shrinking with high osmolarity is hyperosmotic contraction (water deprivation, sweating).","Diarrhea decreases, not increases, ECF volume.","Both compartments expanding with low osmolarity is hyposmotic expansion (SIADH)."]},"fp22":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Losing more water than salt (sweat, water deprivation) = hyperosmotic volume contraction: ECF becomes hypertonic and pulls water out of cells.</p><p>Sweat is hypotonic, so prolonged sweating without drinking removes relatively more water than Na⁺. ECF osmolarity rises (serum Na⁺ 153), creating an osmotic gradient that draws water from the ICF until osmolarities equalize. Both compartments shrink and end up more concentrated. ADH and thirst are stimulated, which is why urine output falls. Plasma proteins rise because plasma water is lost; hematocrit stays about the same, because RBCs also shrink as water leaves them.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It increases because hypotonic ECF draws water into cells</b> — The ECF is hypertonic here, not hypotonic.</li><li><b>It increases because intracellular osmolarity initially decreases</b> — ECF osmolarity rises first; ICF osmolarity doesn't fall.</li><li><b>It remains unchanged because cell membranes are impermeable to water</b> — Cell membranes are highly permeable to water (aquaporins).</li><li><b>It remains unchanged because sodium is restricted to the ECF</b> — Na⁺ stays in the ECF, which is exactly why water shifts out of cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyperosmotic contraction: ECF ↓, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑ (Hct unchanged, protein ↑).</p>","w":["The ECF is hypertonic here, not hypotonic.","ECF osmolarity rises first; ICF osmolarity doesn't fall.","Correct — losing more water than salt makes the ECF hyperosmotic, pulling water out of cells.","Cell membranes are highly permeable to water (aquaporins).","Na⁺ stays in the ECF, which is exactly why water shifts out of cells."]},"fp23":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> When ECF osmolarity rises, water leaves cells (ICF → ECF) until intracellular and extracellular osmolarities are equal.</p><p>Water crosses membranes freely, while Na⁺ is kept extracellular by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (an \"effective osmole\"). Water deprivation first raises ECF osmolarity, so water moves out of cells by osmosis. This partly protects ECF volume but shrinks cells — especially important in the brain, where shrinkage can tear bridging veins. Correcting chronic hypernatremia too quickly drives water back into adapted brain cells, causing cerebral edema.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Movement of NaCl from ECF into ICF</b> — NaCl stays extracellular and doesn't enter cells.</li><li><b>Movement of water from ECF into ICF</b> — Water moves out of, not into, the cells.</li><li><b>Movement of potassium from ICF into ECF</b> — K⁺ movement isn't what shrinks the ICF.</li><li><b>Movement of protein from ICF into ECF</b> — Proteins don't leave cells to cause this.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypernatremia → brain cell shrinkage; correct slowly (≤10–12 mEq/L per day).</p>","w":["NaCl stays extracellular and doesn't enter cells.","Correct — hypertonic ECF draws water out of the cells.","Water moves out of, not into, the cells.","K⁺ movement isn't what shrinks the ICF.","Proteins don't leave cells to cause this."]},"fp24":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Adrenal insufficiency (aldosterone deficiency → renal Na⁺ loss) = hyposmotic volume contraction: ECF ↓, ICF ↑, osmolarity ↓.</p><p>Without aldosterone, the kidney loses more NaCl than water, so ECF volume and osmolarity fall. The now-hypotonic ECF drives water into cells, so the ICF expands. Plasma proteins and hematocrit rise because ECF volume is reduced and RBCs swell. Clinically: hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia (no aldosterone to secrete K⁺), and hyperpigmentation (high ACTH in primary disease).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ECF ↑, ICF ↑, osmolarity ↓</b> — ECF volume falls with Na⁺ loss; it doesn't rise.</li><li><b>ECF ↓, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑</b> — Rising osmolarity fits water loss, not salt loss.</li><li><b>ECF ↑, ICF unchanged, osmolarity unchanged</b> — This doesn't fit — ECF falls and osmolarity drops.</li><li><b>ECF ↓, ICF unchanged, osmolarity unchanged</b> — That is isosmotic contraction (e.g., diarrhea); here osmolarity decreases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Addison disease: ↓Na⁺, ↑K⁺, hypotension, hyperpigmentation.</p>","w":["ECF volume falls with Na⁺ loss; it doesn't rise.","Correct — Na⁺ loss makes the ECF hypotonic, so water moves into cells.","Rising osmolarity fits water loss, not salt loss.","This doesn't fit — ECF falls and osmolarity drops.","That is isosmotic contraction (e.g., diarrhea); here osmolarity decreases."]},"fp25":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> SIADH (retained free water) = hyposmotic volume expansion: ECF ↑, ICF ↑, osmolarity ↓.</p><p>Excess ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels in collecting ducts, so free water is reabsorbed despite low plasma osmolarity (urine inappropriately concentrated). The retained water dilutes the ECF, which then drives water into cells — both compartments expand. Plasma protein and hematocrit fall from dilution. Patients are typically euvolemic clinically because natriuresis limits ECF expansion. Severe hyponatremia causes cerebral edema (confusion, seizures). Causes: CNS injury/surgery, small cell lung cancer, drugs (SSRIs, carbamazepine).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ECF ↓, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑</b> — ECF ↓, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑ is hyperosmotic contraction.</li><li><b>ECF ↓, ICF ↑, osmolarity ↓</b> — SIADH expands, rather than contracts, the ECF.</li><li><b>ECF ↑, ICF unchanged, osmolarity unchanged</b> — Retained free water lowers osmolarity, so it can't be unchanged.</li><li><b>ECF ↑, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑</b> — Osmolarity falls in SIADH; it doesn't rise.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SIADH: low serum osmolality, urine osmolality inappropriately high, euvolemic; treat with fluid restriction.</p>","w":["ECF ↓, ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑ is hyperosmotic contraction.","SIADH expands, rather than contracts, the ECF.","Retained free water lowers osmolarity, so it can't be unchanged.","Correct — retained water expands both compartments and dilutes them.","Osmolarity falls in SIADH; it doesn't rise."]},"fp26":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hypertonic saline = hyperosmotic volume expansion: NaCl stays in the ECF, raising its osmolarity and pulling water out of cells (ICF shrinks).</p><p>Work it out: total osmoles before = 44 L × 280 = 12,320 mOsm. Add 1,491 mOsm and 2 L of water: 13,811 mOsm in 46 L → new osmolarity ≈ 300 mOsm/L. ICF osmoles are unchanged (28 × 280 = 7,840), so new ICF volume = 7,840 ÷ 300 ≈ 26.1 L (a ~2 L decrease). ECF ≈ 46 − 26.1 ≈ 19.9 L (an increase of ~3.9 L, more than the 2 L infused). That's why hypertonic saline is used to shrink swollen brain cells in symptomatic hyponatremia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ICF expands because NaCl enters cells</b> — NaCl stays in the ECF and doesn't enter cells.</li><li><b>ECF contracts because water moves into the ICF</b> — Water moves out of the ICF into the ECF, not the reverse.</li><li><b>ICF remains unchanged because NaCl is extracellular</b> — The ICF changes because water moves osmotically even though NaCl stays extracellular.</li><li><b>ECF and ICF both contract because NaCl is lost in urine</b> — Urinary loss isn't the immediate effect after infusion.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypertonic saline: ECF ↑ (more than volume infused), ICF ↓, osmolarity ↑.</p>","w":["NaCl stays in the ECF and doesn't enter cells.","Correct — hypertonic ECF pulls water from the cells, shrinking the ICF.","Water moves out of the ICF into the ECF, not the reverse.","The ICF changes because water moves osmotically even though NaCl stays extracellular.","Urinary loss isn't the immediate effect after infusion."]},"fp27":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Indicator-dilution principle: volume = amount of indicator ÷ final concentration (corrected for any losses).</p><p>If 2.0 g of a marker distributes evenly throughout a compartment and the final concentration is 0.05 g/L, the volume must be 2.0 ÷ 0.05 = 40 L. Markers must stay in the compartment of interest: TBW — D₂O, tritiated water, antipyrine; ECF — inulin, mannitol, sucrose; plasma — Evans blue, radiolabeled albumin. ICF = TBW − ECF; interstitial = ECF − plasma. A TBW of 40 L in a 50-kg woman is 80% — higher than typical, but the question tests the calculation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>10 L</b> — 10 L doesn't fit — 2.0 ÷ 0.05 = 40.</li><li><b>20 L</b> — 20 L would require a final concentration of 0.1 g/L.</li><li><b>25 L</b> — 25 L doesn't fit the arithmetic.</li><li><b>50 L</b> — 50 L equals her body weight in kg, not her total body water.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> TBW (D₂O), ECF (inulin), plasma (Evans blue/RISA).</p>","w":["10 L doesn't fit — 2.0 ÷ 0.05 = 40.","20 L would require a final concentration of 0.1 g/L.","25 L doesn't fit the arithmetic.","Correct — 2.0 g ÷ 0.05 g/L = 40 L.","50 L equals her body weight in kg, not her total body water."]},"fp28":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Albumin is the main source of plasma oncotic pressure (πc); losing it (nephrotic syndrome) weakens the force that holds fluid in capillaries → edema.</p><p>Starling equation: Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pif) − σ(πc − πif)]. Pc pushes fluid out; πc pulls fluid in. Nephrotic syndrome (&gt;3.5 g/day proteinuria) lowers serum albumin and πc, so net filtration rises all along the capillary bed. Edema appears in dependent areas (legs) and loose tissue (periorbital, especially in the morning). Lymphatic flow increases to compensate but is eventually overwhelmed. Liver failure and malnutrition (kwashiorkor) cause edema by the same mechanism.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure</b> — Higher plasma oncotic pressure would hold fluid in the capillaries.</li><li><b>Decreased interstitial hydrostatic pressure</b> — Lower interstitial pressure favors filtration somewhat, but it isn't the main change in nephrotic syndrome.</li><li><b>Increased interstitial colloid osmotic pressure</b> — Increased interstitial oncotic pressure isn't the main mechanism here.</li><li><b>Decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure</b> — Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure would reduce filtration.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Edema causes: ↑Pc (heart failure, venous obstruction), ↓πc (nephrotic, cirrhosis), ↑Kf (inflammation, burns), lymphatic obstruction.</p>","w":["Higher plasma oncotic pressure would hold fluid in the capillaries.","Lower interstitial pressure favors filtration somewhat, but it isn't the main change in nephrotic syndrome.","Correct — albumin loss lowers πc, so less fluid is held in the capillaries.","Increased interstitial oncotic pressure isn't the main mechanism here.","Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure would reduce filtration."]},"fp29":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cirrhotic ascites = ↑Pc (portal hypertension) + ↓πc (low albumin synthesis), both increasing net filtration.</p><p>Scarred liver tissue obstructs portal flow, raising hydrostatic pressure in splanchnic capillaries and hepatic sinusoids. The failing liver also makes less albumin, lowering πc. Fluid filters into the peritoneal cavity faster than lymphatics can remove it. Splanchnic vasodilation (NO) lowers effective arterial volume, activating RAAS and ADH, which retain more Na⁺ and water, perpetuating ascites. SAAG ≥1.1 g/dL indicates portal hypertension.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased Pc and increased πc</b> — Decreased Pc and increased πc would both reduce filtration.</li><li><b>Increased Pc and increased πc</b> — Raised πc would oppose ascites.</li><li><b>Decreased Pc and decreased πif</b> — Portal pressure is increased, not decreased.</li><li><b>Decreased Pc and decreased πc</b> — Pc is increased in cirrhosis, not decreased.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SAAG ≥1.1 → portal hypertension (cirrhosis, heart failure); &lt;1.1 → peritoneal causes (cancer, TB, nephrotic).</p>","w":["Correct — portal hypertension raises Pc, and low albumin lowers πc.","Decreased Pc and increased πc would both reduce filtration.","Raised πc would oppose ascites.","Portal pressure is increased, not decreased.","Pc is increased in cirrhosis, not decreased."]},"fp30":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Cytotoxic (cellular) edema: ATP depletion → Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase failure → intracellular Na⁺ ↑ → water enters cells.</p><p>Neurons and astrocytes have high ATP demands, so ischemia rapidly stops the Na⁺/K⁺ pump. Na⁺ (with Cl⁻) accumulates intracellularly, raising intracellular osmolarity, and water follows, swelling cells within minutes (cytotoxic edema, seen as restricted diffusion on MRI-DWI). Later, the blood–brain barrier breaks down, allowing protein and fluid into the interstitium (vasogenic edema), which peaks around 3–5 days and can cause herniation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ATP depletion → decreased intracellular Na⁺ → water leaves cells → cellular shrinkage</b> — Na⁺ rises inside cells when the pump fails; it doesn't fall.</li><li><b>ATP depletion → increased extracellular K⁺ uptake → decreased intracellular osmotic pressure → water leaves cells</b> — K⁺ isn't taken up when the pump fails; it leaks out.</li><li><b>ATP depletion → decreased intracellular osmolarity → water enters cells</b> — Intracellular osmolarity rises, not falls.</li><li><b>ATP depletion → increased plasma oncotic pressure → water enters cells</b> — Plasma oncotic pressure isn't involved in cellular edema.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Cytotoxic edema (minutes, DWI bright) vs vasogenic edema (BBB breakdown, tumors, days).</p>","w":["Na⁺ rises inside cells when the pump fails; it doesn't fall.","Correct — pump failure → Na⁺ builds up inside → osmotic water entry.","K⁺ isn't taken up when the pump fails; it leaks out.","Intracellular osmolarity rises, not falls.","Plasma oncotic pressure isn't involved in cellular edema."]},"fp31":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The resting membrane potential is dominated by K⁺ leaking out through K⁺ leak (K2P) channels, pulling Vm toward EK (≈ −94 mV).</p><p>K⁺ is concentrated inside (140 vs 4 mEq/L), so it tends to diffuse out. Each K⁺ that leaves carries positive charge out, leaving behind impermeant anions (proteins, phosphates), so the inside becomes negative. This negativity pulls K⁺ back in; at EK, the electrical and chemical forces balance. EK = 61 log (4/140) ≈ −94 mV. Because resting membranes are far more permeable to K⁺ than to Na⁺, the resting potential sits close to EK.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Inward diffusion of Na⁺ through voltage-gated channels</b> — Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are closed at rest.</li><li><b>Inward diffusion of Cl⁻ through ligand-gated channels</b> — Cl⁻ isn't the main determinant here.</li><li><b>Active transport of K⁺ into the cell by Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase</b> — The pump maintains the gradient, but the K⁺ leak current generates the voltage.</li><li><b>Outward diffusion of Na⁺ through voltage-gated channels</b> — Na⁺ moves inward, not outward, and voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are closed at rest.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nernst: E = 61/z × log([out]/[in]).</p>","w":["Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are closed at rest.","Correct — K⁺ leaking out through leak channels drives Vm toward EK.","Cl⁻ isn't the main determinant here.","The pump maintains the gradient, but the K⁺ leak current generates the voltage.","Na⁺ moves inward, not outward, and voltage-gated Na⁺ channels are closed at rest."]},"fp32":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hypokalemia makes EK more negative because the K⁺ concentration gradient (in/out) becomes larger.</p><p>EK = 61 log([K⁺]out/[K⁺]in). With [K⁺]out = 4: EK ≈ −94 mV. With [K⁺]out = 2.5: EK = 61 log(2.5/140) ≈ −107 mV. A steeper gradient means K⁺ is pushed out more strongly, so a more negative voltage is required to balance it. Cells hyperpolarize, moving away from threshold → muscle weakness, ileus. In the heart, hypokalemia slows repolarization (U waves, flattened T waves, arrhythmias) and increases digoxin toxicity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The K⁺ chemical gradient becomes smaller, reducing K⁺ efflux</b> — Lower extracellular K⁺ makes the gradient larger, not smaller.</li><li><b>The Na⁺ chemical gradient reverses, increasing Na⁺ efflux</b> — The Na⁺ gradient doesn't reverse in hypokalemia.</li><li><b>The intracellular K⁺ concentration falls to equal extracellular K⁺</b> — Intracellular K⁺ stays around 140 mEq/L.</li><li><b>The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase stops contributing to membrane potential</b> — The pump still operates.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypokalemia ECG: flat T waves, U waves, ST depression.</p>","w":["Lower extracellular K⁺ makes the gradient larger, not smaller.","Correct — a steeper gradient makes EK more negative.","The Na⁺ gradient doesn't reverse in hypokalemia.","Intracellular K⁺ stays around 140 mEq/L.","The pump still operates."]},"fp33":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hyperkalemia makes EK less negative → resting depolarization → Na⁺ channels stuck in the inactivated state → reduced excitability (weakness, paralysis).</p><p>Raising [K⁺]out reduces the K⁺ gradient, so EK and the resting potential become less negative. Mild depolarization brings cells closer to threshold (initially more excitable). Sustained depolarization, however, keeps the inactivation gates of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels closed, so fewer channels are available to open, action potentials weaken or fail, and muscles become weak. In the heart: peaked T waves → prolonged PR, widened QRS → sine waves → ventricular fibrillation or asystole. Treatment: IV calcium (stabilizes membranes), insulin + glucose and β₂-agonists (shift K⁺ into cells), and K⁺ removal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased extracellular K⁺ → more negative EK → hyperpolarization → increased Na⁺ channel availability</b> — Hyperkalemia makes EK less negative, not more, and causes depolarization.</li><li><b>Increased extracellular K⁺ → unchanged EK → hyperpolarization → reduced K⁺ efflux</b> — EK does change when extracellular K⁺ changes.</li><li><b>Increased extracellular K⁺ → more positive EK → hyperpolarization → increased Na⁺ channel activation</b> — EK becomes less negative, but the membrane depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes.</li><li><b>Increased extracellular K⁺ → decreased intracellular K⁺ → increased Na⁺ permeability → membrane repolarization</b> — Intracellular K⁺ stays about the same, and the membrane depolarizes rather than repolarizing.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyperkalemia: peaked T → wide QRS → sine wave; give calcium gluconate first.</p>","w":["Correct — depolarization keeps Na⁺ channels inactivated.","Hyperkalemia makes EK less negative, not more, and causes depolarization.","EK does change when extracellular K⁺ changes.","EK becomes less negative, but the membrane depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes.","Intracellular K⁺ stays about the same, and the membrane depolarizes rather than repolarizing."]},"fp34":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> RMP builds in three steps: K⁺ efflux (≈ −94 mV) → small Na⁺ influx (≈ −86 mV) → electrogenic Na⁺/K⁺ pump (≈ −90 mV).</p><p>With only K⁺ channels open, Vm = EK. Adding a little Na⁺ permeability lets some Na⁺ enter (driven by both its concentration gradient and the negative interior), adding positive charge and shifting Vm toward ENa — but only slightly, because PNa is low. Finally, the pump exports 3 Na⁺ for every 2 K⁺ imported, generating a small outward current that makes the inside ~4 mV more negative.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>K⁺ influx → Na⁺ efflux → pump-mediated K⁺ efflux</b> — K⁺ leaves the cell (efflux); it doesn't enter.</li><li><b>K⁺ influx → Na⁺ influx → pump-mediated K⁺ extrusion</b> — The first step is K⁺ efflux, and the pump extrudes Na⁺.</li><li><b>Na⁺ efflux → K⁺ influx → electrogenic Na⁺ influx</b> — This reverses the real directions.</li><li><b>Cl⁻ influx → K⁺ efflux → Na⁺ extrusion</b> — Cl⁻ influx isn't the first step.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pump contributes only a few mV directly; its main role is maintaining gradients.</p>","w":["K⁺ leaves the cell (efflux); it doesn't enter.","The first step is K⁺ efflux, and the pump extrudes Na⁺.","This reverses the real directions.","Cl⁻ influx isn't the first step.","Correct — K⁺ efflux, then small Na⁺ influx, then the electrogenic pump."]},"fp35":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Goldman (GHK) equation: Vm is a weighted average of ion equilibrium potentials; increasing an ion's permeability pulls Vm toward its equilibrium potential.</p><p>At rest, PK ≫ PNa, so Vm sits near EK. Increasing PNa gives Na⁺ more weight, so Vm moves toward ENa (+61 mV) and becomes less negative (depolarizes). This is exactly what happens during an action potential, when Na⁺ channels open and PNa rises transiently to dominate, pushing Vm toward ENa. Conversely, opening more K⁺ or Cl⁻ channels moves Vm toward EK or ECl (hyperpolarization or stabilization, as with inhibitory synapses).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Vm moves toward EK and becomes more negative</b> — More Na⁺ permeability pulls Vm toward ENa, not EK.</li><li><b>Vm moves toward ECl and becomes more negative</b> — Cl⁻ permeability is unchanged.</li><li><b>Vm becomes independent of all ion concentration gradients</b> — Vm depends on both gradients and permeabilities.</li><li><b>Vm becomes equal to zero because Na⁺ permeability dominates all electrical forces</b> — Na⁺ doesn't become totally dominant, and Vm doesn't go to zero.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Vm moves toward the equilibrium potential of the most permeable ion.</p>","w":["More Na⁺ permeability pulls Vm toward ENa, not EK.","Correct — raising Na⁺ permeability shifts Vm toward ENa.","Cl⁻ permeability is unchanged.","Vm depends on both gradients and permeabilities.","Na⁺ doesn't become totally dominant, and Vm doesn't go to zero."]},"fp36":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An ion's equilibrium (Nernst) potential depends only on its concentration ratio (and temperature, valence) — not on how many channels are open.</p><p>Adding K⁺ channels increases K⁺ conductance, so K⁺ current is larger whenever Vm ≠ EK, and K⁺ has more influence on Vm (pulling it closer to EK). But EK itself is unchanged because [K⁺]in and [K⁺]out haven't changed. Remember: concentration gradients set the equilibrium potentials; permeabilities set how strongly each ion influences the actual membrane potential.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>EK becomes more negative</b> — EK depends only on the concentration ratio, which hasn't changed.</li><li><b>EK becomes more positive</b> — EK doesn't move, so it doesn't become more positive.</li><li><b>EK becomes equal to ENa</b> — EK and ENa depend on different ions' gradients.</li><li><b>EK becomes zero because permeability has increased</b> — Permeability doesn't set the equilibrium potential.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Concentrations → equilibrium potential; permeabilities → Vm (GHK).</p>","w":["EK depends only on the concentration ratio, which hasn't changed.","EK doesn't move, so it doesn't become more positive.","Correct — more channels mean more K⁺ current, but EK is unchanged.","EK and ENa depend on different ions' gradients.","Permeability doesn't set the equilibrium potential."]},"fp37":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is electrogenic (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in = one net positive charge out per cycle), contributing a few mV of negativity.</p><p>When the pump is blocked, this outward current stops immediately, so Vm becomes slightly less negative (about 4 mV). Over a longer time, gradients run down (Na⁺ in, K⁺ out), causing further depolarization. Digoxin and ouabain inhibit the pump; in heart failure, the inotropic benefit comes from the secondary rise in intracellular Ca²⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Reduced outward movement of K⁺ through leak channels</b> — K⁺ leak continues; it isn't what the pump contributes.</li><li><b>Increased outward movement of Na⁺ through voltage-gated channels</b> — Na⁺ doesn't flow outward through channels.</li><li><b>Increased inward movement of Cl⁻ through ligand-gated channels</b> — Cl⁻ isn't involved.</li><li><b>Increased active transport of Na⁺ out of the cell</b> — Pump inhibition reduces, not increases, active Na⁺ transport.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pump: direct ≈ −4 mV; indirect = maintaining gradients.</p>","w":["K⁺ leak continues; it isn't what the pump contributes.","Correct — losing the pump's net outward positive current depolarizes slightly.","Na⁺ doesn't flow outward through channels.","Cl⁻ isn't involved.","Pump inhibition reduces, not increases, active Na⁺ transport."]},"fp38":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ischemic cellular swelling: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase failure → Na⁺ accumulates inside → intracellular osmolarity rises → water enters.</p><p>Even with an intact membrane, the cell swells because osmotically active Na⁺ (with Cl⁻) builds up inside. This is reversible injury if blood flow is restored quickly. In skeletal muscle compartments, swelling can raise pressure within fascia, impair perfusion further, and lead to compartment syndrome; reperfusion can worsen this.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased extracellular osmolarity causes water to leave the cell</b> — Water moves into, not out of, the swelling cells.</li><li><b>Increased intracellular K⁺ concentration decreases intracellular osmolarity</b> — Intracellular K⁺ falls, not rises.</li><li><b>Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure forces water into the cell</b> — Plasma oncotic pressure doesn't push water into cells.</li><li><b>Reduced intracellular osmolarity causes water to enter the cell</b> — Intracellular osmolarity rises, not falls.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Reversible injury: cell swelling; irreversible: membrane rupture, Ca²⁺ influx, nuclear changes.</p>","w":["Water moves into, not out of, the swelling cells.","Correct — trapped Na⁺ raises intracellular osmolarity and water follows.","Intracellular K⁺ falls, not rises.","Plasma oncotic pressure doesn't push water into cells.","Intracellular osmolarity rises, not falls."]},"fp39":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Digoxin mechanism: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition → ↑[Na⁺]i → smaller Na⁺ gradient → less Ca²⁺ extrusion by NCX → ↑[Ca²⁺]i → ↑contractility.</p><p>NCX uses the inward Na⁺ gradient to pump Ca²⁺ out. When intracellular Na⁺ rises, this gradient shrinks, so less Ca²⁺ is removed each beat. More Ca²⁺ is taken up into the SR and released in subsequent beats, increasing contractility (positive inotropy). Digoxin also increases vagal tone, slowing AV nodal conduction (useful in atrial fibrillation). Toxicity: nausea, visual changes (yellow vision), arrhythmias; worsened by hypokalemia; treat with anti-digoxin Fab.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased Na⁺ gradient enhances Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchange</b> — The Na⁺ gradient is reduced, not increased.</li><li><b>Increased K⁺ gradient reverses Ca²⁺ diffusion</b> — K⁺ doesn't reverse Ca²⁺ diffusion.</li><li><b>Increased Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase activity increases intracellular Na⁺</b> — Pump activity is inhibited, not increased.</li><li><b>Decreased intracellular Na⁺ increases Ca²⁺ entry through voltage-gated channels</b> — Intracellular Na⁺ rises, not falls.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Digoxin toxicity ↑ with hypokalemia (less K⁺ competing for the pump).</p>","w":["The Na⁺ gradient is reduced, not increased.","Correct — a smaller Na⁺ gradient slows Ca²⁺ removal by NCX.","K⁺ doesn't reverse Ca²⁺ diffusion.","Pump activity is inhibited, not increased.","Intracellular Na⁺ rises, not falls."]},"fp40":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ENa = 61 log([Na⁺]out/[Na⁺]in); raising [Na⁺]in shrinks the gradient and makes ENa less positive. The actual Vm changes little because PNa is low at rest.</p><p>The Nernst potential describes a single ion's balance point. The resting membrane potential is determined by all permeable ions weighted by permeability (GHK). Because resting PNa is small compared with PK, changing ENa has only a modest effect on resting Vm, which stays near its normal value. However, a smaller Na⁺ gradient would reduce the size of the action potential overshoot and weaken Na⁺-coupled transport.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The Nernst potential for Na⁺ will become more positive because intracellular Na⁺ increased</b> — A smaller Na⁺ gradient makes ENa less positive, not more.</li><li><b>The Nernst potential for K⁺ will become more positive because intracellular Na⁺ increased</b> — Intracellular Na⁺ doesn't change EK.</li><li><b>The resting membrane potential will necessarily become equal to EK</b> — Vm reflects several ions weighted by permeability; it isn't forced to EK.</li><li><b>The resting membrane potential is independent of ion concentration gradients</b> — Vm depends on concentration gradients.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nernst = single ion; GHK = several ions + permeabilities.</p>","w":["A smaller Na⁺ gradient makes ENa less positive, not more.","Intracellular Na⁺ doesn't change EK.","Correct — a smaller Na⁺ ratio means a less positive ENa.","Vm reflects several ions weighted by permeability; it isn't forced to EK.","Vm depends on concentration gradients."]},"fp41":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Vm moves toward the equilibrium potential of whichever ion's permeability increases; more PNa → Vm moves toward ENa (depolarization).</p><p>At rest, PK is ~100× PNa, so Vm (−90 mV) sits near EK (−94 mV). Raising PNa increases Na⁺'s weight in the GHK equation. Na⁺ has a large inward driving force (Vm far below ENa), so Na⁺ enters, carrying positive charge in and making the inside less negative. Concentration gradients haven't changed, so ENa and EK are unchanged — only Vm moves. This is the basis of excitatory postsynaptic potentials and the action potential upstroke.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The membrane potential becomes more negative and approaches the K⁺ equilibrium potential</b> — More Na⁺ permeability moves Vm away from EK, not toward it.</li><li><b>The membrane potential remains unchanged because ion concentration gradients determine membrane voltage exclusively</b> — Permeability, not just concentration, determines Vm.</li><li><b>The membrane potential becomes more negative because increased Na⁺ permeability increases Na⁺ efflux</b> — Na⁺ moves inward and depolarizes; it doesn't flow outward.</li><li><b>The membrane potential becomes exactly 0 mV because Na⁺ and K⁺ concentrations become equal</b> — The concentrations don't change, and Vm doesn't go to 0 mV.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ↑PNa → depolarize; ↑PK or ↑PCl → hyperpolarize or stabilize.</p>","w":["More Na⁺ permeability moves Vm away from EK, not toward it.","Correct — Na⁺ enters and Vm shifts toward ENa.","Permeability, not just concentration, determines Vm.","Na⁺ moves inward and depolarizes; it doesn't flow outward.","The concentrations don't change, and Vm doesn't go to 0 mV."]},"fp42":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ionic current I = g(Vm − E). For Na⁺ at −70 mV: −70 − (+65) = −135 mV → large negative (inward) current → depolarization.</p><p>Sign convention: a negative current means positive charge flowing into the cell (inward current), which depolarizes. The driving force tells you direction and strength; conductance (number of open channels) tells you how much current flows. Na⁺ always enters a cell whenever Vm is below ENa. K⁺, with EK ≈ −94 mV, leaves the cell whenever Vm is above EK (outward current, repolarizing).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Na⁺ moves outward and hyperpolarizes the membrane</b> — Vm − ENa is negative, so the Na⁺ current is inward, not outward.</li><li><b>Na⁺ moves outward and depolarizes the membrane</b> — Na⁺ moves inward, not outward.</li><li><b>Na⁺ moves inward and hyperpolarizes the membrane</b> — An inward cation current depolarizes, not hyperpolarizes, the membrane.</li><li><b>Na⁺ has no net movement because Vm is below ENa</b> — Vm being below ENa is exactly why Na⁺ moves inward.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Driving force = Vm − Eion; inward cation current = depolarizing.</p>","w":["Vm − ENa is negative, so the Na⁺ current is inward, not outward.","Na⁺ moves inward, not outward.","Correct — a negative driving force means inward Na⁺ current and depolarization.","An inward cation current depolarizes, not hyperpolarizes, the membrane.","Vm being below ENa is exactly why Na⁺ moves inward."]},"fp43":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The action potential upstroke depends on rapid opening of voltage-gated Na⁺ activation (m) gates — a positive feedback loop.</p><p>Once threshold is reached, depolarization opens some Na⁺ channels → Na⁺ enters → further depolarization → more channels open (Hodgkin cycle). This explosive, regenerative Na⁺ influx drives Vm toward ENa (overshoot to ~+35 mV). If activation gates can't open quickly, there's no regenerative current and no upstroke. Local anesthetics (lidocaine) and tetrodotoxin block Na⁺ channels with the same result — no action potential.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Prolongation of the hyperpolarizing undershoot</b> — The hyperpolarizing undershoot depends on K⁺ channels, which are intact.</li><li><b>Faster repolarization caused by increased Na⁺ entry</b> — Na⁺ entry is decreased, not increased.</li><li><b>Normal depolarization followed by prolonged repolarization</b> — Depolarization itself is impaired.</li><li><b>Increased overshoot caused by enhanced K⁺ permeability</b> — The overshoot depends on Na⁺ influx, not K⁺ permeability.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> TTX, saxitoxin, lidocaine → block Na⁺ channels → no AP.</p>","w":["Correct — without rapid Na⁺ activation there's no upstroke.","The hyperpolarizing undershoot depends on K⁺ channels, which are intact.","Na⁺ entry is decreased, not increased.","Depolarization itself is impaired.","The overshoot depends on Na⁺ influx, not K⁺ permeability."]},"fp44":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Na⁺ channel inactivation (h-gate closure) ends the Na⁺ current and allows repolarization; blocking inactivation prolongs the action potential.</p><p>Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open rapidly and then, about a millisecond later, the inactivation gate plugs the pore. This terminates the inward current so K⁺ efflux can repolarize the membrane. If inactivation fails, Na⁺ keeps entering, depolarization persists, and repolarization is delayed. Toxins that block inactivation (batrachotoxin, some scorpion toxins, ciguatoxin) cause prolonged depolarization and repetitive firing. Inherited defects cause hyperkalemic periodic paralysis, paramyotonia and long-QT syndrome type 3.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Premature termination of the depolarizing phase</b> — Depolarization lasts longer, not shorter.</li><li><b>Immediate hyperpolarization caused by excessive K⁺ entry</b> — K⁺ moves outward, and this drug doesn't affect K⁺ channels.</li><li><b>Complete loss of Na⁺ conductance during the action potential</b> — Na⁺ conductance continues rather than being lost.</li><li><b>Shortening of the absolute refractory period due to rapid Na⁺ recovery</b> — Without inactivation, the refractory period is prolonged, not shortened.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Na⁺ channel states: resting (closed) → open → inactivated → resting (after repolarization).</p>","w":["Depolarization lasts longer, not shorter.","Correct — Na⁺ current persists, so repolarization is delayed.","K⁺ moves outward, and this drug doesn't affect K⁺ channels.","Na⁺ conductance continues rather than being lost.","Without inactivation, the refractory period is prolonged, not shortened."]},"fp45":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Repolarization depends on delayed-rectifier voltage-gated K⁺ channels; blocking them leaves the membrane depolarized longer.</p><p>Voltage-gated K⁺ channels open more slowly than Na⁺ channels. As Na⁺ channels inactivate, outward K⁺ current returns Vm toward rest (and briefly beyond, causing the undershoot). Blocking K⁺ channels (e.g., tetraethylammonium, 4-aminopyridine) reduces this outward current, prolonging the AP. 4-AP (dalfampridine) improves conduction in multiple sclerosis by prolonging APs in demyelinated axons; class III antiarrhythmics (amiodarone, sotalol) block cardiac K⁺ channels, prolonging the QT interval.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Persistent inward Na⁺ current caused by failure of Na⁺ activation</b> — Na⁺ activation is normal; the problem is repolarization.</li><li><b>Increased outward Na⁺ current during the overshoot</b> — Na⁺ moves inward, not outward.</li><li><b>Increased K⁺ conductance during the resting state</b> — K⁺ conductance is blocked, not increased.</li><li><b>Premature closure of Na⁺ activation gates</b> — Na⁺ gates behave normally.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> K⁺ channel blockers → prolonged AP (dalfampridine in MS; class III antiarrhythmics → ↑QT).</p>","w":["Na⁺ activation is normal; the problem is repolarization.","Correct — less outward K⁺ current slows repolarization.","Na⁺ moves inward, not outward.","K⁺ conductance is blocked, not increased.","Na⁺ gates behave normally."]},"fp46":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> During the absolute refractory period, Na⁺ channels are inactivated, so no stimulus of any strength can trigger another action potential.</p><p>From the upstroke until partway through repolarization, Na⁺ channel inactivation gates are closed. They reset only after the membrane repolarizes. During the relative refractory period that follows, some Na⁺ channels have recovered but K⁺ conductance is still high, so a stronger-than-normal stimulus can produce a smaller AP. Refractoriness limits maximal firing frequency and ensures one-way propagation (the region just behind the AP can't be re-excited).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Persistent elevation of K⁺ conductance during the undershoot</b> — The undershoot comes later and would correspond to the relative, not absolute, refractory period.</li><li><b>Failure of the Na⁺ concentration gradient to exist</b> — The Na⁺ concentration gradient is still present.</li><li><b>Increased membrane resistance preventing current spread</b> — Membrane resistance isn't what blocks a second action potential.</li><li><b>Closure of all K⁺ leak channels</b> — K⁺ leak channels don't close.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Absolute refractory = Na⁺ inactivation; relative refractory = ↑K⁺ conductance + partial Na⁺ recovery.</p>","w":["The undershoot comes later and would correspond to the relative, not absolute, refractory period.","The Na⁺ concentration gradient is still present.","Correct — in the absolute refractory period Na⁺ channels are inactivated.","Membrane resistance isn't what blocks a second action potential.","K⁺ leak channels don't close."]},"fp47":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Myelin raises membrane resistance and lowers capacitance; demyelination lets current leak out and shortens the length constant, slowing or blocking conduction.</p><p>In myelinated axons, current generated at one node flows passively down the axon to depolarize the next node (saltatory conduction). With myelin lost, membrane resistance falls (current leaks out) and capacitance rises (more charge is needed to change voltage), so the local current decays before reaching threshold at the next node. Conduction slows or fails (conduction block). Examples: Guillain–Barré syndrome and CIDP (peripheral), multiple sclerosis (central). Nerve conduction studies show slowed velocity and prolonged latencies.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Reduced current leakage and increased distance constant</b> — Demyelination increases, not reduces, current leakage, and decreases λ.</li><li><b>Increased membrane resistance and reduced capacitance</b> — Membrane resistance falls and capacitance rises with demyelination.</li><li><b>Reduced internal resistance caused by loss of myelin</b> — Internal (axial) resistance isn't the main change.</li><li><b>Increased saltatory conduction between adjacent nodes</b> — Saltatory conduction fails rather than increasing.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Myelin: ↑Rm, ↓Cm → ↑λ, ↓τ → faster conduction.</p>","w":["Correct — lower Rm lets current leak, shortening λ and slowing conduction.","Demyelination increases, not reduces, current leakage, and decreases λ.","Membrane resistance falls and capacitance rises with demyelination.","Internal (axial) resistance isn't the main change.","Saltatory conduction fails rather than increasing."]},"fp48":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hyperkalemia → smaller K⁺ gradient → resting depolarization → sustained Na⁺ channel inactivation → fewer APs → weakness.</p><p>Serum K⁺ 6.8 raises EK (less negative), so the resting potential becomes less negative. Although this initially makes cells more excitable, persistent depolarization keeps many Na⁺ channels inactivated, so action potentials can't be generated reliably. Muscles become weak (and cardiac conduction slows). Causes include renal failure, ACE inhibitors, K⁺-sparing diuretics, rhabdomyolysis, and acidosis.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hyperkalemia increases K⁺ efflux and produces membrane hyperpolarization</b> — Hyperkalemia depolarizes, rather than hyperpolarizes, the membrane.</li><li><b>Hyperkalemia increases Na⁺ entry and keeps Na⁺ channels continuously available</b> — Na⁺ channels become inactivated, not more available.</li><li><b>Hyperkalemia increases extracellular Ca²⁺ and shifts Na⁺ threshold to more positive values</b> — Hyperkalemia doesn't raise extracellular Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>Hyperkalemia directly blocks voltage-gated K⁺ channels and prevents repolarizatio</b> — K⁺ doesn't directly block K⁺ channels.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyperkalemia: depolarization → Na⁺ channel inactivation → weakness, arrhythmia.</p>","w":["Hyperkalemia depolarizes, rather than hyperpolarizes, the membrane.","Na⁺ channels become inactivated, not more available.","Correct — a smaller K⁺ gradient causes sustained depolarization with Na⁺ channel inactivation.","Hyperkalemia doesn't raise extracellular Ca²⁺.","K⁺ doesn't directly block K⁺ channels."]},"fp49":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Low extracellular Ca²⁺ lowers the Na⁺ channel activation threshold (more negative) → neurons fire spontaneously → tetany.</p><p>Extracellular Ca²⁺ binds to negative charges on the outer surface of Na⁺ channels, stabilizing them (raising the voltage needed to open them). When Ca²⁺ falls, a smaller depolarization opens Na⁺ channels, so nerves fire spontaneously and repetitively. Signs: perioral and fingertip tingling, muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, Chvostek sign (facial twitch on tapping CN VII) and Trousseau sign (carpal spasm with BP cuff), laryngospasm, seizures and prolonged QT. Hypercalcemia does the reverse — reduced excitability, weakness, constipation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The threshold for Na⁺ channel activation becomes more positive</b> — A more positive threshold describes hypercalcemia (membrane stabilization).</li><li><b>The K⁺ equilibrium potential becomes markedly more positive</b> — EK isn't markedly changed by Ca²⁺.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated Na⁺ channels remain permanently inactivated</b> — Channels fire excessively rather than staying inactivated.</li><li><b>Increased membrane capacitance prevents neuronal depolarization</b> — Capacitance isn't the issue.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypocalcemia: Chvostek, Trousseau, prolonged QT; causes include hypoparathyroidism (post-thyroidectomy), vitamin D deficiency, alkalosis.</p>","w":["Correct — low Ca²⁺ lowers the activation threshold, causing hyperexcitability.","A more positive threshold describes hypercalcemia (membrane stabilization).","EK isn't markedly changed by Ca²⁺.","Channels fire excessively rather than staying inactivated.","Capacitance isn't the issue."]},"fp50":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Length constant λ = √(rm/ri): lower membrane resistance (demyelination) → shorter λ → local potentials decay faster with distance.</p><p>The length constant is the distance over which a passive voltage change falls to 37% of its starting value. A larger λ means current spreads farther before fading. Myelin increases rm, so current spreads to the next node. With demyelination, rm falls; current escapes across the membrane, λ shortens, and the depolarization may be too small to reach threshold at the next node. The time constant (τ = rm·cm) also changes, but λ is the key concept for spatial spread.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased length constant with less decay of local potential</b> — Lower rm decreases, not increases, λ.</li><li><b>Increased time constant caused by increased membrane resistance</b> — Lower Rm shortens, rather than lengthens, the time constant (τ = Rm·Cm).</li><li><b>Increased conduction velocity because current crosses the membrane more easily</b> — Conduction slows when current leaks out of the membrane.</li><li><b>Increased saltatory conduction because more membrane is exposed</b> — Saltatory conduction requires myelin, so it decreases.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Conduction velocity ↑ with ↑ axon diameter (↓ri) and myelination (↑rm, ↓cm).</p>","w":["Lower rm decreases, not increases, λ.","Correct — lower rm means a shorter λ and faster decay of local current.","Lower Rm shortens, rather than lengthens, the time constant (τ = Rm·Cm).","Conduction slows when current leaks out of the membrane.","Saltatory conduction requires myelin, so it decreases."]},"fp51":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Total body water ≈ 60% of body weight in an adult man: 70 kg × 0.6 = 42 L (ICF ≈ 28 L, ECF ≈ 14 L).</p><p>The 60-40-20 rule: TBW 60% of weight, ICF 40%, ECF 20%. ECF splits into interstitial fluid (~3/4, about 10.5 L) and plasma (~1/4, about 3.5 L). Blood volume is about 7% of body weight (~5 L) because it includes cells. Percentages fall with more body fat (women ~50–55%, elderly) and rise in infants (~75%).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>21 liters</b> — 21 L is only 30% of body weight — far too low for a healthy adult.</li><li><b>35 liters</b> — 35 L is 50% of body weight, closer to an adult woman or an elderly person.</li><li><b>50 liters</b> — 50 L is about 71% of body weight — seen only in newborns.</li><li><b>60 liters</b> — 60 L would be about 86% of body weight — not physiologically possible.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 60-40-20: TBW-ICF-ECF as % of body weight.</p>","w":["21 L is only 30% of body weight — far too low for a healthy adult.","35 L is 50% of body weight, closer to an adult woman or an elderly person.","Correct — 70 kg × 60% ≈ 42 L.","50 L is about 71% of body weight — seen only in newborns.","60 L would be about 86% of body weight — not physiologically possible."]},"fp52":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ICF is rich in K⁺, Mg²⁺, proteins and organic phosphates; ECF is rich in Na⁺, Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻.</p><p>The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and selective membrane permeability maintain these differences. Typical values: ICF K⁺ ~140 mEq/L, ECF K⁺ ~4; ECF Na⁺ ~142, ICF Na⁺ ~10–14. Intracellular free Ca²⁺ (~0.0001 mM) is about 10,000-fold lower than extracellular (~2.4 mM ionized ~1.2 mM), allowing Ca²⁺ to act as a signal. Intracellular proteins and phosphates are the main anions inside cells. These gradients drive membrane potentials, excitability and secondary active transport.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ICF has high sodium and chloride concentrations, similar to ECF.</b> — High sodium and chloride describe the ECF, not the ICF.</li><li><b>ICF is less acidic and has a higher pH than ECF.</b> — The ICF is slightly more acidic (pH ~7.0–7.2) than ECF (pH 7.4).</li><li><b>ICF has a fourfold higher calcium concentration than ECF.</b> — Free calcium inside cells is roughly 10,000 times lower than outside.</li><li><b>ICF and ECF have identical ionic compositions due to rapid exchange across cell membranes.</b> — Only water crosses membranes freely; ion compositions differ markedly.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inside: K⁺, Mg²⁺, PO₄³⁻, proteins. Outside: Na⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, Ca²⁺.</p>","w":["High sodium and chloride describe the ECF, not the ICF.","Correct — ICF has high K⁺, Mg²⁺, protein and phosphate.","The ICF is slightly more acidic (pH ~7.0–7.2) than ECF (pH 7.4).","Free calcium inside cells is roughly 10,000 times lower than outside.","Only water crosses membranes freely; ion compositions differ markedly."]},"fp53":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Inulin crosses capillaries but not cell membranes, so it distributes throughout the ECF (plasma + interstitial fluid).</p><p>Choose markers by where they can go. Total body water: D₂O, tritiated water, antipyrine (cross all membranes). ECF: inulin, mannitol, sucrose, ²²Na, ³⁶Cl, iothalamate, thiosulfate (cross capillaries, not cells). Plasma: Evans blue, ¹²⁵I-albumin (stay in vessels). RBC volume: ⁵¹Cr-labeled RBCs. Compartments without a direct marker are calculated: ICF = TBW − ECF; interstitial = ECF − plasma. Inulin is also the gold-standard GFR marker because it is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Radioactive water (tritium)</b> — Tritiated water spreads into every compartment, so it measures total body water.</li><li><b>Evans blue dye (T-1824)</b> — Evans blue binds plasma albumin and stays in vessels, measuring plasma volume.</li><li><b>Antipyrine</b> — Antipyrine crosses cell membranes and measures total body water.</li><li><b>Radioactive chromium (51Cr)-labeled red blood cells</b> — ⁵¹Cr-labeled RBCs stay in the circulation and measure red cell volume.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Inulin: ECF volume and GFR.</p>","w":["Tritiated water spreads into every compartment, so it measures total body water.","Evans blue binds plasma albumin and stays in vessels, measuring plasma volume.","Antipyrine crosses cell membranes and measures total body water.","Correct — inulin measures ECF volume.","⁵¹Cr-labeled RBCs stay in the circulation and measure red cell volume."]},"fp54":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osmolarity = osmoles per liter of solution (mOsm/L); osmolality = osmoles per kg of water (mOsm/kg H₂O).</p><p>Osmolarity counts all dissolved particles, regardless of type (1 mmol NaCl ≈ 2 mOsm because it dissociates). Plasma osmolality ≈ 2[Na⁺] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8 ≈ 285–295. Tonicity is different: it compares effective osmoles (those that can't cross the membrane) to a cell. Urea contributes to osmolarity but not to tonicity because it crosses membranes. Osmotic pressure is ≈ 19.3 mm Hg per mOsm/L.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Tonicity</b> — Tonicity describes how a solution compares to a cell (hypo/iso/hypertonic) and has no unit.</li><li><b>Osmotic pressure</b> — Osmotic pressure is a pressure, measured in mm Hg.</li><li><b>Reflection coefficient</b> — The reflection coefficient is a unitless number (0–1) describing how well a membrane blocks a solute.</li><li><b>Hydration index</b> — \"Hydration index\" isn't a standard physiology term.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Calculated plasma osmolality = 2Na⁺ + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8.</p>","w":["Tonicity describes how a solution compares to a cell (hypo/iso/hypertonic) and has no unit.","Osmotic pressure is a pressure, measured in mm Hg.","The reflection coefficient is a unitless number (0–1) describing how well a membrane blocks a solute.","Correct — mOsm/L defines osmolarity.","\"Hydration index\" isn't a standard physiology term."]},"fp55":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A hypotonic infusion dilutes the ECF, so water moves into cells (ECF → ICF) and cells swell.</p><p>Hypotonic fluids (e.g., 0.45% saline, D5W after glucose is metabolized) lower ECF osmolarity. Water then moves into cells until intracellular and extracellular osmolarities are equal. Final state: ECF volume ↑, ICF volume ↑, osmolarity ↓ in both (hyposmotic expansion). Giving too much hypotonic fluid can cause hyponatremia and brain swelling, especially in children and postoperative patients with high ADH.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Water will flow from the patient's cells into the extracellular fluid.</b> — Water moving out of cells happens with a hypertonic, not hypotonic, infusion.</li><li><b>There will be no net movement of water because the solutions are isotonic.</b> — The solution is hypotonic, not isotonic, so water does move.</li><li><b>The patient's extracellular fluid volume will decrease, and osmolarity will increase.</b> — This is backwards — ECF volume increases and its osmolarity decreases.</li><li><b>The patient's red blood cells will shrink due to water loss.</b> — Red cells swell (and may lyse) in hypotonic fluid; they shrink in hypertonic fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypotonic fluid → cells swell; hypertonic → cells shrink; isotonic → no shift.</p>","w":["Water moving out of cells happens with a hypertonic, not hypotonic, infusion.","Correct — water flows from ECF into cells.","The solution is hypotonic, not isotonic, so water does move.","This is backwards — ECF volume increases and its osmolarity decreases.","Red cells swell (and may lyse) in hypotonic fluid; they shrink in hypertonic fluid."]},"fp56":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hyponatremia = hypotonic ECF → water moves into cells → intracellular edema, most dangerous in the brain.</p><p>Na⁺ is the main extracellular osmole, so low Na⁺ lowers plasma osmolality and water enters cells. Because the skull can't expand, brain swelling raises intracranial pressure: headache, nausea, lethargy, confusion, seizures, coma and herniation. Brain cells adapt over ~48 h by exporting osmolytes; therefore chronic hyponatremia must be corrected slowly (≤8–10 mEq/L in 24 h) to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cell shrinkage due to water movement out of cells.</b> — Cells shrink when the ECF is too concentrated (hypernatremia), not dilute.</li><li><b>Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure.</b> — Colloid osmotic pressure comes from plasma proteins, not sodium.</li><li><b>Decreased total body water with no change in osmolarity.</b> — Osmolarity is low here; total body water is often normal or increased.</li><li><b>Increased interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure.</b> — The problem is water moving into cells, not pressure in the interstitial fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"From low to high, your pons will die\" — correct hyponatremia slowly.</p>","w":["Cells shrink when the ECF is too concentrated (hypernatremia), not dilute.","Colloid osmotic pressure comes from plasma proteins, not sodium.","Correct — low Na⁺ drives water into brain cells.","Osmolarity is low here; total body water is often normal or increased.","The problem is water moving into cells, not pressure in the interstitial fluid."]},"fp57":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Ascites is fluid in a potential space (peritoneal cavity) — transcellular fluid, a specialized part of the ECF.</p><p>Transcellular fluids are separated from plasma by an epithelial layer: peritoneal, pleural, pericardial, synovial, cerebrospinal and intraocular fluids, and GI secretions. Normally they're small (~1–2 L total). Pathological accumulation in potential spaces is an effusion (ascites, pleural effusion, pericardial effusion). In cirrhosis, portal hypertension, low albumin and renal Na⁺ retention drive fluid into the peritoneal cavity.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intracellular fluid</b> — The fluid is outside cells, in a body cavity.</li><li><b>Plasma volume</b> — The fluid has left the blood vessels; plasma volume is often reduced.</li><li><b>Blood volume</b> — Blood volume (plasma + cells) stays inside vessels.</li><li><b>Solid tissue matrix</b> — The solid tissue matrix isn't a fluid compartment.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Transcellular fluid: CSF, pleural, pericardial, peritoneal, synovial, ocular.</p>","w":["The fluid is outside cells, in a body cavity.","The fluid has left the blood vessels; plasma volume is often reduced.","Correct — ascites is transcellular fluid in a potential space.","Blood volume (plasma + cells) stays inside vessels.","The solid tissue matrix isn't a fluid compartment."]},"fp58":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Safety factors against edema ≈ 17 mm Hg: low interstitial compliance (~3), increased lymph flow (~7), protein washdown (~7).</p><p>Capillary pressure must rise by about 17 mm Hg before obvious edema develops. (1) While interstitial pressure is negative, the tissue gel is stiff (low compliance), so small amounts of extra fluid raise interstitial pressure and oppose more filtration. (2) Lymph flow can increase 10- to 50-fold. (3) Extra filtered fluid dilutes interstitial protein, lowering πif and reducing the pull of fluid out of capillaries. Once interstitial pressure becomes positive, compliance increases sharply and fluid accumulates easily (pitting edema).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased compliance of the interstitium at negative interstitial fluid pressures.</b> — Protection comes from low (not increased) compliance at negative interstitial pressure.</li><li><b>Decreased lymph flow from the interstitial spaces.</b> — Lymph flow increases, rather than decreases, as a safety factor.</li><li><b>Increased interstitial fluid protein concentration.</b> — Interstitial protein concentration decreases (washdown), which protects against edema.</li><li><b>Cell membranes becoming impermeable to water during fluid shifts.</b> — Cell membranes remain freely permeable to water.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, cancer surgery) → lymphedema (protein-rich, non-pitting late).</p>","w":["Protection comes from low (not increased) compliance at negative interstitial pressure.","Lymph flow increases, rather than decreases, as a safety factor.","Interstitial protein concentration decreases (washdown), which protects against edema.","Correct — combined safety factor of about 17 mm Hg.","Cell membranes remain freely permeable to water."]},"fp59":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isotonic saline = isosmotic volume expansion: stays in ECF, no osmolarity change, no water shift; plasma protein and hematocrit fall by dilution.</p><p>0.9% NaCl (~308 mOsm/L) has roughly the same effective osmolarity as plasma. Na⁺ is kept out of cells, so the infused fluid stays extracellular. It distributes between plasma (~1/4) and interstitial fluid (~3/4) according to Starling forces. ICF is unchanged. Isotonic crystalloids are therefore used to restore ECF and plasma volume in hypovolemia.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>ECF volume decreases, and ICF volume increases due to water shift.</b> — ECF volume increases, not decreases, and there's no water shift into cells.</li><li><b>ECF and ICF osmolarities both increase.</b> — Isotonic fluid doesn't change osmolarity.</li><li><b>ICF volume decreases, while ECF volume increases, with no change in osmolarity.</b> — Without an osmolarity change, water doesn't leave cells, so ICF is unchanged.</li><li><b>Both plasma protein concentration and hematocrit increase due to concentration.</b> — Dilution lowers plasma protein concentration and hematocrit.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Isotonic saline: only ~25% stays intravascular.</p>","w":["ECF volume increases, not decreases, and there's no water shift into cells.","Correct — ECF expands while plasma protein and hematocrit decrease.","Isotonic fluid doesn't change osmolarity.","Without an osmolarity change, water doesn't leave cells, so ICF is unchanged.","Dilution lowers plasma protein concentration and hematocrit."]},"fp60":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Body fat content is inversely related to percent total body water, because adipose tissue holds little water (~10–20%) versus muscle (~75%).</p><p>Percent TBW differences across people largely reflect body composition: obese people, women and older adults have more fat relative to muscle and so lower percent TBW; lean athletes and infants have higher percent TBW. This affects drug dosing (volume of distribution), fluid replacement calculations and susceptibility to dehydration in the elderly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Age</b> — Percent body water does fall with age, but mainly because of the shift from muscle to fat; body fat is the direct determinant.</li><li><b>Sex (female)</b> — Women have lower percent TBW because of higher body fat; sex is a category, not a quantity that can be inversely proportional.</li><li><b>Lean muscle mass</b> — Muscle is water-rich, so more muscle means more body water.</li><li><b>Hydration status</b> — Better hydration means more body water — a direct relationship.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> TBW: infant ~75%, adult man ~60%, adult woman ~50%, elderly lower.</p>","w":["Percent body water does fall with age, but mainly because of the shift from muscle to fat; body fat is the direct determinant.","Women have lower percent TBW because of higher body fat; sex is a category, not a quantity that can be inversely proportional.","Correct — more fat means a lower percentage of body water.","Muscle is water-rich, so more muscle means more body water.","Better hydration means more body water — a direct relationship."]},"fp61":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An indicator must distribute evenly through the compartment being measured, stay only in that compartment, and not be metabolized (or losses must be corrected for).</p><p>Volume = (amount injected − amount lost) ÷ final concentration. If an ECF marker leaked into cells, it would be diluted in a bigger space and overestimate ECF volume. If it were metabolized or excreted without correction, its concentration would fall and volume would again be overestimated. Markers should also be nontoxic, easily measured, and mix quickly.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It must rapidly attach to red blood cells.</b> — Attaching to red cells is only desirable when measuring red cell volume (e.g., ⁵¹Cr).</li><li><b>It must be freely metabolized by the body.</b> — The indicator must not be metabolized, or the volume would be overestimated.</li><li><b>It must selectively remain within the intracellular fluid.</b> — No indicator stays only inside cells; ICF volume is calculated as TBW − ECF.</li><li><b>It must only permeate into interstitial fluid, not plasma.</b> — The indicator is injected into the blood, so an ECF marker spreads through both plasma and interstitial fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> V = (amount injected − amount excreted) ÷ concentration.</p>","w":["Attaching to red cells is only desirable when measuring red cell volume (e.g., ⁵¹Cr).","The indicator must not be metabolized, or the volume would be overestimated.","Correct — the indicator must stay within the target compartment.","No indicator stays only inside cells; ICF volume is calculated as TBW − ECF.","The indicator is injected into the blood, so an ECF marker spreads through both plasma and interstitial fluid."]},"fp62":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The osmotic coefficient corrects for incomplete independent behavior of ions; for NaCl it's ≈ 0.93.</p><p>Ideally, 1 mmol/L NaCl gives 2 mOsm/L. Because Na⁺ and Cl⁻ attract each other, the effective number of particles is less: 2 × 0.93 ≈ 1.86 mOsm/L. Van't Hoff: π = CRT; at 37 °C, each mOsm/L produces about 19.3 mm Hg. Plasma's corrected osmotic activity (~282 mOsm/L) gives a total osmotic pressure of roughly 5,400–5,500 mm Hg — though only the effective (non-permeant) part drives water across membranes.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>0.5</b> — 0.5 is far too low; it would imply ions barely act independently.</li><li><b>0.75</b> — 0.75 is too low for NaCl in physiological solutions.</li><li><b>1.0</b> — 1.0 is the ideal value with no ion–ion interaction; real solutions fall a little short.</li><li><b>1.25</b> — The coefficient for NaCl doesn't exceed 1.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 1 mOsm/L ≈ 19.3 mm Hg at body temperature.</p>","w":["0.5 is far too low; it would imply ions barely act independently.","0.75 is too low for NaCl in physiological solutions.","Correct — the osmotic coefficient of NaCl is about 0.93.","1.0 is the ideal value with no ion–ion interaction; real solutions fall a little short.","The coefficient for NaCl doesn't exceed 1."]},"fp63":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Insensible water loss (~700 mL/day) = evaporation from the respiratory tract + diffusion through the skin (not sweat).</p><p>Daily water intake (~2,300 mL: ~2,100 ingested + ~200 metabolic) balances output: insensible (skin ~350, lungs ~350), sweat (~100), feces (~100) and urine (~1,400). Insensible loss isn't regulated and rises with fever, hyperventilation, dry air and burns. Sweat, feces and urine are \"sensible\" losses. The kidney adjusts urine output to keep overall water balance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Water loss through sweat during heavy exercise.</b> — Sweat is a sensible loss — visible and felt — and can reach 1–2 L/h in heavy exercise.</li><li><b>Water loss in feces due to severe diarrhea.</b> — Diarrhea is an abnormal, sensible loss.</li><li><b>Water loss via urination as regulated by the kidneys.</b> — Urine is the main regulated route of water loss — the opposite of insensible.</li><li><b>Water synthesis through carbohydrate oxidation.</b> — Carbohydrate oxidation produces metabolic water — a gain, not a loss.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Burns ↑ insensible skin losses; fever ↑ insensible losses (~10% per °C).</p>","w":["Sweat is a sensible loss — visible and felt — and can reach 1–2 L/h in heavy exercise.","Diarrhea is an abnormal, sensible loss.","Correct — respiratory evaporation and skin diffusion are insensible losses.","Urine is the main regulated route of water loss — the opposite of insensible.","Carbohydrate oxidation produces metabolic water — a gain, not a loss."]},"fp64":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Diabetes insipidus → no ADH effect → loss of large volumes of dilute urine (pure water) → hypernatremia if water intake can't keep up.</p><p>ADH (vasopressin) acts on V₂ receptors in collecting duct principal cells → cAMP → aquaporin-2 insertion → water reabsorption. Central DI: deficient ADH secretion (head trauma, surgery, tumors). Nephrogenic DI: kidneys unresponsive (lithium, hypercalcemia, V₂ receptor or AQP2 mutations). Patients have polyuria with low urine osmolality and intense thirst. Water deprivation test plus desmopressin response distinguishes central (urine concentrates with desmopressin) from nephrogenic (no response).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Excessive sodium retention by the kidneys.</b> — Na⁺ rises because water is lost, not because extra Na⁺ is retained.</li><li><b>Increased plasma colloid osmotic pressure.</b> — Colloid osmotic pressure comes from plasma proteins and isn't the mechanism here.</li><li><b>Water shift from ECF to ICF.</b> — Water shifts from cells into the ECF, not the reverse, because the ECF becomes more concentrated.</li><li><b>Intracellular edema</b> — Cells shrink in hypernatremia; they swell in hyponatremia.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Central DI → treat with desmopressin; nephrogenic DI → thiazides, amiloride (for lithium).</p>","w":["Na⁺ rises because water is lost, not because extra Na⁺ is retained.","Correct — primary loss of water raises serum sodium.","Colloid osmotic pressure comes from plasma proteins and isn't the mechanism here.","Water shifts from cells into the ECF, not the reverse, because the ECF becomes more concentrated.","Cells shrink in hypernatremia; they swell in hyponatremia."]},"fp65":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Pitting edema = free (mobile) interstitial fluid that can be pushed aside by pressure; it develops once interstitial pressure becomes positive.</p><p>Normally interstitial fluid is held in a proteoglycan gel. When fluid accumulates and interstitial pressure turns positive, free fluid forms channels and \"rivulets\" within the tissue. Finger pressure displaces it, leaving a dent that slowly refills. Common causes: heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, venous insufficiency. Non-pitting edema occurs with cellular swelling, myxedema (hypothyroidism — GAG deposition) and chronic lymphedema (fibrosis).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The edema is caused primarily by intracellular fluid accumulation.</b> — Swollen cells don't pit; the water is trapped inside cells.</li><li><b>The tissue fluid is clotted with fibrinogen, preventing displacement.</b> — Clotted, fibrinogen-rich fluid causes non-pitting edema.</li><li><b>Lymphatic drainage is completely intact and functioning normally.</b> — Pitting says nothing reassuring about lymphatic drainage.</li><li><b>The interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure is still in the negative range.</b> — Pitting appears only once interstitial pressure has become positive.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Non-pitting: myxedema, chronic lymphedema, cellulitis.</p>","w":["Swollen cells don't pit; the water is trapped inside cells.","Clotted, fibrinogen-rich fluid causes non-pitting edema.","Correct — pitting indicates free fluid in the interstitial spaces.","Pitting says nothing reassuring about lymphatic drainage.","Pitting appears only once interstitial pressure has become positive."]},"fp66":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hematocrit = percentage of blood volume made up of red cells (~40% men, ~36% women); blood volume ≈ 7% of body weight (~5 L).</p><p>Blood = plasma (~60%) + cells (~40%, mostly RBCs). Blood volume can be calculated from plasma volume: blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct). For example, plasma 3 L with Hct 0.4 → blood volume = 3 ÷ 0.6 = 5 L. Hematocrit rises with dehydration (hemoconcentration), polycythemia and high altitude; it falls with anemia, hemodilution and pregnancy. Plasma has far more protein (~7 g/dL) than interstitial fluid (~2 g/dL).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Blood volume averages 10% of body weight.</b> — Blood is about 7% of body weight, not 10%.</li><li><b>Approximately 80% of blood is plasma, and 20% is red blood cells.</b> — The split is about 60% plasma and 40% red cells.</li><li><b>Plasma and interstitial fluid have identical protein concentrations.</b> — Plasma has much more protein than interstitial fluid; that difference creates colloid osmotic pressure.</li><li><b>Blood volume can be directly measured by subtracting interstitial fluid volume from extracellular fluid volume.</b> — ECF minus interstitial fluid equals plasma, not blood; blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct).</p>","w":["Blood is about 7% of body weight, not 10%.","The split is about 60% plasma and 40% red cells.","Correct — hematocrit is the percentage of red cells in blood.","Plasma has much more protein than interstitial fluid; that difference creates colloid osmotic pressure.","ECF minus interstitial fluid equals plasma, not blood; blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct)."]},"fp67":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Physiology = the study of function — the physical and chemical mechanisms that underlie life.</p><p>Anatomy describes structure; physiology explains how structures work and are regulated. Physiology spans levels from molecules and cells to organs and the whole organism, including how systems interact to maintain homeostasis. Pathophysiology studies disordered function in disease.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The study of the gross and microscopic structures of living organisms.</b> — Studying gross and microscopic structure is anatomy and histology.</li><li><b>The field of medicine dedicated to diagnosing and treating diseases based on anatomical changes.</b> — Diagnosing and treating disease based on anatomical changes is clinical pathology/medicine.</li><li><b>The branch of biology that classifies organisms based on evolutionary relationships.</b> — Classifying organisms by evolutionary relationships is taxonomy/phylogenetics.</li><li><b>The study of how genetic information is inherited and expressed.</b> — Studying inheritance and gene expression is genetics.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anatomy = structure; physiology = function.</p>","w":["Studying gross and microscopic structure is anatomy and histology.","Correct — physiology studies the physical and chemical mechanisms of life.","Diagnosing and treating disease based on anatomical changes is clinical pathology/medicine.","Classifying organisms by evolutionary relationships is taxonomy/phylogenetics.","Studying inheritance and gene expression is genetics."]},"fp68":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Claude Bernard's milieu intérieur = the extracellular fluid that surrounds and supplies every cell.</p><p>Cells live in the ECF, not directly in the outside world. The ECF (plasma + interstitial fluid) is continuously mixed by the circulation and exchanged across capillaries, delivering O₂, nutrients and ions and removing waste. Walter Cannon later coined \"homeostasis\" for the processes that keep this internal environment stable.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intracellular fluid</b> — ICF is inside cells, not the environment surrounding them.</li><li><b>Blood plasma</b> — Plasma is only one part of the ECF.</li><li><b>Interstitial fluid</b> — Interstitial fluid is only one part of the ECF; the internal environment includes plasma too.</li><li><b>Cerebrospinal fluid</b> — CSF is a small, specialized transcellular fluid.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bernard → milieu intérieur; Cannon → homeostasis.</p>","w":["ICF is inside cells, not the environment surrounding them.","Plasma is only one part of the ECF.","Interstitial fluid is only one part of the ECF; the internal environment includes plasma too.","CSF is a small, specialized transcellular fluid.","Correct — the internal environment is the extracellular fluid."]},"fp69":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Homeostasis = maintenance of relatively constant internal conditions despite internal or external changes, mostly via negative feedback.</p><p>Regulated variables include temperature, blood pressure, pH, glucose, O₂/CO₂, ion concentrations and volume. In fever, the hypothalamic set point is actually raised by pyrogens (PGE₂). When the fever \"breaks\" (set point returns to normal), the body is now too warm relative to the set point, so sweating and vasodilation dissipate heat until temperature returns toward normal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Metabolism</b> — Metabolism refers to all chemical reactions in the body.</li><li><b>Reproduction</b> — Reproduction is producing offspring.</li><li><b>Pathophysiology</b> — Pathophysiology studies how normal function goes wrong in disease.</li><li><b>Growth</b> — Growth is an increase in size or cell number.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fever = raised set point (PGE₂); NSAIDs lower it by inhibiting COX.</p>","w":["Metabolism refers to all chemical reactions in the body.","Reproduction is producing offspring.","Pathophysiology studies how normal function goes wrong in disease.","Correct — this describes homeostasis.","Growth is an increase in size or cell number."]},"fp70":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gain = correction ÷ remaining error; correction = 20 − 100 = −80, error = +20 → gain = −4.</p><p>Uncorrected change = +100. With the control system, change = +20, so the system corrected 80 units in the opposite direction (−80). Remaining error = +20. Gain = −80 ÷ 20 = −4. The negative sign confirms negative feedback, and a magnitude of 4 means the system removed 80% of the disturbance. A gain of −2 (baroreceptor) removes about two-thirds; −33 (temperature) removes ~97%.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>-0.25</b> — −0.25 is the ratio inverted (20 ÷ 80).</li><li><b>-1</b> — −1 doesn't fit the numbers.</li><li><b>-5</b> — −5 divides the uncorrected change (100) by the error; you must use the correction (80).</li><li><b>-10</b> — −10 doesn't fit the numbers.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Fraction corrected = |gain| ÷ (1 + |gain|).</p>","w":["−0.25 is the ratio inverted (20 ÷ 80).","−1 doesn't fit the numbers.","Correct — −80 ÷ +20 = −4.","−5 divides the uncorrected change (100) by the error; you must use the correction (80).","−10 doesn't fit the numbers."]},"fp71":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Beneficial positive feedback amplifies a process until a defined endpoint: blood clotting, childbirth, the AP upstroke, the LH surge.</p><p>In clotting, thrombin generated at the injury site activates factors V, VIII and XI and platelets, generating more thrombin until a stable clot forms; then anticoagulant mechanisms (antithrombin, protein C) stop the loop. In labor, cervical stretch → oxytocin release → stronger contractions → more stretch, until delivery. These loops are useful because they have a natural \"off switch.\"</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Regulation of blood glucose by insulin.</b> — Insulin lowering high glucose is negative feedback.</li><li><b>Maintenance of body pH by the respiratory and renal systems.</b> — The lungs and kidneys correcting pH toward normal is negative feedback.</li><li><b>Control of body temperature through shivering.</b> — Shivering in response to cold restores temperature — negative feedback.</li><li><b>Regulation of oxygen concentration by hemoglobin.</b> — Hemoglobin buffers tissue O₂ — a stabilizing mechanism, not positive feedback.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Positive feedback loops: clotting, labor (oxytocin), AP upstroke (Na⁺), LH surge (estrogen).</p>","w":["Insulin lowering high glucose is negative feedback.","The lungs and kidneys correcting pH toward normal is negative feedback.","Correct — blood clotting is a beneficial positive feedback loop.","Shivering in response to cold restores temperature — negative feedback.","Hemoglobin buffers tissue O₂ — a stabilizing mechanism, not positive feedback."]},"fp72":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The GI tract digests food and absorbs nutrients (sugars, amino acids, fatty acids) into the ECF/blood.</p><p>Each organ system contributes to homeostasis: respiratory (O₂ in, CO₂ out), GI (nutrients and water), liver (metabolism and detoxification), kidneys (excretion of wastes, water, electrolytes, pH), musculoskeletal (support and movement), immune (defense), nervous and endocrine (control), and integumentary (barrier, thermoregulation). Most nutrients are absorbed in the small intestine; monosaccharides and amino acids go to the liver via the portal vein, while long-chain fats enter lymph as chylomicrons.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Respiratory system</b> — The respiratory system supplies O₂ and removes CO₂.</li><li><b>Renal system</b> — The renal system removes wastes and regulates water, ions and pH.</li><li><b>Musculoskeletal system</b> — The musculoskeletal system provides support and movement but doesn't absorb nutrients.</li><li><b>Immune system</b> — The immune system defends against infection.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Small intestine = main absorptive site; fats → lymph (chylomicrons).</p>","w":["The respiratory system supplies O₂ and removes CO₂.","The renal system removes wastes and regulates water, ions and pH.","The musculoskeletal system provides support and movement but doesn't absorb nutrients.","Correct — the GI tract absorbs nutrients into the blood.","The immune system defends against infection."]},"fp73":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osmolality = osmoles per kilogram of water (mOsm/kg H₂O); osmolarity = per liter of solution.</p><p>Laboratories measure osmolality by freezing-point depression. Normal plasma osmolality ≈ 285–295 mOsm/kg. An osmolal gap (measured − calculated &gt; 10) indicates unmeasured osmoles such as ethanol, methanol, ethylene glycol or mannitol. In dilute body fluids, osmolarity and osmolality are nearly equal.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Osmolarity</b> — Osmolarity is per liter of solution, not per kilogram of water.</li><li><b>Tonicity</b> — Tonicity compares a solution to a cell based on non-penetrating solutes.</li><li><b>Osmotic pressure</b> — Osmotic pressure is expressed as a pressure (mm Hg).</li><li><b>Reflection coefficient</b> — The reflection coefficient is a 0–1 number describing membrane permeability to a solute.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osmolal gap &gt; 10 → toxic alcohols.</p>","w":["Osmolarity is per liter of solution, not per kilogram of water.","Tonicity compares a solution to a cell based on non-penetrating solutes.","Correct — per kg of water is osmolality.","Osmotic pressure is expressed as a pressure (mm Hg).","The reflection coefficient is a 0–1 number describing membrane permeability to a solute."]},"fp74":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Osmotic flow is driven by an osmotic pressure difference; osmotic pressure is the pressure needed to stop it.</p><p>When two solutions are separated by a membrane impermeable to solute, water moves toward the side with more effective solute (higher osmotic pressure). Applying hydrostatic pressure to that side can stop the flow; the pressure exactly needed equals the osmotic pressure difference. In capillaries, the protein-derived colloid osmotic pressure (~25–28 mm Hg) opposes hydrostatic filtration pressure — the Starling balance.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Reflection coefficient; Osmolality</b> — The reflection coefficient describes membrane blocking of a solute, and osmolality is a concentration — neither is a force or stopping pressure.</li><li><b>Tonicity; Osmolarity</b> — Tonicity and osmolarity describe solutions; they aren't a driving force or pressure.</li><li><b>Concentration gradient; Hydrostatic pressure</b> — The pressure applied would be hydrostatic, but the defined term for the pressure needed to stop osmosis is osmotic pressure, and \"concentration gradient\" is vaguer than the osmotic pressure difference.</li><li><b>Solute concentration; Diffusion</b> — Diffusion is movement of solute, not a pressure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Osmotic pressure π = σ × n × C × R × T.</p>","w":["The reflection coefficient describes membrane blocking of a solute, and osmolality is a concentration — neither is a force or stopping pressure.","Correct — osmotic pressure difference drives flow; osmotic pressure stops it.","Tonicity and osmolarity describe solutions; they aren't a driving force or pressure.","The pressure applied would be hydrostatic, but the defined term for the pressure needed to stop osmosis is osmotic pressure, and \"concentration gradient\" is vaguer than the osmotic pressure difference.","Diffusion is movement of solute, not a pressure."]},"fp75":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Reflection coefficient σ = 1 → membrane impermeable to the solute → full osmotic effect; σ = 0 → freely permeable → no effective osmotic pressure.</p><p>Effective osmotic pressure = σ × theoretical osmotic pressure. Albumin at capillary walls has σ ≈ 0.9–1, so it exerts nearly full oncotic pressure. Urea crosses most cell membranes (σ ≈ 0), so it raises osmolality but not tonicity — that's why a high BUN doesn't make cells shrink. Na⁺ is effectively an impermeant osmole across cell membranes because the pump keeps it out.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The membrane is freely permeable to the solute, and no effective osmotic pressure is exerted.</b> — Free permeability with no effective osmotic pressure describes σ = 0.</li><li><b>The solute will diffuse across the membrane down its concentration gradient.</b> — With σ = 1, the solute can't cross the membrane.</li><li><b>The effective osmotic pressure lies between its maximal possible value and zero.</b> — A value between maximal and zero describes σ between 0 and 1.</li><li><b>Water will flow until solute concentrations are equal on both sides of the membrane.</b> — The solute can't cross, and water moves until osmotic (or total) pressures balance — not necessarily until concentrations are equal.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Urea = ineffective osmole (σ ≈ 0); Na⁺, glucose (without insulin), mannitol = effective osmoles.</p>","w":["Free permeability with no effective osmotic pressure describes σ = 0.","With σ = 1, the solute can't cross the membrane.","Correct — σ = 1 means the membrane is impermeable and the solute exerts its full osmotic effect.","A value between maximal and zero describes σ between 0 and 1.","The solute can't cross, and water moves until osmotic (or total) pressures balance — not necessarily until concentrations are equal."]},"fp76":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Water flows from lower effective osmotic pressure (hypotonic) to higher effective osmotic pressure (hypertonic).</p><p>Think of solutes as \"pulling\" water. Solution B has more effective solute, so it pulls water from A. A is hypotonic relative to B; B is hypertonic relative to A. Flow continues until effective osmotic pressures equalize or are balanced by hydrostatic pressure.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Solution A is hypertonic relative to Solution B.</b> — A is hypotonic, not hypertonic, relative to B.</li><li><b>Water will flow from Solution B into Solution A.</b> — This is backwards — water moves toward the higher osmotic pressure (B).</li><li><b>Solution B is hypotonic relative to Solution A.</b> — B is hypertonic, not hypotonic, relative to A.</li><li><b>There will be no net water flow between the solutions.</b> — A difference in effective osmotic pressure always drives net water flow.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> \"Water follows solute.\"</p>","w":["A is hypotonic, not hypertonic, relative to B.","This is backwards — water moves toward the higher osmotic pressure (B).","B is hypertonic, not hypotonic, relative to A.","Correct — water flows from A into B.","A difference in effective osmotic pressure always drives net water flow."]},"fp77":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> ECF volume is set by the total amount of Na⁺ (with Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻) in the ECF; water follows sodium.</p><p>Because water distributes to keep osmolarity equal inside and outside cells, each compartment's volume depends on how much solute it holds. Na⁺ salts are effectively confined to the ECF, so total body Na⁺ content determines ECF volume. The kidneys regulate ECF (and blood) volume by adjusting Na⁺ excretion (RAAS, ANP, sympathetic nerves). In contrast, plasma Na⁺ concentration reflects water balance (ADH, thirst). Edema and hypertension therefore reflect excess total body Na⁺.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The total amount of intracellular protein.</b> — Intracellular protein and K⁺ salts determine ICF, not ECF, volume.</li><li><b>The total amount of water in the body.</b> — Total body water is divided according to where the solutes are; water amount alone doesn't set ECF volume.</li><li><b>The activity of the sodium-potassium pump.</b> — The pump keeps Na⁺ outside cells, but the total amount of Na⁺ sets the volume.</li><li><b>The concentration of glucose in the plasma.</b> — Glucose is a minor solute that normally enters cells.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Na⁺ amount → volume; Na⁺ concentration → water balance.</p>","w":["Intracellular protein and K⁺ salts determine ICF, not ECF, volume.","Total body water is divided according to where the solutes are; water amount alone doesn't set ECF volume.","Correct — total ECF solute (mainly Na⁺ salts) determines ECF volume.","The pump keeps Na⁺ outside cells, but the total amount of Na⁺ sets the volume.","Glucose is a minor solute that normally enters cells."]},"fp78":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> At steady state, ICF and ECF osmolarities are equal because water moves freely across cell membranes.</p><p>Water crosses membranes through the lipid bilayer and aquaporins within seconds to minutes, so any osmotic difference is quickly abolished by water movement. Solute pumps (e.g., Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase) determine how solutes are distributed, but it's water flow that equalizes osmolarity. This is why changes in ECF osmolarity rapidly change cell volume.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Intracellular osmolarity is always higher; active transport of water.</b> — Osmolarities are equal, and water is never actively pumped; it moves passively.</li><li><b>Extracellular osmolarity is always higher; passive diffusion of solutes.</b> — Osmolarities are equal; extracellular osmolarity isn't always higher.</li><li><b>They are equal; active transport of solutes across membranes.</b> — They are equal, but water movement — not active solute transport — equalizes them.</li><li><b>Intracellular osmolarity varies independently of extracellular osmolarity; no specific mechanism.</b> — Water movement tightly links intracellular and extracellular osmolarity.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Water distributes; solutes determine where it goes.</p>","w":["Osmolarities are equal, and water is never actively pumped; it moves passively.","Osmolarities are equal; extracellular osmolarity isn't always higher.","Correct — they're equal because water shifts freely.","They are equal, but water movement — not active solute transport — equalizes them.","Water movement tightly links intracellular and extracellular osmolarity."]},"fp79":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> About 2/3 of TBW is intracellular (ICF ≈ 40% of body weight) and 1/3 extracellular (ECF ≈ 20%).</p><p>For a 70-kg man: TBW ≈ 42 L, ICF ≈ 28 L, ECF ≈ 14 L (interstitial ≈ 10.5 L, plasma ≈ 3.5 L). Remember fractions: ECF is 1/3 of TBW; within the ECF, interstitial fluid is 3/4 and plasma 1/4.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>1/3</b> — 1/3 of TBW is the ECF.</li><li><b>¼</b> — 1/4 is the plasma fraction of the ECF.</li><li><b>¾</b> — 3/4 is the interstitial fraction of the ECF.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> 60-40-20 rule; ECF = 3/4 interstitial + 1/4 plasma.</p>","w":["1/3 of TBW is the ECF.","1/4 is the plasma fraction of the ECF.","Correct — about 2/3 of TBW is intracellular.","3/4 is the interstitial fraction of the ECF."]},"fp80":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> An ECF marker must leave the plasma, spread through the interstitium, stay out of cells — so it must not bind RBCs or plasma proteins.</p><p>If an indicator binds plasma proteins (like Evans blue to albumin) or RBCs, it remains in the vascular compartment and measures plasma or blood volume instead. ECF markers like inulin, mannitol, sucrose and radioactive Na⁺ are unbound, cross capillary walls, and stay outside cells. They also must not be metabolized during the mixing period.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It should freely permeate the cell membrane and enter the cells</b> — Freely entering cells would make it a TBW marker.</li><li><b>It should be metabolized quickly within the body</b> — An indicator must not be metabolized during mixing.</li><li><b>It should remain confined to the plasma compartment after injection</b> — Remaining confined to plasma describes a plasma-volume marker (Evans blue, ¹²⁵I-albumin).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Plasma markers bind albumin; ECF markers don't bind anything.</p>","w":["Freely entering cells would make it a TBW marker.","Correct — it must not be bound to RBCs or plasma proteins.","An indicator must not be metabolized during mixing.","Remaining confined to plasma describes a plasma-volume marker (Evans blue, ¹²⁵I-albumin)."]},"fp81":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Na⁺ (~142 mEq/L) is the main ECF cation; K⁺ (~140 mEq/L) is the main ICF cation.</p><p>ECF cations are dominated by Na⁺, balanced mainly by Cl⁻ (~103) and HCO₃⁻ (~24). The anion gap (Na⁺ − [Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻] ≈ 8–12) reflects unmeasured anions, mostly albumin. Because Na⁺ is the main extracellular osmole, plasma Na⁺ concentration largely determines plasma osmolality (≈ 2 × Na⁺). Mg²⁺ and K⁺ are mainly intracellular; Ca²⁺ is extracellular but present in small amounts.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Potassium (K⁺)</b> — K⁺ is the main intracellular cation.</li><li><b>Magnesium (Mg²⁺)</b> — Mg²⁺ is mainly intracellular.</li><li><b>Calcium (Ca²⁺)</b> — Ca²⁺ in the ECF is only ~2.4 mmol/L (~5 mEq/L total).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻); normal ~8–12.</p>","w":["K⁺ is the main intracellular cation.","Mg²⁺ is mainly intracellular.","Correct — Na⁺ is the main ECF cation.","Ca²⁺ in the ECF is only ~2.4 mmol/L (~5 mEq/L total)."]},"fp82":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Isotonic = same effective osmotic pressure (non-penetrating solutes) → no net water flow. Isosmotic = same total osmolarity.</p><p>Tonicity predicts what happens to cell volume. Example: 300 mOsm/L urea is isosmotic with plasma but hypotonic to cells, because urea enters cells and water follows, causing swelling. 0.9% NaCl is both isosmotic and isotonic. 5% dextrose is initially isosmotic but becomes hypotonic as glucose is taken up and metabolized.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Hypertonic</b> — Hypertonic means higher effective osmotic pressure.</li><li><b>Hypotonic</b> — Hypotonic means lower effective osmotic pressure.</li><li><b>Isosmotic</b> — Isosmotic refers to total osmolarity, which includes permeant solutes that exert no effective pressure.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Tonicity = effective osmolarity; urea is an ineffective osmole.</p>","w":["Hypertonic means higher effective osmotic pressure.","Hypotonic means lower effective osmotic pressure.","Correct — equal effective osmotic pressure is isotonic.","Isosmotic refers to total osmolarity, which includes permeant solutes that exert no effective pressure."]},"fp83":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The hydrophobic core of fatty acid tails is the barrier to polar and charged molecules.</p><p>Phospholipids arrange with hydrophilic heads facing water and hydrophobic tails forming a central oily layer. Small nonpolar molecules (O₂, CO₂, steroids, ethanol) dissolve in this layer and cross. Polar molecules (glucose, amino acids) and ions can't dissolve in it and need transport proteins. Small uncharged polar molecules like water and urea cross slowly (water mainly via aquaporins).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The presence of cholesterol</b> — Cholesterol modulates fluidity and permeability, but the hydrophobic core is the main barrier.</li><li><b>The hydrophilic heads of phospholipids facing the aqueous solutions</b> — The polar heads face the water; the barrier is the hydrophobic interior.</li><li><b>The integral membrane proteins embedded within the bilayer</b> — This bilayer has no proteins, and proteins help transport rather than restrict it.</li><li><b>The peripheral membrane proteins attached to the surface</b> — There are no proteins here, and peripheral proteins don't form the barrier.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Permeability ∝ lipid solubility (partition coefficient) and inversely to size.</p>","w":["Cholesterol modulates fluidity and permeability, but the hydrophobic core is the main barrier.","The polar heads face the water; the barrier is the hydrophobic interior.","Correct — the hydrophobic core blocks polar molecules.","This bilayer has no proteins, and proteins help transport rather than restrict it.","There are no proteins here, and peripheral proteins don't form the barrier."]},"fp84":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Lipid-soluble gases (O₂, CO₂, N₂) diffuse directly through the lipid bilayer very rapidly.</p><p>Diffusion rate through the bilayer is proportional to lipid solubility. Because the lipid component covers most of the membrane surface, gases can cross anywhere, making gas exchange in lungs and tissues extremely fast. Volatile anesthetics are also highly lipid-soluble (Meyer–Overton correlation). Polar solutes, ions and large molecules require proteins.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ions</b> — Ions are charged and need channels.</li><li><b>Glucose</b> — Glucose is polar and needs carriers (GLUTs).</li><li><b>Amino acids</b> — Amino acids are polar and need carriers.</li><li><b>Water-soluble vitamins</b> — Water-soluble vitamins need transporters.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Lipid-soluble = fast diffusion through bilayer (gases, steroids, alcohol).</p>","w":["Ions are charged and need channels.","Glucose is polar and needs carriers (GLUTs).","Amino acids are polar and need carriers.","Correct — lipid-soluble gases diffuse directly through the bilayer.","Water-soluble vitamins need transporters."]},"fp85":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Aquaporins are selective water channels; ADH inserts aquaporin-2 into collecting duct luminal membranes.</p><p>Water crosses the lipid bilayer slowly, so most water movement occurs through aquaporins. AQP1 is constitutive in proximal tubules, descending thin limbs and RBCs. AQP2 is ADH-regulated: ADH → V₂ receptor → Gs → cAMP → PKA → AQP2 vesicles fuse with the apical membrane. AQP3/4 on the basolateral side let water exit to the blood. Mutations in AQP2 or the V₂ receptor cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Ion channels</b> — Ion channels carry ions, not bulk water.</li><li><b>Voltage-gated channels</b> — Voltage-gated channels carry ions.</li><li><b>Ligand-binding receptors</b> — Ligand-binding receptors bind signals; they aren't water pores.</li><li><b>Solute carriers</b> — Solute carriers move solutes, not water.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ADH → V₂ → cAMP → AQP2 insertion (collecting duct).</p>","w":["Ion channels carry ions, not bulk water.","Voltage-gated channels carry ions.","Correct — aquaporins are the main water channels.","Ligand-binding receptors bind signals; they aren't water pores.","Solute carriers move solutes, not water."]},"fp86":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gating = switching of channels between open and closed states in response to voltage, ligands, second messengers or stretch.</p><p>Gating mechanisms: voltage-gated (Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺ channels), ligand-gated extracellularly (nicotinic ACh, GABA-A, NMDA receptors), ligand-gated intracellularly (cAMP/cGMP-gated, IP₃ receptors), and mechanically gated (Piezo, hair cell channels). Once open, selectivity (which ions pass) and conductance (how fast they pass) describe the channel's behavior.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Selectivity</b> — Selectivity describes which ions a channel lets through.</li><li><b>Conductance</b> — Conductance describes how easily ions flow through an open channel.</li><li><b>Saturation</b> — Saturation is a carrier property, not a channel one.</li><li><b>Stereospecificity</b> — Stereospecificity is a carrier property.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Channels: gating + selectivity + conductance. Carriers: saturation + specificity + competition.</p>","w":["Selectivity describes which ions a channel lets through.","Conductance describes how easily ions flow through an open channel.","Saturation is a carrier property, not a channel one.","Correct — opening and closing is gating.","Stereospecificity is a carrier property."]},"fp87":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Symporter (cotransporter) = moves two solutes in the same direction; SGLT uses Na⁺ moving down its gradient to carry glucose uphill.</p><p>Classify transporters by direction and number: uniporter (one solute, e.g., GLUT), symporter (same direction, e.g., SGLT1/2, NKCC2, Na⁺-amino acid transporters), antiporter/exchanger (opposite directions, e.g., NCX, Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger, Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger). Both symporters and antiporters that use the Na⁺ gradient are secondary active transporters.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Uniporter</b> — A uniporter moves one solute only (e.g., GLUT).</li><li><b>Antiporter</b> — An antiporter moves solutes in opposite directions (e.g., NCX).</li><li><b>ATPase ion transporter</b> — ATPases hydrolyze ATP directly; SGLT uses the Na⁺ gradient instead.</li><li><b>Peripheral protein</b> — Peripheral proteins don't span the membrane and don't transport solutes.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> SGLT2 inhibitors (-gliflozins) block renal glucose reabsorption → glucosuria.</p>","w":["A uniporter moves one solute only (e.g., GLUT).","An antiporter moves solutes in opposite directions (e.g., NCX).","Correct — same-direction transport is symport.","ATPases hydrolyze ATP directly; SGLT uses the Na⁺ gradient instead.","Peripheral proteins don't span the membrane and don't transport solutes."]},"fp88":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Primary active transport directly hydrolyzes ATP to move solutes uphill (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, SERCA, PMCA, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase).</p><p>The pump protein itself is an ATPase: ATP phosphorylates it, causing conformational changes that move ions against their gradients. Examples and drugs: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase (digoxin), H⁺/K⁺-ATPase in gastric parietal cells (proton pump inhibitors like omeprazole), SERCA and PMCA (Ca²⁺). ABC transporters (e.g., P-glycoprotein, CFTR) also use ATP.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Simple diffusion</b> — Simple diffusion is passive and moves substances downhill.</li><li><b>Facilitated diffusion</b> — Facilitated diffusion is passive and carrier-mediated.</li><li><b>Secondary active transport</b> — Secondary active transport uses an ion gradient, not ATP directly.</li><li><b>Vesicular transport</b> — Vesicular transport uses ATP but isn't classed as primary active transport.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> PPIs irreversibly inhibit gastric H⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</p>","w":["Simple diffusion is passive and moves substances downhill.","Facilitated diffusion is passive and carrier-mediated.","Correct — directly ATP-powered, uphill transport.","Secondary active transport uses an ion gradient, not ATP directly.","Vesicular transport uses ATP but isn't classed as primary active transport."]},"fp89":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Active transport needs an energy source (ATP directly or an ion gradient) because it moves solutes against their gradient; passive transport doesn't.</p><p>Passive transport (simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, channels, osmosis) moves substances down their electrochemical gradient using kinetic energy alone. Active transport moves substances uphill and so needs energy: primary (ATP hydrolysis) or secondary (energy stored in another ion's gradient). Both carrier-mediated forms (facilitated diffusion and active transport) show saturation.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Active transport occurs down a concentration gradient, while passive transport occurs against it</b> — This is reversed — active transport moves substances uphill.</li><li><b>Active transport does not use carrier proteins, while passive transport always does</b> — Active transport always uses carrier proteins; simple diffusion needs none.</li><li><b>Passive transport is slower than active transport</b> — Speed isn't the defining difference.</li><li><b>Passive transport moves substances uphill, while active transport moves them downhill</b> — This is reversed.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Passive = downhill, no energy; active = uphill, energy.</p>","w":["This is reversed — active transport moves substances uphill.","Correct — energy requirement is the key difference.","Active transport always uses carrier proteins; simple diffusion needs none.","Speed isn't the defining difference.","This is reversed."]},"fp90":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Simple diffusion = passive movement down an electrochemical gradient, with no carrier, no energy and no saturation.</p><p>Rate of simple diffusion (Fick): J = P × A × (C₁ − C₂), where permeability P depends on lipid solubility, size and membrane thickness. Rate rises linearly with concentration difference — it never plateaus. Facilitated diffusion is also passive and downhill but uses a carrier, so it saturates.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It requires a carrier protein</b> — No carrier is needed; that's facilitated diffusion.</li><li><b>It requires metabolic energy</b> — Simple diffusion is passive.</li><li><b>It exhibits saturation kinetics</b> — Saturation is a carrier-mediated feature; simple diffusion is linear.</li><li><b>It transports water-soluble substances more easily than lipid-soluble ones</b> — Lipid-soluble substances cross the bilayer more easily than water-soluble ones.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Linear (diffusion) vs hyperbolic/saturating (carrier) kinetics.</p>","w":["No carrier is needed; that's facilitated diffusion.","Simple diffusion is passive.","Correct — simple diffusion occurs down an electrochemical gradient.","Saturation is a carrier-mediated feature; simple diffusion is linear.","Lipid-soluble substances cross the bilayer more easily than water-soluble ones."]},"fp91":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A diffusion potential is the voltage created when a permeant ion diffuses down its concentration gradient, separating charge.</p><p>If a membrane is permeable only to K⁺ and K⁺ is higher inside, K⁺ diffuses out, leaving negative charge behind. This builds a voltage (inside negative) that opposes further K⁺ efflux. When the voltage exactly balances the chemical gradient, net diffusion stops — that voltage is the equilibrium (Nernst) potential. The resting potential is the combined result of several ions' diffusion potentials.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Membrane potential</b> — Membrane potential is a general term for the voltage across any membrane.</li><li><b>Action potential</b> — An action potential is a rapid, regenerative change in Vm.</li><li><b>Equilibrium potential</b> — The equilibrium potential is the specific diffusion potential at which net flux stops.</li><li><b>Resting potential</b> — The resting potential is the steady Vm made of several ions' contributions.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Diffusion potential → at equilibrium = Nernst potential.</p>","w":["Membrane potential is a general term for the voltage across any membrane.","An action potential is a rapid, regenerative change in Vm.","Correct — the potential created by an ion diffusing is a diffusion potential.","The equilibrium potential is the specific diffusion potential at which net flux stops.","The resting potential is the steady Vm made of several ions' contributions."]},"fp92":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Facilitated and simple diffusion are both passive: downhill and without metabolic energy. Facilitated diffusion uses a carrier.</p><p>Because facilitated diffusion depends on carrier proteins, it shows the three carrier features — saturation (Tm), stereospecificity and competition — whereas simple diffusion doesn't. Example: glucose entry into cells via GLUT-1/4. Neither type can move substances against their gradient.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It does not use a membrane carrier</b> — Facilitated diffusion does use a membrane carrier.</li><li><b>It occurs down an electrochemical potential gradient</b> — This is true but incomplete; C is also true.</li><li><b>It does not require metabolic energy</b> — This is true but incomplete; B is also true.</li><li><b>All of the above</b> — A is false, so \"all of the above\" is wrong.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Facilitated diffusion: passive + carrier → saturable.</p>","w":["Facilitated diffusion does use a membrane carrier.","This is true but incomplete; C is also true.","This is true but incomplete; B is also true.","Correct — both B and C are shared features.","A is false, so \"all of the above\" is wrong."]},"fp93":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Competition: structurally similar molecules compete for the same carrier binding site, slowing each other's transport.</p><p>Example: galactose competes with glucose for SGLT1 in the intestine. In the kidney, many organic anions (penicillin, probenecid, uric acid) compete for the same organic anion transporters — probenecid was historically used to slow penicillin excretion. Competition, like saturation and stereospecificity, arises because carriers have specific, limited binding sites.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Saturation</b> — Saturation is a plateau from limited carriers, not inhibition by a similar molecule.</li><li><b>Stereospecificity</b> — Stereospecificity is selecting a particular isomer.</li><li><b>Conductance</b> — Conductance applies to channels.</li><li><b>Unidirectional transport</b> — \"Unidirectional transport\" isn't a carrier property.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Probenecid blocks secretion of penicillin and reabsorption of urate (via competition).</p>","w":["Saturation is a plateau from limited carriers, not inhibition by a similar molecule.","Stereospecificity is selecting a particular isomer.","Correct — similar molecules compete for the carrier.","Conductance applies to channels.","\"Unidirectional transport\" isn't a carrier property."]},"fp94":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> CFTR is a cAMP/PKA-regulated, ATP-gated Cl⁻ channel (ABC transporter family).</p><p>In airway and intestinal epithelia, CFTR secretes Cl⁻ into the lumen, and Na⁺ and water follow, hydrating secretions. In sweat ducts, CFTR reabsorbs Cl⁻. CF (autosomal recessive; ΔF508 most common) causes thick secretions → recurrent pulmonary infections, pancreatic insufficiency (fat malabsorption, ADEK deficiency), meconium ileus, male infertility (absent vas deferens), and high sweat Cl⁻ (&gt;60 mmol/L). CFTR modulators (ivacaftor, elexacaftor/tezacaftor/ivacaftor) improve channel function.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Na+</b> — CFTR inhibits ENaC; it isn't a Na⁺ channel.</li><li><b>K+</b> — CFTR isn't a K⁺ channel.</li><li><b>HCO3−</b> — CFTR can conduct some HCO₃⁻, but its primary role is as a Cl⁻ channel.</li><li><b>Ca2+</b> — CFTR isn't a Ca²⁺ channel.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> CF diagnosis: sweat Cl⁻ &gt; 60 mmol/L; newborn screen ↑ immunoreactive trypsinogen.</p>","w":["CFTR inhibits ENaC; it isn't a Na⁺ channel.","CFTR isn't a K⁺ channel.","Correct — CFTR is a Cl⁻ channel.","CFTR can conduct some HCO₃⁻, but its primary role is as a Cl⁻ channel.","CFTR isn't a Ca²⁺ channel."]},"fp95":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP — electrogenic.</p><p>The pump binds 3 intracellular Na⁺ and ATP, phosphorylates itself, changes shape to release Na⁺ outside, binds 2 extracellular K⁺, dephosphorylates and returns K⁺ inside. It maintains low intracellular Na⁺ (for secondary transport and excitability), high intracellular K⁺ (resting potential), and cell volume. It consumes a large share of resting energy, especially in neurons and kidney tubules. Inhibited by digoxin/ouabain; stimulated by insulin, β₂-agonists, thyroid hormone and aldosterone.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>3 Na+ into the cell and 2 K+ out of the cell</b> — Both directions are reversed.</li><li><b>2 Na+ into the cell and 3 K+ out of the cell</b> — Both directions and numbers are wrong.</li><li><b>2 Na+ out of the cell and 3 K+ into the cell</b> — The numbers are reversed.</li><li><b>1 Na+ out of the cell and 1 K+ into the cell</b> — The pump isn't 1:1; it's 3:2 (electrogenic).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Pump stimulators: insulin, β₂-agonists, aldosterone, T₃ (shift K⁺ into cells).</p>","w":["Both directions are reversed.","Both directions and numbers are wrong.","Correct — 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in.","The numbers are reversed.","The pump isn't 1:1; it's 3:2 (electrogenic)."]},"fp96":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Secondary active transport is \"secondary\" because its energy comes indirectly from ATP — through the Na⁺ gradient built by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</p><p>The transporter itself never hydrolyzes ATP. Instead, Na⁺ moving down its steep inward gradient powers the uphill movement of another solute: glucose (SGLT), amino acids, Ca²⁺ out (NCX), H⁺ out (NHE). If the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase is inhibited (e.g., by ouabain or ATP depletion), the gradient dissipates and all these secondary processes slow.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It does not use carrier proteins</b> — Secondary active transport always uses carrier proteins.</li><li><b>It directly hydrolyzes ATP</b> — Directly hydrolyzing ATP is primary active transport.</li><li><b>It moves solutes downhill</b> — The second solute moves uphill, against its gradient.</li><li><b>It only transports large molecules</b> — It transports small solutes (glucose, amino acids, ions), not only large molecules.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Block the Na⁺ pump → secondary transport fails.</p>","w":["Secondary active transport always uses carrier proteins.","Directly hydrolyzing ATP is primary active transport.","Correct — energy is derived indirectly from ATP through the ion gradient.","The second solute moves uphill, against its gradient.","It transports small solutes (glucose, amino acids, ions), not only large molecules."]},"fp97":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Bulk ICF and ECF are electroneutral; only a tiny layer of excess charge at the membrane creates the resting potential.</p><p>The membrane acts like a capacitor: a thin layer of excess anions lines the inner surface and excess cations the outer surface. The number of ions needed to create −70 to −90 mV is minute compared with total ion concentrations, so bulk concentrations aren't measurably changed. That's why every solution can be described as electroneutral while a membrane potential still exists.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The overall body is positively charged due to an excess of cations in the ECF.</b> — The body as a whole is electrically neutral.</li><li><b>The intracellular fluid (ICF) has a net negative charge because it contains anions without matching cations.</b> — The bulk ICF is neutral; only a thin layer at the membrane carries excess negative charge.</li><li><b>The extracellular fluid (ECF) and ICF are in electrical equilibrium.</b> — A potential difference exists across the membrane, so ECF and ICF aren't in electrical equilibrium.</li><li><b>The cell membrane is equally permeable to all ions at rest, leading to electrical neutrality across the membrane.</b> — At rest the membrane is far more permeable to K⁺ than to other ions.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Membrane = capacitor; tiny charge separation → large voltage.</p>","w":["The body as a whole is electrically neutral.","The bulk ICF is neutral; only a thin layer at the membrane carries excess negative charge.","A potential difference exists across the membrane, so ECF and ICF aren't in electrical equilibrium.","At rest the membrane is far more permeable to K⁺ than to other ions.","Correct — the bulk ICF is electroneutral."]},"fp98":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The membrane potential arises from an uneven distribution of ions (charge) across the membrane, held apart by the insulating bilayer.</p><p>Pumps create concentration gradients; selective channels let certain ions (mostly K⁺) diffuse, separating a small amount of charge. The lipid bilayer, an insulator, prevents charges from simply recombining. This separated charge is the potential difference (Vm). Maintaining it requires continuous energy expenditure by pumps.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>The law of conservation of electrical charge states that charges cannot be separated across a membrane.</b> — Charges are conserved but can be separated across a membrane — that's what creates Vm.</li><li><b>Separating positive and negative charges across an insulator requires energy.</b> — Separating charge does require energy, but that's a supporting principle; the disequilibrium itself is the uneven charge distribution.</li><li><b>Like charges attract, and opposite charges repel, leading to an even distribution across the membrane.</b> — This is reversed — opposite charges attract and like charges repel.</li><li><b>The phospholipid bilayer of the cell membrane acts as a conductor, allowing free movement of charges.</b> — The bilayer is an insulator, not a conductor.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Bilayer = insulator (capacitor); channels = resistors.</p>","w":["Charges are conserved but can be separated across a membrane — that's what creates Vm.","Separating charge does require energy, but that's a supporting principle; the disequilibrium itself is the uneven charge distribution.","This is reversed — opposite charges attract and like charges repel.","The bilayer is an insulator, not a conductor.","Correct — uneven ion distribution creates Vm."]},"fp99":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> A membrane permeable only to K⁺ develops a diffusion potential equal to EK ≈ −94 mV (inside negative).</p><p>K⁺ diffuses out down its concentration gradient (140 inside vs 4 outside). Each K⁺ that leaves carries a positive charge out, leaving the interior negative. The growing negativity opposes further K⁺ efflux until equilibrium: EK = −61 log(140/4) ≈ −94 mV. For Na⁺ (higher outside), the same logic gives ENa ≈ +61 mV (inside positive).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Approximately +61 mV, positive inside</b> — +61 mV is ENa, and K⁺ efflux makes the inside negative, not positive.</li><li><b>Approximately +61 mV, negative inside</b> — +61 mV is ENa's value, not EK's.</li><li><b>Approximately -94 mV, positive inside</b> — The magnitude is right, but the polarity is wrong — the inside is negative.</li><li><b>0 mV, as the membrane is selectively permeable</b> — Selective permeability is exactly what creates a diffusion potential.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> EK ≈ −94 mV; ENa ≈ +61 mV; ECa ≈ +120 mV; ECl ≈ −70 to −90 mV.</p>","w":["+61 mV is ENa, and K⁺ efflux makes the inside negative, not positive.","Correct — about −94 mV, inside negative.","+61 mV is ENa's value, not EK's.","The magnitude is right, but the polarity is wrong — the inside is negative.","Selective permeability is exactly what creates a diffusion potential."]},"fp100":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The Nernst equation gives the equilibrium potential for a single ion: E = (61/z) log(Cout/Cin) at 37 °C.</p><p>z is the ion's valence (+1 for Na⁺/K⁺, +2 for Ca²⁺, −1 for Cl⁻). The equation reflects the voltage needed to exactly balance an ion's concentration gradient. For the actual membrane potential with several permeable ions, use the Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation, which weights each ion by permeability. Gibbs–Donnan describes how impermeant charged molecules (like intracellular proteins) affect the distribution of permeant ions.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation</b> — GHK handles multiple ions at once, not a single ion.</li><li><b>Gibbs-Donnan equation</b> — Gibbs–Donnan describes how non-diffusible ions shift the distribution of diffusible ones.</li><li><b>Resting membrane potential equation</b> — \"Resting membrane potential equation\" isn't a named equation.</li><li><b>Diffusion potential equation</b> — \"Diffusion potential equation\" isn't a named equation.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Nernst = one ion; GHK = many ions weighted by permeability.</p>","w":["GHK handles multiple ions at once, not a single ion.","Gibbs–Donnan describes how non-diffusible ions shift the distribution of diffusible ones.","Correct — the Nernst equation gives a single ion's equilibrium potential.","\"Resting membrane potential equation\" isn't a named equation.","\"Diffusion potential equation\" isn't a named equation."]},"fp101":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> With typical concentrations, EK ≈ −90 to −94 mV (K⁺ higher inside) and ENa ≈ +61 mV (Na⁺ higher outside).</p><p>Apply the Nernst equation: E = 61 log(Cout/Cin). Na⁺: 61 log(142/14) = 61 × log 10 ≈ +61 mV. K⁺: 61 log(4/140) ≈ −94 mV. A quick sign check: an ion concentrated outside gives a positive equilibrium potential (it wants to enter, making inside positive); an ion concentrated inside gives a negative one. The resting potential (−70 to −90 mV) lies close to EK because PK dominates at rest.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>EK⁺ ~ +61 mV, ENa⁺ ~ -90 mV</b> — The signs are reversed.</li><li><b>EK⁺ ~ -70 mV, ENa⁺ ~ +70 mV</b> — −70 mV is a typical RMP, not EK, and ENa isn't +70 mV here.</li><li><b>EK⁺ ~ -95 mV, ENa⁺ ~ +70 mV</b> — The ENa value is wrong for these concentrations.</li><li><b>EK⁺ ~ -95 mV, ENa⁺ ~ +125 mV</b> — +125 mV is too high for ENa (it's in the range of ECa).</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> ECa ≈ +120 to +130 mV (10,000-fold gradient, z = 2).</p>","w":["The signs are reversed.","Correct — EK about −90 mV, ENa about +61 mV.","−70 mV is a typical RMP, not EK, and ENa isn't +70 mV here.","The ENa value is wrong for these concentrations.","+125 mV is too high for ENa (it's in the range of ECa)."]},"fp102":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> The small Na⁺ leak into the cell pulls the RMP away from EK toward ENa, making it less negative than EK.</p><p>GHK tells us Vm is a weighted average of equilibrium potentials. K⁺ dominates (high permeability), so Vm is near EK, but Na⁺ has a small permeability (PNa ≈ 1/100 PK in large nerves, more in many neurons) and a large inward driving force. That inward Na⁺ current depolarizes Vm slightly relative to EK. The electrogenic pump works in the other direction (makes Vm ~4 mV more negative).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Greater permeability of the membrane to K⁺ than other ions</b> — High K⁺ permeability pulls Vm toward EK, not away from it.</li><li><b>The electrogenic nature of the Na⁺/K⁺ pump transporting 3 Na⁺ out for 2 K⁺ in</b> — The pump's electrogenic effect makes Vm more negative, not less.</li><li><b>The presence of non-diffusible protein anions inside the cell</b> — Intracellular protein anions don't push Vm above EK.</li><li><b>The Gibbs-Donnan effect caused by impermeant anions</b> — The Gibbs–Donnan effect helps set ion distributions but doesn't make Vm less negative than EK.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> RMP ≈ −70 to −90 mV; EK ≈ −94 mV; difference due to Na⁺ leak.</p>","w":["High K⁺ permeability pulls Vm toward EK, not away from it.","The pump's electrogenic effect makes Vm more negative, not less.","Intracellular protein anions don't push Vm above EK.","Correct — Na⁺ leak makes the RMP less negative than EK.","The Gibbs–Donnan effect helps set ion distributions but doesn't make Vm less negative than EK."]},"fp103":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Gibbs–Donnan effect: impermeant anions (intracellular proteins) attract permeant cations into the cell and repel permeant anions.</p><p>Because negatively charged proteins can't leave the cell, electroneutrality requires extra cations inside. At Donnan equilibrium, the side with impermeant anions has higher total concentration of diffusible ions, creating an osmotic gradient favoring water entry. Without the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumping Na⁺ out (functionally making Na⁺ impermeant), cells would swell and burst. At capillaries, the same effect (plasma proteins attracting cations) adds to plasma oncotic pressure.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Cations are repelled from the intracellular space.</b> — Cations are attracted to, not repelled from, the intracellular space.</li><li><b>Plasma protein concentration becomes equal to interstitial fluid protein concentration.</b> — Plasma protein concentration stays much higher than interstitial protein.</li><li><b>Chloride ions are prevented from crossing the membrane.</b> — Cl⁻ can still cross; it's partly repelled, so intracellular Cl⁻ is lower.</li><li><b>Electroneutrality is violated in both compartments.</b> — Each compartment remains electroneutral.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Donnan effect + pump → cell volume regulation.</p>","w":["Cations are attracted to, not repelled from, the intracellular space.","Plasma protein concentration stays much higher than interstitial protein.","Cl⁻ can still cross; it's partly repelled, so intracellular Cl⁻ is lower.","Correct — impermeant anions pull permeant cations inward.","Each compartment remains electroneutral."]},"fp104":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Membrane potentials also store energy — e.g., the proton-motive force across the inner mitochondrial membrane drives ATP synthase (oxidative phosphorylation).</p><p>The electron transport chain pumps H⁺ from the mitochondrial matrix into the intermembrane space, creating a pH gradient and an electrical potential (~150–180 mV, matrix negative). H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase, driving ATP formation (chemiosmotic theory). Uncouplers (2,4-dinitrophenol, thermogenin in brown fat) dissipate this gradient, releasing energy as heat. Other uses of membrane potentials: electrical signaling and providing driving forces for secondary transport.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Facilitating ligand-gated channel activity</b> — Ligand-gated channels respond to chemicals; this isn't energy production.</li><li><b>Providing the driving force for primary active transport pumps</b> — Primary active pumps are powered by ATP, not by Vm.</li><li><b>Causing the opening of voltage-gated channels during depolarization</b> — Opening voltage-gated channels during depolarization is signaling, not energy production.</li><li><b>Maintaining overall electrical neutrality of the body</b> — The body is already electroneutral; Vm doesn't serve this purpose.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Uncouplers (DNP, thermogenin) → heat instead of ATP.</p>","w":["Ligand-gated channels respond to chemicals; this isn't energy production.","Primary active pumps are powered by ATP, not by Vm.","Correct — energy storage and ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation.","Opening voltage-gated channels during depolarization is signaling, not energy production.","The body is already electroneutral; Vm doesn't serve this purpose."]},"fp105":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Graded potentials can sum: temporal (repeated inputs at one site) and spatial (inputs at several sites at once).</p><p>Graded (local) potentials vary in size with stimulus strength, decay with distance and time, and can be depolarizing (EPSPs) or hyperpolarizing (IPSPs). They add algebraically at the axon hillock (trigger zone, highest density of Na⁺ channels). If the sum reaches threshold, an all-or-none action potential fires. Summation is how neurons integrate thousands of synaptic inputs.</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>It occurs only when two stimuli of different strengths arrive simultaneously.</b> — Summation also occurs with stimuli of equal strength and over time, not only with simultaneous different stimuli.</li><li><b>It prevents action potentials from being generated.</b> — Summation is how subthreshold inputs reach threshold and generate action potentials.</li><li><b>It requires a suprathreshold stimulus to initiate.</b> — Summation works with subthreshold stimuli.</li><li><b>It is an all-or-none phenomenon.</b> — All-or-none describes the action potential, not graded potentials.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Graded potentials: decremental, summable, no refractory period. APs: all-or-none, propagated, refractory.</p>","w":["Summation also occurs with stimuli of equal strength and over time, not only with simultaneous different stimuli.","Summation is how subthreshold inputs reach threshold and generate action potentials.","Correct — graded potentials add together.","Summation works with subthreshold stimuli.","All-or-none describes the action potential, not graded potentials."]},"fp106":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Relative refractory period: a second AP can fire, but only with a suprathreshold (stronger) stimulus.</p><p>After the absolute refractory period, Na⁺ channels gradually recover from inactivation, but not all are available, and K⁺ conductance is still elevated (often with afterhyperpolarization). Therefore, more depolarizing current is needed to reach threshold, and the resulting AP may be smaller. The relative refractory period allows higher firing rates with stronger stimuli (rate coding of stimulus intensity).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Resting membrane potential</b> — At rest, a normal threshold stimulus is enough.</li><li><b>Depolarization phase</b> — During depolarization the cell is already firing and absolutely refractory.</li><li><b>Absolute refractory period</b> — No stimulus can fire an AP during the absolute refractory period.</li><li><b>Repolarization phase</b> — Most of repolarization falls within the absolute refractory period; the relative refractory period is the named answer.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Absolute RP: no AP possible; relative RP: suprathreshold stimulus needed.</p>","w":["At rest, a normal threshold stimulus is enough.","During depolarization the cell is already firing and absolutely refractory.","No stimulus can fire an AP during the absolute refractory period.","Correct — a suprathreshold stimulus is required in the relative refractory period.","Most of repolarization falls within the absolute refractory period; the relative refractory period is the named answer."]},"fp107":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Acute hyperkalemia depolarizes the resting membrane, bringing it closer to threshold → increased excitability (initially).</p><p>Raising [K⁺]out makes EK less negative, so the RMP depolarizes toward threshold, and cells fire more easily. If depolarization is sustained or severe, Na⁺ channels inactivate (accommodation), and excitability then decreases, causing weakness, paralysis and cardiac conduction block. This biphasic effect explains why the question specifies \"acute.\"</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased cellular excitability due to hyperpolarization.</b> — Hyperkalemia depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes.</li><li><b>Decreased cellular excitability due to accommodation and inactivation gate closure.</b> — Accommodation with inactivation gate closure describes sustained or chronic hyperkalemia.</li><li><b>No significant effect on resting membrane potential.</b> — RMP changes significantly with [K⁺]out.</li><li><b>Increased cellular excitability due to hyperpolarization.</b> — Hyperpolarization would decrease excitability, and hyperkalemia doesn't cause it.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hyperkalemia: acute ↑excitability → sustained ↓excitability (Na⁺ inactivation).</p>","w":["Hyperkalemia depolarizes rather than hyperpolarizes.","Accommodation with inactivation gate closure describes sustained or chronic hyperkalemia.","Correct — acutely, depolarization increases excitability.","RMP changes significantly with [K⁺]out.","Hyperpolarization would decrease excitability, and hyperkalemia doesn't cause it."]},"fp108":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes cells (EK more negative), moving Vm farther from threshold → decreased excitability.</p><p>With lower [K⁺]out, K⁺ has a stronger tendency to leave, so EK and the RMP become more negative. More depolarization is required to reach threshold, so skeletal muscle becomes weak (even paralyzed in severe cases or hypokalemic periodic paralysis) and smooth muscle slows (ileus). In the heart, hypokalemia prolongs repolarization, causing U waves and increasing arrhythmia risk (especially with digoxin).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Increased excitability due to depolarization.</b> — Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes rather than depolarizes.</li><li><b>Increased excitability due to hyperpolarization.</b> — Hyperpolarization decreases, rather than increases, excitability.</li><li><b>Decreased excitability due to depolarization.</b> — Hypokalemia doesn't depolarize the membrane.</li><li><b>No significant effect on membrane potential.</b> — Vm changes with [K⁺]out.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Hypokalemia: weakness, ileus, U waves, arrhythmias.</p>","w":["Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes rather than depolarizes.","Hyperpolarization decreases, rather than increases, excitability.","Correct — hyperpolarization moves Vm further from threshold.","Hypokalemia doesn't depolarize the membrane.","Vm changes with [K⁺]out."]},"fp109":{"x":"<p class=\"bl\"><b>Key concept:</b> Myelination (and larger fiber diameter) increases conduction velocity; myelin enables saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier.</p><p>Myelin increases membrane resistance (less leak) and decreases capacitance (less charge needed to change voltage), so local current spreads farther and faster to the next node, where Na⁺ channels are concentrated. The AP \"jumps\" node to node. Larger diameter lowers internal resistance, also speeding conduction. Fastest fibers: large myelinated Aα (~70–120 m/s); slowest: small unmyelinated C fibers (~0.5–2 m/s). Myelin is made by Schwann cells (PNS) and oligodendrocytes (CNS).</p><h5>Why the other choices are wrong</h5><ul><li><b>Decreased fiber diameter.</b> — Smaller diameter raises internal resistance and slows conduction.</li><li><b>Increased internal resistance.</b> — Higher internal resistance slows conduction.</li><li><b>Demyelination.</b> — Demyelination slows or blocks conduction.</li><li><b>Decreased membrane resistance in myelinated areas.</b> — Lower membrane resistance lets current leak out and slows conduction.</li></ul><p class=\"hy\"><b>High-yield:</b> Conduction velocity ∝ diameter (unmyelinated: √d; myelinated: d) and myelination.</p>","w":["Smaller diameter raises internal resistance and slows conduction.","Higher internal resistance slows conduction.","Demyelination slows or blocks conduction.","Correct — myelination makes conduction saltatory and fast.","Lower membrane resistance lets current leak out and slows conduction."]}});
